window.DSH_DAILY_DATA = {"updatedAt":"2026-11-18 23:10 CST","dates":["2026-11-18","2026-11-17","2026-11-16","2026-11-13","2026-11-12","2026-11-10","2026-11-09","2026-11-06","2026-11-05","2026-11-04","2026-11-03","2026-11-02","2026-11-01","2026-10-30","2026-10-29","2026-10-28","2026-10-27","2026-10-26","2026-10-23","2026-10-22","2026-10-21","2026-10-20"],"batches":{"2026-11-18":{"papers":[{"id":"2611.21997","title":"Fundamental physics with quantum sensors in space","summary":"Quantum sensors such as atomic clocks, atom interferometers, and atomic magnetometers can be used as sensitive probes of physics beyond the Standard Model. Space offers microgravity, long baselines, low environmental noise, access to varying gravitational potentials, and unique opportunities involving the Sun, Moon, planets, and asteroids. These proceedings of the Aspen Center for Physics workshop on \"Fundamental physics in space with quantum sensors,\" held February 22-27, 2026, summarize the scientific case, current capabilities, and space mission concepts for investigating dark matter, testing general relativity, and detecting gravitational waves in frequency bands inaccessible from the ground.","authors":"Jason Arakawa, Daniel J. Blumenthal, Oliver Buchmueller, Dmitry Budker, Luigi Cacciapuoti, Emily D. Caldwell, Andrei Derevianko, Benjamin P. Dix-Matthews, Michael A. Fedderke, Naceur Gaaloul, Susan Gardner, Jonah Glick, Paul Hamilton, Elena Jordan, Hidetoshi Katori, Peter Kurczynski, David R. Leibrandt, Anna L. Marchant, Indra Periwal, Igor Pikovski, Marianna S. Safronova, Christian Sanner, Sascha W. Schediwy, Volodymyr Takhistov, Yu-Cheng Tien, Yu-Dai Tsai, Jason R. Williams","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.IM","gr-qc","hep-ph","physics.atom-ph","quant-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle","sensing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21997"},{"id":"2611.22481","title":"Entanglement in restricted experimental setups","summary":"Entanglement is a necessary resource for steering and Bell nonlocality, yet whether a state is separable takes no account of what an experiment can prepare or measure. We define two notions of separability relative to an experiment, one on either side of separability. Inner separability confines the local states to the preparable sets. Outer separability lets the local factors be self-adjoint operators positive on the available effects but not necessarily positive semidefinite. We find exactly when each departs from separability and that outer separability keeps the operational content of separability for the available measurements: outer separable states, entangled or not, admit local hidden variable models for every behaviour these measurements generate and, when the steered party is unrestricted, local hidden state models for every assemblage the steering party's measurements generate. Such hidden state models do not imply outer separability in general, but we identify conditions under which they do.","authors":"Rafiul Nakib, Martin Pl\\'avala","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22481"},{"id":"2611.22029","title":"Exact Dynamic Scaling of Quantum Jump Statistics in Monitored Many-Body Systems","summary":"Quantum measurement has opened a new frontier in nonequilibrium many-body physics, revealing striking phenomena such as measurement-induced entanglement transitions, whose experimental observation typically requires costly postselection. Quantum jump statistics offer another route to studying such measurement-induced many-body phenomena without postselection, motivating the search for universality in measurement outcomes themselves. In this Letter, we discover that fluctuation of the number of quantum jumps within a subsystem exhibits novel dynamic scaling, characterized by diffusive time scale and anomalous super-Poissonian system-size scaling that depend on the subsystem size in monitored many-body systems. For free fermions under continuous monitoring, we exactly determine the scaling exponent and the scaling function by deriving an exact finite-size solution of the Liouvillian autocorrelation function. We also analytically show that the dynamic scaling with the same scaling exponent and the scaling function even holds for interacting fermions in the limit of large measurement strength. Our work provides a novel framework to classify measurement-induced nonequilibrium many-body universality through experimentally friendly, postselection-free quantities.","authors":"Kazuki Yamamoto, Taiki Ishiyama, Toranosuke Matsubara, Kazuya Fujimoto, Tomohiro Sasamoto, Ryusuke Hamazaki","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.stat-mech","cond-mat.quant-gas","cond-mat.str-el","quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22029"},{"id":"2507.15453","title":"Entanglement preservation and Clauser-Horne nonlocality in electromagnetically induced transparency quantum memories","summary":"Understanding how decoherence-induced loss affects entanglement and nonlocal correlations in quantum memories is important for quantum information science. Here we develop a quantitative description of photonic quantum states retrieved from electromagnetically induced transparency (EIT) memories in a ground-state-decoherence-limited regime. Using the dark-state-polariton formalism, we derive the attenuation factor of the retrieved probe mode from the EIT dynamics. This attenuation depends on the coupling field and ground-state coherence decay and is not introduced as an independent phenomenological loss parameter. Within a specified quantum-limited pure-loss model, we use this attenuation to obtain the reduced photonic density operator. We show that, in the symmetric case, the complete retrieved state of an initially path-entangled single photon remains entangled for any nonzero storage efficiency, despite acquiring a vacuum component. In contrast, violation of the unconditional Clauser-Horne inequality under displaced on/off detection without post-selection requires an intrinsic efficiency above 89.7%. We further derive the retrieved N-qubit photonic density operator for multiple independently operating EIT memories. These results establish a quantitative connection between EIT memory dynamics, retrieved quantum states, and operational nonlocality benchmarks under explicit channel assumptions.","authors":"Po-Han Tseng, Yong-Fan Chen","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15453"},{"id":"2510.20096","title":"All-Gaussian State Discrimination Beyond the Coherent Helstrom Bound","summary":"A core problem in communications is the optimal discrimination of binary-phase-shift-keyed (BPSK) signals. A longstanding goal has been to reach the fundamental quantum limit, known as the Helstrom bound, for BPSK signals encoded in coherent states. However, due to technical constraints, proposals for reaching the bound remain impractical. In this letter we take an alternative approach: using only Gaussian optics - displaced squeezed states and homodyne detection - we achieve discrimination of BPSK signals with error rates below what can be achieved using coherent states and any quantum measurement.","authors":"Angus Walsh, Lorcan Conlon, Biveen Shajilal, Ozlem Erkilic, Jiri Janousek, Syed Assad, Jie Zhao, Ping Koy Lam","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20096"},{"id":"2611.08151","title":"Machine learning quantum data to accelerate quantum Monte Carlo","summary":"Simulating strongly correlated electrons in molecules and materials remains a central goal for quantum computing. A promising strategy uses quantum processors to prepare trial wavefunctions that guide classical quantum Monte Carlo (QMC). However, the enormous computational cost of repeatedly processing quantum measurements has severely limited the practical scalability of this approach. Here, we overcome this bottleneck by compressing quantum measurement data into machine-learning models that efficiently provide the wavefunction overlaps needed by QMC. This accelerates quantum-classical QMC by orders of magnitude, enabling chemically accurate simulations on up to 40 qubits, more than doubling the scale of previous hardware demonstrations with comparable accuracy. We demonstrate our approach on hydrogen chains and strongly correlated iron-sulfur clusters using measurements from Google's Willow processor. By enabling efficient reuse of quantum-state information on the classical coprocessor, our framework addresses a fundamental bottleneck in hybrid quantum algorithms that will persist even into the fault-tolerant era.","authors":"Tong Jiang, Andrew Zhao, Shashwat Kumar, Jigyasa Nigam, Eliott Rosenberg, Ryan Babbush, Andrew D. Baczewski, William J. Huggins, Joonho Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","physics.chem-ph"],"topics":["foundations","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08151"},{"id":"2510.17423","title":"Energy calibration of bulk events in the BULLKID detector","summary":"BULLKID is a cryogenic, solid-state detector designed for direct searches of particle Dark Matter candidates, with mass $\\lesssim 1$ GeV/c$^2$, and coherent neutrino-nucleus scattering. It is based on an array of dice carved in 5 mm thick silicon crystal, sensed by phonon-mediated Kinetic Inductance Detectors. In previous works, the array was calibrated with bursts of optical photons, which are absorbed in the first hundreds nanometers of the dice and give rise to surface events. In this work, we present the reconstruction of bulk events through the 59.5 keV $\\gamma$-ray generated by an $^{241}$Am source, which emulates more closely the interaction of Dark Matter and neutrinos. The peak resolution is $5\\%~(\\sigma)$ and its position is shifted by less than $10\\%$ with respect to the optical calibration. We observe that the resolution is further improved by a factor $2$ combining the signal from neighboring dice. These results confirm the performance of the detector in view of the physics goals of the BULLKID-DM experiment for dark matter search.","authors":"M. Folcarelli, D. Delicato, A. Acevedo-Renter\\'ia, L. E. Ardila-Perez, L. Bandiera, M. Calvo, M. Cappelli, R. Caravita, F. Carillo, U. Chowdhury, D. Crovo, A. Cruciani, A. D'Addabbo, M. De Lucia, G. Del Castello, M. del Gallo Roccagiovine, F. Ferraro, S. Fu, R. Gartmann, M. Grassi, V. Guidi, D. Helis, T. Lari, L. Malagutti, A. Mazzolari, A. Monfardini, T. Muscheid, D. Nicol\\`o, F. Paolucci, D. Pasciuto, L. Pesce, C. Puglia, D. Quaranta, C. M. A. Roda, S. Roddaro, M. Romagnoni, G. Signorelli, F. Simon, A. Tartari, E. V\\'azquez-J\\'auregui, M. Vignati, K. Zhao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["physics.ins-det"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.17423"},{"id":"2611.21714","title":"21 cm Cosmology of Boosted Dark Matter","summary":"The formation history of low-mass halos is sensitive to the properties of dark matter. At low redshift, nonlinear evolution and baryonic processes can obscure this information. The 21 cm signal provides access to a high-redshift stage of structure formation, when the abundance and clustering of low-mass halos retain direct sensitivity to the mass and interactions of dark matter. Boosted dark matter (BDM) realizes this possibility through a two-component scenario in which the annihilation of the heavier component converts part of the mass difference into kinetic energy of the lighter component. Self-interactions redistribute this energy and generate pressure support, suppressing small-scale density perturbations even for dark matter masses in the MeV range. We calculate the global 21 cm brightness temperature and its power spectrum in BDM cosmology. The reduced abundance of low-mass halos delays the global absorption feature and shifts the characteristic redshift evolution of the power spectrum. We assess the projected sensitivity of SKA1-Low to these signatures. The sensitivity is strongest at intermediate light-component fractions, where both components remain sufficiently abundant for self-heating to affect structure formation. For $\\sigma_{\\rm self1}/m_1\\simeq0.1\\,{\\rm cm^2\\,g^{-1}}$, models with $0.2\\lesssim r_1\\lesssim0.7$ can be distinguished from the fiducial $\\Lambda$CDM prediction at the $95\\%$ confidence level under the optimistic forecast. We compare our results with existing direct-detection bounds from SENSEI and prospects for BDM searches at large-volume neutrino experiments, including Super-/Hyper-Kamiokande, JUNO, and DUNE. This comparison highlights the unique and complementary role of high-redshift surveys in testing BDM models.","authors":"Min Sub Lee, Jeong Han Kim, Kyoungchul Kong, Jong-Chul Park","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21714"},{"id":"2611.21719","title":"Feebly-Interacting Particle Searches","summary":"In the last several years, feebly-interacting particles (FIPs) have moved from \"exotica\" to the mainstream of particle physics. This development is fueled by three motivations: FIPs are one of two broad possibilities for new particles, FIPs are as well motivated by dark matter as WIMPs, and FIPs are connected to important problems in particle physics. I review these motivations and then briefly highlight recent progress in a few areas, including axion-like particles, dark photons and other gauge bosons, the X17 anomaly, quirks, and the Forward Physics Facility.","authors":"Jonathan L. Feng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21719"},{"id":"2611.21728","title":"Plugging the Pion Chimney","summary":"Heavy QCD axions and other gluon-coupled axion-like particles (ALPs) with masses from MeV to GeV are well motivated and have been searched for extensively. However, a window around the neutral pion mass, the so-called pion chimney, has escaped every search. There the ALP mixes strongly with the $\\pi^0$ and therefore shares its experimental signatures. We show that the chimney can be closed with existing LHC data. The key point is that mixing changes the lifetimes of the two mass eigenstates but does not make them equal, and at LHC energies the states are boosted enough for the lifetime to decide whether they escape the detector or decay inside it. The ATLAS monojet search constrains the first case and the ATLAS inclusive photon measurement the second, and together they exclude the chimney up to the cutoff of the ALP effective theory. Above the cutoff, LHC searches for the colored states of the simplest ultraviolet completions exclude the rest. We also show that the bound from the $K^+$ width does not reach the chimney, because an ALP passes the same selection as a $\\pi^0$, and we derive an independent, purely kinematic constraint from the measured $\\pi^\\pm$-$\\pi^0$ mass splitting.","authors":"Nikita Blinov, Andrew M. Evans, Shirley Weishi Li","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21728"},{"id":"2611.21736","title":"Active Galactic Nuclei as Beyond the Standard Model Laboratories: a White Paper","summary":"Multi-messenger and multi-wavelength observations of Active Galactic Nuclei (AGN) can provide otherwise inaccessible knowledge about physics Beyond the Standard Model (BSM), and may even lead to its discovery. Realizing this potential hinges on two parallel efforts: advancing our understanding of AGN and of the dark matter around them, and identifying the BSM signatures that are at once promising and robust against the gaps in that understanding. These fields have attracted growing interest in recent years, also motivated by the IceCube association of neutrinos to individual AGN, yet we have only begun to explore their cross-fertilization. This white paper offers a new perspective on this interdisciplinary endeavor. It concisely reviews the state of the art in each field and at their interface, and identifies the key open questions that must be addressed to strengthen our prospects for learning about BSM physics with AGN.","authors":"Gonzalo Herrera, Filippo Sala, Piero Ullio, Misty C. Bentz, Carlos Blanco, Roberto Caiozzo, Marina Cerme\\~no, Matteo Cerruti, James M. Cline, Andrea Giovanni De Marchi, Pierfrancesco Di Cintio, Alessandro Granelli, R. Andrew Gustafson, Bradley J. Kavanagh, Cristina Lagunas Gualda, Kohta Murase, Takuya Okawa, Xavier Rodrigues, Lorenzo Ubaldi, Gabrijela Zaharijas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21736"},{"id":"2611.21737","title":"Thermal Composite Dark Matter at LZ","summary":"We show that stable fermionic resonances of Composite Higgs models provide a thermal inelastic Dark Matter interpretation of the high-energy nuclear-recoil event LZ230616. Their stability follows from an exact accidental parity of the strong sector. Annihilation channels from composite interactions link the thermal relic mass to the strong coupling, allowing masses well beyond the pure electroweak prediction. We identify three scenarios in which the neutral states form a split pseudo-Dirac pair, distinguished by the origin of the inelastic coupling to the $Z$ boson. In the pure electroweak regime, the pair consists of the neutral components of a complex Higgsino-like doublet. Its unsuppressed inelastic $Z$ coupling requires Dark Matter masses above 20 TeV. In the pure strong regime, the suppressed $Z$ coupling of neutral states with vanishing hypercharge arises from strong interactions alone, allowing for smaller splittings and TeV-scale masses. In the third regime, which interpolates between the previous two, the neutral components of multiplets with different hypercharge are mixed. This enhances their inelastic coupling, accommodating the LZ event for masses of a few TeV and splittings around 300 keV, with mixing angles of order $10^\\circ$. All three cases provide a thermal Dark Matter candidate able to explain the LZ event, consistently with the solar-capture bounds, the LZ high-energy sideband, and gamma-ray indirect searches.","authors":"Dario Buttazzo, Andrea Di Lecce, Roberto Franceschini, Giulio Marino, Paolo Panci","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21737"},{"id":"2611.21764","title":"$B\\to K a$ decays in QCD factorization and revised bounds on ALP couplings","summary":"Rare flavor-changing processes such as $B\\to K a$ offer sensitive probes of axion-like particles (ALPs) for masses below a few GeV. We study this process in the framework of QCD factorization and ALP effective theory, in which the ALP couples to the Standard Model (SM) fermions through shift-invariant derivative interactions. In contrast to previous studies, where flavor violation arises solely from flavor-changing ALP couplings, we also include the contributions proportional to flavor-conserving ALP couplings, in which the flavor change is mediated by the weak interactions of the SM. Based on this analysis, we derive upper bounds on both flavor-changing and flavor-conserving ALP couplings at low energies. Then we consider the UV scenario of a \"minimally flavor-violating ALP\", which features flavor-universal ALP-fermion couplings at very high energies, and evaluate the resulting constraints on the UV couplings.","authors":"Upalaparna Banerjee, Cristhian Calderon, Matthias Neubert","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21764"},{"id":"2511.10731","title":"Keeping It Renormalizable: Minimal Baryogenesis induced Asymmetric Dark Matter","summary":"Many asymmetric dark matter scenarios have been proposed to date. Among them, perhaps the most motivated ones are those in which the dark matter asymmetry is induced from the baryon/lepton asymmetries via chemical equilibration without any new sources of CP violation. However, most of the models put forward along these lines have been excluded by now and/or are based on complicated setups. In this letter, we present a new, simple, and viable scenario. It assumes only two new fields: a scalar singlet and an inert scalar doublet, and is based only on renormalizable interactions, that slowly generate the dark matter asymmetry from the Standard Model Higgs asymmetry. The successful asymmetry transfer requires small (but technically natural) couplings which in turn make the model CP conserving in practice. The model allows for the direct detection of dark matter in the upcoming generation of experiments, and the inert doublet is predicted to be light enough to be potentially produced and observed at the LHC and future colliders, $46\\,{\\rm GeV}<m_{H'}<580\\,{\\rm GeV}$. Quite remarkably, the mass and lifetime of the inert doublet may be inferred from direct detection experiments, indicating long-lived compressed electroweak states that can be searched for at the HL-LHC.","authors":"Miguel Escudero, Thomas Hambye, Chandan Hati","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10731"},{"id":"2605.29132","title":"Residual Nonextensivity from WIMP Freeze-Out: A Phenomenological Memory Channel","summary":"In this work we formulate the thermal decoupling, or freeze-out, of weakly interacting massive particle dark matter within a dynamical Tsallis framework. The parameter $q_\\chi$ is promoted to a function of $x=m_\\chi/T$, describing a relaxation toward extensivity that need not be completed before chemical decoupling. We adopt a sectorial approximation during freeze-out, with an extensive Standard Model bath, and specify the statistical weights and effective kinetic closure used for the dark matter abundance. The total derivatives of the equilibrium density contain the variation of $q_\\chi$, which changes the rate at which the abundance must evolve to follow its instantaneous reference state. The residual value $q_\\chi^{\\rm fo}$ then provides the input for a phenomenological memory channel into the electron-positron and neutrino sectors, while photons remain extensive. We derive the corresponding chain rule terms in the radiation energy and entropy balances and formulate the photon-reheating problem without assuming that comoving electromagnetic entropy is automatically conserved when $q$ varies. The resulting construction connects the freeze-out history with the neutrino-to-photon temperature ratio and $N_{\\rm eff}$. We discuss the physical motivation, closure assumptions, and conditions under which this description can be used, without presenting numerical results or observational fits.","authors":"Matias P. Gonzalez","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","cond-mat.stat-mech","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.29132"},{"id":"2606.16204","title":"Dark Matter Attenuation inside the Earth: A Boltzmann Equation Approach","summary":"For strongly interacting or boosted dark matter, propagation through the Earth can involve sizable scattering and energy loss, reshaping the underground flux in energy, direction, and normalization. Scattered particles may still fall within the detector acceptance, so the detector-side signal depends on phase-space transport from the Earth's surface to the underground detector. In this work, we formulate this transport problem with the Boltzmann equation. Its integral solution organizes successive scattering effects as a deterministic expansion in scattering orders. We analyze the transport equation in flat-Earth and spherical-Earth geometries, and apply the method to Dirac dark matter with an isoscalar vector interaction. The iterative solution agrees well with the Monte Carlo spectra.","authors":"Chuan-Yang Xing, Chen Xia","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.16204"},{"id":"2606.30910","title":"Phenomenology of Long-Lived Dark Photons and Axion-Like Particles in a Mixed Portal Framework","summary":"We investigate the phenomenology of a dark photon \\(A'\\) and an axion-like particle (ALP) ,\\(a\\), connected through a mixed portal framework which simultaneously allows the conventional visible decay $(A'\\rightarrow f\\bar f)$ and the exotic cascade process $(A'\\rightarrow a\\gamma\\rightarrow3\\gamma)$. We derive the relevant decay widths, branching ratios, and Lorentz-boosted decay lengths, and introduce a dominance parameter \\(D=\\Gamma(A'\\to a\\gamma)/\\Gamma_{\\rm SM}\\) to distinguish Standard Model-dominated and cascade-dominated regions, with the transition occurring at $(D=1)$. A detailed analysis of both light $(0.1\\leq m_{A'}\\leq10~{\\rm GeV})$ and heavy $(10\\leq m_{A'}\\leq100~{\\rm GeV})$ dark-photon scenarios shows that the exotic channel can substantially modify the expected dark-photon signatures, transforming otherwise long-lived or detector-stable states into experimentally accessible displaced multi-photon events. In addition, the ALP sector itself may exhibit long-lived particle behavior, leading to distinct displaced diphoton signatures. Our results show that mixed dark-photon-ALP portals offer a rich LLP phenomenology that can be explored at future high-luminosity lepton colliders such as the FCC-ee.","authors":"Caglar Zorbilmez, Beyhan Tatar, Azmi Ali Altintas, Cihan Bayindir, Fatih Ozaydin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30910"},{"id":"2608.04990","title":"Thermal Alignment as a Pathway to Axion Dark Matter","summary":"We study the relic energy of an axion whose homogeneous displacement is damped by a thermal gauge plasma. Dissipation is accompanied by thermal fluctuations. To calculate the energy at decoupling, we retain the covariance between the axion field and its velocity. For linear Langevin dynamics, positivity of the noise covariance provides a finite-interval lower bound on the energy generated by the bath, independently of the initial state. We consider a renormalizable $SU(2)_h\\times SU(3)_\\ell$ theory in which a scalar transition separates the early topological susceptibility and Chern--Simons diffusion from the confining potential acting at late times. With the specified scalar history and a local plasma response, fluctuations generated during the final cooling interval carry energy comparable to the coherent-only dark matter benchmark. The relic-density integral extends over finite momenta and cannot be inferred from the homogeneous topological diffusion rate. We also include the chiral-condensate term in the scalar free energy. For the chirally broken benchmark, this term removes the stationary high-temperature solution of the polynomial potential. Thus, a consistent microscopic realization requires both an assessment of thermal axion production and scalar phases that remain stationary in the full free energy.","authors":"Imtiaz Khan, G. Mustafa, Chengxun Yuan, Farkhod Botirov, Ahmadjon Abdujabbarov, Farruh Atamurotov","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04990"},{"id":"2611.21780","title":"Cross-correlating the SPHEREx extragalactic background light with CMB lensing II: theoretical predictions confronted with lightcone simulations","summary":"Cross-correlating intensity maps with the lensing of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) rejects the foregrounds and noise that do not trace the dark matter. In the near infrared, the remaining signal combines the integrated galaxy light (IGL), the intrahalo light (IHL) and several emission lines. Accurate modeling of all three is essential to forecast detectability and interpret measurements. We present simulated SPHEREx intensity maps of all three on a lightcone, painted on halos from the Hidden Valley $N$-body simulation, and the CMB-lensing convergence of the same lightcone, over $100\\,{\\rm deg}^2$ to $z \\approx 6$. We assign each halo its lines and continuum by stellar-population synthesis, in two models whose metallicities are fixed or follow its stellar mass. Halo-model predictions from the simulation's luminosity-halo mass relations and measured clustering, without free parameters, match the auto-spectra of the IGL and lines, and their cross-spectra with lensing, across the band: at $2.2\\,\\mu$m, to within $5\\%$ above $\\ell \\approx 400$ and $7\\%$ above $\\ell = 200$, respectively. The agreement requires nonlinear clustering: linear theory describes the cross-spectrum of a line up to $\\ell \\approx 1000$-$1600$ at $2.2\\,\\mu$m, but fails for the IGL and the convergence at multipoles of a few hundred. With the noise of a SPHEREx deep field and of Simons Observatory lensing, the unfiltered cross-spectra, each component taken alone, reach signal-to-noise ratios of $9$-$21$ for the lines, $20$ for the IGL and $15$ for the IHL. A filter that removes radial modes longer than about $600$ Mpc keeps $62\\%$ of the IGL's signal-to-noise and at most $20\\%$ of the lines', with fitted amplitudes within about $1\\sigma$ of the predictions before and after filtering. The emission model changes a channel's intensity by up to a factor of three, but the line detectability only by tens of percent.","authors":"Azadeh Moradinezhad Dizgah, Zucheng Gao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21780"},{"id":"2611.22387","title":"Probing Velocity-Dependent WIMP Dark Matter Annihilation in Large-Scale Structure","summary":"The unresolved gamma-ray background (UGRB) provides a promising avenue for probing the cosmological annihilation of weakly interacting massive particle (WIMP) dark matter through its cross-correlation with large-scale structure (LSS). While most previous studies have assumed a velocity-independent (s-wave) annihilation cross section, many well-motivated particle physics models predict annihilation rates that depend on the relative velocity of dark matter particles. In this work, we develop a generalized halo-model framework to investigate the cosmological signatures of velocity-dependent WIMP annihilation. Parameterizing the velocity-weighted annihilation cross section as $\\sigma v_{\\rm rel}\\propto v_{\\rm rel}^{n}$, we consider four representative cases corresponding to Sommerfeld-like (n=-1), s-wave (n=0), p-wave (n=2), and d-wave (n=4) annihilation. We show that velocity dependence modifies not only the overall annihilation emission but also the halo population dominating the signal, leading to distinct effective halo biases, redshift distributions, and angular clustering properties. As a result, different annihilation mechanisms leave characteristic signatures in the UGRB-LSS cross-correlation that cannot be captured by the overall gamma-ray intensity alone. As an illustration of the formalism, we perform a statistical forecast for the cross-correlation between the Fermi Large Area Telescope (LAT) UGRB and the Two Micron All Sky Survey Redshift Survey (2MRS) galaxy catalog and derive the projected sensitivities to the velocity-independent annihilation coefficient for these four annihilation scenarios. Our results demonstrate that cosmological cross-correlations provide a powerful and complementary probe of velocity-dependent dark matter annihilation, offering a new avenue for constraining the particle physics of dark matter through its imprints on large-scale structure.","authors":"Wen-Qing Guo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22387"},{"id":"2611.22498","title":"Using the 0i Einstein equation to break dark-sector degeneracies","summary":"Over the past decade, cosmological observations have started to challenge the $\\Lambda$CDM model. If these tensions persist, they immediately raise one fundamental question: what new physics could be responsible for them? More specifically, do these deviations arise from a modification of gravity, from an evolving or clustering dark energy component, or from dark matter properties beyond the cold dark matter paradigm? In this paper we show that, with current methods, any observed deviation can always be attributed to more than one sector, preventing us from determining if the tension is due to gravity, dark energy or dark matter. The main reason is that current tests rely on unobservable quantities. These quantities are then either related to observables by assuming the validity of specific equations, or compared with theoretical predictions computed within a given class of theories. Both approaches inevitably break the model independence of the tests, leading to degeneracies between gravity, dark energy, and dark matter. To overcome this limitation, we construct a set of three tests that rely only on observable quantities and that, when combined, can distinguish between the three sectors. We show that the $0i$ Einstein equation -- which is never tested in standard analyses -- adds crucial information to that provided by the gravitational slip equation and the Euler equation.","authors":"Camille Bonvin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22498"},{"id":"2504.14456","title":"Background dynamics and cosmological tests of interacting, viscous $H^2+H^{-2}$ dark energy","summary":"We study dark-sector energy exchange and bulk viscosity in the HHDE model, whose density combines terms proportional to the square and inverse square of the Hubble parameter. Five viscosity cases and seven interactions give 35 fluid realizations governed by a common autonomous system. When the intrinsic dark-energy pressure is reconstructed from the continuity equation, dark-energy viscosity leaves the expansion history unchanged. Interaction and dark-matter viscosity enter through their net contribution, producing further exact background degeneracies. Each realization admits stable de Sitter attractors in physical parameter domains, either dominated by dark energy or containing residual dark matter sustained by exchange and viscous stress. Representative posterior trajectories connect the inferred present states to these endpoints. Using supernovae, cosmic chronometers, DESI baryon acoustic oscillations and a compressed Planck distance prior, the density-product interaction yields a Hubble constant of $71.15^{+1.53}_{-1.64}$ km s$^{-1}$ Mpc$^{-1}$. Conditional on the applicability of that prior, the preferred transfer from dark energy to dark matter lowers the minimum chi-squared by 75.16 relative to noninteracting HHDE and reduces the discrepancy with the local distance-ladder measurement to approximately one standard deviation when both uncertainties are combined.","authors":"Guo Chen, Wei Fang, Chao-Jun Feng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.14456"},{"id":"2607.19494","title":"The silent dark sector: a field-theory approach to unified dark fluids with vanishing speed of sound","summary":"We introduce a novel class of field-theory models that provide a unified description of the dark sector as a single perfect fluid with vanishing sound speed. These models also admit the interpretation of cold dark matter interacting with vacuum energy while avoiding any phantom behavior and including standard $\\Lambda$CDM as a particular case. Built upon a single scalar field invariant under transverse diffeomorphisms, the framework naturally implements the constraints imposed in the so called mimetic models. We analyze a particular simple example exhibiting an effectively evolving dark energy and find that it can reproduce the DESI best-fit CPL evolution with the same number of free parameters.","authors":"Javier de Cruz P\\'erez, Dar\\'io Jaramillo-Garrido, Antonio L. Maroto, Prado Mart\\'in-Moruno","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19494"},{"id":"2611.18876","title":"Searching for Anisotropic Cosmic Birefringence with SPT-3G","summary":"We search for anisotropic cosmic birefringence using two years of data from the SPT-3G instrument on the South Pole Telescope. Anisotropic cosmic birefringence refers to a theorized dependence of the refractive index of the vacuum on the polarization of light that varies across the Universe in a statistically isotropic manner. Such an effect would manifest as a direction-dependent mixing of the $E$ and $B$ polarization modes of the cosmic microwave background (CMB), caused by rotation of the polarization angle as photons travel from the CMB last scattering surface to us. We present a measurement of the power spectrum of the rotation angle $\\alpha$ in six bins across the angular multipole range $20 < L < 2048$, finding no evidence for cosmic birefringence with the spectrum consistent with zero. At 95% confidence, we set a new best upper limit on the amplitude of a scale-invariant birefringence power spectrum of $A_{\\rm CB}<0.027$. Additionally, we place upper limits on a scale-dependent white noise model. We translate the scale-invariant upper limit to constraints on the axion coupling constant and primordial magnetic field strength, finding 95% confidence limits of $g_{\\alpha\\gamma} < 1.7\\times 10^{-2}/H_I$ and $B_{\\rm 1\\,Mpc} < 9.6\\,{\\rm nG}$ respectively. We expect these limits to improve several-fold with the complete SPT-3G survey.","authors":"A. Coerver, M. Rahimi, D. Anbajagane, E. Anderes, A. J. Anderson, B. Ansarinejad, M. Archipley, L. Balkenhol, D. R. Barron, P. S. Barry, K. Benabed, A. N. Bender, B. A. Benson, F. Bianchini, L. E. Bleem, S. Bocquet, F. R. Bouchet, E. Camphuis, M. G. Campitiello, J. E. Carlstrom, J. Carron, C. L. Chang, P. M. Chichura, A. Chokshi, T. -L. Chou, T. M. Crawford, C. Daley, T. de Haan, K. R. Dibert, M. A. Dobbs, M. Doohan, D. Dutcher, C. Feng, K. R. Ferguson, N. C. Ferree, K. Fichman, A. Foster, S. Galli, A. E. Gambrel, A. K. Gao, F. Ge, F. Guidi, S. Guns, N. W. Halverson, E. Hivon, G. P. Holder, W. L. Holzapfel, J. C. Hood, A. Hryciuk, N. Huang, T. Jhaveri, F. K\\'eruzor\\'e, A. R. Khalife, L. Knox, K. Kornoelje, C. -L. Kuo, K. Levy, Y. Li, A. E. Lowitz, C. Lu, G. P. Lynch, T. J. Maccarone, A. S. Maniyar, E. S. Martsen, F. Menanteau, M. Millea, J. Montgomery, Y. Nakato, T. Natoli, A. Ouellette, Z. Pan, P. Paschos, K. A. Phadke, K. Prabhu, W. Quan, A. Rahlin, C. L. Reichardt, M. Rouble, J. E. Ruhl, A. C. Silva Oliveira, A. Simpson, J. A. Sobrin, A. A. Stark, J. Stephen, C. Tandoi, Q. Taylor, C. Trendafilova, J. D. Vieira, A. G. Vieregg, A. Vitrier, Y. Wan, N. Whitehorn, W. L. K. Wu, M. R. Young, J. A. Zebrowski","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18876"},{"id":"2611.21911","title":"Self-gravitating Lee-Huang-Yang fluids as dark matter halos","summary":"We propose a model of dark matter halos based on a self-gravitating Bose fluid whose effective equation of state is dominated by Lee-Huang-Yang quantum fluctuations. The model is governed by coupled generalized Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson equations, which incorporate quantum pressure, Lee-Huang-Yang-induced repulsion, and gravitational attraction. By retaining the full quantum pressure, we examine the halo structure from its central region to its asymptotic regime. We first derive the Jeans dispersion relation and analyze how the Lee-Huang-Yang fluctuations alter the gravitational instability criterion and the associated characteristic length and mass scales. We then obtain analytical expressions for the central and large-radius behaviors of the equilibrium solutions. To verify the validity and the performance of the model, we fit the model-predicted curves with a sample of 20 galaxies from the SPARC database and compare the results with those obtained from Navarro-Frenk-White, standard Bose-Einstein condensate, and quantum-droplet dark matter models. We find that the pure-LHY model reproduces a broad range of observed galactic kinematics, yielding reduced $\\chi^2$ values and a lower sample average compared to the aforementioned models. These results indicate that the combined effects of quantum pressure, LHY quantum fluctuations and the self-gravity provide additional flexibility in describing both the central and extended outer regions of galactic halos. We also discuss the implications of the inferred halo parameters and emphasize their dependence on the assumed effective equation of state and its microscopic interpretation. The pure-LHY model constitutes an ideal platform for exploring the role of quantum-fluctuation-induced interaction in the formation and structure of dark matter halos.","authors":"Abdelaali Boudjemaa","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21911"},{"id":"2611.22134","title":"Finite-wavelength corrections to dynamical friction in fuzzy dark matter: a causal linear-response approach","summary":"A massive body moving through fuzzy dark matter (FDM) generates a density wake whose gravitational backreaction produces dynamical friction. We investigate how finite-wavelength effects modify this force using causal linear-response theory and the statistical Wigner formulation. For a homogeneous Maxwellian background and general isotropic finite-size perturbers, we derive an analytic representation of the gravitational drag and recover the classical Chandrasekhar result under matched regularization conditions. In the controlled semiclassical regime, the leading finite-wavelength correction is quadratic in $\\hbar/m_\\chi$ and factorizes into a velocity-dependent function and a ratio of spectral moments characterizing the source structure. Remarkably, this correction reduces the drag magnitude independently of the detailed radial source profile within the adopted Maxwellian model. Gaussian and exponential sources illustrate this result. We further establish a controlled remainder bound and clarify the nonuniform behavior of the point-source limit. These findings identify the physical conditions under which wave effects modify classical dynamical friction.","authors":"Yu Wang, Meilin Liu, Haiguang Xu, Shoupan Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22134"},{"id":"2610.19522","title":"Nambu's Dynamics, Strings and Gravitized Quantum Theory","summary":"In this article we present a new view on string theory that is rooted in quantum foundations extended to the domain of quantum gravity in the form of gravitized quantum theory, in which the canonical probability measure captured by the Born rule of quantum theory, turns out to be dynamical and contextual. We claim that this gravitization of quantum theory is the essence of string theory. String theory, according to this view, originates in foundations of quantum theory and Nambu's dynamics, which features in the non-perturbative definition of string theory in terms of a T-duality covariant and non-commutative formulation, called the metastring. Nambu's dynamics and the associated cubic and hypercubic objects also appear as the natural language for exploring the new observables and phenomena implied by a dynamical Born rule, including triple and higher order quantum interference, that can be investigated in experiments involving massive quantum probes, and that are also needed for direct empirical tests of the Born rule, a cornerstone of quantum theory.","authors":"Tristan H\\\"ubsch, Djordje Minic","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["foundations","qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19522"},{"id":"2611.21889","title":"Quantum Fisher information matrices for time-evolved bosonic Gaussian thermal states","summary":"Quantum Fisher information (QFI) is a fundamental quantum metrological quantity arising from the second order term in a Taylor expansion of a smooth divergence evaluated at nearby quantum states. The $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix is a QFI measure arising from the $\\alpha$-$z$ R\\'enyi relative entropy, and QFI measures corresponding to various underlying smooth divergences may be recovered from the $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix, including the standard symmetric logarithmic derivative (SLD) QFI. Bosonic Gaussian states are common probe states for quantum metrological tasks and admit a complete characterization in terms of their first and second moments. In this work, we consider the quantum metrological task of estimating parameters that characterize a Gaussian unitary time evolution of a faithful bosonic Gaussian thermal state. We establish a formula for the $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix of faithful bosonic Gaussian thermal states that have undergone a Gaussian unitary time evolution. As an example, we calculate entries of the $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix for a two-mode Gaussian thermal state that has undergone a beam splitting operation. For a parametrized family of time-evolved bosonic Gaussian states, we also derive a formula for the SLD operator, which is known to provide a measurement scheme that saturates the quantum Cr\\'amer-Rao bound and thus achieves the ultimate precision for single parameter estimation.","authors":"Hailey S. Murray, Diego Estrada, Mark M. Wilde","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["sensing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21889"},{"id":"2611.22428","title":"Generating Massive Mechanical Superpositions through Qubit-Mediated Interference","summary":"We propose a method towards demonstrating a quantum superposition of a massive mechanical resonator without the need for exact ground-state cooling, a requirement that becomes increasingly difficult to satisfy for low-frequency resonators. Our scheme relies on coupling the resonator to a spin-qubit. Using magnetic force coupling between the motion of the mechanical resonator and the spin-qubit, the resonator is forced into attracted and repelled states as the spin-qubit is coherently controlled. Interference between the resonator states can be observed when a series of $\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$-pulses is applied to the spin-qubit within the coherence time of the resonator. The protocol describes a true which-path interference experiment suitable for use at ultra-low temperatures, since it requires only three $\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$-pulses, separated in time by half a mechanical period, thus minimizing the heat-load and duration of the experiment. By analysing the experimental requirements necessary to successfully execute the protocol, we show that the protocol is suitable for resonators in a broad regime of resonance frequencies, ranging from 1kHz resonators, which have the advantage of a relatively high spin-coupling, to resonators of more than 100kHz, whose mechanical mode is easier to cool. We show that interference remains visible for resonator mode occupations exceeding 10 phonons. Lastly, we observe that an implementation of the protocol with low-frequency mechanical resonators provides a promising approach towards testing objective collapse models.","authors":"Jos\\'e P. Dupont, Loek van Everdingen, Tjerk H. Oosterkamp, Louk Rademaker","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22428"},{"id":"2105.06037","title":"A Diamond Quantum Oscilloscope","summary":"Quantum-enhanced metrology has achieved remarkable precision in parameter estimation, but extending these advantages to continuous time-dependent fields (waveform sensing) remains a formidable challenge due to the infinite-dimensional nature of the signal. Here, we report the first experimental realization of a room-temperature solid-state quantum oscilloscope based on a sequential quantum difference amplification strategy. Within a zero-order-hold reconstruction framework, our method completely bypasses the need for massive entanglement. Crucially, we demonstrate that the overall waveform reconstruction error unequivocally follows the theoretically predicted Heisenberg scaling of \\(O(N^{-1/2})\\), decisively surpassing the classical \\(O(N^{-1/3})\\) limit. This transformative precision requires approximately 30-fold fewer quantum resources than conventional methods to achieve the same waveform error. Furthermore, we demonstrate the reconstruction of a multicomponent waveform and distinctly resolve weak high-frequency components that are entirely drowned in the shot noise of classical sampling. These results transition quantum metrology from parameter estimation into a practical instrument, establishing a genuine quantum advantage in continuous waveform reconstruction and paving the way for critical applications such as nanoscale nuclear magnetic resonance imaging, where detecting extremely weak signals is crucial for resolving molecular structures.","authors":"Yang Dong, Yu Zheng, Wang Jiang, Zhi-Wei Liu, Shao-Chun Zhang, Xiang-Dong Chena, Guang-Can Guo, Fang-Wen Sun","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["sensing","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06037"},{"id":"2511.22476","title":"Improved parameter initialization for the (local) unitary cluster Jastrow ansatz","summary":"The unitary cluster Jastrow (UCJ) ansatz and its variant known as local UCJ (LUCJ) are promising choices for variational quantum algorithms for chemistry due to their combination of physical motivation and hardware efficiency. The parameters of these ansatzes can be initialized from the output of a coupled cluster, singles and doubles (CCSD) calculation performed on a classical computer. However, truncating the number of repetitions of the ansatz, as well as discarding interactions to accommodate the connectivity constraints of near-term quantum processors, degrade the approximation to CCSD and the resulting energy accuracy. In this work, we propose two methods to improve the parameter initialization. The first method, which is applicable to both expectation value- and sample-based algorithms, uses compressed double factorization of the CCSD amplitudes to improve or recover the CCSD approximation. The second method, which is applicable to sample-based algorithms, uses approximate tensor network simulation to improve the quality of samples produced by the ansatz circuit. Both methods perform classical preprocessing to construct the initial circuit parameters and can be followed by parameter optimization using quantum measurements, although we do not perform such optimization in this work. We validate our methods using exact state vector simulation on systems of up to 52 qubits, as well as experiments on superconducting quantum processors using up to 65 qubits. Our results indicate that our methods can significantly improve the output of both expectation value- and sample-based quantum algorithms.","authors":"Wan-Hsuan Lin, Fangchun Liang, Mario Motta, Haimeng Zhang, Kenneth M. Merz Jr., Kevin J. Sung","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.22476"},{"id":"2610.25518","title":"Randomness Certification from Statistically Hiding Commitments","summary":"Randomness certification aims to guarantee that the output of an untrusted quantum device contains genuine randomness that remains unpredictable even given quantum side information held by an adversary. For a classical verifier interacting with a single quantum device, such a guarantee cannot rely on the physical separation underlying standard Bell tests and must instead follow from computational restrictions on the prover. Building on the framework of computational Bell inequalities, we distinguish classical and quantum virtual-input hiding, showing that the former suffices for proofs of quantumness, whereas the latter enables randomness certification against quantum side information. Our main technical contribution is to establish quantum virtual-input hiding for a commitment-based protocol derived from an earlier proof of quantumness (Morimae and Yamakawa, CRYPTO, 2024). We show that statistical hiding together with ordinary computational binding against quantum polynomial-time senders suffices, without requiring stronger quantum binding notions such as sum-binding or collapse-binding. This yields a randomness-certification protocol from post-quantum statistically hiding bit commitments. We give three instantiations. The NOVY commitment from any post-quantum one-way permutation yields randomness certification in the standard model with an inefficient classical verifier. Post-quantum trapdoor claw-free functions additionally provide trapdoor inversion, enabling an efficient classical verifier. In the quantum random-oracle model, a hash-based commitment yields randomness certification without additional computational assumptions, again with an inefficient verifier. These instantiations separate the cryptographic properties sufficient for randomness certification from the additional structure required for efficient verification within our commitment-based framework.","authors":"Ilya Merkulov, Rotem Arnon","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25518"},{"id":"2611.21560","title":"Optimal Quantum Sensing Algorithms for Discrete-Frequency Spectroscopy","summary":"Spectroscopy is a versatile tool that drives scientific discovery and underpins modern technologies. Quantum sensing offers promising opportunities to advance spectroscopy through enhanced sensitivity and spectral and spatial resolution. However, existing approaches largely focus on improving general measurement capabilities, often overlooking the structure of specific sensing tasks and leaving potential performance gains untapped. Here, we develop an algorithmic approach to quantum sensing tailored to various spectroscopic tasks, providing new avenues for improvement. Specifically, we consider general time-varying signals supported on a known set of $N$ candidate frequencies, a setting we call discrete-frequency spectroscopy. We formulate three types of practically motivated sensing tasks---Detection, Search, and Learning---that require progressively more spectral information. For all tasks, we design optimal quantum sensing algorithms achieving a $\\sqrt{N}$ speedup over conventional frequency-scanning approaches. Optimal Detection and Search need only a single-qubit sensor under $\\pi$-pulse control, whereas optimal Learning provably requires coherent processing with a quantum computer. End-to-end numerical simulations incorporating various experimental imperfections predict up to two-orders-of-magnitude speedup in Detection. As an example, we analyze the feasibility of our Detection and Search algorithms for real-world molecular screening based on state-of-the-art nitrogen-vacancy centers. We also show how quantum-computing-enhanced Learning enables wireless communication in a weak-signal regime where conventional methods fail. Our results establish a practical route toward realizing quantum advantages via designing task-specific sensing algorithms.","authors":"Yu-Jie Liu, Richard R. Allen, Ruohan Shen, Bingtian Ye, Timothy T. Chang, Joonhee Choi, Soonwon Choi","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["sensing","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21560"},{"id":"2611.21681","title":"Remark on internal relaxation in large number qubit systems","summary":"For a time $t_{c}$ of calculation in an $N$ qubit quantum computer that is much shorter than the intrinsic Heisenberg time $t_H$, $t_c\\ll t_H$, the dynamics of the system is effectively irreversible and the couplings between the qubits give rise to a relaxation time scale $\\tau_r$. If $t_H\\gg t_c >\\sim \\tau_r$ one looses quantum coherence exponentially fast. Since $t_H$ scales exponentially with $N$ there is a threshold $N_c$ such that for $N\\gg N_c$ one must be aware of possible intrinsic relaxation during calculation. As an order of magnitude estimation we find $N_c\\approx \\ln(\\tau_r/T)$ where $T$ is a typical system specific oscillation time of a single qubit. $\\tau_r$ can be estimated as the typical inverse transition rate between two parts of the system, one of which is treated as an environment of the other.","authors":"Martin Jan{\\ss}en","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.stat-mech"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21681"},{"id":"2611.21748","title":"Restoring quantum neural network trainability through reusable calibration","summary":"Quantum neural networks (QNNs) rely on measured quantum observables to guide optimization, but hardware noise can suppress the parameter-dependent signals essential for learning. Here, we develop a structure-preserving error-mitigation framework that improves QNN trainability through reusable calibration of the quantum circuit's effective noise response. By calibrating a reference circuit with the same gate architecture as the trainable circuit, we construct regularized estimators of local quantum features that remain fixed throughout training. We quantify how calibration errors and response mismatches affect the recovery of both observables and their derivatives. Numerical simulations demonstrate enhanced loss-landscape contrast, faster convergence, and improved classification performance, and an IBM Quantum experiment confirms improved closed-loop variational optimization under hardware noise. We further demonstrate calibration reuse in a modular QNN comprising 25 independent four-qubit channels, corresponding to 100 qubits in aggregate. Our results establish reusable feature calibration as a practical approach to improving noise-limited quantum machine learning without repeated calibration at every training step.","authors":"Ruizhe Shen, Xianquan Yan, Kenji Kawaguchi, Ching Hua Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21748"},{"id":"2611.21749","title":"Robust Passive Quantum Memory in Three Dimensions, Part I: Thermal and Zero-Temperature Perturbative Stability","summary":"Whether a three-dimensional quantum many-body system with geometrically local, bounded-degree interactions can passively protect encoded quantum information has remained a central open question since Kitaev introduced topological quantum computation. Passive protection requires stability against both thermal noise at nonzero temperature and generic static Hamiltonian perturbations Balasubramanian, Davydova, and Lin (BDL) recently established unperturbed three-dimensional thermal self-correction using a recursively defined family of stabilizer Hamiltonians. However, they left open whether their construction remains stable against quantum perturbations, even at zero temperature. In this paper, we show that a slight variant of the BDL code family achieves stability along both boundary axes of the perturbation-temperature phase diagram as the system size grows. Following BDL, in the absence of perturbation it is thermally stable below a critical temperature, achieving a stretched-exponentially long memory lifetime when coupled to any local Markovian thermal bath generated by single-site system-bath interactions satisfying detailed balance with bounded coupling rates. At zero temperature under arbitrary weak, superpolynomially decaying quantum perturbations, the family maintains a uniform bulk spectral gap, superpolynomially small ground-space splitting, and a superpolynomially long closed-system unitary memory lifetime for quasi-locally dressed logical observables. To prove this zero-temperature stability, we establish local topological quantum order across the multiscale hierarchy using a linearly dilated relaxation of the usual Bravyi-Hastings-Michalakis criteria. In the companion paper, we combine the tools developed here with finite-temperature worldline cluster expansions to establish passive self-correction throughout the phase diagram's positive-temperature perturbed interior.","authors":"Patrick Hayden","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.str-el","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21749"},{"id":"2611.21785","title":"Microwave-optical quantum transduction with quantum-well exciton polaritons","summary":"Linking superconducting quantum processors over optical fiber requires microwave-optical quantum transducers that are efficient, high-bandwidth, and low-noise. This combination has been out of reach because of the weak nonlinearities of conventional electro-optic (EO) materials. We propose a transducer with an engineered, strong EO nonlinearity using the quantum-confined Stark effect (QCSE) of quantum-well excitons. The QCSE transducer leverages the strong light-matter interactions and extreme polarizability of quantum well excitons. Strong coupling to an optical cavity forms exciton-polaritons whose vacuum EO coupling is up to five orders of magnitude larger than in a typical thin-film lithium niobate device. We describe a GaAs/AlGaAs stepped-quantum-well transducer design with predicted transduction efficiencies above 60% and fewer than 0.01 added noise quanta over a $\\sim$60 MHz bandwidth, using pump powers below 10 $\\mu$W and loaded optical and microwave quality factors of only $3\\times10^4$ and $10^3$. We further show that the cavity protection of the polariton provides robustness against inhomogeneous broadening of the quantum well excitons.","authors":"Nicholas S. Yama, Mohammad Khalifa, Yuxuan Wei, Adbhut Gupta, Loren N. Pfeiffer, Feng Wang, Alp Sipahigil","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21785"},{"id":"2611.21840","title":"Quantum-Classical Separation in Probability Distribution Transport via Nonlocal Games","summary":"In many-body physics and generative artificial intelligence, locality provides a natural notion of phase equivalence: two quantum states or classical probability distributions are regarded as in the same phase when local dynamics can reversibly transform one into the other. This raises a fundamental question: can local quantum dynamics transport classical probability distributions in ways that local classical dynamics cannot? We establish a quantum-classical separation in distribution transport: two classical distributions can be connected exactly and reversibly by a constant-depth local quantum circuit, whereas classical stochastic transport requires a substantially larger spatial range. We demonstrate this separation explicitly on a one-dimensional chain and develop a general construction principle on graphs, based on nonlocal games. Moreover, the source distribution can itself be generated by a simple local classical process, establishing a quantum advantage in distribution generation as well. Our results reveal a fundamental distinction between classical and quantum local reversibility, even when both the initial and final states are entirely classical.","authors":"Fangjun Hu, Xun Gao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.CC","cs.LG","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21840"},{"id":"2611.21906","title":"Probing the real-time decoding accuracy bounds in trapped ion architectures","summary":"Fault tolerant quantum computers will rely on real-time decoders that process syndromes faster than the QEC cycle duration. The millisecond cycle times offered by trapped ion platforms relax low-latency decoding demands, but simulations and experiments have not yet exploited this time budget to reach maximum logical error suppression. We show how a hybrid sliding window decoding scheme composed of a lower-accuracy and a higher-accuracy decoder can yield substantially improved logical error rates, while preserving low latency. All windows are handled by the lower-accuracy decoder, with some windows being infrequently switched to the higher-accuracy decoder. We extract soft-output information from the lower-accuracy decoder efficiently for any QLDPC code, which allows us to identify decoding windows of low confidence. We simulate memory experiments of bivariate bicycle (BB) codes, using trapped-ion informed noise models. Depending on the physical error rate and BB code, we find up to 3-4 orders of magnitude logical error rate reduction compared to the $\\texttt{BP-LSD}$ decoder, and up to 2-3 orders of magnitude lower logical error rate compared to the $\\texttt{Beamsearch}$ decoder. By leveraging available classical processing time, our decoding protocol significantly boosts decoding accuracy, ultimately reducing the demands on increasing code size.","authors":"Arianna Meinking, Evangelia Takou, Kenneth R. Brown","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21906"},{"id":"2611.21928","title":"Simplifying models of noisy stabilizer QEC on leakage qubits by Pauli twirling","summary":"The growing capabilities of quantum error-correction (QEC) experiments make it increasingly important to develop methods that predict or assess logical performance over many QEC cycles. Prior work has shown that a stabilizer QEC cycle subject to Pauli-stochastic noise can be modeled by a classical Markov process over syndrome states, with a logical Pauli channel attached to each syndrome transition. Here, we extend this framework to systems in which physical qubits are subject to leakage. We first develop the theory of leakage-dephased Pauli twirling and show that twirled processes can be expressed in terms of Pauli maps on the computational subspace, conditioned on transitions between leakage patterns. We apply this theory to noisy stabilizer QEC with leakage, demonstrating that a QEC cycle with leakage can be modeled by a classical Markov process over the joint syndrome and leakage patterns, with a logical Pauli channel attached to each joint transition. Finally, we derive a transfer-matrix description of repeated QEC cycles and an exact transfer-matrix expression for the success probability of a QEC memory experiment. This framework connects leaky error models of the individual components of the QEC cycle to many-cycle logical performance and provides a foundation for logical characterization and benchmarking methods that can account for leakage.","authors":"Alex Kwiatkowski, Aaron J. Friedman","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21928"},{"id":"2611.22087","title":"Commutator-guided repair and classical-accessibility boundaries for shallow quantum circuits","summary":"Trainability does not guarantee low-energy arrival: a shallow quantum circuit can be optimizable yet fail, under fixed depth and evaluation budgets, to reach Hamiltonian-selected states. We introduce a commutator-guided repair for this operational failure mode. Low-order host--Hamiltonian commutators and fixed same-support companion axes define a restricted pool of local Pauli rotations, from which a compact completion layer is selected before full reoptimization. Across 189 paired six-qubit trials, unrepaired circuits achieve 0/189 strict successes, a non-deployable per-trial control envelope achieves 109/189, and two predeclared selectors achieve 133/189 and 127/189. The repair loses to matched controls for transverse-field Ising, is weak for pure-ZZ MaxCut, and remains size-limited for weighted MIS. Under fixed-depth geometric locality, repaired losses and gradients are classically computable, while corresponding ground-state targets admit low-bond-dimension tensor-network approximations through the tested sizes. The method reveals a trainability--arrival--classical-accessibility tradeoff rather than quantum advantage.","authors":"Yifeng Peng, Sunpreet Singh Arora, Harshal Shah, Srinivasan Raghuraman, Kim Wagner, Bin Yan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22087"},{"id":"2611.22108","title":"Perturbative Recovery of Stabilizer-Subspace Correlations for Quantum Computational Chemistry","summary":"Stabilizer-based subspace methods can substantially reduce the quantum resources required for electronic structure calculations, but the resulting projection neglects Hamiltonian couplings to configurations outside the retained subspace. In this work, we introduce Stabilizer-Subspace Perturbation Theory (SSPT) to recover the associated correlation contributions, using the Contextual Subspace Variational Quantum Eigensolver (CS-VQE) as a representative application. The central idea is to exploit the Pauli terms eliminated by stabilizer projection as perturbative operators that generate an external manifold from the optimized reference state. Their energetic contributions are evaluated using an Epstein-Nesbet-type second-order correction or a resolvent-based formulation that retains couplings within the external space. By exploiting the factorized structure of the stabilizer-constrained reference state, the required matrix elements can be evaluated through Pauli expectation values on the reduced contextual register without explicitly reconstructing the full-space wave function. Molecular benchmarks demonstrate substantial reductions in projection-induced energy errors, allowing chemical precision to be achieved with fewer contextual qubits in several cases. These results establish stabilizer-guided perturbation theory as a promising strategy for improving the accuracy of resource-efficient quantum chemistry calculations.","authors":"Qianjun Yao, Jie Liu, He Li","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","physics.chem-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22108"},{"id":"2611.22118","title":"Variational Relevance-Informed Sparsification of Term Measurements for Variational Quantum Algorithms","summary":"Repeated measurements are a major bottleneck in variational quantum algorithms (VQAs). We uncover and characterize parameter-specific sparsity in the matrix of qubit-wise commuting (QWC) group contributions to parameter-shift gradients: most absolute contribution mass concentrates in a subset of groups whose relevance varies across parameters and optimization states. Building on this structure, we introduce a learned controller that predicts group relevance from Hamiltonian, circuit, light-cone, and current-state features, then selects measurements through a tunable coverage threshold. Exact contributions provide offline supervision without being required at deployment. Across 15 molecular VQE settings, our method saves 49.6% of nominal gradient-measurement shots with a mean absolute energy gap of 5.67 mHa to Full-QWC. We then ask whether large language models (LLMs) can solve the same selection problem without fine-tuning. The results reveal both promise and a clear limitation: LLMs can identify useful measurement groups, yet without guidance, local models do not consistently prioritize the physical information that matters most. Our learned controller uses these cues more effectively. This contrast points toward a broader direction for AI-assisted science: combining the flexibility of general-purpose models with explicit physical representations, task-specific objectives, and domain-grounded validation.","authors":"Yifeng Peng, Bin Yan, Harshal Shah, Srinivasan Raghuraman, Kim Wagner, Sunpreet Singh Arora","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22118"},{"id":"2611.22125","title":"Exceptional-point Sensing with Quantum Advantage","summary":"Exceptional-point sensors exploit singular non-Hermitian dynamics to enhance their response to weak perturbations. Recently, however, a no-go result has been established for a gain-loss-balanced dimer coupled to independent, phase-insensitive reservoirs: the exceptional-point susceptibility amplifies the signal and reservoir noise equally, precluding any quantum advantage. Here, we circumvent this limitation by engineering non-Hermitian dynamics in particle-hole Nambu space. A single common reservoir acts on modes forming a quantum-mechanics-free subsystem and generates an enhanced response protected from additional noise. We demonstrate quantum-metrological advantage for weak-signal sensing and static-parameter estimation. A continuous interpolation of the operator basis at fixed exceptional-point drift, with a singular midpoint, reveals how reservoir noise vanishes toward the protected limit. Our results provide a route to exceptional-point sensing with genuine quantum-metrological advantage through reservoir engineering.","authors":"Chuming Wang, Xinyao Guo, Dezhi Zhang, Mingchen Liu, Yifei Li, Haixing Miao, Yiqiu Ma","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22125"},{"id":"2611.22166","title":"Demonstration of a Qubit Control Scheme Compatible with Superconducting Digital Signals","summary":"Superconducting quantum computing faces a critical scalability bottleneck arising from the escalating complexity of traditional analog control infrastructure. Single-Flux-Quantum(SFQ) systems operate at cryogenic temperatures and offer a potential route to addressing these challenges. Using microwave signals at qubit-dependent frequencies to trigger SFQ pulses requires many independent analog microwave sources, limiting the potential to reduce control wiring and increase integration density. A scalable SFQ control system therefore needs a scheme that can address qubits with different transition frequencies using a common clock. Here, we propose and experimentally demonstrate a control scheme for implementing both single- and two-qubit gates. The scheme uses guiding waveforms to rapidly generate bipolar high-speed pulse sequences on a common clock, providing a basis for future implementation in SFQ control systems. Numerically, this scheme achieves a simulated fidelity of 99.99% for 40ns $R(\\varphi, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$ gates with arbitrary initial phases and 99.9% for 100ns controlled-Z(CZ) gates. Experimentally, by utilizing an arbitrary waveform generator(AWG) operated at a fixed sampling rate of 25GSa/s to deliver the bipolar high-speed pulse sequences, we successfully demonstrate 40ns single-qubit gates with an average gate fidelity of 99.94% estimated using cross-entropy benchmarking(XEB) and 120ns CZ gates with 99.63% fidelity. These results demonstrate the feasibility of common-clock encoded pulse control for both single- and two-qubit gates.","authors":"Chenyin Sun, Yu Xu, Zihua Chen, Dayu Li, Tianzuo Sun, Chen Zha, Yuan Li, Jin Lin, Lei Jiang, Tao Rong, Tao Jiang, Shaoyu Zhan, Xueshen Wang, Shijian Wang, Wenhui Cao, Yeru Wang, Xiaomin Wang, Ming Gong, Yulin Wu, Yuhuai Li, Gang Wu, Hui Deng, Kai Yan, Sirui Cao, Futian Liang, Fusheng Chen, Qingling Zhu, Yangsen Ye, Hao Rong, Shaojun Guo, Haoran Qian, Haibin Zhang, Yisen Hu, Shaowei Li, Jinjin Li, Cheng-Zhi Peng, Xiaobo Zhu, Jian-Wei Pan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22166"},{"id":"2611.22247","title":"CHEQ: A Concurrent Hardware Evaluation Platform for QEC Decoders via Batch-Invariant Circuit-Level Noise Sampling","summary":"Hardware quantum error correction (QEC) decoders require validation of logical error suppression and timing compliance before deployment. Rare logical failures and deadline violations demand extensive sampling, while algorithm-level software evaluation does not directly measure hardware response times. Circuit-level noise models contain many error mechanisms but few firings per shot at low physical error rates, making exhaustive per-shot scanning inefficient. We present CHEQ, an FPGA platform integrating on-chip sampling, concurrent decoder evaluation, scoring, and RISC-V control. Its Sampling Units use geometric skipping to sample firing locations from detector error models and amortize mechanism traversal across shots within each batch. Under independent random draws and exact geometric sampling, the shot distribution is invariant to batch size, enabling a memory-throughput tradeoff. A throughput model guides joint selection of the Sampling Unit count, batch size, and decoder count under FPGA resource constraints. Across evaluated conditions, the pooled one-sided 95% upper bound on the weighted root-mean-square relative firing-rate difference from Stim is 0.16%. At a batch size of 4,096, a Sampling Unit uses only 4.8%-11.2% of a Helios decoder's LUTs for code distances 7, 9, 11, and 13. On an AMD VCU118 FPGA at code distance 7 and physical error rate 10^-3, two Sampling Units supply 20 Helios decoders at 49.2 million shots/s, completing 10^10 shots in 3.4 minutes without host-side shot generation.","authors":"Sechan Park, Kyeongwon Lee, Mundo Jung, Chaebin Jung, Woojoo Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22247"},{"id":"2611.22315","title":"Circuit compilation in the quspect quantum SDK: selecting routings after coupling-constrained resynthesis","summary":"quspect is an open-source quantum computing SDK that provides circuit construction, C++ state-vector simulation with adjoint gradients, support for variational and combinatorial-optimization algorithms, Hamiltonian simulation, control-pulse envelopes and backends for several cloud quantum processors. Here we describe and evaluate its circuit compiler. Routing and synthesis interact: inserting a SWAP can change the circuit structure that later optimization exploits. At its highest optimization level, the compiler evaluates a screened set of routing candidates by their cost after coupling-constrained resynthesis. The pipeline combines parity-state A* search for small CNOT-phase regions, heuristic synthesis for larger regions, and numerical three-qubit instantiation with a per-block 10^-9 check. For a fixed phase polynomial, A* certifies the minimum CX count when it reaches a goal within budget. On an evaluation set of 55 circuits from 41 arXiv papers on five topologies, the compiler uses 0.616 times as many two-qubit gates as Qiskit O3 (95% circuit-cluster bootstrap interval 0.555-0.679) at 0.592 times its depth. BQSKit level 2 reaches 0.853 and tket 0.930. On a 17-qubit superconducting processor, the compiled circuits had the fewest CZ gates in all six scenarios of the main run. Ablations assess the contributions within the integrated pipeline.","authors":"Xusheng Xu, Runqing Zhang, Hongtao Xue, Jize Han, Jingwei Wen, Ruoyu Zhou, Lei Jin, Xiaoxiao Hu, Gui-Lu Long","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22315"},{"id":"2611.22389","title":"Nonlinear function regression using quantum ensemble learning","summary":"Recent advances in artificial intelligence and quantum technologies have driven the emergence of quantum machine learning, yet imperfections in near-term quantum devices remain a major obstacle for achieving practical quantum advantage. In the absence of fault-tolerant machines, ensemble learning, which combines multiple imperfect quantum devices, may unlock new quantum computing applications. Here, we introduce two ensemble-learning algorithms, namely Bagging and AdaBoost, for enhancing the accuracy of nonlinear regression problems. While Bagging trains variational quantum regressors independently, AdaBoost employs a sequential training strategy and achieves significantly better prediction accuracy. By incorporating systematic feature selection into our ensemble learning algorithms, we enhance their predictive power for highly nonlinear functions. We further improve prediction accuracy through a feature-to-qubit mapping strategy based on repeated feature encoding. The performance of our ensemble-based algorithms improves with both the number of regressors in the ensemble and the size of each regressor. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach using four benchmarking functions with varying degrees of nonlinearity by relying on a feature pool limited to first- and second-order polynomial terms.","authors":"Yueen Chen, Mohammad Sharifian, Abolfazl Bayat","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22389"},{"id":"2611.22391","title":"Strong eigenstate thermalization in Singer-cycle Clifford circuits","summary":"Eigenstate thermalization hypothesis (ETH) provides a central framework for understanding thermalization in isolated quantum systems. Recently, the infinite-temperature ETH has been established for almost all Floquet eigenstates in linear feedback shift register quantum circuits. Here we introduce brickwork Clifford circuits with $L$ qubits where the induced transformation of Pauli operators is represented by an irreducible symplectic matrix over the finite field $\\mathbb F_2$ of the maximal order $2^L+1$, known as a Singer cycle. For these Singer-cycle Clifford circuits, we prove a strong version of ETH: all Floquet eigenstates are thermal at infinite temperature without exceptions. We derive uniform bounds on both diagonal deviations from thermal expectation values and off-diagonal matrix elements of arbitrary observables supported on a fixed number of sites. The results indicate that long-time averages of observables approach the infinite-temperature values in the thermodynamic limit for arbitrary initial states. We also show that the temporal fluctuation of expectation values of observables is exponentially suppressed in the thermodynamic limit, implying that each observable remains close to the infinite-temperature value for almost all times.","authors":"Naoto Tsuji","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.stat-mech"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22391"},{"id":"2611.22530","title":"Quantum Codes for Distributed Quantum Fault Sensing","summary":"Targeted recovery in modular quantum processors requires identifying the component affected by a fault. Independent qubits can monitor faults through repeated preparation and measurement. Bell-pair probes improve the guaranteed correct-site probability for unknown local channels, but require partners kept away from the fault. Can entanglement among exposed probes supply this advantage without protected storage? We establish a quantum-code criterion for optimal distributed fault sensing of one unknown local qubit channel with strength in a prescribed positive range. For one interrogation with zero false alarms and no background noise, suitable code states attain the optimal worst-case correct-site probability, improving on independent probes by 50% with every qubit exposed. Exact attainment is equivalent to mutually orthogonal input and single-qubit Pauli error subspaces. Pairwise entanglement gives no improvement, and entanglement depth at most four cannot exceed 95.54% of the optimum without protected ancillas. Approaching the optimum without protected ancillas also requires a number of probes proportional to the array size to participate in larger entangled factors. For a persistent fault under memoryless depolarizing background of fixed strength 0 < eta < 1, independent probes already attain the optimal logarithmic interrogation scaling at fixed success between zero and one and a positive false-alarm budget. In three 1000-site comparisons with specified readouts, an encoded-probe protocol certifies fewer interrogations than independent probes and only one more than optimally decoded ideal Bell pairs, while storing no protected qubits.","authors":"Xiu-Hao Deng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.mes-hall"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22530"},{"id":"2611.22546","title":"Multilevel Effects and the Limits of the Quantum Rabi Model in Ultrastrong Coupling system","summary":"The spectrum of artificial atoms coupled to resonators in the ultrastrong coupling regime have been analyzed using the quantum Rabi model over a wide range of coupling strengths. However, the accuracy of models obtained by truncating multilevel artificial atoms to two levels, as well as the correspondence between effective parameters extracted from spectral fitting and the bare parameters of the circuit Hamiltonian, depends on the multilevel structure of the system. In this study, I analyze the spectrum of an ultrastrongly coupled circuit consisting of a multilevel superconducting artificial atom and a resonator using a circuit Hamiltonian, and investigate the effects of truncating higher atomic levels on the low-energy spectrum. Based on L\\\"owdin partitioning, I evaluate the contributions of virtual transitions through higher atomic levels and show that diagonal energy corrections reduce the errors arising from simple two-level truncation over a wide parameter range. I also show that there are parameter regimes in which readjustment of the effective Rabi parameters successfully reproduces the multilevel spectrum, while systematic deviations remain in other regimes. These results demonstrate the necessity of separately evaluating two-level truncation, perturbative corrections, and effective-parameter fitting in ultrastrongly coupled systems, and provide guidance for quantitative spectral analysis of multilevel quantum circuits.","authors":"Akiyoshi Tomonaga","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22546"},{"id":"2611.22621","title":"Entanglement Lower Bounds for Symmetric Quantum Circuits","summary":"Symmetric circuits require every symmetry of the input to extend to an automorphism of the circuit. In the classical setting, their connection to descriptive complexity yields strong unconditional lower bounds. We extend this approach to the quantum model of symmetric computation defined by Castro-Silva, Gur, and Strelchuk (ITCS 2026), proving a tradeoff between circuit size and entanglement width (the smallest $b$ such that, on every computational-basis input, the state after each layer factors into states on at most $b$ qubits each). Our main result identifies a graph property decidable in classical polynomial time that no polynomial-size family of symmetric quantum circuits on $n$-vertex graphs with entanglement width $o(n/\\log n)$ can compute. The bound holds for circuits with arbitrary one-qubit and two-qubit gates and unrestricted depth, and remains valid under the weaker notion of symmetry with respect to even permutations of vertices. Our proof is based on a new quantum pebble game based on the partial trace. The resulting indistinguishability theorem transfers classical pebble-game lower bounds to quantum circuits with restricted entanglement. By contrast, without entanglement restrictions, we show that every graph property in BQP can be computed by a uniform family of polynomial-size symmetric quantum circuits. Restricting to circuits with no entanglement at all, we show that polynomial-size symmetric quantum circuits with entanglement width 1 can compute every graph property definable in fixed-point logic with counting. Finally, we give an efficient symmetric implementation of QAOA for MAX 3XOR, providing a starting point for studying entanglement lower bounds in quantum optimization algorithms.","authors":"Davi Castro-Silva, Ryan Dancy, Sergii Strelchuk","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.CC","cs.LO","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22621"},{"id":"2611.22633","title":"Measurement-Conditioned Bloch-Sphere State Steering with a Continuous-Variable Pointer","summary":"We investigate measurement-conditioned state steering of a qubit coupled to a continuous-variable pointer through a state-dependent displacement interaction. A position measurement on the pointer induces a nonunitary conditional transformation of the qubit, allowing its post-measurement state to move along a trajectory on the Bloch sphere. We derive the conditional state, the measurement-outcome distribution, and the corresponding Bloch-sphere coordinates. For a real Gaussian pointer, the conditional state follows a meridian, whereas a phase-engineered Gaussian pointer produces a trajectory with a measurement-dependent azimuthal angle. We further describe outcome-dependent unitary feedback for preparing a prescribed target state from a known initial qubit state. We distinguish this state-preparation task from implementing a universal deterministic single-qubit gate, which does not follow from the proposed interaction and measurement alone. Finally, we derive the quantum Fisher information of the joint qubit--pointer state for estimating the displacement-generating parameter and discuss the implications of pointer squeezing, phase engineering, and experimental implementation. The results provide a framework for analysing conditional Bloch-sphere steering using continuous-variable measurement resources.","authors":"Necati \\c{C}elik","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22633"},{"id":"2611.21715","title":"The SymTFT and Quantum Phases of Matter","summary":"The symmetry topological field theory (SymTFT) provides a unified framework for ordinary and generalized symmetries of quantum many-body systems. By encoding symmetry in a topological theory in one higher dimension, it separates symmetry data from the details of a specific quantum system. In this review, we give a pedagogical introduction to this framework and survey its applications to phases of quantum matter. For fusion category symmetries in ${(1+1)}$d, we explain how the SymTFT characterizes anomalies, classifies gapped phases, and organizes the symmetry structure of gapless phases. We also give a broad overview of further developments of this framework, including extensions to symmetries in higher dimensions, continuous symmetries, fermionic systems, mixed states, and spacetime symmetries.","authors":"Salvatore D. Pace, Sakura Schafer-Nameki, Carolyn Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.str-el","hep-ph","hep-th","quant-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21715"},{"id":"2611.22232","title":"MASQ: Measurement-Adaptive Sampling for Near-Clifford Quantum Error Correction Circuits","summary":"Classical sampling of quantum error correction (QEC) circuits supports fault-tolerant protocol evaluation, but estimating rare logical failures requires many independent shots. Frame-active-state simulators isolate non-stabilizer information in a dense active-state vector, yet dense-state processing remains exponential in the active width. This paper presents MASQ, an exact measurement-adaptive sampler for near-Clifford QEC circuits based on selective materialization of non-Clifford rotations. At each measurement boundary, MASQ materializes only rotations in a measurement-relevant closure capturing direct and indirect non-commuting dependencies, deferring the rest to limit active-state expansion. Stabilizer-assisted elimination further removes rotations certified to be invisible to all future measurements. A circuit-dependent schedule is computed once and shared across shots, while adaptive caching of conditional Born probabilities and batched execution reduce repeated sampling work while preserving the measurement-record distribution. Across 19 QEC benchmarks, MASQ reduces peak active width by up to 40 active virtual qubits and achieves up to 8,098-fold CPU throughput speedup over Clifft on multi-round circuits.","authors":"Chaebin Jung, Sangmin Jeon, Kyeongwon Lee, Sechan Park, Woojoo Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.ET","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22232"},{"id":"2507.10784","title":"Quantum Advantage in Storage and Retrieval of Isometry Channels","summary":"Storage and retrieval refer to the task of encoding an unknown quantum channel $\\Lambda$ into a quantum state, known as the program state, such that the channel can later be retrieved. There are two strategies for this task: classical and quantum strategies. The classical strategy uses multiple queries to $\\Lambda$ to estimate $\\Lambda$ and retrieves the channel based on the estimate represented in classical bits. The classical strategy turns out to offer the optimal performance for the storage and retrieval of unitary channels. In this work, we analyze the asymptotic performance of the classical and quantum strategies for the storage and retrieval of isometry channels. We show that the optimal fidelity for isometry estimation is given by $F = 1-{d(D-d)\\over n} + O(n^{-4/3})$, where $d$ and $D$ denote the input and output dimensions of the isometry, and $n$ is the number of queries. This result indicates that, unlike in the case of unitary channels, the classical strategy is suboptimal for the storage and retrieval of isometry channels, which requires $n = \\Theta(\\epsilon^{-1})$ to achieve the diamond-norm error $\\epsilon$. We propose a more efficient quantum strategy based on port-based teleportation, which stores the isometry channel in a program state using only $n = \\Theta(1/\\sqrt{\\epsilon})$ queries, achieving a quadratic improvement over the classical strategy. As an application, we extend our approach to general quantum channels, achieving improved program cost compared to prior results by Gschwendtner, Bluhm, and Winter [Quantum \\textbf{5}, 488 (2021)].","authors":"Satoshi Yoshida, Jisho Miyazaki, Mio Murao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.10784"},{"id":"2508.05346","title":"Geometric encoding of turbulence for end-to-end quantum simulation","summary":"Simulating multiscale turbulence in engineering remains challenging. Any quantum advantage for such simulations requires efficient initial-state preparation. Yet exact loading of an arbitrary classical snapshot requires exponential quantum resources. We introduce \"turbuloscope\", a physics-informed geometric method that generates statistically representative turbulent initial states in a gate-based register from a parameterized Pauli-spinor multiscale distribution. Scale invariance renders conditional rotations approximately affine in Gray-code space, enabling linear-depth, ancilla-free preparation. The generalized Madelung transform and Hopf fibration map the encoded Pauli spinor to fluid observables and vortex tubes. The qubit count scales logarithmically with the Reynolds number, yielding an exponential reduction in preparation overhead relative to full-grid classical spectral synthesis. We classically emulate a 30-qubit preparation circuit and generate a turbulent field over one billion grid points at a scale-inferred Reynolds number of 35,000, exhibiting Kolmogorov's five-thirds energy spectrum, tangled vortex tubes, and intermittency. This method provides scalable initial states for quantum simulation of turbulence and multiscale systems.","authors":"Zhaoyuan Meng, Xiao-Ming Zhang, Xiao Yuan, Yue Yang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","nlin.CD","physics.flu-dyn"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.05346"},{"id":"2510.21075","title":"Harnessing Intrinsic Noise for Quantum Simulation of Open Quantum Systems","summary":"Simulating open quantum systems on quantum computers is challenging because dissipative dynamics are nonunitary, whereas gate-based quantum algorithms are typically built from unitary operations. Conventional approaches can overcome this mismatch by embedding nonunitary processes into enlarged unitary circuits, often with ancillary-qubit and gate overhead. In this work, we propose an alternative perspective in which we selectively preserve the physical noise to emulate nonunitary channels. More specifically, we present a partial encoding algorithm that iteratively leverages intrinsic noise to mimic the dynamics of a target open-system. We benchmark the convergence of this algorithm against the dissipative exciton transport Hamiltonian and retrieve their dynamics. By utilizing intrinsic hardware noise to emulate the target nonunitary dynamics, this approach avoids the dual overhead of error-correcting that noise and using ancillary qubits to re-introduce nonunitarity. This significantly reduces both qubit requirements and physical fidelity thresholds, paving the way for more efficient techniques to simulate open quantum systems on quantum computers.","authors":"Sameer Dambal, Akira Sone, Yu Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21075"},{"id":"2601.17675","title":"Realisation of a Protected Cat-Qutrit Manifold via Engineered Quantum Tunnelling","summary":"Engineering quantum tunnelling in phase space has emerged as a viable method for creating a protected logical qubit manifold with biased-noise properties. A promising approach is to combine a Kerr nonlinearity with a multi-photon drive, resulting in a system known as a Kerr parametric oscillator (KPO). In this work, we implement a three-photon KPO and explore its potential as a protected bosonic qutrit. We confirm quantum coherence by demonstrating three-photon Rabi oscillations and performing direct Wigner function measurements that reveal the formation of three-component cat-like states. Crucially, we observe a breathing-like dynamic in phase space, a characteristic feature of driven quantum systems. This dynamic arises from macroscopic temporal interference between the cat-qutrit manifold and the excited states. The frequency of resulting oscillations in the mean photon number provides a direct, time-domain measurement of the energy gap separating the qutrit from the excited states, thereby establishing an experimental hallmark of qutrit manifold protection. Furthermore, we identify a parasitic higher-order pump term as the primary mechanism constraining the mean photon number, highlighting its mitigation as a requisite for maximising protection. Our findings elucidate the basic quantum properties of the three-photon KPO and establish the first step towards its use as an alternative qutrit platform.","authors":"Sangil Kwon, Daisuke Hoshi, Toshiaki Nagase, Daichi Sugiyama, Hiroto Mukai, Kengo Takemura, Rintaro Kojima, Yu Zhou, Shohei Watabe, Fumiki Yoshihara, Jaw-Shen Tsai","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17675"},{"id":"2601.20263","title":"A Quantum Photonic Approach to Graph Coloring","summary":"Gaussian Boson Sampling (GBS) is a quantum computational model that leverages linear optics to solve sampling problems believed to be classically intractable. Recent experimental breakthroughs have demonstrated quantum advantage using GBS, motivating its application to real-world combinatorial optimization problems. In this work, we reformulate the graph coloring problem as an integer programming problem using the independent set formulation. This enables the use of GBS to identify cliques in the complement graph, which correspond to independent sets in the original graph. Our method is benchmarked against classical heuristics and exact algorithms on two sets of instances: Erd\\H{o}s-R\\'enyi random graphs and graphs derived from a smart-charging use case. The results demonstrate that GBS can provide competitive solutions, highlighting its potential as a quantum-enhanced heuristic for graph-based optimization.","authors":"Jesua Epequin, Pascale Bendotti, Joseph Mikael","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cs.DM"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.20263"},{"id":"2602.06171","title":"Quantum-enhanced Markov Chain Monte Carlo for Combinatorial Optimization","summary":"Quantum computing offers an alternative paradigm for addressing combinatorial optimization problems compared to classical methods. However, despite recent hardware improvements, the execution of empirical quantum optimization experiments at scales known to be hard for state-of-the-art classical solvers is not yet in reach. In this work, we offer a different way to approach combinatorial optimization with near-term quantum computing. Motivated by the promising results observed in using quantum-enhanced Markov chain Monte Carlo (QeMCMC) for approximating complicated probability distributions, we combine ideas of sampling from the device with QeMCMC together with warm-starting and parallel tempering, in the context of combinatorial optimization. We demonstrate empirically that our algorithm recovers the global optima for instances of the Maximum Independent Set problem (MIS) up to 117 decision variables using 117 qubits on IBM quantum hardware. We observe an indication of more favorable scaling relative to the tested classical MCMC methods on the chosen MIS instances. MIS is practically relevant across domains such as financial services and molecular biology. Although some MIS instances with a few hundred variables can be difficult for classical solvers, the instances studied here are solved efficiently by CPLEX.","authors":"Kate V. Marshall, Daniel J. Egger, Michael Garn, Francesca Schiavello, Sebastian Brandhofer, Christa Zoufal, Stefan Woerner","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06171"},{"id":"2604.07214","title":"Quantum Gibbs sampling through the detectability lemma","summary":"Gibbs state preparation is an important subroutine in quantum computing. In this work we use the detectability lemma to improve Gibbs state preparation. Specifically, we design new Gibbs state preparation methods that do not rely on simulating Lindbladian evolution, thus avoiding the overhead from it. For local Lindbladians consisting of $M$ terms, this approach reduces the cost by a factor of $O(M)$. We also combine the detectability lemma operator and quantum singular value transformation to implement ground state projection operators of frustration-free Hamiltonians, resulting in a quadratic speedup in the spectral gap dependence. Applying this method to Lindbladians for the Gibbs state of local commuting Hamiltonians, we achieve quadratically better dependence on the Lindbladian spectral gap.","authors":"Di Fang, Jianfeng Lu, Yu Tong, Chu Zhao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cs.NA","math-ph","math.MP","math.NA"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07214"},{"id":"2605.02565","title":"Sample-Based Quantum Diagonalization with Amplitude Amplification","summary":"Recently, sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) has emerged as a promising approach to compute ground and excited states of problem Hamiltonians. This method classically diagonalizes a Hamiltonian in a subspace that is spanned by samples obtained from a quantum computer. However, by its nature, SQD suffers from a fundamental sampling problem, as some basis states that are required for a targeted accuracy may only be sampled extremely rarely. To alleviate this limitation, we introduce the SQD-AA algorithm that combines SQD with amplitude amplification (AA). SQD-AA uses AA to sequentially reduce probabilities of already measured bitstrings, thus making the observation of new ones more likely. We observe a reduction in the total query complexity of more than a factor 100 for algebraically and exponentially decaying model distributions, and analytically show a quadratic advantage for the latter. Moreover, we evaluate real molecules in an early fault-tolerant scenario and compare SQD-AA to SQD and iterative quantum phase estimation (iQPE). For almost all considered examples, we observe the lowest total number of T-gates for SQD-AA while only requiring circuits that are 2-4 orders of magnitude shallower than those needed for iQPE. Given this substantial reduction in circuit depth compared to iQPE while saving 2 orders of magnitude in total runtime compared to SQD, we expect a significant regime in early fault-tolerance where SQD-AA runs feasibly, but iQPE circuits are too deep to execute confidently.","authors":"Nina Stockinger, Ludwig N\\\"utzel, Michael J. Hartmann","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.02565"},{"id":"2611.06448","title":"Helium States Inspired by Quantum Photonics Circuit","summary":"We present a four-mode Fock-state photonics implementation of the variational quantum deflation (VQD) algorithm to calculate the ground-state and excited-state energies for the neutral helium atom in a CAS(2,2) Subspace. Its CAS(2,2) electronic Hilbert space is encoded into a bosonic Fock-space representation and subsequently optimized using a continuous-variable quantum circuit. The circuit architecture under study is constructed of single-mode phase shifts, beam splitters, and cross-Kerr interactions. The variational circuit is initialized in the Fock state $\\ket{1100}$ and its performance is benchmarked against exact diagonalization of the active-space configuration-interaction (CASCI) Hamiltonian. We also extend the method to optimize from the first to three excited states of the helium atom with the aid of the variational quantum deflation $(VQD)$ method in a CAS(2,2). We then investigate the performance of this circuit architecture as a basis for implementing the VQD method. In the ideal noiseless simulation setting, we see that the survival probability of the ground and the excited states within the target $CAS$ subspace is unity, because the total photon number and the photon number in the two spin sectors $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are preserved. We demonstrate that the proposed approach enables the determination of the ground and excited-state energies within the CAS(2,2) electronic Hilbert space using a continuous-variable quantum circuit and the variational quantum deflation ($VQD$) algorithm, while satisfying the orthogonality conditions with negligible errors.","authors":"Forouzan Mirmasoudi, Badie Ghavami","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.mtrl-sci"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06448"},{"id":"2611.18091","title":"Efficient Iterative Symmetrization algorithm for quantum computing and hadron applications","summary":"Efficient algorithms for the (anti)symmetrization of quantum registers are necessary for upcoming few- and many-body physics applications, from atomic to particle-physics scales. We propose a recursive algorithm (anti)symmetrizing the last particle added to memory, each time one is created or destroyed, respect to the rest of already (anti)symmetrized ones. Given a generalized Dicke state (GDS) of $(j-1)$ quantum registers, each assigned to a particle, the algorithm produces another GDS of $j$ registers (of $n_{qb}$ qubits each) using a mere $\\mathcal{O}(j\\log_2^2 j + j n_{qb})$ $\\mathtt{CNOT}$ and single-qubit gates, and $3j+\\lceil\\log_2j\\rceil+\\mathcal{O}(\\max(n_{qb},\\log_2 j))$ clean auxiliary qubits. The (anti)symmetrizing operator constructed for this purpose depends on the quantum numbers of the additional particle being introduced or ``created''. The algorithm improves upon the construction presented in Appendix A of \\cite{Galvez-Viruet:2024hry} by almost two powers of $j$ and is on par with the most efficient methods to date, while ideally suited for dynamical memory management with variable particle number. We exemplify its applicability with a small selection of problems from hadron physics which are expected to be addressable with upcoming quantum computers.","authors":"Nahia J. Dios-Bilbao, Adri\\'an Casta\\~no-Garc\\'ia, J. J. G\\'alvez-Viruet, Felipe J. Llanes-Estrada, Mario Logros\\'an-\\'Alvarez, Nicol\\'as M. Arenaza","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-th","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18091"},{"id":"2611.21239","title":"What kind of quantum advantage is this?","summary":"Quantum advantage encompasses fundamentally different claims, from resource reductions to exponential separations in sample complexity. Evaluating these claims requires distinguishing how an algorithm achieves an advantage from what is necessary for the separation and how strongly it is supported against classical alternatives. We propose a multi-label taxonomy that makes these distinctions explicit, classifying structural, interference, and coherent mechanisms while separately recording their necessity and the evidence for the classical comparison. Coherent mechanisms are further divided into memory and output, reflecting distinct restrictions on quantum information processing. A two-part entanglement-breaking test distinguishes loss of information from loss of algorithmic efficiency. The hidden subgroup problem illustrates the distinction: among the cases examined, cross-copy coherence is information-theoretically necessary only for the symmetric group. We present this as a diagnostic methodology rather than a theory of quantum advantage: for a new claim, it identifies the relevant adversary, separates the mechanisms a protocol uses from those shown to be necessary, and records the strength of the evidence and the quality of the advantage separately.","authors":"Christophe Pere","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21239"},{"id":"2608.27944","title":"Quantum geometric bounds at finite temperature for one-dimensional chiral systems","summary":"The geometry and topology of quantum states are intimately related at zero temperature through exact bounds that constrain geometric quantities from below by topological invariants. At finite temperature, however, the analogous relations remain unclear. Here we establish rigorous geometric lower bounds for one-dimensional chiral-symmetric systems at finite temperature within the Uhlmann's framework for mixed states. We show that the Bures length is bounded by a continuous geometric phase angle. We further derive a temperature-dependent bound that interpolates between the zero-temperature limit and a trivial high-temperature regime. Our results are verified analytically and numerically using the Su-Schrieffer-Heeger model and the spinless Kitaev chain model. Finally, we discuss potential ways to detect the geometry of the density matrix in quantum circuits, with the quantum imaginary-time evolution method.","authors":"Peng He, Hai-Tao Ding, Yu-Guo Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.mes-hall","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.27944"},{"id":"2611.21807","title":"On the Structure of the Nuclear-Clock Isotope $^{229}$Th Beyond Quadratic Order","summary":"Precision measurements of the exceptionally low-energy nuclear transition of $^{229}$Th, offer an extraordinary sensitivity to a variety of new light fields. However, interpreting such measurements requires nuclear-structure information beyond what is currently constrained by spectroscopy. We develop a systematic description of the electric multipole interactions governing the hyperfine structure of ${}^{229}$Th, including the hexadecapole contribution, and perform a symmetry-based analysis of the resulting hyperfine splittings in crystalline environments. Hyperfine spectroscopy generally constrains combinations of nuclear moments and crystal-field gradients, leading to degeneracies when higher multipole interactions are allowed. We then combine experimental nuclear observables, particle-rotor relations, and a deformed-Fermi charge-density model to investigate the electromagnetic contribution to the nuclear clock's sensitivity to variations in the fine-structure constant. We find that higher-order nuclear deformation and state-dependent differences in the radial charge distribution of the clock states can substantially alter the predicted sensitivity, introducing large uncertainties that are not constrained by existing measurements. Our results highlight the importance of combining nuclear and solid-state theory with complementary measurements, including spectroscopy of higher-spin states, to constrain the nuclear structure and improve predictions of the clock's sensitivity to new physics. Our symmetry-based analysis also provides a framework for using hyperfine spectroscopy to investigate local defect symmetries in crystalline hosts.","authors":"Chaja Baruch, Doron Gazit, Hans-Werner Hammer, Fiona Kirk, Joachim Kopp, Gil Paz, Ekkehard Peik, Gilad Perez, Yotam Soreq, Konstantin Springmann","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th","physics.atom-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21807"},{"id":"2611.21810","title":"Probing scalar-mediated Lorentz violation with hydrogen masers and spin-precession of molecules","summary":"We propose using hydrogen maser spectroscopy and molecular spin-precession experiments, traditionally employed in precision frequency measurements and searches for the electron electric dipole moment, as complementary probes of light scalar-mediated, spin-dependent fifth forces. We investigate their sensitivity to Lorentz-violating interactions mediated by scalars with masses ranging from $\\mathcal{O}(10^{-4}~\\mu\\mathrm{eV})$ to $\\mathcal{O}(10~\\mathrm{keV})$. While hydrogen maser spectroscopy and molecular spin-precession measurements probe different combinations of Lorentz-violating couplings, we find that the strongest projected sensitivities can be achieved using spin-precession of ultracold molecules, benefiting from their extended coherence times. These experimental approaches provide access to previously unexplored regions of parameter space, probing interaction ranges extending from centimeter to sub-angstrom scales. Our proposals highlight the potential of precision atomic and molecular experiments to search for new spin-dependent scalar-mediated interactions and violations of fundamental spacetime symmetries.","authors":"Djuna Croon, Tanmay Kumar Poddar","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","physics.atom-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21810"},{"id":"2611.22201","title":"Quality Control for the LGAD sensors of the ATLAS High Granularity Timing Detector","summary":"The High Granularity Timing Detector (HGTD) currently in the production phase will mainly mitigate the pile-up effect in the ATLAS forward region and serve for bunch-by-bunch luminosity measurements in the high-luminosity (HL) phase of the Large Hadron Collider (LHC). The HGTD utilizes Low Gain Avalanche Detector (LGAD) technology, covering the pseudorapidity range between 2.4 and 4.0, and provides high-precision timing information to distinguish collisions occurring close in space but well-separated in time. A pre-production batch of 8-inch silicon wafers was fabricated and a systematic quality control test campaign was carried out. This paper describes the Quality Control (QC) required for full-size (15 $\\times$ 15 array) sensors, including leakage-current and breakdown measurements performed on all sensors at the vendor sites, as well as more detailed electrical characterizations at the participating ATLAS-HGTD institutes. In addition, the electrical characterization of full-size sensors after irradiation is reported.","authors":"K. Ma, W. Sun, M. Ait Tamlihat, H. M. Alfanda, N. Atanov, O. Atanova, I. Azzouzi, J. Barreiro Guimar\\~aes Da Costa, L. T. Bazzano Hurrell, T. Beau, D. Benchekroun, F. Bendebba, Y. Bimgdi, A. Blot, A. Boikov, D. Boumediene, C. Brito, A. S. Brogna, L. Cadamuro, Y. Cai, N. Carolino, N. Cartalade, Y. Che, X. Chen, M. Christiansen, P. Cistac, S. E. D. Clawson, L. D. Corpe, G. Costi, C. G. Crozatier, O. Cunha, L. D'Eramo, S. Dahbi, D. Dannheim, G. Daubard, Yu. I. Davydov, J. Debevc, Y. Degerli, E. Delagnes, F. Deliot, C. de La Taille, M. Dhellot, C. Dimitriadis, P. Dinaucourt, G. Di Gregorio, P. J. Dos Santos De Assis, C. Duan, O. Duarte, F. Dulucq, S. El Farrkh, Y. El Ghazali, A. El Moussaouy, R. Estevam de Paula, A. Falou, L. Fan, Z. Fan, Y. Fan, K. Farman, F. Fassi, M. Ferreira, F. Filthaut, F. Fischer, G. Frattari, J. Fu, P. Fust\\'e, J. Garc\\'ia Rodriquez, G. Gaspar De Andrade, Z. Ge, M. G. D. Gololo, R. Gon\\c{c}alo, M. Gouighri, S. Grinstein, K. Gritsay, F. Guilloux, S. Guindon, J. Guo, A. Haddad, S. E. D. Hammoud, L. Han, A. M. Henriques Correia, B. Hiti, B. Hodkinson, J. Hofner, S. Hou, P. J. Hsu, K. Hu, Y. Huang, X. Huang, C. Insa, J. Jeglot, X. Jia, S. F. Koch, G. Kramberger, M. Kuriyama, B. Y. Ky, D. Lacour, A. Lafarge, B. Lakssir, A. Lantheaume, D. Laporte, A. Leopold, T. J. Lepota, M. Li, Y. Li, L. Li, S. Li, H. Li, Z. Li, Z. Liang, S. Liang, C. Liao, M. A. Lisboa Leite, Y. L. Liu, K. Liu, K. Liu, Y. W. Liu, H. Lu, M. Lu, Y. J. Lu, F. L. Lucio Alves, X. Ma, D. Macina, R. Madar, N. Makovec, S. Malyukov, I. Mandi\\'{c}, T. Manoussos, S. Manzoni, G. Martin-Chassard, F. Martins, L. Masetti, R. Mazini, E. Mazzeo, R. Mekouar, R. Menegasso, J. -P. Meyer, Y. Miao, M. Mihovilovic, M. Milovanovic, M. Missio, V. Moskalenko, N. Mouadili, A. Moussa, Y. Nie, I. Nikolic-Audit, C. C. Ohm, H. Okawa, M. Ouchrif, C. P\\'en\\'elaud, B. Pascual Dias, Y. Peng, J. Pinol Bel, P. -O. Puhl, C. Puigdengoles Olive, M. Puklavec, M. Qi, J. Qin, H. Ren, S. Ridouani, R. T. Rimer, V. Rogozin, W. Roth, L. Royer, F. Rudnyckyj, G. T. Saito, A. Salem, H. Santos, Ph. Schwemling, N. Seguin-Moreau, L. Serin, R. P. Serrano Fernandez, Q. Sha, A. Shaikovskii, L. Shan, R. Shen, X. Shi, P. Skomina, H. Smitmanns, H. L. Snoek, A. P. Soulier, A. Stein, H. Stenzel, J. Strandberg, X. Sun, Y. Sun, K. Tariq, K. Tau, Y. Tayalati, S. Terzo, S. A. Thayil, S. Trincaz-Duvoid, L. F. Van Duren Watak, U. M. Vande Voorde, I. Velkovska, R. V. Vieira, L. A. Vieira Lopes, A. Visibile, M. Vogt, J. Wan, H. Wang, S. Wang, Z. Wang, C. Wang, Y. Wang, Y. Wang, A. Wang, T. Wang, T. Wang, S. M. Wang, Q. Weitzel, C. Wen, C. Wu, J. Wu, Y. Wu, W. Wu, Y. Wu, M. Wu, L. Xia, H. Xu, D. Xu, L. Xu, Z. Yan, X. Yang, H. Yang, H. Yang, J. Ye, Y. Yi, I. Youbi, J. Yuan, I. Zahir, T. Zhang, J. Zhang, Z. Zhang, L. Zhang, D. Zhang, M. Zhao, Z. Zhao, G. Zheng, X. Zheng, Z. Zhou, Y. Zhu, X. Zhuang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22201"},{"id":"2603.03353","title":"Improved Pion-Kaon Identification in Heavy-Ion Collisions with a Two-Dimensional Transformation","summary":"Accurate identification of charged pions and kaons is essential for precision measurements in relativistic heavy-ion collisions, but becomes increasingly challenging at intermediate and high transverse momentum because of the increasing overlap of the TPC and TOF particle identification responses. In this work, we quantitatively benchmark a two-dimensional shift-and-rotation PID procedure that combines the $n\\sigma_{\\pi}$ and mass-squared $(m^{2})$ information. The benchmark is performed using Au+Au collision events generated with the AMPT model, where the detector response is modeled using $p_{T}$-dependent PID parameters extracted from STAR data. Since the true particle identities and input yields are known in AMPT but are not used during the reconstruction, the procedure can be evaluated through a direct closure test that is not possible with experimental data alone. The two-dimensional procedure is compared with a reference one-dimensional method consisting of an $|n\\sigma_{\\pi}|<$ 3 preselection followed by a Gaussian fit to the $m^{2}$ distribution. While both methods perform well when pion and kaon PID responses are well separated, the two-dimensional procedure provides improved yield closure as the PID distributions increasingly overlap at high $p_{T}$. The reconstructed-to-input yield ratio remains within 2\\% of unity up to $p_T =$ 3 GeV/$c$, whereas the reference one-dimensional procedure shows a larger deviation. Reaction-plane $v_{2}$ closure tests show that both the reference one-dimensional and two-dimensional PID procedures reproduce the AMPT input $v_{2}$ within the statistical uncertainties over the studied $p_{T}$ range. These results demonstrate that the two-dimensional procedure substantially improves the high-$p_{T}$ yield reconstruction without introducing a detectable bias in the extracted $v_{2}$ under the STAR-like detector response considered here.","authors":"Shaowei Lan, Bijun Fan, Like Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","nucl-ex","physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.03353"},{"id":"2609.03822","title":"Optimisation of importance sampling implementation in rare-event shielding simulations using Geant4","summary":"Rare-event search experiments demand ever-lower backgrounds, and the resulting radio-pure materials and suppressive shielding make related background simulations computationally expensive without event biasing. We present a method for optimising the widely used importance splitting and Russian roulette technique for rare-event searches, in which a whole number of equal-width importance layers fill the shielding geometry. Balancing relative uncertainty against execution time, we optimise the layer thickness for both $\\gamma$-rays and neutrons, and across different shielding materials and thicknesses. Expressed as a multiple of the primary particle's mean free path, the optimal thickness generalises across energies for a given particle type and yields a physics-based prediction applicable to any shielding design. The same trend reveals that over-biasing is possible, with relative uncertainty worsening at high layer counts for fixed execution time. The optimal configuration gives an $\\mathcal{O}$(20) gain in computational efficiency over the unbiased case.","authors":"P. Knights, L. J. Milligan, K. Nikolopoulos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","physics.comp-ph","physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03822"},{"id":"2610.13634","title":"A machine-learning method for the estimation of the background from jets misidentified as hadronic tau lepton decays","summary":"Backgrounds arising from quark- and gluon-initiated jets misidentified as hadronically decaying tau leptons constitute a major experimental challenge for analyses involving tau leptons. The MUltivariate Fake-Factor INference (MUFFIN) approach, a machine-learning-based method for estimating this background directly from observed events, is presented. Boosted decision tree reweighting is used to learn multidimensional, per-object correction factors directly from observed data, thereby modelling the jet to hadronic tau lepton misidentification probability across the kinematic and topological phase space without relying on low-dimensional parametrisations. Performance studies are presented using proton-proton collision data collected by the CMS experiment at the CERN LHC at $\\sqrt{s}$ = 13.6 TeV, corresponding to an integrated luminosity of 62.4 fb$^{-1}$. Compared to low-dimensional parametrisation methods, improved modelling of key kinematic observables is achieved, particularly in regions of high $p_\\mathrm{T}$. The improved modelling leads to a reduction of up to 70% in the systematic uncertainty associated with jets being misidentified as hadronically decaying tau leptons. The MUFFIN method provides a robust and scalable method for background estimation in precision measurements and searches for beyond-the-standard-model physics involving tau lepton final states.","authors":" CMS Collaboration","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13634"},{"id":"2611.21733","title":"Baryonification V: Thermal Sunyaev-Zel'dovich maps from dark-matter-only lightcones","summary":"Baryonic feedback is a major theoretical uncertainty for Stage-IV weak lensing (WL) surveys. The thermal Sunyaev-Zel'dovich (tSZ) effect traces the hot gas redistributed by feedback, so combining it with WL can calibrate feedback and improve cosmological constraints. Doing so with simulation-based inference requires correlated lensing and Compton-$y$ maps across many cosmologies and feedback scenarios. We present two methods to generate Compton-$y$ maps from dark-matter-only $N$-body light cones using the component-wise baryonification (BFC) model, which produces separate gas and stellar fields and computes the gas pressure from hydrostatic equilibrium. The two methods, painting pressure profiles onto halos or redistributing the signal along the baryonified gas, agree to within a few per cent. We calibrate the model to the fiducial and strongest AGN feedback FLAMINGO simulations by jointly fitting gas mass fractions and thermal pressure profiles, measuring the non-thermal pressure from the simulated gas velocities rather than treating it as a free parameter. The predicted Compton-$y$ profiles match FLAMINGO within halo-to-halo scatter, and when painted onto the FLAMINGO halos we reproduce the tSZ power spectrum of both feedback models to $\\sim5\\%$ for $500\\lesssim\\ell\\lesssim3000$. Applied to a \\emph{CosmoGridV1-like} $N$-body simulation, our maps agree with FLAMINGO within $\\sim10\\%$ for $300\\lesssim\\ell\\lesssim8000$; the remaining differences come from the few hundred most massive clusters, and removing them from both halo catalogs brings the two into agreement. Our method thus enables accurate and efficient tSZ forward models from $N$-body simulation suites such as CosmoGridV1, an important step towards joint WL and tSZ simulation-based inference for Euclid, LSST, and the Simons Observatory.","authors":"Michael Kova\\v{c}, Andrina Nicola, Jozef Bucko, Siyu Bian, Aurel Schneider, Robert Reischke, Jessica A. Cowell, Alexandre Refregier, Sambit K. Giri, Matthieu Schaller, Joop Schaye","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21733"},{"id":"2611.21821","title":"A Forecast for Testing the Decreasing $H_0$ Trend in Time-Delay Cosmography","summary":"In 2019 the H0LiCOW/TDCOSMO collaboration reported a decreasing Hubble constant $H_0$ trend with lens redshift in the flat $\\Lambda$CDM model at low statistical significance ($< 2 \\sigma$). The signal is expected if physics is missing from the $\\Lambda$CDM model in the late universe. In the wake of recent TDCOSMO 2026 results, we revisit the feature. Although the statistical significance of the trend has increased in a like-for-like comparison with earlier studies, propagating mass-sheet transformation (MST) uncertainties reduces the significance to $\\sim 1 \\sigma$. As a result, Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) currently favours a constant $H_0$ over a decreasing $H_0$ in the ratio 2.4:1. Nevertheless, we highlight good agreement on a negative slope with independent observables and forecast that approximately 40 and 90 lenses will be required to substantiate the trend at $\\sim 2 \\sigma$ and $\\sim 3 \\sigma$ significance, respectively, if the feature is physical and current uncertainties are representative. Even now, it is evident that the TDCOSMO 2026 $H_0$ constraint $H_0 \\approx 71$ km/s/Mpc reflects an average over the lens redshift distribution and is naturally anchored at intermediate lens redshifts.","authors":"Shane Brady, Eoin \\'O Colg\\'ain, M. M. Sheikh-Jabbari","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21821"},{"id":"2611.21729","title":"Supernova Bounds on Neutrino Dipole Moments from a Modern Perspective","summary":"Neutrinos may have nonstandard magnetic dipole moments $\\mu_\\nu$. In the dense cores of supernovae (SNe), these would convert part of the neutrinos into sterile, right-handed states through the electromagnetic fluctuations of the charged plasma. The streaming sterile neutrinos with 100-MeV energies can convert back to standard neutrinos in the Galactic magnetic field, leading to a high-energy neutrino flux unobserved from the historical SN 1987A. We reassess the production rates, including scattering on both protons and electrons, and, building upon advances in SN simulations and in the description of the Milky Way magnetic field, we revisit the bounds on magnetic dipole moments. We find for Dirac electron neutrinos $\\mu_{\\nu_e} \\lesssim 4.8 \\times 10^{-13} \\mu_B$, superseding all existing terrestrial and astrophysical constraints.","authors":"Damiano F. G. Fiorillo, Martina La Rosa, Federico Testagrossa, Edoardo Vitagliano","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21729"},{"id":"2611.22266","title":"Gravitational waves from cosmological first-order phase transitions with fluctuation-induced friction","summary":"We study gravitational-wave production in a cosmological first-order phase transition with fluctuation-induced friction using three-dimensional lattice simulations of two coupled scalar fields. Kinetic and gradient energy in the additional field $s$ accumulates near the expanding bubble walls of $\\phi$ and affects their motion. The contribution from $s$ exceeds that from $\\phi$ at the spectral peak and has a slope similar to that of the bulk flow model over a finite interval above the peak. In the high-wave-number (UV) direction, $\\phi$ dominates and the total spectrum develops a broad feature. For the larger coupling, slowly contracting false vacuum remnants persist long after bubble merger.","authors":"Jun-Ye Chen, Dongdong Wei, Zong-Kuan Guo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22266"},{"id":"2605.24089","title":"Exploring the Dark Sector: Interacting Radiation in Light of Modern Cosmological Probes","summary":"[abridged] We constrain a phenomenological dark radiation (DR) framework consisting of free-streaming and fluid-like components, providing a model-independent extension of the standard radiation sector. Using Planck CMB data, DESI DR2 BAO measurements, and Pantheon+ and DES Y5 (Dovekie) supernova samples, we derive constraints on additional relativistic degrees of freedom and assess their impact on cosmological tensions. We obtain $N_{\\rm fld}<0.66$ (95% C.L.) from CMB data alone, while the combination with BAO yields $N_{\\rm fs}=2.93\\pm0.23$ and $N_{\\rm fld}=0.36^{+0.16}_{-0.21}$ (68% C.L.), consistent with Standard Model expectations for free-streaming radiation. The DR framework significantly alleviates the Hubble tension through an enhanced early-time expansion rate, which reduces the sound horizon scale. The tension with SH0ES is reduced from highly significant in $\\Lambda$CDM to statistically non-significant for CMB+BAO data according to the $\\mathcal{T}$-statistic. Bayesian model comparison shows no decisive preference for DR over $\\Lambda$CDM when SH0ES is excluded, with results in the regime of weakly disfavoured. However, including SH0ES data leads to decisive Bayesian evidence in favour of the DR scenario. Overall, DR provides a compelling framework for resolving the Hubble tension. When CMB, BAO, Pantheon$+$ and SH0ES data are considered, we find an increased effective radiation content, $N_{\\rm tot}=3.63^{+0.13}_{-0.15}$, with a fraction of free-streaming radiation, $f_{\\rm fs}=0.392\\pm 0.026$, a reduced sound horizon scale, $r_d = 141.8^{+1.3}_{-1.2}\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}$, and a higher primordial helium fraction, $Y_{\\rm He}=0.2530 \\pm 0.0017$, which lies at the level of approximately $\\sim 2$-$2.5\\sigma$ above direct determinations from metal-poor H II regions, while remaining broadly consistent with other abundance measurements within current uncertainties.","authors":"Matteo Mescia, H\\'ector Gil-Mar\\'in","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.24089"},{"id":"2611.05504","title":"Gravitational waves flooding the Universe after wall domination","summary":"We discuss a possible early Universe scenario, where radiation era is interrupted by the epoch of domination of domain walls eventually annihilating into particles. During this period the Universe undergoes accelerated expansion, while the comoving correlation length of the domain wall network remains constant. The network serves as a source of tensor modes, which get frozen upon exiting the horizon similarly to the case of inflationary fluctuations. The modes re-enter the horizon during the subsequent stage of radiation domination and since then contribute to the gravitational wave background. The period of wall domination is manifested in the spectral shape of gravitational waves via a distinctive plateau replacing a peak, which is characteristic for the scenario with sub-dominant walls. The gravitational waves are strong enough to be observed with present-day and future facilities in a broad range of frequencies. In particular, their energy density is sufficient to produce a significant impact on the effective number of neutrino species, unless the duration of wall domination is suitably constrained.","authors":"Sabir Ramazanov, Zhen-Min Zeng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05504"},{"id":"2505.01492","title":"Photo- and Hadrodisintegration constraints on massive relics decaying into neutrinos","summary":"We perform a detailed study of the cosmological constraints on the decay of a relic particle $\\phi$ into neutrinos, $\\phi \\rightarrow \\nu \\bar{\\nu}$, in particular those arising from the observed light-element abundances in the early Universe. We focus on the late-time disintegration of the light elements previously synthesised during BBN. Several processes are relevant, including final-state radiation associated with the decay, as well as subsequent interactions of the injected neutrinos with the thermal background neutrinos or between themselves. All processes generically contribute to the production of electromagnetic and often also hadronic material and may therefore induce late-time photodisintegration and hadrodisintegration reactions, i.e.~the destruction of light elements that have previously been formed during BBN. Here, we examine this scenario with a Monte-Carlo inspired probabilistic approach rather than Boltzmann techniques, taking into account all of these different reactions as well as their interplay. We find the resulting constraints to be very significant, covering a broad range of previously unexplored masses and lifetimes of the relic source particle.","authors":"Sara Bianco, Paul Frederik Depta, Jonas Frerick, Thomas Hambye, Marco Hufnagel, Kai Schmidt-Hoberg","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01492"},{"id":"2606.02729","title":"Probabilistic Causality from Graviton Fluctuations","summary":"We compute the commutator of a scalar field minimally coupled to gravity at leading order in $G_N$. The commutator is operator-valued, with terms involving derivatives of Dirac deltas supported on the Minkowski light cone. When evaluated on classical/coherent graviton states, these terms ``bend\" the support of the commutator in precisely the way required to recover standard causality on a classical curved spacetime. However, these terms are also associated with a variance and are thus a source of uncertainty in the causal relations between events. We quantify this effect for a thermal state of gravitons at temperature $T$. In the large-time limit, the uncertainty of the lightcone position is given by $$ \\text{Var}(\\delta x) =\\frac{4G_NTt}{3}\\, , $$ where $\\delta x$ is the displacement from the classical lightcone at $|\\vec x| = t$. We obtain this result by subtracting a universal vacuum contribution that is logarithmically UV divergent and subleading at late times.","authors":"Giordano Cintia, Federico Piazza, Samuel Ramos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.02729"},{"id":"2606.11062","title":"A friendly phantom: Late-time AdS-to-dS transition and cosmological tensions","summary":"We present Ph-$\\Lambda_{\\rm s}$CDM, a phantom-scalar realization within general relativity of the sign-switching cosmological-constant idea, $\\Lambda_{\\rm s}$CDM, in which a phantom scalar evolving on a bounded hyperbolic-tangent potential induces a smooth mirror AdS-to-dS transition in the late-time dark-energy density. The wrong-sign kinetic term, usually viewed as pathological, becomes the mechanism lifting the field from a negative- to a positive-energy vacuum-like regime. The construction also shows that the field can become repulsive while its energy density is still negative. The cosmology nevertheless remains controlled: total energy stays positive, the late-time attractor is de Sitter rather than a Big Rip, and the dynamics remain safely infrared. Ph-$\\Lambda_{\\rm s}$CDM thus offers a concrete late-time mechanism with the potential to address multiple cosmological tensions.","authors":"\\\"Ozg\\\"ur Akarsu, Leandros Perivolaropoulos, A. Emrah Y\\\"ukselci, Alexander Zhuk","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11062"},{"id":"2607.27344","title":"Inflation in unimodular loop quantum cosmology","summary":"We study inflation in the setting of unimodular loop quantum cosmology, where time evolution is defined in unimodular time rather than with respect to a free, massless scalar field as is standard in loop quantum cosmology. The unimodular setting leads to a natural Schr\\\"odinger time evolution in a time coordinate with clear geometric meaning, defined independently of any particular matter content; an inflaton can be included but is not needed as a clock. We review the unimodular version of loop quantum cosmology and comment on possible connections to full (unimodular) loop quantum gravity. Then, focusing on semiclassical effective equations, we derive analytical solutions in simple cases such as a constant potential, emphasising the use of a unimodular time coordinate. We also discuss numerical solutions for phenomenologically interesting cases such as a quadratic potential and Starobinsky inflation, comparing different possible choices of initial conditions. In particular, we show that choosing an $\\alpha$-attractor potential allows for models of a bounce either dominated by kinetic or potential energy, which are compatible with observations while potentially including observable imprints of the quantum-gravity regime.","authors":"Steffen Gielen, Rita B. Neves","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.27344"},{"id":"2608.01762","title":"Primordial Correlators from a Kaluza-Klein Graviton Continuum","summary":"Cosmological collider signals are usually discussed for isolated massive particles, whose exchange produces characteristic logarithmic oscillations in primordial correlators. In this work, we study how this signal is modified when the exchanged states form a continuous mass spectrum. We first develop a spectral representation for inflationary correlators mediated by a continuum field. In the soft limit, the non-analytic part of the seed function is expressed as a Fourier--Laplace transform of the spectral weight with respect to logarithmic momentum variables. This representation shows that a continuum superposes clock signals with different frequencies and can dephase the sharp oscillation associated with a single massive particle. We then realize this mechanism in an RS2-like inflationary braneworld, where the inflaton is localized on a de Sitter brane in a five-dimensional AdS bulk. The tensor sector contains a localized massless graviton and a continuum of Kaluza-Klein gravitons starting at \\(m=3H/2\\). We derive the KK wavefunctions and identify the brane spectral weight fixed by the continuum wavefunction on the brane. Applying this weight to the inflaton four-point function, we find a smooth seed function rather than a persistent logarithmic clock oscillation. This behavior follows from the fact that the continuum starts at zero clock frequency, while the KK spectral weight vanishes near threshold. Our results provide a concrete higher-dimensional example in which deviations from the standard cosmological collider signal encode the structure of a continuum high-energy spectrum.","authors":"Shuntaro Aoki","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle","qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01762"},{"id":"2609.26767","title":"Tachyonic particle production in strongly supercooled phase transitions","summary":"In strongly supercooled cosmological phase transitions, which generically lead to strong, observable gravitational-wave backgrounds, the scalar field typically transitions to a value well below the minimum of its potential. We study particle production caused by the subsequent evolution of the field. Because some modes feature temporarily tachyonic masses, they can be efficiently produced, and a significant fraction of the energy density released in the phase transition can get converted to particles rather than gradient energy. This has consequences for the shape and amplitude of the gravitational-wave spectrum caused by the interactions in bubbles and sources a new, high-frequency signal.","authors":"Mateusz Kulejewski, Bogumila Swiezewska, Jorinde van de Vis","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26767"},{"id":"2611.21712","title":"Hidden wormholes from an action principle in $D=4$","summary":"We present new black hole solutions for the class of gravitational effective field theories in four spacetime dimensions that extend General Relativity while retaining the graviton as the only degree of freedom of gravitational origin. We consider terms up to cubic order in curvature, solve the resulting field equations, and find one-way hidden wormholes as numerical solutions: these are regular black holes with an interior wormhole throat that is crossable in one direction and leads to a cosmological spacetime in its interior. They are regular and have no inner horizon, thus evading the mass inflation instability that afflicts conventional regular black hole geometries.","authors":"Francesco Del Porro, Alessia Platania, Samuele Silveravalle","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21712"},{"id":"2611.21722","title":"Cosmic Censorship in the Landscape of SUSY-Broken AdS Vacua","summary":"We investigate signatures of weak cosmic censorship in non-supersymmetric vacua of both IIA and IIB string theory. In particular, we examine the Penrose Inequality, which can be proven holographically using Ryu-Takayanagi, and can be independently proven in SUSY AdS vacua for spherically symmetric initial data. We prove that any EFT of scalars coupled to gravity in AdS, including in particular those of string compactifications, satisfies both the Penrose Inequality and a positive mass theorem if a function that we call a \"weak fake superpotential\" exists. We demonstrate that this condition is satisfied globally for the DGKT skew-whiffed vacua in IIA. Since there is no known nonperturbative instability in this DGKT vacuum, this provides evidence that the IIA SUSY-broken AdS vacua may be absolutely stable. We also find the weak fake superpotential exists locally in field space in some SUSY-broken AdS vacua in a KKLT construction in IIB. Thus, within the framework considered here, some false SUSY-broken KKLT AdS vacua are stable against vacuum decay. Conversely, we find that some KKLT vacua do not have a weak fake superpotential, and correspondingly violate the Penrose Inequality and fail to have a positive mass theorem; the DGKT vacua only violate the Penrose Inequality in the presence of designer gravity boundary conditions. In all cases studied, this is due to false vacuum decay. We discuss the implications of these violations for holography and black hole thermodynamics in these SUSY-broken AdS vacua.","authors":"Netta Engelhardt, Richard Nally","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21722"},{"id":"2611.22041","title":"Quantum Born Geometries from Modular Quantization","summary":"A structural link between quantization and \"thermal-doubled\" non-commutative Born geometry (with its split-quaternionic structure) is uncovered via Tomita-Takesaki first-quantized modular theory for complexified and polarized ortho-symplectic spaces. Implications for the modular algebraic quantum gravity program (arXiv:1007.4094) hint at further deep modifications of the notions of space and geometry (thermal quantum space-time hypothesis) waiting to be further explored. Speculative suggestions in the direction of A.Sakharov and L.Boyle-K.Finn-N.Turok CPT-mirror cosmologies will be mentioned, among several others. The present work is based on the manuscript \"Modular Quantization and Non-commutative Space-time\" (now on alphaXiv) that has been shared, with a selected number of researchers, since end of April 2026.","authors":"Paolo Bertozzini, Roberto Conti","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th","math-ph","math.MP","math.OA","math.SG"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22041"},{"id":"2607.24277","title":"Modified Mukhanov-Sasaki equation and primordial perturbations in $\\kappa$-deformed non-commutative space-time","summary":"We study the inflationary primordial perturbations in $\\kappa$-Minkowski non-commutative space-time, a Lie-algebraic type deformation of canonical space-time motivated by quantum gravity scenarios. Employing the $\\kappa$-deformed star product formalism, we construct the bilinear action for curvature perturbations and derive the $\\kappa$-deformed Mukhanov-Sasaki equation and obtain the perturbative solutions. Further, we compute the primordial power spectrum and spectral index, showing that the leading order corrections to the power spectrum induces scale-dependent term proportional to $(\\ln k)^2$. The spectral index also exhibits an explicit $\\ln k$ dependence, which persists even when the slow-roll parameters are constant. We also perform a Bayesian MCMC analysis using ACT DR6 data and constrain the $\\kappa$-deformation length scale to $\\lambda=1.93^{+1.88}_{-1.33}\\times 10^{-29}m$ at $1\\sigma$ CL, approximately four orders of magnitude larger than the Planck scale, demonstrating that the $\\kappa$-deformed space-time offers a potential window into quantum gravity phenomenology through precision cosmology.","authors":"Lei Yang, Vishnu Rajagopal, Puxun Wu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24277"},{"id":"2603.23824","title":"Gravitational-lensing-modulated leptonic CP violation","summary":"The discovery of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos has recently prompted interest in the role of gravity in neutrino flavor conversion. In this work, we investigate the gravity-modulated CP violation (CPV) in neutrino oscillations in the Reissner--Nordstr\\\"om and Hayward spacetimes. Employing analytical and numerical methods, we reveal the interplay among the CPV, the properties of neutrinos, and the space-time. The shapes of the flavor-oscillation curves show that CPV signals encode both space-time geometry and mass ordering, and that the imprints of $m_l$ are intrinsically correlated with the singular versus nonsingular structure of the background metric. In particular, our finding demonstrate that the CPV observables exceed the limitation that, in asymptotically flat space-time, gravity neither damps nor amplifies neutrino-oscillation amplitudes.","authors":"Ze-Wen Li, Shu-Jun Rong, Ya-Ru Wang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23824"},{"id":"2604.12854","title":"Mixed-helicity bracket of celestial symmetries","summary":"Celestial symmetries of gravity and gauge theory can be enhanced to a $w_{1+\\infty}$ algebra and an $S$-algebra respectively, when restricting to a single graviton/gluon helicity sector. Difficulties in combining both sectors in the full theory have been pointed out in the previous literature. In this work, we face this problem from the covariant phase space perspective and analyze in detail the structure of the mixed-helicity bracket of the higher-spin charges for both gravity and Yang--Mills theory. We show that, when restricting one of the two helicities to the wedge sector, a closed algebra can be obtained for all spins in terms of a notion of shadow charge we introduce. Furthermore, when focusing on the lower spin subalgebra sectors, in the case of gravity, we show that a dual mass extension of the BMS algebra can be consistently constructed; in the case of Maxwell theory, inclusion of magnetic charges allows us to recover a non-vanishing expression for the electromagnetic central charge previously obtained through different methods.","authors":"Daniele Pranzetti, Domenico Giuseppe Salluce","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12854"},{"id":"2606.08080","title":"Weyl conformal geometry vs Riemannian geometry of Weyl gauge invariant (dressed) metric","summary":"Weyl conformal geometry is the natural underlying geometry of gauge theories of Weyl group (of dilatations and Poincar\\'e symmetry), such as Weyl quadratic gravity and its generalisation, Weyl-Dirac-Born-Infeld action (WDBI). These are local, Weyl-anomaly free (quantum) gauge theories of gravity. We describe Weyl gauge symmetry from a more familiar Riemannian view of Weyl gauge invariant dressed fields by the Wilson line of dilatations. Weyl geometry can then be seen as Riemannian geometry of non-local dressed metric ($g_{\\mu\\nu}^*$), at the \"cost\" of (gauge-induced) non-commutativity in the UV, due to Wilson line. Then Weyl quadratic gravity and WDBI actions of Weyl geometry, which are Weyl gauge invariant in $d$ dimensions, have the same expression in Riemannian geometry defined by $g^*_{\\mu\\nu}$. This is a non-local map and dual description of the two geometries and actions in the symmetric phase. Unlike for the metric, the equation of motion of Weyl gauge field ($\\omega_\\mu$) does not commute with the dressing of the metric. Quantum non-locality, in particular entanglement, and non-commutativity are (gauge-invariant) physical artefacts of \"translating\" (local) Weyl geometry and Weyl gauge covariance into our real-world Riemannian geometry of Weyl gauge invariant observables, and they are evidence of Weyl gauge symmetry. At lower energies, $\\omega_\\mu$ becomes massive, can decouple and Einstein-Hilbert action and commutativity are recovered. The case of a light $\\omega_\\mu$ is also discussed.","authors":"D. M. Ghilencea, V. -M. Mandric","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-ph","hep-th","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.08080"},{"id":"2609.10677","title":"Stokes Phenomena between AdS/CFT and dS/CFT","summary":"As parameters are varied, the set of saddle points contributing to a (path) integral may change discontinuously, leading to a corresponding change in the asymptotic expansion of the integral. This behavior is known as the Stokes phenomenon. We explore this phenomenon in the context of the analytic continuation problem relating the AdS/CFT and dS/CFT correspondences. In this paper, we study these correspondences for three-dimensional pure gravity and two-dimensional Liouville theory, using independent calculations in bulk minisuperspace and in the boundary Liouville zero-mode. In the bulk, the dS contour selects a single saddle and yields the tunneling wave function. Upon continuation to AdS, the contour instead selects an infinite family of saddles. The boundary calculation independently reproduces the same Stokes structure at leading semiclassical order, providing a nontrivial holographic consistency check. Our construction also offers a contour prescription for the conformal factor problem in Euclidean AdS$_3$ quantum gravity within minisuperspace.","authors":"Masazumi Honda, Kotaro Shinmyo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10677"},{"id":"2610.11664","title":"Extremal black hole mimickers: geometry, stability, and long-delay signal trains","summary":"We present a spacetime that serves as a horizonless mimicker of extremal black holes. Unlike previously studied mimickers of black holes with non-degenerate horizons, whose geometries are of the wormhole type and consist of two asymptotically flat regions connected by a very long throat, the mimicker of an extremal black hole possesses a single asymptotically flat region at one end and an infinitely long throat at the other. In the throat region, the spacetime approaches the direct-product geometry $M_{1,1} \\times S^2$, where $M_{1,1}$ is two-dimensional Minkowski spacetime and $S^2$ is the two-sphere. We investigate scalar-field dynamics and the fate of the Aretakis instability. Although Aretakis-like modes arise in a local analysis of the throat, they do not lead to a physical instability of the mimicker. A scalar perturbation initially localized in the throat gives rise, at late times, to a sequence of signals with a characteristic separation $t_{\\rm delay}\\sim 1/\\lambda$, where $\\lambda\\ll 1$ is a deformation parameter. This timescale is parametrically larger than the echo time $t_{\\rm echo}\\sim \\ln(1/\\lambda)$ characteristic of non-extremal black-hole mimickers. We refer to this sequence as a {\\it long-delay signal train}. Geometrically, the periodicity of the long-delay signals is associated with periodic geodesics on $S^2$. We then extend the construction to extremal Kerr. In this case, the throat region approaches a generalized product geometry involving $M_{1,1}$ and a two-dimensional surface $\\Sigma_2$. The corresponding signals are expected to form a quasi-periodic signal train, reflecting the quasi-periodic geodesic motion on $\\Sigma_2$. We also comment on the generalization to wormhole mimickers of non-extremal black holes and on the implications of these results for unitarity in the context of the AdS/CFT correspondence.","authors":"Sergey N. Solodukhin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11664"},{"id":"2611.21752","title":"Mapping the multimessenger horizon for $\\gamma$-ray counterparts of cosmic neutrinos","summary":"The joint detection of high-energy neutrinos and $\\gamma$-rays offers a powerful probe of the most energetic astrophysical accelerators, linking hadronic interactions to observable signatures across cosmic distances. Understanding the conditions under which these two messengers can be simultaneously observed is essential for identifying and characterizing the sources of cosmic neutrinos. In this work, we examine the detectability of $\\gamma$-ray cascades associated with neutrino production in source environments that are gamma-ray transparent, focusing on how electromagnetic cascades evolve as $\\gamma$-rays propagate through intergalactic space. By modeling photon interactions with the extragalactic background light and deflections in intergalactic magnetic fields, we determine the minimum neutrino fluence for transient emission and neutrino flux for steady emission required to produce a $\\gamma$-ray counterpart detectable by $\\gamma$-ray observatories, such as Fermi-LAT, HAWC, or LHAASO. This defines a ``multimessenger horizon\" -- the region in flux-distance parameter space where neutrinos and $\\gamma$-rays are jointly observable. We consider both energy-binned injection tied to IceCube's sensitivity and a continuous power-law spectrum extending from TeV to PeV energies. Our results reveal that detectability depends strongly on redshift, magnetic field strength, and photon energy, with higher-energy cascades suffering greater attenuation inside filamentary structures. The framework provides new constraints on source opacity, baryon loading, and neutrino production mechanisms, offering a predictive tool for interpreting current events and guiding future multimessenger campaigns.","authors":"Tianyi Ding, Jose Alonso Carpio, Ali Kheirandish, Kohta Murase","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21752"},{"id":"2611.21788","title":"Neutron-Rich Disks, Nickel-Rich Ejecta: Can Direct-Horizon Collapsars Explain Early $r$-Process Enrichment?","summary":"Very massive stars above the pair-instability mass gap may collapse directly into black holes surrounded by massive disks. These \"direct-horizon collapsars\" accrete at $\\dot{M} \\gtrsim 10\\,M_\\odot\\,{\\rm s}^{-1}$ and have been proposed to eject a substantial amount of $r$-process material, powering \"super-kilonovae\". We present the first three-dimensional general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamic simulations of direct-horizon collapsars with M1 neutrino transport, following a $\\approx 150\\,M_\\odot$ helium core from collapse through the escape of magnetically driven outflows. Extreme accretion rates neutronize the disks to $Y_{\\rm e} \\approx 0.1$-$0.25$ and, at early times, allow them to satisfy the gravitational-instability and cooling criteria for fragmentation. However, the strong poloidal fields needed to eject the neutron-rich gas promptly also drain the disks and lift their electron degeneracy. The neutron-rich ejecta composition is set in a brief phase, such that the ejecta carry $\\sim 0.1$-$0.3\\,M_\\odot$ of nuclei with $A \\geq 69$, including $\\lesssim 2\\times10^{-4}\\,M_\\odot$ of lanthanides, alongside $\\sim 1$-$4\\,M_\\odot$ of $^{56}$Ni. Assuming proportional retention and mixing of Eu and Fe, these yields imply $[{\\rm Eu/Fe}]\\approx-0.4$ to $+0.3$, and simple dilution estimates permit enrichment at $[{\\rm Fe/H}]\\lesssim-2.6$. Direct-horizon collapsars are therefore potential prompt contributors to iron and $r$-process enrichment at low metallicity. Their predicted Eu/Fe ratios fall below those of strongly $r$-process-enhanced stars, but uncertainties in the relative yields, particularly of iron, may close this gap. The ${}^{56}{\\rm Ni}$-rich ejecta would power luminous supernovae with rise times of weeks, potentially accompanied by exceptionally powerful gamma-ray bursts, rather than lanthanide-rich super-kilonovae.","authors":"Danat Issa, Ore Gottlieb","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE","astro-ph.SR"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21788"},{"id":"2611.22417","title":"Relativistic tidal excitation of g- and i-modes in neutron star binaries","summary":"When the gravitational wave frequency of an inspiraling binary matches a normal-mode frequency of a neutron star (NS), the mode can be resonantly excited. The excitation transfers orbital energy to the star and shifts the phase of the emitted gravitational-wave (GW) signal, thereby providing a potential probe of the NS interior. However, estimates of this phase shift in the literature vary by orders of magnitude due to different approximations and formulations used in calculating the tidal coupling strength. We revisit this problem using a fully relativistic scattering formalism, which serves as our benchmark calculation and provides a gauge-invariant mode response together with an effective tidal-mode coupling strength. We calculate the phase shifts induced by the excitation of stratification g-modes, crustal interface i-modes, and hadron-quark interface i-modes, and estimate their detectability with Fisher-matrix forecasts for current and next-generation detectors. In our models, direct g-mode excitation remains below the detection thresholds, even for the strongest stratification considered. Crust-core i-modes at large shear speeds provide the most favorable prospects and crust melting can produce a substantially larger phase shift. Hadron-quark i-modes remain below the next-generation detection thresholds.","authors":"Binyu Zhong, Zhiqiang Miao, Zhen Pan, Huan Yang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22417"},{"id":"2611.22044","title":"Current Accretion State versus Black Hole Mass: Imprints on Multiphase Gas in IllustrisTNG","summary":"The coupling between AGN feedback and the multiphase circumgalactic medium (CGM) is central to understanding galaxy evolution. In a previous paper, we showed that the black hole mass ($M_{\\rm BH}$) correlates negatively with cold gas mass in IllustrisTNG100. We now extend this to the Eddington ratio ($\\lambda_{\\rm Edd}$), which traces the current accretion state, using the same sample of 5089 central galaxies at $z=0$ under the same group-specific partial correlation framework, controlling for stellar and dark matter halo mass. We find that $\\lambda_{\\rm Edd}$ correlates positively with warm gas in star-forming galaxies ($\\rho \\approx 0.6$-$0.7$), persisting after jointly controlling for $M_{\\rm BH}$, the mass in the other non-star-forming gas phases, and specific star formation rate. The $\\lambda_{\\rm Edd}$-cold gas correlation instead reverses sign, from negative in star-forming galaxies ($\\rho \\approx -0.32$) to positive in quenched galaxies ($\\rho \\approx 0.40$). Controlling for $M_{\\rm BH}$ leaves this correlation essentially unchanged in all three galaxy types. Further controlling for the total gas mass in the other phases strengthens the star-forming correlation and weakens the quenched correlation to $\\rho \\approx 0.21$, which remains significant. These results establish the Eddington ratio as a phase- and type-dependent tracer of the gas-black hole connection: the cold gas reservoir records the integrated black hole growth, the warm gas in star-forming galaxies carries the signature of the current accretion state, and the sign reversal of the cold-gas correlation marks the transition between the star-forming and quenched regimes.","authors":"Xiaoxia Zhang, Taotao Fang, Shulan Yan, Si-Yue Yu, Feng Yuan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22044"},{"id":"2602.05955","title":"Improved Heavy Dark Matter Annihilation Search from Dwarf Galaxies with HAWC","summary":"Understanding dark matter's elusive nature is crucial for the framework of particle physics and expanding the Standard Model. This analysis utilizes the High Altitude Water Cherenkov (HAWC) gamma ray Observatory to indirectly search for dark matter (DM) by studying gamma ray emission from dwarf spheroidal galaxies (dSphs). Selected for their high ratio of dark matter to baryonic matter, dSphs are useful for this type of search owing to the low background emission. In comparison to previous HAWC studies, we significantly improve our sensitivity to DM from dSphs due to improvements to our event reconstruction and reduced hadronic contamination. We expanded the number of dSphs studied, DM annihilation channels into the Standard Model (SM), and the amount of data collected on each previously studied dSph. We searched for DM signals in each dSph using the latest version of the algorithms used to reconstruct data from the primary detector of the HAWC instrument. We report that we do not detect evidence of DM from dSphs, so we place upper limits for the velocity-weighted DM annihilation cross-section ($\\langle\\sigma v \\rangle$) on the order of $10^{-23}~\\text{cm}^3\\text{s}^{-1}$ for a DM mass range of $1-10^4$ TeV.","authors":"A. Albert, R. Alfaro, C. Alvarez, A. Andr\\'es, E. Anita-Rangel, M. Araya, J. C. Arteaga-Vel\\'azquez, D. Avila Rojas, H. A. Ayala Solares, R. Babu, P. Bangale, E. Belmont-Moreno, A. Bernal, K. S. Caballero-Mora, T. Capistr\\'an, A. Carrami\\~nana, F. Carre\\'on, S. Casanova, A. L. Colmenero-Cesar, U. Cotti, J. Cotzomi, S. Couti\\~no de Le\\'on, E. De la Fuente, C. de Le\\'on, P. Desiati, N. Di Lalla, R. Diaz Hernandez, M. A. DuVernois, J. C. D\\'iaz-V\\'elez, K. Engel, T. Ergin, C. Espinoza, N. Fraija, S. Fraija, A. Galv\\'an-G\\'amez, J. A. Garc\\'ia-Gonz\\'alez, F. Garfias, N. Ghosh, A. Gonzalez Mu\\~noz, M. M. Gonz\\'alez, J. A. Gonz\\'alez, J. A. Goodman, J. Gyeong, J. P. Harding, S. Hern\\'andez-Cadena, I. Herzog, D. Huang, F. Hueyotl-Zahuantitla, P. H\\\"untemeyer, A. Iriarte, S. Kaufmann, D. Kieda, K. Leavitt, W. H. Lee, J. Lee, H. Le\\'on Vargas, J. T. Linnemann, A. L. Longinotti, G. Luis-Raya, C. Lundy, K. Malone, O. Martinez, J. Mart\\'inez-Castro, H. Mart\\'inez-Huerta, J. A. Matthews, P. Miranda-Romagnoli, P. E. Mir\\'on-Enriquez, J. A. Morales-Soto, E. Moreno, M. Mostaf\\'a, M. Najafi, A. Nayerhoda, L. Nellen, M. U. Nisa, R. Noriega-Papaqui, N. Omodei, E. Ponce, Y. P\\'erez Araujo, E. G. P\\'erez-P\\'erez, C. D. Rho, A. Rodriguez Parra, D. Rosa-Gonz\\'alez, M. Roth, H. Salazar, D. Salazar-Gallegos, A. Sandoval, M. Schneider, J. Serna-Franco, M. Shin, A. J. Smith, Y. Son, R. W. Springer, O. Tibolla, K. Tollefson, I. Torres, R. Torres-Escobedo, F. Ure\\~na-Mena, E. Varela, L. Villase\\~nor, X. Wang, Z. Wang, I. J. Watson, H. Wu, S. Yu, X. Zhang, H. Zhou","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05955"},{"id":"2605.19040","title":"IceCube Second Track Data Release IceTracks-DR2: Data from 2008-2022 for Neutrino Source Searches","summary":"We present IceCube's latest release of muon track data for neutrino point-source searches, extending the previously published 10-year dataset to cover 14 years of observations (April 6, 2008 - May 23, 2022). This release features an updated event selection and improved detector calibration for data recorded after June 1, 2010. The release also includes binned instrument response functions and effective areas, enabling the community to perform sensitive searches for steady and transient neutrino sources. We report on key science results obtained with this dataset using internal IceCube analysis tools and compare them to those derived from analyses based on the binned response functions included in this public release. To facilitate reproducible research, we provide benchmark results obtained using this data release and publicly available software. This release represents IceCube's most sensitive and comprehensive publicly available all-sky muon track dataset to date and should be preferred over previous releases.","authors":"R. Abbasi, M. Ackermann, J. Adams, J. A. Aguilar, M. Ahlers, J. M. Alameddine, S. Ali, N. M. Amin, K. Andeen, C. Arg\\\"uelles, Y. Ashida, S. Athanasiadou, S. N. Axani, R. Babu, X. Bai, A. Balagopal V., S. W. Barwick, V. Basu, R. Bay, J. J. Beatty, J. Becker Tjus, P. Behrens, J. Beise, C. Bellenghi, S. Benkel, S. BenZvi, D. Berley, E. Bernardini, D. Z. Besson, E. Blaufuss, L. Bloom, S. Blot, F. Bontempo, J. Y. Book Motzkin, C. Boscolo Meneguolo, S. B\\\"oser, O. Botner, J. B\\\"ottcher, J. Braun, B. Brinson, Z. Brisson-Tsavoussis, R. T. Burley, D. Butterfield, K. Carloni, J. Carpio, N. Chau, Y. C. Chen, Z. Chen, D. Chirkin, S. Choi, A. Chubarov, B. A. Clark, G. H. Collin, D. A. Coloma Borja, A. Connolly, J. M. Conrad, D. F. Cowen, C. De Clercq, J. J. DeLaunay, D. Delgado, T. Delmeulle, S. Deng, P. Desiati, K. D. de Vries, G. de Wasseige, T. DeYoung, J. C. D\\'iaz-V\\'elez, S. DiKerby, T. Ding, M. Dittmer, A. Domi, L. Draper, L. Dueser, D. Durnford, K. Dutta, M. A. DuVernois, T. Ehrhardt, L. Eidenschink, A. Eimer, C. Eldridge, P. Eller, E. Ellinger, D. Els\\\"asser, R. Engel, H. Erpenbeck, W. Esmail, S. Eulig, J. Evans, P. A. Evenson, K. L. Fan, K. Fang, K. Farrag, A. R. Fazely, A. Fedynitch, N. Feigl, C. Finley, D. Fox, A. Franckowiak, S. Fukami, P. F\\\"urst, J. Gallagher, E. Ganster, A. Garcia, M. Garcia, E. Genton, L. Gerhardt, A. Ghadimi, C. Glaser, T. Gl\\\"usenkamp, J. G. Gonzalez, S. Goswami, A. Granados, D. Grant, S. J. Gray, S. Griffin, K. M. Groth, D. Guevel, C. G\\\"unther, P. Gutjahr, C. Ha, A. Hallgren, L. Halve, F. Halzen, L. Hamacher, M. Handt, K. Hanson, J. Hardin, A. A. Harnisch, P. Hatch, A. Haungs, J. H\\\"au{\\ss}ler, K. Helbing, J. Hellrung, B. Henke, L. Hennig, F. Henningsen, L. Heuermann, R. Hewett, N. Heyer, S. Hickford, A. Hidvegi, C. Hill, G. C. Hill, R. Hmaid, K. D. Hoffman, A. Hollnagel, D. Hooper, S. Hori, K. Hoshina, M. Hostert, W. Hou, M. Hrywniak, T. Huber, K. Hultqvist, K. Hymon, A. Ishihara, W. Iwakiri, M. Jacquart, S. Jain, O. Janik, M. Jansson, M. Jin, N. Kamp, D. Kang, W. Kang, A. Kappes, L. Kardum, T. Karg, A. Karle, A. Katil, M. Kauer, J. L. Kelley, M. Khanal, A. Khatee Zathul, A. Kheirandish, T. Kim, H. Kimku, F. Kirchner, J. Kiryluk, C. Klein, S. R. Klein, Y. Kobayashi, S. Koch, A. Kochocki, R. Koirala, H. Kolanoski, T. Kontrimas, L. K\\\"opke, C. Kopper, D. J. Koskinen, P. Koundal, M. Kowalski, T. Kozynets, A. Kravka, N. Krieger, T. Krishnan, K. Kruiswijk, E. Krupczak, A. Kumar, E. Kun, N. Kurahashi, C. Lagunas Gualda, L. Lallement Arnaud, M. J. Larson, F. Lauber, J. P. Lazar, K. Leonard DeHolton, A. Leszczy\\'nska, C. Li, J. Liao, C. Lin, Q. R. Liu, Y. T. Liu, M. Liubarska, C. Love, L. Lu, F. Lucarelli, W. Luszczak, Y. Lyu, M. Macdonald, E. Magnus, Y. Makino, E. Manao, S. Mancina, A. Mand, I. C. Mari\\c{s}, S. Marka, Z. Marka, L. Marten, I. Martinez-Soler, R. Maruyama, J. Mauro, F. Mayhew, F. McNally, K. Meagher, A. Medina, M. Meier, Y. Merckx, L. Merten, J. Mitchell, L. Molchany, S. Mondal, T. Montaruli, R. W. Moore, Y. Morii, A. Mosbrugger, D. Mousadi, E. Moyaux, T. Mukherjee, M. Nakos, U. Naumann, L. Neste, M. Neumann, H. Niederhausen, M. U. Nisa, K. Noda, A. Noell, A. Novikov, A. Obertacke, V. O'Dell, A. Olivas, R. Orsoe, J. Osborn, E. O'Sullivan, B. Owens, V. Palusova, H. Pandya, A. Parenti, N. Park, V. Parrish, E. N. Paudel, L. Paul, C. P\\'erez de los Heros, T. Pernice, T. C. Petersen, J. Peterson, S. Pick, M. Plum, A. Pont\\'en, V. Poojyam, B. Pries, R. Procter-Murphy, G. T. Przybylski, L. Pyras, C. Raab, J. Rack-Helleis, N. Rad, M. Ravn, K. Rawlins, Z. Rechav, A. Rehman, I. Reistroffer, E. Resconi, C. D. Rho, W. Rhode, L. Ricca, B. Riedel, A. Rifaie, E. J. Roberts, S. Rodan, M. Rongen, A. Rosted, C. Rott, T. Ruhe, L. Ruohan, D. Ryckbosch, J. Saffer, D. Salazar-Gallegos, P. Sampathkumar, A. Sandrock, G. Sanger-Johnson, M. Santander, S. Sarkar, M. Scarnera, M. Schaufel, H. Schieler, S. Schindler, L. Schlickmann, B. Schl\\\"uter, F. Schl\\\"uter, N. Schmeisser, T. Schmidt, A. Scholz, F. G. Schr\\\"oder, S. Schwirn, S. Sclafani, D. Seckel, L. Seen, M. Seikh, S. Seunarine, P. A. Sevle Myhr, R. Shah, S. Shah, S. Shefali, N. Shimizu, B. Skrzypek, R. Snihur, J. Soedingrekso, D. Soldin, P. Soldin, G. Sommani, D. Song, C. Spannfellner, G. M. Spiczak, C. Spiering, J. Stachurska, M. Stamatikos, T. Stanev, T. Stezelberger, T. St\\\"urwald, T. Stuttard, G. W. Sullivan, I. Taboada, S. Ter-Antonyan, A. Terliuk, A. Thakuri, M. Thiesmeyer, W. G. Thompson, J. Thwaites, S. Tilav, K. Tollefson, J. A. Torres, S. Toscano, D. Tosi, K. Upshaw, A. Vaidyanathan, N. Valtonen-Mattila, J. Valverde, J. Vandenbroucke, T. Van Eeden, N. van Eijndhoven, L. Van Rootselaar, J. van Santen, J. Vara, F. Varsi, M. Venugopal, M. Vereecken, S. Vergara Carrasco, S. Verpoest, D. Veske, A. Vijai, J. Villarreal, C. Walck, A. Wang, E. H. S. Warrick, C. Weaver, P. Weigel, A. Weindl, J. Weldert, A. Y. Wen, C. Wendt, J. Werthebach, M. Weyrauch, N. Whitehorn, C. H. Wiebusch, D. R. Williams, L. Witthaus, G. Wrede, X. W. Xu, J. P. Yanez, Y. Yao, E. Yildizci, S. Yoshida, R. Young, F. Yu, S. Yu, T. Yuan, S. Yun-C\\'arcamo, A. Zander Jurowitzki, A. Zegarelli, S. Zhang, Z. Zhang, P. Zhelnin, P. Zilberman, C. Zilleruelo Ca\\~nas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19040"},{"id":"2608.05106","title":"Constraining Lorentz invariance violation from the depth of air-shower maximum","summary":"Hypothetical Lorentz invariance violation (LIV) remains strongly constrained but has not been excluded as a possible manifestation of new high-energy physics. In subluminal photon-sector LIV, suppressed Bethe$\\unicode{x2013}$Heitler pair production modifies the electromagnetic component of extensive air showers. We present a simulation-based toy analysis of this effect using published Pierre Auger fluorescence-detector distributions of the reconstructed depth of air-shower maximum. The modified distributions of the depth of the shower maximum are folded with the Auger detector-response parametrizations and compared with the data using free composition fractions in each reconstructed-energy bin. Within this approach, the comparison gives a sensitivity to LIV compatible with that expected from ultra-high-energy photon-induced showers. We obtain $M_{\\rm LIV}\\gtrsim 6.5\\times 10^{18}\\,\\text{GeV}$ for the photon-sector LIV mass scale $M_{\\rm LIV}$ ($95\\%$ confidence level). This result should not be interpreted as a detector-level experimental limit because the detector response and reconstruction are simplified, and the result remains composition dependent. However, the approach presented here opens the way for constraining LIV in hadronic air showers with more detailed analyses.","authors":"Nickolay S. Martynenko, Grigory I. Rubtsov, Petr S. Satunin, Andrey K. Sharofeev, Sergey V. Troitsky","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05106"},{"id":"2608.28975","title":"Little Red Dots as Shock-Powered High-Energy Neutrino Sources","summary":"Little Red Dots (LRDs) are compact, high-redshift sources whose physical nature remains uncertain. Their optical spectra motivate a scenario in which their emission may be powered by shocks interacting with dense surrounding material. We investigate whether such interactions can power LRDs and contribute to the diffuse high-energy neutrino intensity measured by IceCube. In our simplified model, a fast central-engine outflow drives a shock through stationary or slow outflowing material, followed by a prescribed quasi-stationary interaction near the LRD photosphere. We consider the time-averaged neutrino emission from shock-active LRDs without requiring a single shock to persist throughout the LRD lifetime. We explore parameter ranges motivated by the observed and inferred properties of LRDs, finding solutions with shock luminosities 2.0e43 < L_s < 3.5e44 erg/s. Using an analytical framework for cosmic-ray acceleration and hadronic interactions in dense shock environments, we calculate the resulting high-energy neutrino emission and integrate it over the cosmological LRD population. For our fiducial cosmic-ray parameters, the average predicted contribution below 2e5 GeV increases from 0.2% for the lowest-luminosity quintile to 2% for the highest-luminosity quintile, while an illustrative higher-efficiency case reaches 13% of the IceCube diffuse neutrino intensity. These estimates assume all observed LRDs are shock-active. A smaller active fraction or a reduced covering factor would decrease the predicted intensity, whereas survey incompleteness could increase it. Improved models and population synthesis are needed to constrain the total LRD contribution.","authors":"Tyco Mera, Chris Ashall, Shunsaku Horiuchi, Rohan Naidu, Kyle Medler, Peter Hoeflich","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.28975"},{"id":"2609.28057","title":"A Diagnostic Method for Proto-Neutron Star Magnetic Fields by Supernova Fallback Neutrinos","summary":"Young isolated neutron stars exhibit a wide range of magnetic field strengths. To understand the origin of this diversity, it is important to observationally constrain magnetic fields during the proto-neutron star (PNS) phase. We propose a novel diagnostic of PNS magnetic fields using neutrinos associated with supernova fallback. By performing one-dimensional general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamic simulations of the supernova fallback onto a magnetized PNS, we investigate how the energy spectra of the fallback neutrinos depend on the PNS magnetic field strength and the fallback accretion rate. We find that stronger magnetic fields and lower accretion rates produce softer spectra because the fallback neutrinos are emitted mainly near the magnetospheric radius, which becomes larger under these conditions. To quantify this spectral softening, we define a hardness ratio based on event rates above and below 30 MeV and introduce a hardness-event-rate diagram. Using this diagram, we identify the parameter region in which our diagnostic is applicable. Its applicability depends on the PNS magnetic field strength, fallback accretion rate, onset time of the fallback phase, and neutrino mass ordering. We also investigate the dependence on the PNS radius and find that a larger radius produces softer spectra, indicating a degeneracy between the magnetic field and PNS compactness. An independent constraint on the PNS compactness would therefore enable us to better constrain the magnetic field. These results suggest that future multimessenger observations of galactic supernovae can provide a new window into the NS diversity.","authors":"Akihiro Inoue, Yudai Suwa, Ryuichiro Akaho, Shinsuke Takasao, Kazumi Kashiyama, Hiroyuki R. Takahashi","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28057"},{"id":"2611.21741","title":"Smuggling baryon asymmetry through strong washout","summary":"We propose a scheme in which a primordial net baryon number generated at or around the unification scale may survive subsequent strong washout phases associated with active sphalerons and interactions of right-handed neutrinos inherent to thermal leptogenesis. A simple realization of this, namely, a long-lived scalar population injecting the net $B-L$ it carries into the Standard Model matter sector late enough to be safe from a strong washout, is constructed and studied in the context of a simple Pati-Salam scalar sector with a heavy $SU(4)_C$ sextet scalar.","authors":"K{\\aa}re Fridell, Michal Malinsk\\'y, Javier Perez-Soler","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21741"},{"id":"2611.21747","title":"Perturbative QCD Analysis for the Electric Dipole Moment of $\\Xi$ and $\\Sigma$ Hyperons from CP-Violating Dipole Interactions","summary":"Hyperon electric dipole moments (EDMs) probe CP-violating interactions beyond the Standard Model. Motivated by the recent BESIII measurement [Ablikim et al., Science 393, 1027] and the perturbative QCD analysis of the $\\Lambda$ EDM form factor $d_\\Lambda$ [Chen et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 136, 051902], we extend the perturbative QCD calculation of the EDM form factor to the $\\Xi$ and $\\Sigma$ hyperons. We derive factorization formulas from quark EDM and chromoelectric dipole moment contributions in terms of leading-twist light-cone distribution amplitudes (LCDAs). Using lattice-QCD results for the LCDAs and the strange-quark dipole moment ranges inferred from the BESIII $d_\\Lambda$ bound and neutron EDM constraints, we obtain the conditional estimates $-4.8 \\times 10^{-18} < d_{\\Xi^-} < 1.8\\times10^{-18}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ and $-6.1\\times10^{-19} < d_{\\Sigma^+} < 2.4\\times10^{-19}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ at the scale of ${J/\\psi}$ mass. Dedicated searches at BESIII and the future Super Tau-Charm Factory could further constrain these quark-level sources of CP violation.","authors":"Xi-Yu Mu, Kai-Bao Chen, Jian-Ping Ma, Xuan-Bo Tong","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21747"},{"id":"2611.21757","title":"Analytic next-to-leading order electroweak corrections to Higgs boson pair production in the forward limit","summary":"We calculate next-to-leading order electroweak corrections for Higgs boson pair production in the gluon fusion channel, in the forward limit. We perform an analytic calculation and obtain a final result for the partonic cross section that depends explicitly on all kinematic parameters and particle masses. We describe our method, which can be readily applied to similar processes. We observe rapid convergence within the validity range of the forward expansion. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the entire phase space can be covered when this approach is combined with the high-energy expansion.","authors":"Joshua Davies, Dominik Grau, Kay Sch\\\"onwald, Matthias Steinhauser, Daniel Stremmer","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21757"},{"id":"2611.21763","title":"A new angle on coherence-violating logarithms in color-singlet processes","summary":"We introduce a new formulation for the resummation of coherence-violating logarithms for the transverse energy flow in rapidity gaps in the Drell-Yan process. Our formalism allows us to resum coherence-violating logarithms in the gap cross section in the phenomenologically important regime where the gap size is comparable to the logarithm of the transverse energy. In the formal small gap limit, our result reduces to the known state-of-the-art resummation of coherence-violating logarithms for the Drell-Yan process. However, we identify sizable new corrections even for moderate gap widths that significantly modify the phenomenological picture for LHC applications. This work will form an important element of future extensions to a range of global and non-global observables.","authors":"Mrinal Dasgupta, Alexander Fraley, Pier Francesco Monni, Saad Nabeebaccus","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21763"},{"id":"2611.21813","title":"Probing $\\tau$ lepton dipole moments at multi-TeV muon colliders","summary":"We investigate the sensitivity of future multi-TeV muon colliders to the electromagnetic and weak dipole moments of the $\\tau$ lepton within the dimension-six Standard Model Effective Field Theory. We consider $\\tau^+\\tau^-$ production through $\\mu^+\\mu^-$ annihilation and vector-boson scattering, electroweak-boson-associated $\\tau$ production, and the radiative Higgs decay $H\\to\\tau^+\\tau^-\\gamma$. At high energies, the dipole-induced contributions quadratic in the Wilson coefficients become particularly important, with associated Higgs production, $\\mu^+\\mu^-\\to\\tau^+\\tau^-H$, providing the strongest sensitivity through energy-enhanced contact interactions. The remaining channels offer complementary probes of the underlying dipole operators. For a $10$~TeV muon collider with an integrated luminosity of $10\\,\\mathrm{ab}^{-1}$, we obtain combined sensitivities at the nominal $95\\%$ confidence level of $|\\Delta a_\\tau|<1.2\\times10^{-5}$, $|d_\\tau|<6.6\\times10^{-20}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$, $|\\Delta a_\\tau^Z|<1.8\\times10^{-5}$, and $|d_\\tau^Z|<1.0\\times10^{-19}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. Compared with FCC-ee projections, the muon collider offers substantially stronger sensitivity to the electric dipole moments, while both facilities offer complementary probes of magnetic dipole moments. These results motivate further studies incorporating realistic detector effects and systematic uncertainties.","authors":"Dario Buttazzo, Gabriele Levati, Yang Ma, Fabio Maltoni, Paride Paradisi, ZeQiang Wang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21813"},{"id":"2611.22036","title":"Double parton scattering singularities in two-loop gluon-fusion amplitudes","summary":"Gluon fusion to two massive colour singlets can develop a double parton scattering (DPS) singularity as $p_T\\to0$: each gluon splits, and its daughters fuse pairwise. We determine two-loop logarithms using a common kernel and derive finite matching functions analytically for gluon and quark splittings. Normalised to one loop, the cubic coefficient changes sign between channels. Complete $gg\\to HH$, $gg\\to AA$ and $gg\\to ZZ$ amplitudes confirm the predicted colour, helicity and parity dependence and phase. The kernel gives leading DPS logarithms for other pairs with this DPS configuration.","authors":"Arunima Bhattacharya","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22036"},{"id":"2611.22237","title":"Flavor Entanglement as a CPT Violation Witness in Neutrino Oscillations","summary":"We present a comprehensive study linking the single-particle flavor entanglement of a \\(\\nu_{e} \\rightarrow \\nu_{\\mu}\\) oscillation to CPT violation using the concurrence. In a two-flavor vacuum with CP-conserving mixing, CPT invariance strictly demands \\(\\mathcal{C}_{\\nu}(L) = \\mathcal{C}_{\\bar{\\nu}}(L)\\), meaning any measured difference \\(\\Delta\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathrm{CPT}} \\equiv \\mathcal{C}_{\\nu} - \\mathcal{C}_{\\bar{\\nu}}\\) serves as a clean, background-free witness of CPT violation. We derive exact, first-order analytical relations connecting this entanglement asymmetry to the standard probability asymmetry, the baseline reflection sums, and the long-baseline decoherence floor. To rigorously test the experimental viability of this framework, we perform extensive numerical simulations. We demonstrate that the framework projects stringent sensitivity limits on Standard-Model Extension (SME) parameters at DUNE-like densities, isolate a coordinate-independent CPT invariant, and show that the signal remains robust across realistic, variable Earth matter density profiles. Crucially, we identify a distinct 24-hour sidereal modulation of the entanglement asymmetry, providing a definitive temporal \"smoking gun\" to disentangle CPT violation from matter effects. Finally, we show that at PeV astrophysical energies, the reflection-sum mismatch scales up to \\(10^{13}\\) meters, opening a novel frontier for CPT tests beyond terrestrial experiments.","authors":"Bipin Singh Koranga, Biswmkwr Boro, Vivek Kumar Nautiyal","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22237"},{"id":"2611.22421","title":"Top-Quark Physics after Evidence for Toponium","summary":"The top quark decays before it hadronizes, and toponium, the t\\bar t bound state, was long thought unobservable as a resonance. CMS and ATLAS each report an excess of top-quark pairs at threshold, above five standard deviations over fixed-order quantum chromodynamics (QCD), and CMS confirms it in a second decay channel. Its angular distributions match the color-singlet pseudoscalar quasi-bound state \\eta_t of nonrelativistic QCD, although an additional pseudoscalar boson is not excluded and higher-order fixed-order QCD accounts for part of the enhancement. We review the theory, ATLAS's nominal Green's-function signal model, and the measurements, whose cross sections range from about 3 to 13 pb. Part of the spread comes from the signal model and part persists between analyses using the same model. We discuss consequences for entanglement, the top mass, and heavy-pseudoscalar searches. We then review the top-quark program, from mass definitions to electroweak precision, flavor, the charge-conjugation and parity (CP) structure of the top Yukawa coupling, supersymmetry, multi-top production, and effective field theory. We close with prospects at the High-Luminosity LHC and future colliders, where an e^+e^- threshold scan can determine the top-quark mass in a short-distance scheme to a few tens of MeV.","authors":"Howard Baer, Vernon Barger","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22421"},{"id":"2611.22424","title":"Triangle singularity with different dynamical couplings","summary":"We carry out a systematic study of the impact of dynamical couplings of the vertices on the manifestations of the triangle singularity (TS) in the loop amplitude. Twelve combinations of external and internal spin-parity quantum numbers, covering the $PPS$, $VPP$, $VVP$, $VPA$, $VVV$, $VVA$, and $VVS$ effective vertices, are enumerated. Each loop amplitude is reduced via the Passarino--Veltman decomposition into scalar integrals $A_{0}$, $B_{0}$, and $C_{0}$, whose Lorentz-scalar coefficients encode the dynamical content of the vertices. Using the masses and kinematics of the $\\eta(1405)\\to\\pi^{+}\\pi^{-}\\pi^{0}$ transition via the $K^{*}\\bar{K}K$ loop with the intermediate $f_{0}(980)$ resonance in the $\\pi^+\\pi^-$ invariant spectrum as a common numerical benchmark for the twelve spin-parity assignments, we find that the same loop topology produces three distinct phenomena which manifest through the lineshapes, i.e. a narrow peak dominated by the $C_{0}$ contribution; a suppressed peak in which the $B_{0}$ and $C_{0}$ function contributions are comparable; and a cusp-like profile in which the coefficient of the leading Landau logarithm vanishes at the physical TS point. These results indicate that the visibility of a TS enhancement is not fixed by the Landau condition alone but reshaped by the detailed dynamics involved in the loop transitions.","authors":"Jun Wang, Xiao-Hai Liu, Qiang Zhao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22424"},{"id":"2611.22615","title":"Lie-Poisson Formulation of Dissipative Spin Hydrodynamics","summary":"Covariance and the second law cannot fix how fluid flow couples to spin, since reversible couplings produce no entropy. We show that the spin commutation relations and the Poincar\\'e algebra fix these couplings. Reversibly, a flow exerts no torque on the spin: its magnitude per particle is conserved, its direction is Fermi--Walker transported, and spin--shear couplings reflect a deforming triad. The magnitude changes only through higher multipoles, as in spinor condensates, or dissipation, whose first-order spin--shear coupling reorients the spin away from the vorticity above a critical shear rate.","authors":"Zi-Hao Liu, Mei Huang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.soft","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22615"},{"id":"2212.06862","title":"Global Fit of Modified Quark Couplings to EW Gauge Bosons and Vector-Like Quarks in Light of the Cabibbo Angle Anomaly","summary":"There are two tensions related to the Cabibbo angle of the CKM matrix. First, the determinations of $V_{us}$ from $K_{\\mu 2}$, $K_{\\ell3}$, and $\\tau$ decays disagree at the $3\\sigma$ level. Second, using the average of these results in combination with $\\beta$ decays (including super-allowed $\\beta$ decays and neutron decay), a deficit in first-row CKM unitarity with a significance of again about $3\\sigma$ is found. These discrepancies, known as the Cabibbo Angle anomaly, can in principle be solved by modifications of $W$ boson couplings to quarks. However, due to $SU(2)_L$ invariance, $Z$ couplings to quarks are also modified and flavour changing neutral currents can occur. In order to consistently assess the agreement of a new physics hypothesis with data, we perform a combined analysis for all dimension-six Standard Model Effective Field Theory operators that generate modified $W$ couplings to first and second generation quarks. We then study models with vector-like quarks, which are prime candidates for a corresponding UV completion as they can affect $W$-quark couplings at tree level, and we perform a global fit including flavour observables (in particular loop effects in $\\Delta F=2$ processes). We find that the best fit can be obtained for the $SU(2)_L$ doublet vector-like quark $Q$ as it can generate right-handed $W$-$u$-$d$ and $W$-$u$-$s$ couplings as preferred by data.","authors":"Andreas Crivellin, Matthew Kirk, Teppei Kitahara, Federico Mescia","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.06862"},{"id":"2404.04021","title":"Vacuum correlations of the stress-energy-momentum tensor with constituent quarks","summary":"The two point correlation function of the stress-energy-momentum tensor describes the propagation of a space-time ``micro-earthquake'' in the vacuum. In the framework of the path integral formulation of field theory in curved space-time, we discuss the Ward-Takashi identity for two-point Green's function of the stress-energy-momentum tensor for a general case of a non-conformal theory. The identity constrains the longitudinal part of the correlator, with the vacuum expectation value of the stress-energy-momentum, non-zero in a non-conformal theory. The obtained formula is demonstrated on the free massive Dirac fermion theory, treated at the one-loop level. This example befits a class of phenomenological chiral quarks models which have been used successfully in numerous applications in the soft non-perturbative regime of strong interactions. We stress the role of the constraints following from the Ward-Takahashi identity for the correlation functions in these models and point out the differences with the low-energy theorems (sum rules), not complying to the symmetry requirements. We also show the temporal representation of the two-point correlators, which is an object amenable to lattice QCD simulations.","authors":"Mirko Serino, Wojciech Broniowski, Enrique Ruiz Arriola","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-lat","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04021"},{"id":"2503.02931","title":"Relativistic Navier-Stokes description of the quark-gluon plasma radial flow","summary":"Using relativistic Navier-Stokes in the well-posed Bemfica-Disconzi-Noronha-Kovtun (BDNK) formulation, with a QCD equation of state and transport coefficients, we perform simulations that describe the radial flow in central Pb-Pb collisions measured at the LHC, providing a proof of principle for its viability in heavy-ion phenomenology. We identify causal frames that retain the Landau-frame interpretation, and verify frame robustness. M\\\"uller-Israel-Stewart simulations with identical input show large second-order effects, exceeding the frame dependence of relativistic Navier-Stokes, as well as causality violations.","authors":"Yago Bea","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.02931"},{"id":"2504.00069","title":"Transverse Energy--Energy Correlators at Small $x$ for Photon--Hadron Production","summary":"We study the transverse energy--energy correlator (TEEC) observable in photon--hadron and photon--jet production in p+p and p+A collisions at small $x$. We derive the relevant expressions in the high-energy limit of the scattering where the dipole picture is applicable and show how the dependence on the fragmentation function of the hadron cancels due to the momentum-sum rule. The nonperturbative scattering with the target nucleus is expressed in terms of the dipole amplitude, which also describes nonlinear gluon saturation effects. The TEEC observable is computed in the RHIC and LHC kinematics, and we show that it can be sensitive to the dipole amplitude, making it a potentially good observable for studying saturation effects.","authors":"Zhong-Bo Kang, Robert Kao, Meijian Li, Jani Penttala","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00069"},{"id":"2504.06656","title":"Machine Learning in the Hunt for Charged Higgs Bosons at Gamma-Gamma Colliders in the Type III Two Higgs Doublet Model","summary":"We study charged Higgs boson pair production, $\\gamma\\gamma\\to H^{+}H^{-}$, in the Type-III Two-Higgs-Doublet Model (2HDM-III) for $100\\leq M_{H^{\\pm}}\\leq190~\\text{GeV}$ at a $\\gamma\\gamma$ collider with $\\sqrt{s}=3~\\text{TeV}$ and $\\mathcal{L}_{int}=3000~\\text{fb}^{-1}$. Two benchmark points, BP1 ($\\tan\\beta=10$, $\\cos(\\beta-\\alpha)=-0.2$) and BP2 ($\\tan\\beta=20$, $\\cos(\\beta-\\alpha)=-0.1$), are chosen in agreement with theoretical constraints, flavor-physics data and the signal strengths of the $125~\\text{GeV}$ Higgs boson. The total width of $H^{\\pm}$ is $10^{-4}$--$10^{-1}~\\text{GeV}$, i.e. a narrow resonance, and $\\mathrm{BR}(H^{+}\\to\\tau\\nu_{\\tau})$ falls from $4.6\\times10^{-3}$ (BP1) and $2.9\\times10^{-4}$ (BP2) at $100~\\text{GeV}$ to $1.8\\times10^{-4}$ and $1.2\\times10^{-5}$ at $190~\\text{GeV}$. The $\\tau\\nu_{\\tau}$ and $tb$ final states are analyzed with four TMVA classifiers (BDT, MLP, Likelihood and LikelihoodD) using $1.0\\times10^{5}$ generated events per process. For $171$ signal and $604$ background events, the MLP and BDT reach AUC values of $0.934$ and $0.933$ and maximum significances $S/\\sqrt{S+B}$ of $9.86$ and $9.82$, compared with $0.870$ and $8.54$ for LikelihoodD and $0.815$ and $7.55$ for Likelihood. The preselection cuts raise the significance by only $0.09$--$0.91\\%$, depending on the classifier. The quoted significances are statistical; systematic uncertainties are not included.","authors":"Abdul Quddus, Ijaz Ahmed, Jamil Muhammad, M. A. Arroyo-Ure","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.06656"},{"id":"2511.15779","title":"SURFing to the Fundamental Limit of Jet Tagging","summary":"Beyond the practical goal of improving search and measurement sensitivity through better jet tagging algorithms, there is a deeper question: what are their upper performance limits? Generative surrogate models with learned likelihood functions offer a new approach to this problem, provided the surrogate correctly captures the underlying data distribution. In this work, we introduce the SUrrogate ReFerence (SURF) method, a new approach to validating generative models. This framework enables exact Neyman-Pearson tests by training the target model on samples from another tractable surrogate, which is itself trained on real data. We argue that the EPiC-FM generative model is a valid surrogate reference for JetClass jets and apply SURF to show that modern jet taggers may already be operating close to the true statistical limit. By contrast, we find that autoregressive GPT models unphysically exaggerate top vs. QCD separation power encoded in the surrogate reference, implying that they are giving a misleading picture of the fundamental limit.","authors":"Ian Pang, Darius A. Faroughy, David Shih, Ranit Das, Gregor Kasieczka","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.LG","hep-ex","hep-ph","physics.data-an"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15779"},{"id":"2601.02887","title":"Study of $CP$ violation in $\\Lambda_b^0\\rightarrow N^*M$ decays with the final-state rescattering mechanism","summary":"In this work, we investigate the charmless nonleptonic two-body $\\Lambda_b$ decays within the framework of final-state rescattering mechanism. Using this framework, we evaluate the relevant hadronic triangle diagrams by performing the loop integrations, retaining both their absorptive and dispersive parts. Based on the established formalism, we analyze the $\\Lambda_b \\to N^*(1535,1520)M$ decay processes with $M =K_S, K^*_0(700)$, $f_0(500,980), \\rho(770), \\bar{K}^{*0}$, $\\phi$, and predict various physical observables, such as their branching ratios, direct and partial-wave $CP$ asymmetries, as well as decay asymmetry parameters. These two-body decay processes are expected to contribute primarily to the subsequent four-body decay channels, such as $\\Lambda_b^0 \\to p\\,\\pi^-\\,\\pi^+\\,\\pi^-$, whose $CP$ asymmetry measurements will be accessible at the LHCb experiment.","authors":"Hui-Qiang Shang, Tian-Liang Feng, Jing Gao, Qin Qin, Fu-Sheng Yu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02887"},{"id":"2602.06895","title":"MadSpace -- Event Generation for the Era of GPUs and ML","summary":"MadSpace is a new modular phase-space and event-generation library written in C++ with native GPU support via CUDA and HIP. It provides a unified compute-graph-based framework for phase-space construction, adaptive and neural importance sampling, and event unweighting. It includes a wide range of mappings, from the standard MadGraph multi-channel phase space to optimized normalizing flows with analytic inverse transformations. All components operate on batches of events and support end-to-end on-device workflows. A high-level Python interface enables seamless integration with machine-learning libraries such as PyTorch.","authors":"Theo Heimel, Olivier Mattelaer, Ramon Winterhalder","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06895"},{"id":"2602.15100","title":"Dispersive analysis of the $\\boldsymbol{\\phi \\to \\gamma \\pi^0 \\pi^0}$ process","summary":"We present an analysis of the radiative decay $\\phi \\to \\gamma \\pi^0 \\pi^0$ in a dispersive framework, where the two-pion subsystem undergoes strong final-state interactions that cover the $f_0(500)$ and $f_0(980)$ regions. We employ a coupled-channel Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es framework that allows for a consistent treatment of two scalar resonances and crossed-channel singularities induced by the Born and vector-meson exchanges. We explicitly verify the equivalence between the modified and standard Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es representations for vector-meson pole contributions when the isoscalar Omn\\`es matrix is chosen asymptotically bounded, and we adopt the standard representation in decay kinematics. This yields, for the first time, a parameter-free dispersive prediction for the kaon Born rescattering, which provides a dominant contribution. To obtain a good fit to the KLOE and SND data, we employ a once-subtracted coupled-channel dispersion relation with heavier left-hand cut contributions and two unknown subtraction constants. The results demonstrate the consistency among the data for $\\pi\\pi$ scattering, $\\gamma\\gamma$ fusion, and $\\phi $ radiative decay, thereby validating the underlying dispersive formalism and the input used for the hadronic Omn\\`es matrix and left-hand cuts.","authors":"Bai-Long Hoid, Igor Danilkin, Marc Vanderhaeghen","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15100"},{"id":"2603.02300","title":"One-point energy correlator for deep inelastic scattering at small $x$","summary":"We derive the expressions for the one-point energy correlator (OPEC) in deep inelastic scattering in the high-energy (small-$x$) limit within the Color Glass Condensate (CGC) framework. The OPEC is computed as a function of the angle between the energy flow and the target proton or nucleus, enabling a systematic exploration of different momentum scales in the scattering process. Owing to the momentum sum rule, the dependence on fragmentation functions cancels, leaving the dipole amplitude as the only nonperturbative input. As a result, the OPEC provides a sensitive and promising probe of gluon saturation dynamics at small $x$. We present numerical results for representative kinematic configurations relevant to the future Electron--Ion Collider, demonstrating sizable nuclear suppression effects within the CGC framework and highlighting the consistency of this observable with predictions from gluon saturation.","authors":"Zhong-Bo Kang, Robert Kao, Meijian Li, Jani Penttala","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.02300"},{"id":"2604.26537","title":"Particle seismology: mechanical and gravitational properties from parton-hadron duality","summary":"The internal structure of hadrons is characterized by form factors which correspond to matrix elements of currents. Among those, the stress-energy-momentum tensor is a universally conserved quantity providing the gravitational form factors, from which mechanical properties may be derived via the response to the space-time fluctuations. They have received much attention because of their role as moments of the Generalized Parton Distributions, where the stress-energy-momentum tensor couples to two photons, and more recently, due to the explicit lattice QCD determination for the pion and nucleon. In these lectures we attempt a pedagogical review of the topic from a purely hadronic point of view, based on the notion of dispersion relations, meson dominance, and parton-hadron duality. We show that despite the overwhelming simplicity of the approach, a rather successful description of the lattice QCD data is achieved.","authors":"Enrique Ruiz Arriola, Wojciech Broniowski","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-lat","hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26537"},{"id":"2605.09000","title":"Optimizing Yukawa couplings to suppress Dimension-five Proton Decay in $SU(5)$ GUT","summary":"The minimal supersymmetric $SU(5)$ grand unified theory (GUT) provides a highly compelling framework for physics beyond the Standard Model (SM). However, it suffers from a severe phenomenological challenge: rapid proton decay mediated by colored-Higgsino exchange via dimension-five operators. Resolving this issue often requires adjustments to the Yukawa couplings and the potential sectors, generating a vast and complex parameter space where traditional brute-force numerical scans are rendered computationally intractable due to the curse of dimensionality. In this paper, we overcome this limitation by applying machine learning optimization techniques. We investigate a supersymmetric $SU(5)$ model extended with $\\mathbf{45}$ and $\\overline{\\mathbf{45}}$ Higgs representations, defining a loss function based on the partial decay width of $p \\to K^+ \\bar{\\nu}$. Utilizing the Adam optimizer, we systematically explore the 33-dimensional parameter space to identify regions that suppress proton decay. Furthermore, we vary $\\tan \\beta$ to thoroughly investigate whether the optimized proton lifetime can consistently exceed the stringent experimental lower bound of $5.9 \\times 10^{33}$ years established by the Super-Kamiokande collaboration.","authors":"Naoyuki Haba, Junpei Ikemoto, Yasuhiro Shimizu, Toshifumi Yamada","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09000"},{"id":"2606.01966","title":"Reinterpreting the ATLAS HHH$\\to 6b$ Search with CheckMATE and Rivet: Validation, TRSM Benchmarks, and HL-LHC Prospects","summary":"We present an implementation in CheckMATE and Rivet of the ATLAS collaboration search for triple Higgs boson production in a six $b$-jets final state. The search relies on event selection using a deep neural network and a statistical model based on the HS3 format. Owing to a rich validation material provided by ATLAS, we perform a thorough validation of neural network input features and exclusion limits in the Standard Model and its extension with two additional singlet scalar fields (TRSM). Finally, we discuss expected performance of the search at the High Luminosity LHC in several scenarios of systematic uncertainty. We present projected exclusion limits for a set of TRSM benchmark models beyond those considered by ATLAS.","authors":"Tomasz Procter, Krzysztof Rolbiecki, Andrzej Si\\'odmok","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.01966"},{"id":"2606.06105","title":"Bulk viscosity of a binary mixture: the role of the intra-species interaction","summary":"The bulk viscosity $\\zeta$ is a transport coefficient which is of central importance for various areas of modern physics. In particular, its determination for a fluid with two components is challenging, since it involves a complex interplay of multiple microscopic processes that operate on different time scales. Within the Chapman-Enskog framework, based on a series expansion of the Boltzmann distribution function, many previous works have derived the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order result for the $\\zeta$ of a mixture. However, such a result fails to reproduce relevant physical features of the system, especially when the masses of the two components are similar. In this work we improve the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order Chapman-Enskog result by deriving the $\\zeta$ at the 2$^{\\text{nd}}$ order in the expansion. We show that this improved formula encodes many physical properties that the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order result misses: under specific conditions, the 2$^{\\text{nd}}$ order result can be qualitatively and quantitatively very different from the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order one. Moreover, this result is compared against the $\\zeta$ evaluated within the Green-Kubo formalism, by means of a numerical solution of the Relativistic Boltzmann equation. The agreement with respect to this benchmark is significantly improved when moving from the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ to the 2$^{\\text{nd}}$ order CE result.","authors":"Gabriele Parisi, Vincenzo Nugara, Shams Ul Arfeen, Salvatore Plumari, Vincenzo Greco","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06105"},{"id":"2606.07744","title":"Open quantum system approach to the transverse momentum broadening of a colour dipole","summary":"Using the open quantum systems formalism, we study the propagation of a quark-antiquark pair propagating through a dense QCD plasma of size $L$ and transverse momentum broadening transport coefficient $\\hat q$, and we derive the Lindblad evolution equation for the density matrix of the system. We focus on the boosted regime where the opening angle $\\theta_{q\\bar q}$ of this effective colour dipole satisfies $\\theta_{q\\bar q}\\ll 1$. In the correlation limit where the quark-antiquark relative transverse momentum $p_\\perp$ is much larger than the imbalance $q_\\perp$ as well as the medium typical transverse momentum scale $Q_s=\\sqrt{\\hat q L}$, we demonstrate that the resulting Wigner distribution displays quasi factorisation between a hard factor describing the hard splitting producing the $q\\bar q$ pair and the transverse momentum imbalance $q_\\perp$-distribution encoding the broadening induced by the medium. The factorisation is violated by a \"colour decoherence\" factor that controls the $\\theta_{q\\bar q}$ dependence of the $q_\\perp$-distribution through the ratio $\\theta_{q\\bar q}/\\theta_c$, with $\\theta_c \\sim (\\hat q L^3)^{-1/2}$. The open quantum systems approach enables us to clarify the role of this critical angle $\\theta_c$ and its associated critical time $t_c$ in the genuine quantum decoherence of the density matrix in colour and kinematic space: in particular, $t_c$ controls both the suppression of the off-diagonal elements of the density matrix in colour space and the transition between singlet and octet states. We find, however, that colour decoherence sets in earlier than the full decoherence of the density matrix, thereby marking the onset of classical behaviour in the system. Finally, we investigate the corrections beyond the quasi-factorised picture due to the quantum diffusion term in $p_\\perp$ of the Lindblad equation and we find that these corrections are mild.","authors":"Fran\\c{c}ois Arleo, Pietro Benzoni, Paul Caucal, Pol Bernard Gossiaux","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07744"},{"id":"2606.09768","title":"Dispersive analysis of the $\\boldsymbol{J/\\psi \\to \\gamma \\pi^0 \\pi^0}$ process","summary":"We present a dispersive amplitude analysis of the low-energy $\\pi^0\\pi^0$ system in the radiative decay $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma\\pi^0\\pi^0$, using the mass-independent BESIII $0^{++}$ and $2^{++}$ intensities, the total spectrum, and the measured $0^{++}-2^{++}$ $E1$ phase difference. The isoscalar $S$-wave is described by a coupled-channel $\\pi\\pi/K\\bar K$ Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es representation, which implements the strong final-state interactions associated with the $f_0(500)$ and $f_0(980)$. The $D$-wave is treated with a single-channel $\\pi\\pi$ Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es representation, where we identify all kinematic constraints of helicity amplitudes before transforming them to the experimental $E1$, $M2$, and $E3$ multipoles. Smooth short-distance production of a pseudoscalar-meson pair is encoded in subtraction polynomials, while left-hand-cut effects are estimated and found to be numerically subleading. We identify the negative solution of the BESIII $0^{++}-2^{++}$ $E1$ phase ambiguity, after using the modulo-$\\pi$ freedom of production amplitudes, as the phase solution compatible with unitarity constraints, showing that the measured phase information can be accommodated with the Omn\\`es phase motion without requiring large additional phases. By normalizing the BESIII intensities with the extracted branching fraction, we fix the absolute scale of the fitted amplitudes, making them suitable as input for future dispersive studies of two-pion contributions to gravitational form factors.","authors":"Bai-Long Hoid, Igor Danilkin, Anna Testa, Marc Vanderhaeghen","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.09768"},{"id":"2607.03343","title":"First look at the evaluation of two-loop Feynman integrals for radiative return processes","summary":"Precision studies of radiative return processes at low-energy electron--positron colliders require next-to-next-to-leading order QED predictions retaining full dependence on the electron mass. We present the calculation of planar two-loop four-point Feynman integrals relevant for initial-state radiation contributions to these processes. The calculation presents considerable analytical complexity, due to the presence of a nested square root and of integrals associated with elliptic geometries. We construct differential equations for the Feynman integrals which are polynomial in the dimensional regulator, and are suitable for numerical integration. We demonstrate stable numerical evaluations throughout the physical region relevant for low-energy experiments, despite the presence of large hierarchies of scales. Our results provide essential building blocks for NNLO predictions for radiative return processes.","authors":"Mattia Pozzoli, William J. Torres Bobadilla","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.03343"},{"id":"2607.04132","title":"Left-right symmetry breaking in $E_6^L\\times E_6^R$ occurs only in spacetime -- with possible implications for strong $CP$","summary":"Two-sided octonionic $E_6^L\\times E_6^R$ unification trinifies each factor and so carries two colour groups: the visible $SU(3)_c$ and a second $SU(3)_{c'}$ which, gauged like the first, would colour the charged lepton and must be suppressed by hand. We argue no such device is needed: the left-right symmetry acts in spacetime, not internally -- in the gravi-weak reading $SU(2)_R$ is the gravitational frame group and the exchange is ordinary parity on the Dirac field; a spacetime operation cannot double the internal colour. Colour is single and vector-like, $SU(3)_{c'}=SU(3)_c$, and the electron is a colour singlet. The right sector contributes one colour-blind datum, $\\sqrt m$ (values: the exceptional-Jordan trace split, an input; $N_R$ fixes only the colour representation). An anomaly no-go shows that no gauged $U(1)$ on the visible fermions can carry the $\\sqrt m$ pattern: the dark-electromagnetic $U(1)_{\\rm dem}$ carries the parity mirror of electric charge on the dark sector, the visible fermions are dem-neutral, and no visible fifth force is predicted. Hypercharge is recovered as the consistency relation $Y=Q-T^3_L$, $Q=N/3$. As an application, parity restricts the QCD angle to $\\{0,\\pi\\}$; $\\theta_{\\rm QCD}=0$ is selected, not derived, by matching to the sign content of the quark mass determinant, whose phase vanishes because the flavour rotors have real determinant. Hence $\\bar\\theta=0$ at tree level, conditional on the gravi-weak identification, with a nonzero CKM phase. Loop stability is open: the two-loop $\\bar\\theta$ of the minimal universal-seesaw parity model arises from scalar exchange, so the absence of a gauged $W_R$ here does not remove its mechanism, which the Higgs-bridge sector shares. We tag throughout what is derived, what is input, and what is open.","authors":"Tejinder P. Singh","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04132"},{"id":"2607.13743","title":"Polarized observables in $\\tau \\to VV \\nu_{\\tau}$ decays","summary":"We study polarization observables in $\\tau^- \\to V_1^- V_2^0 \\nu_\\tau$ decays for $(V_1^-, V_2^0)=(\\rho^-, \\rho^0), (\\rho^-, \\omega), (K^{*-}, \\rho^0), (K^{*-}, \\omega), (\\rho^-, \\bar{K}^{*0})$ using the helicity formalism. While the obtained branching fraction for $\\tau^-\\to \\rho^-\\rho^0\\nu_\\tau$ is consistent with that of angular momentum algebra method, the results for the other four channels are comparable with (smaller than) those from the effective chiral model (the aforementioned method). Our results for the longitudinal polarization fractions $f_L$ indicate that nonnegligible corrections to the endpoint limit ($f_L = 1/3$), a consequence of Lorentz symmetry, are present at the scale of tau mass. Future measurements of $f_L$ at Belle II for $\\tau^- \\to V_1^- V_2^0 \\nu_\\tau$ decays will provide crucial information on polarization patterns observed in nonleptonic charm decays.","authors":"Vindhyawasini Prasad, Hiroyuki Umeeda","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.13743"},{"id":"2608.05304","title":"GOOFy-compatible 3HDMs and beyond","summary":"Beyond conventional Higgs-family and general CP transformations, one may also consider a broader class of non-standard \"GOOFy\" transformations, in which the scalar fields and their conjugates are assigned related but inequivalent transformations. Although these transformations are not symmetries of a full Lagrangian in the conventional sense, some nevertheless stabilise the scalar potential. Their impact on the quadratic sector has not yet been systematically classified. We develop a sign-orbit technique to determine the bilinear structures compatible with these transformations. Applied to three-Higgs-doublet models, and formulated so as to extend to larger multi-Higgs sectors, the method shows that a non-vanishing GOOFy-compatible quadratic sector exists only when two independent conditions are met: the underlying group admits a non-trivial sign character, and that character is realised within the tensor product of the scalar representation and its conjugate. As a key consequence, we demonstrate that the renormalisation-group stability of the $r_0 \\to -r_0$ manifold in isolation is not a generic feature of multi-Higgs potentials, but rather a direct consequence of special algebraic properties of $SU(2)$. These results provide a classification of the admissible GOOFy quadratic structures and their invariant bilinear subspaces, highlighting the algebraic obstructions to their renormalisation-group stability.","authors":"I. de Medeiros Varzielas, A. Kun\\v{c}inas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05304"},{"id":"2608.16266","title":"Amplified Isospin Breaking from Coupled-Channel Dynamics Near Threshold","summary":"We identify the dynamical origin of amplified isospin breaking in near-threshold states. Using a pole-residue-based measure defined directly from the pole couplings, rather than from decay observables that are also affected by different final-state kinematics, we show that threshold proximity of the pole alone is insufficient: large isospin breaking requires a sizable interaction in the companion isospin channel. In particular, the isospin-violating pole-coupling ratio vanishes when the companion-channel interaction is switched off, even at finite threshold splitting. Applied to the $X(3872)$, the observed large isospin breaking points to a nearby partner pole, $W_{c1}$, as predicted in previous studies. As a concrete realization of the previously discussed leading/shadow-pole interchange, we find that the $W_{c1}$ pole, which predominantly affects the line shapes near the charged threshold in the physical case, does not evolve into the pure $I=1$ eigenstate in the isospin limit; instead, that eigenstate is continuously connected to a more distant shadow pole.","authors":"Xiang-Kun Dong, Feng-Kun Guo, Christoph Hanhart, Teng Ji, Ulf-G. Mei{\\ss}ner","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16266"},{"id":"2609.00912","title":"Radiative pion-pair production in disperon QED","summary":"Based on the previously developed disperon QED method for incorporating data input into loop processes in Monte Carlo codes, we calculate $ee\\to\\pi\\pi\\gamma$ at NLO and include the pion vector form factor within the form-factor scalar QED approximation wherever it is physically justified. In particular, this includes insertions of the two-pion two-photon Compton tensor into loop diagrams. We also consider a subset of contributions beyond, involving the pion Compton tensor with three photons. For these terms the form-factor scalar QED approach is less well justified, but we gain insight into the associated theory uncertainty for selected experimental scenarios. All calculations are implemented and made available within the Monte Carlo framework McMule.","authors":"Yizhou Fang, Sophie Kollatzsch, Adrian Signer, Max Zoller","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.00912"},{"id":"2609.20953","title":"Prospects for testing Lorentz symmetry with highly charged ions","summary":"We investigate the prospects for testing Lorentz symmetry using highly charged ions. We derive the Lorentz-violating energy shifts relevant to Zeeman and Zeeman-hyperfine transitions within the ground state of H-like ions and to Zeeman and Zeeman-fine transitions within the ground state of B-like ions. The resulting signals include sidereal variations through the first harmonic of the sidereal frequency for H-like ions and through the third harmonic for B-like ions. For certain SME coefficients whose contributions scale with the fourth power of the electron momentum, the large electron momentum in H-like ions can provide sensitivity competitive with the best existing bounds at the precision achievable in H-like-ion spectroscopy. We also find that B-like-ion spectroscopy can provide competitive sensitivity to certain SME coefficients.","authors":"Arnaldo J. Vargas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20953"},{"id":"2610.11937","title":"Reassessing the $X(2370)$ in $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$ beyond the one-dimensional mass projections","summary":"The BESIII Collaboration recently extracted the properties of the $X(2370)$ from a fit to the one-dimensional $K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$ invariant-mass spectrum in $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$. The intermediate two-body structures visible in the mass correlations, including the $K_0^*(1430)$ bands, require a coherent treatment of cross-channel interference when interpreting the enhancement near 2.3 GeV. We reassess the need for an additional $X(2370)$ contribution by fitting the published data using an effective coupled-channel framework with an approximate detection efficiency depending on the three-body invariant mass. The three-body parent amplitude is parameterized using a $K$-matrix that describes scattering among several quasi-two-body channels. The seven- and eight-bare-state models give similar mass distributions. Adding the eighth bare state lowers the Poisson deviance by 1.22% and produces an additional pole at $E=(2419-i152)$ MeV, substantially broader than the $X(2370)$ mass-width reference. This pole is model-dependent, and its identification with the $X(2370)$ is not clear. The present analysis does not establish the need for an additional $X(2370)$ contribution in $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$, thereby weakening the flavor-singlet argument based on the suppression of $K^*\\bar K$ relative to the total $K\\bar K\\pi$ contribution. A more comprehensive experimental amplitude analysis, incorporating coherent interference, mass and angular correlations, and detector effects across related decay channels, is needed to establish the role and decay properties of the $X(2370)$.","authors":"Xiang-Kun Dong, Teng Ji, Meike K\\\"u{\\ss}ner, Ulf-G. Mei\\ss ner","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11937"},{"id":"2610.21307","title":"Nonleptonic decays of the double-charmed baryon $\\Xi_{cc}^{+}$ with dynamically determined baryon wave functions","summary":"Motivated by the recent observation of the double-charmed baryon $\\Xi_{cc}^{+}$ by the LHCb Collaboration, we systematically investigate the two-body nonleptonic decays of the $\\Xi_{cc}^{+}$ into a single-charmed baryon and a light pseudoscalar meson. In our calculation, both factorizable and nonfactorizable decay amplitudes are evaluated within the framework of nonrelativistic quark model combined with the baryon wave functions dynamically determined by numerically solving the three-body Schr\\\"{o}dinger equation with a nonrelativistic potential model. In particular, the nonfactorizable $W$-exchange amplitudes are estimated under pole model by considering $J^{P}=1/2^{\\pm}$ low-lying intermediate charmed-baryon poles, and our results show that these contributions are comparable to or even dominate the factorizable amplitudes in several decay channels. Moreover, assuming a lifetime of $\\tau_{\\Xi_{cc}^{+}}=47~\\text{fs}$, we predict relatively large branching fractions for several CF decays, including $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Xi_{c}^{\\prime0}\\pi^{+})=8.90\\%$, $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Omega_{c}^{0}K^{+})=7.09\\%$, $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Sigma_{c}^{+}\\bar{K}^{0})=6.92\\%$, $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Xi_{c}^{+}\\pi^{0})=5.28\\%$, and $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Sigma_{c}^{++}K^{-})=5.17\\%$. These channels are promising candidates for future experimental searches at the LHCb.","authors":"Yu-Shuai Li, Hong-Tao An","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21307"},{"id":"2611.04110","title":"Holographic spin alignment of vector mesons in a spinning black-hole background","summary":"Holographic predictions for vector-meson spin alignment may depend on the background geometry and on the meson model. We examine the opposite polarization preferences observed for charmonium and light vector mesons by placing \\(J/\\psi\\) and \\(\\phi\\) in one soft-wall model on the planar limit of a five-dimensional equal-spin Myers--Perry AdS black hole. Spectral functions and spin alignment are computed within the same current-correlator framework; the nonrotating results of Sheng et al. [Phys. Rev. D 110, 056047 (2024)] are recovered at \\(a=0\\). At \\(T=0.15~\\mathrm{GeV}\\), \\(J/\\psi\\) retains a clear resonance peak, whereas \\(\\phi\\) shows no narrow peak and instead behaves as a broad distribution consistent with unstable \\(s\\bar{s}\\) pairs. For \\(J/\\psi\\) along \\(z\\), rotation enhances the positive deviation of \\(\\rho_{00}^{h}\\) from \\(1/3\\) at low and intermediate momenta but suppresses it at high momenta; along \\(x\\), the effect is small. For \\(\\phi\\), rotation enhances the negative deviation, more along \\(z\\) than along \\(x\\). The opposite helicity-frame signs and the opposing azimuthal and rapidity trends of the global alignment provide a reference for interpreting data: trends inconsistent with these would challenge the simplest single homogeneous rotating-medium interpretation. We distinguish model-dependent numerical details from qualitative features stable within this model class.","authors":"Fei Wang, Zi-qiang Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04110"},{"id":"2611.05635","title":"Proton generalized parton distributions from electromagnetic form factors: A theoretically constrained phenomenological reconstruction","summary":"We present a theoretically constrained phenomenological reconstruction of the unpolarized, chiral-even, leading-twist proton generalized parton distributions $H^q$ and $E^q$. At zero skewness, valence-quark distributions are combined with flavor-dependent exponential profiles calibrated simultaneously against phenomenological representations of the proton and neutron electromagnetic form factors. The construction imposes exact valence and anomalous-magnetic-moment normalizations, the low-scale condition $B^{u+d}(0)=0$, the required endpoint behavior, and the complete Gaussian impact-parameter positivity bound. The resulting parametrization provides a reasonable description of the electromagnetic form factors and their slopes while revealing the tension between strict GPD consistency and a precision reproduction of all low-$Q^2$ observables within the adopted ansatz. We extend the distributions to nonzero skewness using normalized double distributions and introduce an independent $D$-term to complete the relevant Mellin polynomials. We examine electromagnetic and energy--momentum-tensor form factors, impact-parameter distributions, positivity, polynomiality, and illustrative mechanical properties. The analysis distinguishes information constrained by electromagnetic data from additional assumptions governing skewness and the mechanical sector, thereby providing a transparent elastic baseline for future studies of nucleon-to-resonance transition GPDs.","authors":"A. Capelo-Astudillo, T. Aguilar, M. Conde-Correa, A. Duenas-Vidal, P. G. Ortega, J. Segovia","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05635"},{"id":"2611.07518","title":"Power corrections to heavy-quark spin correlations in small-$x$ deep-inelastic scattering","summary":"Heavy-quark spin correlations in small-$x$ deep-inelastic scattering receive corrections beyond the back-to-back limit. For a rotationally symmetric target at leading power, the target dependence cancels from the normalized spin density matrix after full azimuthal averaging, for fixed kinematics and spin axes related by transverse light-front boosts. We calculate the first nonvanishing correction at tree level for finite quark mass and finite $N_c$ in a homogeneous Gaussian target. The expansion through fourth order in the dipole sizes includes logarithms, contact terms and all interference contributions at that order. Comparison with the improved transverse-momentum-dependent approximation separates kinematic corrections from terms with additional insertions of the target current. Numerical integration of the unexpanded Gaussian kernel tests the approximation at 28 parameter points. Spin correlations differ between the two targets in all 12 kinematic comparisons; in one comparison both spin states remain separable. At fixed photon virtuality, the leading large-$P$ behavior of the fourth-order correction to the normalized transverse spin state is generically proportional to $m\\ln P/P^3$. We also compare two fixed Gaussian targets using unpolarized momentum distributions and joint spin measurements. For a purely transverse photon, three narrow momentum bins and an ideal measurement of both spins along the common quark-momentum axis defined by the light-front boost, spin information adds 3.5-3.7 percent to the expected log likelihood ratio after the overall normalization is fitted. This increase depends on the spin analyzing power and bin selection. The results quantify a model-dependent effect under idealized measurement conditions.","authors":"Meisen Gao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07518"},{"id":"2611.14593","title":"Strongly supercooled first-order phase transitions: monopole abundance, entropy production and homogeneity consistency","summary":"We investigate whether monopole production in the early universe from a first-order phase transition in a unified gauge theory can be significantly suppressed if the phase transition is subject to strong supercooling but still completes successfully. This is facilitated by adopting a Gaussian nucleation model and assuming the false vacuum energy released is transferred to sectors which behave effectively as radiation in terms of cosmological evolution. We distinguish three criteria of phase-transition completion: the comoving measure completion (CMC) characterized by the eventual vanishing of the false-vacuum fraction (FVF), the physical volume completion (PVC) characterized by the eventual vanishing of the physical volume of false vacuum, and the asymptotic thermodynamic completion (ATC), which refers to the finiteness of total entropy production. Assuming an idealized Friedmann-Lema\\^itre-Robertson-Walker (FLRW) cosmology, it is shown that the three criteria share exactly the same completion boundary, away from which they are completely equivalent. It is further shown that monopole abundance can be arbitrarily suppressed for parameter points approaching the completion boundary from the successful side. Nevertheless, for parameter points too close to the completion boundary, justification of the self-consistency of a homogeneous FLRW cosmology is difficult. We propose the use of the Hubble-scale FVF root-mean-square fluctuation to diagnose the homogeneity consistency issue, and find that arbitrarily strong suppression of monopole abundance relative to a reasonable Kibble estimate is not justifiable in terms of homogeneity consistency. These findings also have implications for gravitational wave production in strongly supercooled phase transitions.","authors":"Chen Zhang, Yue Niu, Xin Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14593"},{"id":"2611.16807","title":"Solar Neutrinos at the ESSnuSB experiment","summary":"We investigate the sensitivity of the ESSnuSB large underground water-Cherenkov (WC) detector to two key solar neutrino signatures: the MSW spectral upturn in the $^8$B electron neutrino survival probability $P_{ee}(E_\\nu)$, and the first direct observation of the rare hep neutrino flux. The analysis incorporates detector systematic uncertainties (energy resolution and flux normalization) and models environmental backgrounds from cosmic-ray muon spallation and $^{222}$Rn contamination. We adopt a shape-driven analysis strategy with a floating overall normalization, so that the discovery power relies on the spectral distortion rather than on the absolute event rate. In a realistic scenario with 90% spallation rejection efficiency, informed by the Super-Kamiokande experience and recent JUNO analyses, residual spallation backgrounds prevent either signal from reaching $5\\sigma$ significance within 20 years of exposure. For the hep flux, we present a detailed sensitivity study to both its discovery and its precision, and quantify how the required spallation veto efficiency and recoil energy threshold jointly determine the achievable significance for both signals. At the adopted thresholds, discovery-level sensitivity for both signals requires approximately 99% spallation rejection over a 20-year exposure.","authors":" ESSnuSB Collaboration, J. Aguilar, D. Bar\\v{c}ot, E. Baussan, A. K. Bhattacharyya, M. Blennow, M. Bogomilov, B. Bolling, E. Bouquerel, F. Bramati, A. Branca, G. Brunetti, I. Bustinduy, C. J. Carlile, J. Cederkall, T. W. Choi, S. Choubey, P. Christiansen, I. Christodoulou, E. Cristaldo Morales, P. Cupia{\\l}, D. D'Ago, H. Danared, J. P. A. M. de Andr\\'e, E. A. D\\'iaz, M. Dracos, I. Efthymiopoulos, T. Ekel\\\"of, M. Eshraqi, G. Fanourakis, A. Farricker, E. Fasoula, T. Fukuda, S. Gago, N. Gazis, Th. Geralis, M. Ghosh, A. Giarnetti, O. Gilanliogullari, G. Gokbulut, C. Hagner, L. Hali\\'c, S. G. Hern\\'andez, J. Hiegel, M. Hooft, K. E. Iversen, N. Jachowicz, M. Jakkapu, M. Jensen, I. Karakoulias, E. Kasimi, A. Kayis Topaksu, B. Kli\\v{c}ek, K. Kordas, B. Kova\\v{c}, A. Leisos, A. Longhin, M. L\\'opez, C. Maiano, S. Marangoni, J. G. Marcos, C. Marrelli, D. Meloni, M. Mezzetto, N. Milas, J. L. Mu\\~noz, K. Niewczas, M. Oglakci, T. Ohlsson, M. Olveg{\\aa}rd, A. Opanasenko, M. Pari, D. Patrzalek, G. Petkov, Ch. Petridou, P. Poussot, A. Psallidas, F. Pupilli, M. L. Reguera, D. Saiang, E. Salehi, D. Sampsonidis, A. Scanu, C. Schwab, F. Sordo, G. Stavropoulos, M. Stip\\v{c}evi\\'c, R. Tarkeshian, F. Terranova, T. Tolba, M. Topp-Mugglestone, E. Trachanas, R. Tsenov, A. Tsirigotis, S. E. Tzamarias, M. Vanderpoorten, G. Vankova-Kirilova, N. Vassilopoulos, S. Vihonen, J. Wurtz, V. Zeter, O. Zormpa","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16807"},{"id":"2408.15006","title":"Long- and short-range longitudinal flow decorrelations in the quark-gluon plasma","summary":"The longitudinal structure of the quark-gluon plasma (QGP) remains poorly constrained. Using a transport-model calculation of two-particle elliptic-flow correlations $V_{2\\Delta}(\\Delta\\eta)$ in relative pseudorapidity $\\Delta\\eta$, we find two distinct initial-state components of flow decorrelation: a long-range component driven by the global geometry of participating nucleons and a short-range component reflecting local energy-deposition fluctuations. This short-range component extends to $|\\Delta\\eta|\\sim 4$ and can be misidentified as final-state nonflow. For isobar-like nuclei with different shapes, the calculation predicts an experimentally testable fingerprint of this component: it dilutes the deformation contribution at small $|\\Delta\\eta|$, so that the $V_{2\\Delta}(\\Delta\\eta)$ ratio between deformed and near-spherical systems, such as U+U-to-Au+Au, rises with $|\\Delta\\eta|$. Separating long- and short-range flow decorrelations from nonflow gives access to the longitudinal initial structure and dynamics of the QGP.","authors":"Jiangyong Jia, Shengli Huang, Chunjian Zhang, Somadutta Bhatta","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["nucl-th","hep-ph","nucl-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.15006"},{"id":"2508.18022","title":"Precision Measurements of the Electroweak Mixing Angle in the Region of the Z pole at the Large Hadron Collider","summary":"In the Standard Model (SM), the effective leptonic weak mixing angle $\\sin^2\\theta^\\ell_{\\rm eff}$ can be predicted to very high accuracy by using the experimental values of the mass of the Z boson ($M_Z$), the Fermi constant, the fine structure constant, the mass of Higgs boson, the quark masses, and the value of the Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) strong coupling ($\\alpha_s$) at $M_Z$. Therefore, deviations of measurements from SM predictions can be indications of new physics. With recent advances in experimental and analysis techniques, measurements of $\\sin^2\\theta^\\ell_{\\rm eff}$ at the Large Hadron Collider (LHC), extracted from the forward-backward asymmetry ($A_{\\rm FB}$) in Drell-Yan dilepton events, are now competitive with previously published measurements at $e^+e^-$ collider experiments. We report on a more precise extraction of the effective leptonic weak mixing angle via an updated analysis of the CMS Drell-Yan $A_{\\rm FB}$ measurements at 13 TeV. We obtain $\\sin^2\\theta^\\ell_{\\rm eff}$=0.23154$\\pm$0.00024 with additional PDF constraints from measurements of the W decay lepton charge asymmetry at 13 TeV and the W/Z cross section ratios. It is the most precise single experiment measurement to date and is in excellent agreement with the SM 2026 indirect prediction of 0.23152$\\pm$0.00006.","authors":"Arie Bodek, Hyon-San Seo, Un-Ki Yang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18022"},{"id":"2607.00079","title":"QFT as a set of ODEs: higher dimensions","summary":"Correlation functions of local operators in Quantum Field Theory (QFT) in Anti-de Sitter space (AdS) are completely fixed by the QFT data: the set of scaling dimensions $\\Delta_i$ and OPE coefficients $C_{ijk}$ of the boundary operators, and the bulk-boundary (BOE) coefficients $b^{\\hat\\Phi}_i$ encoding how bulk fields decompose into boundary operators. In this work, we generalize the ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that govern the variation of the QFT data under a bulk relevant deformation, originally derived for AdS$_2$ \\cite{Loparco:2026fki}, to the cases of AdS$_3$ and AdS$_4$. We demonstrate that these flow equations natively capture the mechanism of merger-annihilation when a boundary operator hits marginality, as well as level repulsion when different $\\Delta_i$'s approach each other. Furthermore, we address the practical implementation of the framework: we propose substituting the ODE for the OPE coefficients with the crossing equation for greater efficiency, and we observe that Pad\\'e approximants dramatically improve the convergence of the sums over boundary operators, at least in free theories. Altogether, these advances lay the groundwork for the future application of the flow equations to the study of strongly coupled QFTs in AdS and their flat space limits.","authors":"Fabiana De Cesare, Manuel Loparco","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.00079"},{"id":"2607.10403","title":"The muon Moonshot: Moon subsurface tomography with upward-going muons","summary":"We propose a novel muon Moonshot concept for lunar subsurface tomography based on upward-going muons originating from the lunar regolith. Unlike Earth, the Moon lacks an atmosphere, leaving dense regolith below and a near-vacuum environment above. Consequently, most downward-going hadrons are absorbed before decaying, while upward-going hadrons escaping the regolith can decay in flight, producing a significant flux of lunar muons. These muons can be detected by instruments on the lunar surface or in near-lunar orbit. We perform Monte Carlo simulations to investigate their energy spectra, angular distributions, and integrated fluxes under various theoretical and detector configurations. The results indicate that the lunar muon flux is sensitive to detector altitude under idealized geometry. This dependence demonstrates its potential as a novel, non-invasive probe of shallow subsurface voids. We also present case studies on the detection of underground cavities and water resources. For shallow overburden, large cavity signals remain detectable with estimated backgrounds and systematics. Weaker water signals remain challenging. Potential implementations in future lunar missions are also discussed.","authors":"Zimo Hu, Leyun Gao, Zhengyun You, Qite Li, Qiang Li, Yuhong Yu, Liangwen Chen, Xueheng Zhang, Zhiyu Sun","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.EP","astro-ph.IM","hep-ex","hep-ph","physics.space-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.10403"},{"id":"2611.01117","title":"LH$\\mu$C Concept: Energy-Frontier Antimuon-Proton Collisions at CERN","summary":"The concept of the LH$\\mu$C, an energy-frontier antimuon-proton collider based on the HL-LHC, is presented. After recalling why energy-frontier lepton-hadron colliders are indispensable as QCD explorers and reviewing the history of muon-proton collider proposals, two ways of realising $\\mu p$ collisions at the LHC are compared: with muon-collider beams and with ultra-cold $\\mu^+$ beams as proposed for $\\mu$TRISTAN. For the latter, a $\\mu$TRISTAN-type booster and the re-use of the LHeC energy-recovery-linac tunnel are discussed; colliding 1 TeV antimuons with 7 TeV protons gives $\\sqrt{s}=5.3$ TeV and peak luminosities of $8.8\\times10^{33}$ and $7.2\\times10^{33}$ cm$^{-2}$s$^{-1}$, respectively. The detector concept and the physics potential, including searches for colour-octet muons and R-parity-violating squarks, are discussed together with the main challenges.","authors":"Arda Goroglu, Saleh Sultansoy","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-ex","physics.acc-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01117"},{"id":"2611.22092","title":"Search for the low-mass isotropic signature of QCD instantons in $pp$ collisions at $\\sqrt{s} = 13$ TeV using the ATLAS detector","summary":"This Letter presents the first search for QCD instanton-induced events at the LHC. Instantons are semi-classical solutions of the Yang-Mills equations of QCD and correspond to non-perturbative tunneling between topologically distinct vacuum sectors. The search is performed in 1.6 $\\text{nb}^{-1}$ of very low instantaneous luminosity proton-proton collision data collected by the ATLAS detector at 13 TeV. Angular variables based on reconstructed charged-particle tracks are used to discriminate the expected rest-frame isotropy of low-mass instanton events from the minimum-bias background. The minimum-bias event background is predicted using a data-driven technique and reweighted Monte Carlo models. A profile-likelihood fit is performed in a two-dimensional classifier variable to extract a possible signal contribution, yielding an excess of low-mass isotropic events with an observed significance of $3.5\\sigma$.","authors":" ATLAS Collaboration","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22092"},{"id":"2610.20774","title":"Signatures of Leptophilic Baryogenesis at Muon Colliders","summary":"The baryon asymmetry of the Universe may be produced in a model with 3 Higgs fields, a Higgs Troika, without the need for a strong first order electroweak phase transition. The couplings of the new Higgs fields to Standard Model fermions are assumed in accord with the \"Spontaneous Flavor Violation (SFV)\" framework, in order to avoid large deviations from flavor physics constraints. We consider a leptophilic version of SFV and outline its potential signatures at future muon colliders, envisioned to be multi-TeV partonic center of momentum machines. Hence, they provide optimal venues to search for the leptophilic Higgs Troika baryogenesis mechanism. The projected reach for signals of the new scalars allows for probing the key components of the baryogenesis model at scales that accommodate cosmological requirements.","authors":"Hooman Davoudiasl, Ian M. Lewis, Miguel A. Soto Alcaraz","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20774"},{"id":"2512.23992","title":"Prospect for measurement of CP-violating parameters of $B_s^0 \\to \\phi\\gamma$ at the Tera Z factory","summary":"$b \\to s\\gamma$ transition is a critical flavor-changing neutral current (FCNC) process that could be used to probe CP violation (CPV) and new physics (NP). We quantify the anticipated precision for measuring $B_s^0 \\to \\phi\\gamma$ at the CEPC Z pole operation, showing that the relative statistical uncertainty could be as low as 0.16\\%, improved by approximately two orders of magnitude compared to existing measurements. Additionally, we perform a time-dependent analysis of the $B_s^0 \\to \\phi\\gamma$ decay, accounting for $B_s^0/\\bar{B}_s^0$ mixing extract the mixing-induced and CP-violating parameters $\\boldsymbol{\\mathcal{A}_{\\phi\\gamma}^\\Delta}$, $\\boldsymbol{C_{\\phi\\gamma}}$ and $\\boldsymbol{S_{\\phi\\gamma}}$. Using central value from LHCb measurement as input, we evaluate the anticipated accuracy of measurements of these parameters. The projected statistical uncertainties are $\\sigma_{A_{\\phi\\gamma}^{\\Delta}{}^{\\text{stat}}} = 0.021$, $\\sigma_C^{\\text{stat}} = 0.0092$ and $\\sigma_S^{\\text{stat}} = 0.0096$, and the systematic uncertainties are $\\sigma_{A_{\\phi\\gamma}^{\\Delta}{}^{\\text{syst}}} = 0.035$, $\\sigma_C^{\\text{syst}} = 0.0027$ and $\\sigma_S^{\\text{syst}} = 0.0064$. Furthermore, the 1$\\sigma$ sensitivity boundaries for NP in this study are found to be $\\mathcal{A}_{\\phi\\gamma}^\\Delta < -0.05$ or $\\mathcal{A}_{\\phi\\gamma}^\\Delta > 0.15$, $\\mathcal{C}_{\\phi\\gamma} < -0.02$ or $\\mathcal{C}_{\\phi\\gamma} > 0.04$, and $\\mathcal{S}_{\\phi\\gamma} < -0.04$ or $\\mathcal{S}_{\\phi\\gamma} > 0.04$. We also conduct a relevant detector optimization study by establishing the correlation between the anticipated precision and the intrinsic resolution of the ECAL, as well as the performance of the PID system.","authors":"Hengyu Wang, Hanhua Cui, Yongfeng Zhu, Hao Liang, Yuexin Wang, Kaili Zhang, Yi Wang, Weizheng Song, Lingfeng Li, Shanzhen Chen, Manqi Ruan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23992"},{"id":"2602.10658","title":"Inclusive and multiplicity-dependent pseudorapidity densities of charged particles in pp collisions at $\\mathbf{\\sqrt{s} = 13.6}$ TeV","summary":"The distribution of the pseudorapidity density of primary charged particles (${\\rm d}N_{\\rm ch}/ {\\rm d}\\eta$) in $|\\eta|<1.5$ is measured in pp collisions at a center-of-mass energy of 13.6 TeV using the ALICE detector. Tracks are reconstructed with the upgraded Inner Tracking System and Time Projection Chamber, and results are reported for events having at least one charged particle in $|\\eta|<1$. The multiplicity dependence of the pseudorapidity density of charged particles is also explored with the multiplicity determined by the forward detectors. The average charged-particle pseudorapidity density at midrapidity ($|\\eta|<0.5$) is measured to be $\\langle \\mathrm{d}N_{\\mathrm{ch}}/\\mathrm{d}\\eta \\rangle = 7.10 \\pm 0.20$. Combined with lower-energy measurements, the results follow a power-law scaling with the center-of-mass energy. These measurements provide a new reference for charged-particle production at the highest proton$-$proton collision energy available at the LHC.","authors":" ALICE Collaboration","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["nucl-ex","hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10658"},{"id":"2611.21860","title":"Anomaly inflow for simplicial K\\\"{a}hler fermions","summary":"We study massive K\\\"{a}hler Dirac fermions propagating on finite triangulations of various three dimensional manifolds with one or more boundaries. To probe the anomaly structure of these models we decompose the K\\\"{a}hler field into two reduced K\\\"{a}hler fields and choose boundary conditions that ensure that any edge modes are necessarily massless. We then show how to factorize the Pfaffian of this system into a product of bulk and boundary sectors each of which can separately pick up a phase under a global $Z_4$ transformation of the fermions that simultaneously changes the sign of the mass. These phases are topological, depending only on the index of the lattice K\\\"{a}hler operator in that sector. For the boundary sector a non-trivial phase signals the presence of a 't Hooft anomaly. We study cases where the boundary is anomalous but an inflow from the bulk cancels its phase. Canceling this anomaly for a non-orientable boundary in the absence of the bulk recovers the known classification of topological superconductors. We also give a continuum interpretation of these results in terms of inflow from a gravitational Chern Simons theory which is induced by integrating out massive bulk K\\\"{a}hler Dirac fermions. In the presence of a boundary the latter gives rise to Wen-Zee terms that mediate the anomaly inflow mechanism.","authors":"Simon Catterall","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-lat","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21860"},{"id":"2609.24861","title":"Quantum vacuum inversion and multi-kink unbinding in the non-degenerate double sine-Gordon model","summary":"Quantum fluctuations can unbind a composite soliton while the correction to its mass stays small. We show this beyond perturbation theory for the multi-kinks of the non-degenerate double sine-Gordon model. At one loop the vacuum at the centre of the multi-kink becomes the true vacuum at a coupling of order the classical lifting of the vacua. Tensor-network calculations of the lattice theory show that this inversion survives beyond one loop and has a continuum limit, and that the Gaussian effective potential captures it. On finite chains the multi-kink unbinds when the tension of the string of central vacuum between its two halves falls through zero","authors":"Jonathan Lozano-Mayo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th","cond-mat.str-el","hep-lat","nlin.PS"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24861"},{"id":"2611.21743","title":"Center anomalies without twists: torsion, exact degeneracies, and condensates","summary":"Mixed center anomalies are usually exposed by 't Hooft's twisted boundary conditions in a Hamiltonian formalism. On $\\mathbb {RP}^3$, torsion makes the twist unnecessary: a center operator on the untwisted $SU(2)$ Hilbert space anticommutes with time reversal in Yang-Mills theory at $\\theta=\\pi$ and with the chiral symmetry of super Yang-Mills theory, so every level is at least doubly degenerate. The relevant semiclassical object is the BPST instanton, which descends to a charge-$\\tfrac12$ saddle on the orbifold $\\mathbb R^4/\\mathbb Z_2$. We complete it in two ways: applying a conformal transformation maps the orbifold to $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb {RP}^3$, with the saddle describing the tunneling event between the two center sectors; by resolving the orbifold singularity, we obtain the Eguchi-Hanson space, which gives the gaugino condensate at its weak-coupling value.","authors":"Mohamed M. Anber","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.str-el","hep-lat","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21743"},{"id":"2611.21726","title":"Not All Holographic Entropy Inequalities Are Free of Area Law Ultraviolet Divergences","summary":"In the AdS/CFT correspondence, linear constraints on subsystem entropies, which are necessary conditions for the validity of the Ryu-Takayanagi proposal, define the holographic entropy cone. We disprove a prior claim that all holographic constraints of this type are free of area law divergences (superbalanced). We analyze one explicit counterexample and explain how to easily find more; we report signatures of $\\mathcal{O}(10^5)$ counterexamples. Our findings invalidate multiple prior claims and conjectures.","authors":"Xin-Xiang Ju, Bartlomiej Czech","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21726"},{"id":"2611.21751","title":"From Top to Bottom: Quark Mass Hierarchies in Heterotic String Theory","summary":"Standard Model fermion masses exhibit large hierarchies, spanning more than five orders of magnitude in the quark sector alone. In this paper, we study whether these hierarchies can be generated perturbatively at tree level in smooth heterotic Calabi-Yau compactifications, where the supergravity approximation remains under computational control. In such backgrounds, physical Yukawa couplings combine holomorphic superpotential integrals with matter field Kahler metrics. The former can be computed algebraically, while the latter require solving a coupled system of partial differential equations for the Ricci-flat metric, Hermitian Yang-Mills connections, and harmonic bundle-valued forms. Within this framework, we identify two new tree-level mechanisms by which complex structure moduli can generate fermion mass hierarchies and show analytically and numerically that both mechanisms are obstructed for previously studied tetra-quadric models. Motivated by these obstructions, we scan the existing databases of tens of thousands of complete intersection Calabi-Yau line bundle models and find a unique smooth quotient admitting a rank-three up-quark Yukawa matrix at tree level. Evaluating the physical couplings on this manifold and evolving them under the renormalization group, we obtain realistic mass hierarchies for the top, charm, up, and bottom quarks in the smooth supergravity regime.","authors":"Yuri Chervonyi, Fabian Ruehle, Nenad Tomasev","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th","cs.AI"],"topics":["qgravity","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21751"},{"id":"2611.21754","title":"Giant gravitons without giant gravitons","summary":"We demonstrate that the partition function of a free bosonic $U(N)$ multi-matrix model admits a giant graviton expansion. For $d$ matrices, there are $d$ distinct giant gravitons, and the expansion can be characterized by a $d$-node quiver with bifundamentals connecting the nodes. This suggests the intriguing possibility of a more generalized holography where models without any obvious gravitational dual can nevertheless be given a dual brane interpretation. The giant graviton expansion of this multi-matrix model is somewhat unconventional though, as the giant graviton partition functions will necessarily have intrinsic $N$-dependence.","authors":"Blake J. Bottesi, James T. Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21754"},{"id":"2611.21787","title":"FLZ integrability in the 't Hooft model","summary":"We study the 't Hooft equation describing mesons in large-$N_c$ two-dimensional QCD with arbitrary unequal quark masses. Using its Fredholm formulation and the associated Baxter finite-difference equation, we investigate the relations between spectral determinants and canonical Baxter $Q$-functions. We derive special-point identities for the Baxter solutions by exploiting finite-rank commutator relations of the underlying integral operators. We also determine the spectral residues of the inhomogeneous solutions and obtain exact expressions for eigenfunction normalization factors in terms of values at distinguished complex points. These results establish the unit-residue property of the Baxter expressions entering the Fateev-Lukyanov-Zamolodchikov (FLZ) spectral-determinant identities. As a further application, we prove the nondegeneracy of the meson spectrum for arbitrary unequal quark masses. We further identify a broader class of integral operators exhibiting the same mechanism, which we refer to as FLZ integrability, and illustrate its applicability to the meson spectral problem in Ising field theory. Our approach reveals a connection between the analytic structure of Baxter equations and the theory of completely integrable Fredholm operators.","authors":"Alexey Litvinov, Egor Shestopalov, Nikita Slavnov","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21787"},{"id":"2611.22570","title":"Non-integrability of open strings via Pohlmeyer reduction","summary":"We study integrable and non-integrable boundary conditions for classical strings from the point of view of Pohlmeyer reduction. We first consider the integrable case of strings ending on Y=0 non-maximal giant gravitons. Pohlmeyer reduction of such strings leads to a sine-Gordon model, for which a criterion for boundary integrability was provided by Ghoshal and Zamolodchikov. We confirm that the Pohlmeyer field satisfies this condition. We then turn to the main focus of our work, which is open strings on AdS3. By evaluating the Ghoshal-Zamolodchikov criterion for the corresponding Pohlmeyer-reduced models, we are able to distinguish between integrable and non-integrable cases, in line with expectations from recent analytic and numerical studies. We conclude that Pohlmeyer reduction can provide a useful perspective on open-string non-integrability.","authors":"Divania Carelse, Laila Tribelhorn, Konstantinos Zoubos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22570"},{"id":"2610.35818","title":"Holographic Approaches to Hadron Structure","summary":"We give an introduction to and an overview of holographic approaches to QCD and hadron structure. After an introduction to the string-theoretic foundations of holographic QCD, we review the predictions of top-down and bottom-up models for the phenomenology of mesons, baryons, and glueballs. We focus on the top-down model following from probe flavor branes in the Witten model of low-energy Yang-Mills theory and, in the more phenomenological approach of bottom-up models, mostly on minimal models based on five-dimensional anti-de Sitter space, which also implement global symmetries and their anomalies consistently, but allow the high-energy behavior to better match real QCD.","authors":"Luigi Cappiello, Anton Rebhan, Shigeki Sugimoto","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35818"},{"id":"2611.21368","title":"Astrophysical Constraints for Bosonic Dark Matter in and Around Quark Stars","summary":"We investigate the structure, stability, and mass-radius relations of compact stars composed of quark matter within the non-ideal MIT Bag Model mixed with self-interacting bosonic dark matter with a $\\phi^n$ self-interaction potential. By solving the coupled two-fluid Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff equations for boson masses between 200 MeV and 1 GeV and self-interaction strengths with $n=4$ and $n=40$, we show that strong self-interactions and higher boson masses enhance the stability of compact star configurations at high central pressures, though they reduce maximum overall masses. Ultra-compact solutions with $C \\ge 1/3$ capable of mimicking black holes are significantly restricted compared to hadronic models, emerging only for strong self-interactions and dark matter fractions exceeding 90\\%. Depending on the parameters, the compact stars form either extended dark matter halos or compact cores: stronger self-interactions and higher boson masses suppress the radial extension to favor compact cores of dark matter. Matching our models against observational constraints highlights PSR J1614$-$2230 and XTE J1814$-$338 as potential window of opportunity for dark matter detection. Notably, XTE J1814$-$338 could confirm a dark sector, given its requirement for light boson masses ($m_b \\approx 200\\text{--}300\\text{ MeV}$) and strongly DM-dominated configurations.","authors":"J\\'ulia Tartera Barber\\`a, Sarah Louisa Pitz, J\\\"urgen Schaffner-Bielich, Laura Tolos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-th","nucl-th"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21368"},{"id":"2402.10129","title":"Holographic covering and the fortuity of black holes","summary":"We propose a classification of BPS states in holographic CFTs into monotone and fortuitous, based on their behaviors in the large $N$ limit. Intuitively, monotone BPS states form infinite sequences with increasing rank $N$, while fortuitous ones exist within finite ranges of consecutive ranks. A precise definition is formulated using supercharge cohomology. We conjecture that under the AdS/CFT correspondence, monotone BPS states are dual to smooth horizonless geometries, and fortuitous ones are responsible for typical black hole microstates and give dominant contributions to the entropy. We present supporting evidence for our conjectures in the ${\\cal N}=4$ SYM and symmetric product orbifolds.","authors":"Chi-Ming Chang, Ying-Hsuan Lin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10129"},{"id":"2512.11754","title":"Modular Witten Diagrams and Quantum Extremality","summary":"We study entanglement entropy for ball-shaped regions in excited states of holographic conformal field theories. The excited states are prepared by the Euclidean path integral in the CFT with a source turned on for some double-trace operator, with a small, $O(1)$ amplitude $\\lambda$. On the gravity side, the double-trace operator deforms the bulk geometry as well as the entanglement structure of the state of bulk matter fields. By the quantum extremal surface formula, this leads to a deformation of the shape of the entanglement wedge, an effect which becomes manifest in the entanglement entropy at $O(\\lambda^2 G_N)$. On the CFT side, we explicitly calculate the entanglement entropy perturbatively in the source amplitude to $O(\\lambda^2)$, in terms of modular-flowed correlation functions of double-trace operators. We then evaluate these modular-flowed correlation functions using Witten diagrams. This calculation involves a Schwinger-Keldysh contour ordering prescription in the bulk, which we motivate using analytic continuation from Euclidean replica correlators. Focusing on a particular graviton-exchange diagram, we rewrite it in a form where it manifestly reproduces the canonical energy term present in the quantum Ryu-Takayanagi formula, including the shape deformation of the entanglement wedge due to backreaction and quantum effects.","authors":"Abhirup Bhattacharya, Onkar Parrikar","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.11754"},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/sgtd-c4zg","id":"doi:10.1103/sgtd-c4zg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/sgtd-c4zg","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Four-Loop Nonsinglet Splitting Functions in QCD","summary":"The scale evolution of parton distributions is governed by splitting functions. We compute the four-loop splitting functions in perturbative QCD that control the evolution of quark nonsinglet distributions. We confirm previous partial results and obtain, for the first time, fully analytic expressions for all nonsinglet contributions at this order. These allow us to extract the analytic form of the four-loop virtual and rapidity anomalous dimensions entering logarithmic resummation. We provide precise numerical representations of the splitting functions suitable for parton evolution.","authors":"Thomas Gehrmann, Andreas von Manteuffel, Vasily Sotnikov, Tong-Zhi Yang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/zk63-p5q3","id":"doi:10.1103/zk63-p5q3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/zk63-p5q3","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Lattice QCD determination of the γ Z box contribution to the proton weak charge","summary":"","authors":"Zhao-long Zhang, Xu Feng, Mikhail Gorchtein, Lu-Chang Jin, Chuan Liu, Chien-Yeah Seng","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/4ccm-yks2","id":"doi:10.1103/4ccm-yks2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4ccm-yks2","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Universal quantum gate set for multimode Schrödinger cat states stabilized by nonlocal dissipation engineering","summary":"","authors":"Jesper Lind-Olsen, Jonas Lidal, Tron Omland, Joakim Bergli","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/5qfs-y95h","id":"doi:10.1103/5qfs-y95h","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5qfs-y95h","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Interplay between the chiral and deconfinement transitions from a Curci-Ferrari-based Polyakov loop potential","summary":"We couple the two-flavor Nambu–Jona-Lasinio model to a gluonic background corresponding to the gauge-field expectation value in the center-symmetric Landau gauge. Low-energy features in this gauge are captured by a center-symmetric extension of the Curci-Ferrari model and provide a good grasp on key aspects of the confinement/deconfinement transition. Within this framework, we can investigate the interplay between the chiral and deconfinement transitions. Compared to other approaches based on multiparameter of the Polyakov loop potential fixed from comparison to finite-temperature lattice data, the modeling of the glue sector in the present setup depends on only one phenomenological parameter that can be fixed by comparison to lattice data in the vacuum. We detail the structure of the phase diagram, with special emphasis on the finite density axis, and compute thermodynamical observables relevant for applications. We also highlight the properties of the recently introduced net quark number response of the medium as a sensible probe of the phases of QCD, in particular as a tool to disambiguate the nature of certain regions of the phase diagram where the use of the Polyakov loops could lead to misinterpretations. Finally, we critically assess the sensitivity of our results to the various parameters, both in the glue sector and in the chiral sector.","authors":"V. Tomas Mari Surkau, Urko Reinosa","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/gc5v-8ws1","id":"doi:10.1103/gc5v-8ws1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gc5v-8ws1","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Timing-based search for magnetic monopoles with the NOvA detector on the surface","summary":"We report a search for a magnetic monopole component of the cosmic-ray flux in a 2743-live-day exposure of the NOvA experiment’s Far Detector, a 14 kt segmented liquid scintillator detector designed primarily to observe GeV-scale electron neutrinos. No events consistent with monopoles were observed, setting an upper limit on the flux of 8 × 10 − 16 cm − 2 s − 1 sr − 1 at 90% CL for monopole speed 6 × 10 − 4 < β < 5 × 10 − 3 and mass greater than 10 9 GeV . Because of NOvA’s small overburden of 3 meters-water equivalent, this constraint covers a previously unexplored low-mass region.","authors":"S. Abubakar, M. A. Acero, B. Acharya, P. Adamson, N. Anfimov, A. Antoshkin, E. Arrieta-Diaz, L. Asquith, A. Aurisano, N. Balashov, P. Baldi, B. A. Bambah, E. F. Bannister, A. Barros, J. Barrow, A. Bat, T. J. C. Bezerra, V. Bhatnagar, B. Bhuyan, J. Bian et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/twqv-gm4t","id":"doi:10.1103/twqv-gm4t","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/twqv-gm4t","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Astrophysical constraints on hadron-quark crossover in hybrid neutron stars","summary":"","authors":"Gábor László Kasza, György Wolf","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/t7fc-nw9m","id":"doi:10.1103/t7fc-nw9m","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/t7fc-nw9m","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Investigating the Ω c spectroscopy in two-body Ω b decays","summary":"We investigate the 1 S -, 1 P -, 2 S -, and 1 D -wave Ω c ( c s s ) spectroscopy through the nonleptonic decays of Ω b − baryon within the constituent quark model. For the lowest-lying 1 S -wave Ω c state, we obtain the branching fractions B ( Ω b − → Ω c 0 π − , Ω c 0 ρ − ) = ( 0.9 8 − 0.11 − 0.56 + 0.24 + 0.73 , 5.3 3 − 0.60 − 2.93 + 1.31 + 3.74 ) × 10 − 3 , which are consistent with existing model predictions. For Ω c ( 3000 ) , Ω c ( 3050 ) , Ω c ( 3065 ) , and Ω c ( 3090 ) observed in the proton-proton and e + e − collisions and interpreted as members of the 1 P -wave multiplet (collectively denoted as Ω c * * ), we predict the branching fractions of Ω b − → Ω c * * π − , Ω c * * ρ − at the level of 10 − 3 . Assigning the newly observed Ω c ( 3327 ) baryon to a 1 D -wave excitation with J P = 5 / 2 + or 7 / 2 + , we obtain B [ Ω b − → Ω c ( 3327 ) 0 π − , Ω c ( 3327 ) 0 ρ − ] = ( 1.1 9 − 0.13 − 0.35 + 0.29 + 0.39 , 5.1 3 − 0.58 − 1.48 + 1.26 + 1.70 ) × 10 − 3 or ( 3.7 7 − 0.43 − 1.09 + 0.93 + 1.25 , 0.6 5 − 0.07 − 0.18 + 0.16 + 0.22 ) × 10 − 3 , respectively. The pronounced differences between these two scenarios provide a clear discriminant that can be tested in future measurements at the LHCb.","authors":"Juan Wang, Kai-Lei Wang, Yu-Kuo Hsiao","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/mz47-v4fr","id":"doi:10.1103/mz47-v4fr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mz47-v4fr","published":"2026-11-18","title":"Full-stack analog Gaussian optical quantum computing platform with one hundred inputs","summary":"","authors":"Shota Yokoyama, Atsushi Sakaguchi, Warit Asavanant, Kan Takase, Yi-Ru Chen, Hironari Nagayoshi, Jun-ichi Yoshikawa, Takahiro Kashiwazaki, Asuka Inoue, Takeshi Umeki, Toshikazu Hashimoto, Takuji Hiraoka, Akira Furusawa, Hidehiro Yonezawa","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]}]},"2026-11-17":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/rx4t-twrx","id":"doi:10.1103/rx4t-twrx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/rx4t-twrx","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Branes Screening Quarks and Defect Operators","summary":"Here we show Wilson lines do not necessarily exhibit Coulomb scaling laws in AdS/BCFT at zero temperature. The area difference between a surface that returns to the boundary, and one that plunges into the bulk, determines the potential between two quarks. This classic AdS/CFT calculation is extended to Wilson surfaces associated to general ( p − 1 )-form symmetries in boundary conformal field theories (BCFTs) by embedding a Karch-Randall (KR) brane. Work done on the boundary charge under a dilatation determines the coefficient of this potential in closed form. Consequently, Coulomb law scaling in subregion size Γ is recovered only above the critical angle for the brane, θ c , p , where the configuration is dilatation invariant. The potential between the two quarks (or defect operators) vanishes precisely when the surface connecting them ceases to exist at θ c , p . Thus operators are fully screened in this phase transition from Coulomb law to perimeter law, for which we compute the order parameter C p of the transition. At finite temperature a horizon breaks dilatation invariance, and so p analogs of islands continue to exist below θ c , p .","authors":"Andreas Karch, Marcos Riojas","topics":["qgravity","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/8dsh-5n9z","id":"doi:10.1103/8dsh-5n9z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8dsh-5n9z","published":"2026-11-17","title":"From integrability to chaos in tensionless AdS 3 string theory","summary":"","authors":"Matthias R. Gaberdiel, Beat Nairz, Cheng Peng","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/q42c-xphx","id":"doi:10.1103/q42c-xphx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/q42c-xphx","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Quantum algorithm for modular flow","summary":"","authors":"Ian T. Lim, Isaac H. Kim","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/4kmf-nzsm","id":"doi:10.1103/4kmf-nzsm","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4kmf-nzsm","published":"2026-11-17","title":"No-go theorem for quantum heat engines powered purely by quantum measurements in the steady regime","summary":"","authors":"Kenta Koshihara, Kazuya Yuasa","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/mphl-ky3z","id":"doi:10.1103/mphl-ky3z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mphl-ky3z","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Adaptive noise-mitigated distributed variational quantum sensing with federated learning","summary":"","authors":"Ratun Rahman, Atit Pokharel, Ashlyn D. Burch, Dinh C. Nguyen","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/f76w-qyvw","id":"doi:10.1103/f76w-qyvw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/f76w-qyvw","published":"2026-11-17","title":"From state-space transport to measurement-aware distinguishability in quantum sensing","summary":"","authors":"Arnaud Coatanhay, Angélique Drémeau","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/qcd2-mkkr","id":"doi:10.1103/qcd2-mkkr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/qcd2-mkkr","published":"2026-11-17","title":"From barren plateaus to SPSA optimization in variational quantum eigensolvers","summary":"","authors":"Zhen Qin","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/z1h6-1shn","id":"doi:10.1103/z1h6-1shn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/z1h6-1shn","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Parametric-amplification-enhanced Gaussian quantum metrology in non-Markovian environments","summary":"","authors":"Jia-Xin Peng, Wei Li, Zhu-Cheng Zhang, Chengsong Zhao, Zhong-Wei Yu, Chunfeng Wu, Zhe Sun","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/s76k-wzbt","id":"doi:10.1103/s76k-wzbt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/s76k-wzbt","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Giant number-parity effect and scalable spin squeezing in Luttinger liquids","summary":"","authors":"Filippo Caleca, Saverio Bocini, Fabio Mezzacapo, Tommaso Roscilde","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1t4c-884t","id":"doi:10.1103/1t4c-884t","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1t4c-884t","published":"2026-11-17","title":"GOOFy-compatible 3HDMs and beyond","summary":"Beyond conventional Higgs-family and general CP transformations, one may also consider a broader class of nonstandard “GOOFy” transformations, in which the scalar fields and their conjugates are assigned related but inequivalent transformations. Although these transformations are not symmetries of a full Lagrangian in the conventional sense, some nevertheless stabilize the scalar potential. Their impact on the quadratic sector has not yet been systematically classified. We develop a sign-orbit technique to determine the bilinear structures compatible with these transformations. Applied to three-Higgs-doublet models, and formulated so as to extend to larger multi-Higgs sectors, the method shows that a nonvanishing GOOFy-compatible quadratic sector exists only when two independent conditions are met: the underlying group admits a nontrivial sign character, and that character is realized within the tensor product of the scalar representation and its conjugate. As a key consequence, we demonstrate that the renormalization-group stability of the r 0 → − r 0 manifold in isolation is not a generic feature of multi-Higgs potentials, but rather a direct consequence of special algebraic properties of S U ( 2 ) . These results provide a classification of the admissible GOOFy quadratic structures and their invariant bilinear subspaces, highlighting the algebraic obstructions to their renormalization-group stability.","authors":"I. de Medeiros Varzielas, A. Kunčinas","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/wxp1-wzkp","id":"doi:10.1103/wxp1-wzkp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wxp1-wzkp","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Supersymmetry of cuts in pure gauge theory and gravity","summary":"At tree-level, scattering amplitudes involving only gluons or gravitons are unaffected by supersymmetry, allowing them to be efficiently encoded by and extracted from those of maximally supersymmetric ( N = 4 , 8) theories. This fails beyond tree-level, of course, but much less than would be expected. We show that all the leading singularities of (submaximally or) nonsupersymmetric theories can be organized into superfunctions, in terms of which all helicity components can be effectively encoded. These functions differ from those of maximally supersymmetric theories by an extent determined by loop order—broken into a sum over 2 L supersymmetric pieces.","authors":"Jacob L. Bourjaily","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/n153-jnfc","id":"doi:10.1103/n153-jnfc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/n153-jnfc","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Portal matter and scotogeniclike Dirac neutrino masses","summary":"Loops of portal matter (PM) fields, carrying both Standard Model and dark charges, can generate the necessary kinetic mixing between the ordinary and dark photons in vector portal scenarios thus allowing for interactions between visible and dark sector fields. Here we show that the field content of a previously considered model based on a partial E 6 -like UV completion of such setups can generate light Dirac neutrino masses in the interesting range, ∼ 0.05 eV , at the one-loop level similar to what happens in scotogenic dark matter scenarios. While general E 6 -like PM scenarios have been shown to be easily probed at colliders, such as the High Luminosity Large Hadron Collider, uniquely testing this specific subclass of these setups in a direct fashion is found to be somewhat more challenging.","authors":"Thomas G. Rizzo","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/s1x9-ct53","id":"doi:10.1103/s1x9-ct53","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/s1x9-ct53","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Asymptotically safe gravitational form factors from the proper-time flow equation","summary":"We study the renormalization group flow of nonlocal form factors in four-dimensional quantum gravity within the proper-time formalism at quadratic order in the curvature expansion. We show that, in the presence of a running Newtonian coupling and a fixed negative cosmological constant, the flow equations can be integrated down to k = 0 , allowing the reconstruction of the full momentum dependence of the form factors. The running of Newton’s coupling generates nontrivial momentum-dependent corrections to the effective field theory behavior, with the Planck scale setting the crossover between the IR and UV regimes. In the infrared, these corrections are organized as an expansion in powers of q 2 / m p 2 , while in the ultraviolet they are organized in powers of m p 2 / q 2 . Within this framework, we also analyze the properties of the dimensionless flow, identifying its fixed-point structure and constructing asymptotically safe solutions. We show that the integrated asymptotically safe solutions admit a finite and cutoff-independent Λ → ∞ limit when the non-Gaussian fixed-point solution is imposed as the ultraviolet boundary condition. This removes UV logarithmic contributions and ensures independence from the renormalization scale μ . The resulting renormalized asymptotically safe form factors at k = 0 display a power-law decay ∼ 1 / q 2 in the ultraviolet and reproduce the expected logarithmic structure in the infrared, with the Planck scale replacing the renormalization scale.","authors":"Emiliano Maria Glaviano","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/jd25-5p7w","id":"doi:10.1103/jd25-5p7w","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jd25-5p7w","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Modular S 4 scotogenic model with flavored resonant leptogenesis","summary":"We construct a radiative neutrino mass model that combines the scotogenic mechanism with modular S 4 flavor symmetry. The entire lepton flavor structure is governed by holomorphic modular forms of a single complex modulus τ , eliminating the need for flavon fields. Beyond the Standard Model, the particle content consists of two right-handed Majorana fermions assigned to the S 4 doublet representation and an inert scalar doublet odd under a Z 2 parity. Neutrino masses emerge at one loop through the scotogenic mechanism, and the lightest Z 2 -odd state serves as a dark matter candidate. A comprehensive scan of the parameter space demonstrates consistency with all five neutrino oscillation observables at the 3 σ level. Having exactly two right-handed neutrinos forces the light neutrino mass matrix to rank two, leaving one neutrino massless and selecting normal ordering as the only viable option. The framework predicts a total neutrino mass in the narrow window ∑ m ν ≃ 0.059 – 0.06 eV , well within current cosmological bounds, and an effective Majorana mass m β β ≃ ( 1.3 – 3.5 ) × 10 − 3 eV relevant for neutrinoless double beta decay searches. The structure of the right-handed Majorana mass matrix, together with the phenomenologically relevant regime M ε ≪ M , yields a quasidegenerate heavy-neutrino spectrum, enabling flavored resonant leptogenesis at M 1 ∼ 10 5 GeV . Integration of the full three-flavor Boltzmann equations confirms that the observed baryon asymmetry is reproduced, establishing that neutrino masses, leptonic mixing, and the baryon asymmetry of the Universe all find a common explanation within this framework.","authors":"Abhishek, V. Suryanarayana Mummidi","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/58n4-7yrw","id":"doi:10.1103/58n4-7yrw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/58n4-7yrw","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Photoproduction of J / ψ in peripheral oxygen-oxygen collisions","summary":"We investigate J / Ψ photoproduction in peripheral oxygen-oxygen ( O O ) collisions at the Large Hadron Collider (LHC), considering different models for the nuclear photon flux, the overlap function, and the dipole-proton scattering amplitude. We present predictions for the corresponding rapidity distributions and total cross sections. Our results indicate that an experimental study of J / Ψ photoproduction in peripheral O O collisions is, in principle, feasible. In addition, they suggest that combining results for this final state in O O collisions with those obtained in P b P b collisions can provide important constraints on the description of photon-induced processes in peripheral collisions.","authors":"Pedro E. A. da Costa, André V. Giannini, Victor P. Gonçalves, Bruno D. Moreira","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4kss-bv46","id":"doi:10.1103/4kss-bv46","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4kss-bv46","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Radiative corrections to elastic lepton-proton scattering with focus on two-photon-exchange diagrams","summary":"Lepton (electron and muon) scattering experiments are excellent tools to gain insight into the nucleon structure. Elastic electron-proton scattering probes the spatial distribution of charge and magnetization inside the proton, and comparing electron-proton and muon-proton scattering data tests lepton universality. The availability of a plethora of scattering data with increased precision and observed discrepancies such as the proton form factor puzzle and the proton radius puzzle motivated a renewed effort to improve the theoretical framework. Realizing that the one-photon-exchange approximation, i. e. the Born approximation, is not sufficient, radiative corrections in QED, especially the two-photon-exchange (TPE) diagrams, are under investigation. The TPE diagrams are of special interest among the radiative corrections, since their hadronic-structure-dependent contribution generates an additional dependence of the cross section on the virtual-photon polarization parameter beyond the Born approximation that is not captured by standard model-independent radiative correction procedures, and that directly impacts the extraction of proton form factors. In this work, we present a complete calculation of QED radiative corrections to elastic electron-proton and muon-proton scattering at next-to-leading order, taking into account loop-momentum-dependent form factors. In the discussion of their numerical impact on lepton-proton scattering cross sections, we pay special attention to the elastic nucleon contribution to the TPE diagrams and compare them with existing theoretical predictions and lepton-proton scattering data.","authors":"Daniel Crowe, Syed Mehedi Hasan, Doreen Wackeroth","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2spx-9pk6","id":"doi:10.1103/2spx-9pk6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2spx-9pk6","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Alignment and enhanced multi-Higgs production","summary":"Contrary to conventional expectations, we identify a class of extended scalar-sector scenarios in which final states with two, three, or four Higgs bosons constitute the leading discovery channels for new physics at the LHC. In these scenarios, higher-dimensional interactions, together with suppressed Higgs-scalar mixing near the alignment limit, reorganize the decay patterns of new scalar states, suppressing conventional modes while enhancing multi-Higgs final states. We illustrate the emergence of dominant triple- and quadruple-Higgs signatures in two representative realizations: a single-scalar extension of the standard model, where higher-dimensional operators suppress conventional two-body decays while preserving couplings to higher-multiplicity Higgs final states, and a two-singlet scenario, where similar signatures arise through cascade decays with a simpler operator structure. In both cases, the new scalar states can be produced via gluon fusion, yielding potentially observable rates for multi-Higgs production at the LHC. Although both realizations lead to identical final states, they exhibit distinct kinematic features reflecting their underlying topologies, providing a direct handle on the dynamics.","authors":"Subhojit Roy, Carlos E. M. Wagner","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/np68-6hgn","id":"doi:10.1103/np68-6hgn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/np68-6hgn","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Systematic study of baryon-baryon interactions in singly bottomed systems","summary":"In this work, we systematically investigate the baryon-baryon interactions in singly bottomed dibaryon systems within the chiral quark model and search for possible bound states. By exploring the baryon-baryon interaction, we find that low-isospin channels tend to generate deeper attractive interactions and are prone to form bound states. We also find that decuplet-decuplet systems tend to show stronger attraction and support a larger number of bound states, whereas octet-octet systems generally exhibit weaker attraction and fewer bound solutions. The binding behavior of octet-decuplet systems generally lies between these two cases. Several bound states are obtained, which are Δ Σ b * with I J = 1 2 0 , 1 2 1 , 1 2 2 , 1 2 3 , and 3 2 3 ; the Δ Σ b with I J = 1 2 1 , 1 2 2 , 3 2 1 , and 3 2 2 ; the N Σ b * with I J = 1 2 2 ; the Σ Σ b with I J = 00 , 01, and 11; the Σ Σ b * with I J = 01 and 02; the Σ * Σ b with I J = 01 and 02; and the Σ * Σ b * with I J = 00 , 01, 02, 03, and 13. Further investigations of the corresponding scattering processes are still required to determine whether these bound-state candidates can be identified. These states deserve further experimental investigation.","authors":"Yuxuan Du, Yanyue Pan, Xinmei Zhu, Zhiyun Tan, Hongxia Huang, Jialun Ping","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/87fc-8nhn","id":"doi:10.1103/87fc-8nhn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/87fc-8nhn","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Spin-dependent fermion potentials from mixed tensor couplings of massive spin-1 and spin-2 bosons","summary":"Searches for weak spin-dependent forces are commonly interpreted in terms of a basis of sixteen rotationally invariant two-fermion potentials V 1 − 16 , including potentials which violate parity (P) and time-reversal (T) invariance. We derive finite-range coordinate-space potentials generated by a massive spin-1 boson with vector, axial-vector, tensor, and pseudotensor couplings to Dirac fermions. In addition to the familiar vector-vector, vector-axial-vector, and axial-vector-axial-vector interactions, we obtain the mixed vector-tensor, vector-pseudotensor, axial-vector-tensor, and axial-vector-pseudotensor potentials, together with tensor-tensor, tensor-pseudotensor, and pseudotensor-pseudotensor terms. We retain the contact terms needed in atomic-scale applications and give compact expressions that include both orderings of inequivalent fermion vertices. We then extend the analysis to a massive spin-2 mediator described by a symmetric Fierz-Pauli field. Besides the conventional coupling to the conserved fermion energy-momentum tensor, we consider a symmetric axial-tensor current. Its nonconservation for massive fermions makes the longitudinal spin-2 helicities contribute to axial-tensor–axial-tensor exchange. The leading minimal spin-2 interactions are either P , T even or P odd and T even; in particular, they do not generate a T -odd potential. Heavy-mediator contact limits and the distinction between the M → 0 massive-spin-2 limit and a genuine massless graviton are also discussed. We map the resulting interactions onto the sixteen-potential Dobrescu-Mocioiu basis and use this mapping to translate existing experimental constraints into numerical limits on spin-1 and spin-2 coupling products in the small- and large-mediator-mass regimes, together with representative limits at finite mediator masses. The derived relations between the boson-exchange coupling constants and the coefficients of the basis potentials also allow published exclusion curves to be rescaled to arbitrary mediator masses.","authors":"D. Khadka, V. V. Flambaum","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/k5jy-619y","id":"doi:10.1103/k5jy-619y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/k5jy-619y","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Role of the Σ ( 1430 ) ( 1 / 2 − ) in the J / ψ → Λ Λ ¯ π 0 reaction","summary":"We study the J / ψ → Λ ¯ Λ π 0 reaction, an isospin-violating reaction, by looking at the trios of a baryon-antibaryon-pseudoscalar meson that conform to a singlet of SU(3) in the u , d , s quarks. These terms conserve isospin; however, once the final-state interactions of meson-baryon and meson-antibaryon are taken into account, the isospin is violated due to the different masses of particles within the same isospin multiplets. Since the reaction is tied to the interaction of particles, only resonances that are dynamically generated by these interactions show up in the reaction. In this sense, our approach produces the Σ ( 1430 ) ( 1 / 2 − ) state but not the Σ ( 1385 ) ( 3 / 2 + ) state. Comparing with the BESIII data we observe that, within the limited statistics of the experiment, the data show a structure around M π Λ = 1430 MeV that is reproduced by the theory, and no signal is seen for the Σ ( 1385 ) ( 3 / 2 + ) as the theory predicts. We call for a future update of the experiment once better statistics become available.","authors":"Yu-Shan Ren, Wen-Tao Lyu, Eulogio Oset","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ymgd-32dj","id":"doi:10.1103/ymgd-32dj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ymgd-32dj","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Fierz-complete four-quark interactions and the QCD phase diagram","summary":"The dynamics of Fierz-complete four-quark interactions and its influence on the QCD phase diagram have been investigated within the functional renormalization group approach to QCD at finite temperature and densities. It is found that in the vacuum the pion and sigma channels play the overwhelmingly dominant role, and all the other channels are negligible. However, when it is near the critical end point (CEP), the magnitude of four-quark couplings in other channels increases sizably, and they become more and more important. In comparison to the single scalar-pseudoscalar channel of four-quark interactions, the dynamics of Fierz-complete four-quark interactions increases a bit the curvature of the phase boundary and moves the CEP to the location of larger baryon chemical potential and smaller temperature.","authors":"Zi-ning Wang, Li-jun Zhou, Chuang Huang, Rui Wen, Shi Yin, Wei-jie Fu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ln3w-814r","id":"doi:10.1103/ln3w-814r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ln3w-814r","published":"2026-11-17","title":"QCD axion and neutrino masses","summary":"The strong C P problem and the origin of neutrino masses are among the clearest motivations for physics beyond the Standard Model. The Peccei-Quinn mechanism offers an elegant dynamical solution to the strong C P problem, predicting the QCD axion, while neutrino masses point to new degrees of freedom or interactions beyond the Standard Model. We explore the possibility that these two phenomena arise from a common origin by identifying the Peccei-Quinn symmetry-breaking scale with the scale responsible for neutrino mass generation. We perform a systematic and unified analysis of this connection in both Dine-Fischler-Srednicki-Zhitnitsky and Kim-Shifman-Vainshtein-Zakharov realizations of the Peccei-Quinn mechanism, considering the Type-I, Type-II, and Type-III seesaw mechanisms, the Zee radiative model, and the colored seesaw. For each scenario, we determine the axion couplings to photons and neutrinos and examine their phenomenological consequences. A robust prediction of all the frameworks considered is the existence of axion-neutrino couplings. We show that these interactions can significantly affect the axion decays. If the QCD axion makes up the observed dark matter, these couplings also lead to a large monochromatic neutrino flux from axion decays, opening a complementary path to test these theories in future neutrino experiments.","authors":"Hridoy Debnath, Pavel Fileviez Pérez, Grace Miller","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/qrbz-6kt7","id":"doi:10.1103/qrbz-6kt7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/qrbz-6kt7","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Gravitational wave detection via photon-graviton scattering and quantum interference","summary":"","authors":"K. Hari, S. Shankaranarayanan","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/lfxh-hzh6","id":"doi:10.1103/lfxh-hzh6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lfxh-hzh6","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Near-horizon modifications in finite N holography","summary":"If one extends the AdS/CFT extrapolate dictionary to large but finite N , we are expected to obtain nonperturbative violations of bulk semiclassicality and microcausality. Previously this was achieved by implementing a late boundary time cutoff, while smearing the boundary operator via the Hamilton-Kabat-Lifschytz-Lowe kernel. By performing explicit bulk reconstructions in the backgrounds of near-horizon modified AdS 2 and Banados-Teitelboim-Zanelli black holes, we recover similar deviations from semiclassicality as above. For these black hole mimickers, the near-horizon modification is controlled by a throat parameter which sets the scale of this deviation. In three bulk dimensions, probe dynamics, for a fixed radial band, also exhibits a dip-ramp-plateau structure in their spectral form factor when averaged over the throat parameter. Such structure has also been found recently in the background with a stretched horizon or a brick wall.","authors":"Rishkrith Bairy, Mrityunjay Nath, Debajyoti Sarkar","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/xy9q-tfbz","id":"doi:10.1103/xy9q-tfbz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xy9q-tfbz","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Axionlike particles and lepton flavor violation in muonic atoms","summary":"We explore the potential of the Mu2e experiment to probe the lepton-flavor-violating process μ − e − → e − e − in a muonic atom within a simplified axionlike particle (ALP) framework featuring flavor-violating e − μ couplings and a flavor-diagonal pseudoscalar coupling to electrons, which also allows for possible invisible ALP decays into a dark sector. We compute the ALP-mediated contribution to the transition rate and show that, at fixed couplings, the branching ratio increases for lighter mediators and scales as ( Z − 1 ) 3 , favoring heavier nuclei. We compare the model against constraints from μ → e γ , μ → 3 e , μ → e γ γ , μ → e + inv , and muonium-antimuonium conversion, as well as from the anomalous magnetic moments of the electron and muon. Additional astrophysical and beam-dump limits on the electron coupling are also discussed. A key result is that Δ a e provides one of the most stringent probes of the parameter space and, in the global scan, excludes the largest fraction of sampled points. After applying the laboratory constraints used in the scan, the viable branching ratio for μ − e − → e − e − in aluminum drops to at most O ( 10 − 20 ) , while the resonant region 2 m e < m a < m μ − m e is much more heavily suppressed. The highest achievable values are closely tied to B ( μ → 3 e ) near its current limit, indicating that the upcoming Mu3e experiment will explore the most promising region relevant for this muonic-atom signal. Our analysis shows that, although a light ALP can parametrically enhance μ − e − → e − e − at fixed couplings, existing bounds—especially Δ a e , μ → 3 e , μ → e γ , and muonium oscillations—severely limit the observable rate.","authors":"Girish Kumar, Alexey A. Petrov","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/fjgk-jcwx","id":"doi:10.1103/fjgk-jcwx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fjgk-jcwx","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Leptonic C P phase determination from Fisher information in T2K and NO ν A","summary":"The precise determination of the leptonic C P phase ( δ C P ) remains one of the central objectives of current and future long-baseline (LBL) neutrino oscillation experiments. Quantum estimation theory provides a natural framework to quantify the ultimate precision limits for estimating physical parameters encoded in quantum states. In this work, we employ the quantum Fisher information to investigate how much information about δ C P is intrinsically encoded in neutrino states and how efficiently it is extracted in present LBL experiments such as T2K and NO ν A . We first analyze the intrinsic quantum sensitivity of neutrino and antineutrino states and demonstrate how matter effects generate a neutrino mass-ordering dependent information structure. To compare the intrinsic information content of the quantum state with the information experimentally accessible through flavor measurements, we compute the event-level Fisher information from reconstructed event spectra using Poisson statistics including backgrounds and systematics. We find that both experiments extract only a small fraction of the total information available in the underlying quantum state. This extraction efficiency becomes particularly suppressed near maximally C P -violating regions, where the reconstructed event spectra exhibit reduced sensitivity to small variations in δ C P . We also apply the same framework to the reactor mixing angle θ 13 , providing a benchmark for comparison with the estimation of δ C P . Our analysis provides a complementary information-theoretic perspective on precise estimation of oscillation parameters in LBL neutrino experiments.","authors":"Neetu Raj Singh Chundawat, Luis A. Delgadillo, Yu-Feng Li","topics":["sensing","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/x57w-258b","id":"doi:10.1103/x57w-258b","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/x57w-258b","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Comprehensive interpretation of the LZ high-energy recoil event: A minimal U ( 1 ) B dark sector and its experimental tests","summary":"","authors":"Mattia Di Mauro","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/mbrg-yvg1","id":"doi:10.1103/mbrg-yvg1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mbrg-yvg1","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Lepton g − 2 nonuniversality of hadronic contributions and a sub-GeV window to new physics","summary":"","authors":"Siyuan Li, Vladimir Pascalutsa, Maxim Pospelov","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4x6h-ltnq","id":"doi:10.1103/4x6h-ltnq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4x6h-ltnq","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Strong first-order electroweak phase transitions and gravitational waves in the normal two-Higgs-doublet model: A comparative study of the four Yukawa types and thermal resummation schemes","summary":"","authors":"Jin-Hwan Cho, Dongjoo Kim, Jinheung Kim, Soojin Lee, Jeonghyeon Song","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1v96-xfgb","id":"doi:10.1103/1v96-xfgb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1v96-xfgb","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Estimation of the cumulant b 4 and sextic self-coupling of the QCD axion at finite temperature and density","summary":"","authors":"Xinguang Li, Zhao Zhang","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/2cxf-9srg","id":"doi:10.1103/2cxf-9srg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2cxf-9srg","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Verifiable quantum advantage via optimized DQI circuits","summary":"","authors":"Tanuj Khattar, Noah Shutty, Craig Gidney, Adam Zalcman, Noureldin Yosri, Dmitri Maslov, Ryan Babbush, Stephen P. Jordan","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/kqyv-lvvy","id":"doi:10.1103/kqyv-lvvy","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kqyv-lvvy","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Benchmarking Gaussian and Non-Gaussian Input States with a Hybrid Sampling Platform","summary":"The original boson sampling paradigm—consisting of multiple single-photon input states, a large interferometer, and multi-channel click detection—was originally proposed as a photonic route to quantum computational advantage. Its non-Gaussian resources, essential for outperforming any classical system, are provided by single-photon inputs and click detection. Yet the drive toward larger experiments has led to the replacement of experimentally demanding single-photon sources with Gaussian states, thereby diminishing the available non-Gaussianity—a critical quantum resource. As the community broadens its focus from the initial sampling task to possible real-world applications, it becomes crucial to quantify the performance cost associated with reducing non-Gaussian resources and to benchmark sampling platforms that employ different input states. To address this need, we introduce the Paderborn Quantum Sampler (PaQS), a hybrid platform capable of performing sampling experiments with eight Gaussian or non-Gaussian input states in a 12-mode interferometer within a single experimental run. This architecture enables direct, side-by-side benchmarking of distinct sampling regimes under otherwise identical conditions—only the input states vary between configurations. By employing a semi-device-independent framework based on matrix of moments approaches that offers certification that does not rely on prior knowledge of the interferometer or the input states, we verify that the observed data cannot be reproduced using classical resource states—a prerequisite for demonstrating quantum advantage. Applying this framework, we show that non-Gaussian inputs offer a distinct advantage in the verification of nonclassicality under our proposed metric.","authors":"Michael Stefszky, Kai-Hong Luo, Jan-Lucas Eickmann, Simone Atzeni, Florian Lütkewitte, Cheeranjiv Pandey, Fabian Schlue, Jonas Lammers, Mikhail Roiz, Timon Schapeler, Laura Ares, Milad Yahyapour, Alexander Kastner, Joschua Martinek, Michael Mittermair, Carlos Sevilla-Gutiérrez, Marius Leyendecker, Oskar Kohout, Dmitriy Mitin, Ronald Holzwarth et al.","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/1d5g-5s8g","id":"doi:10.1103/1d5g-5s8g","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1d5g-5s8g","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Classical analog emulation of quantum circuits via time-averaged dynamic states","summary":"","authors":"Matias Miguel Castillo Valle, Tim Tuuva, Benjamin Apffel, Hervé Lissek, Romain Fleury, Mathieu Padlewski","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/mhhs-c3x9","id":"doi:10.1103/mhhs-c3x9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mhhs-c3x9","published":"2026-11-17","title":"Cavity-mediated two-qubit gate: Tuning to optimal performance with NISQ-era quantum simulations","summary":"A variety of photon-mediated operations are critical to the realization of scalable quantum information processing platforms and their accurate characterization is essential for the identification of optimal regimes and their experimental realizations. Such light-matter interactions are often studied with a broad variety of analytical and computational methods that are constrained by approximation techniques or by computational scaling. Quantum processors present a new avenue to address these challenges. Here, we develop and implement an algorithm compatible with NISQ (noisy intermediate scale quantum) devices that allows a comprehensive characterization of cavity mediated two-qubit gates over a broad parameter space. In particular, we investigate the underlying quantum state transfer between the qubits, and illustrate its efficiency as a function of the detunings and couplings between the qubits and the cavity, and as a function of the damping factor of the cavity. Our algorithm is validated by a comparison with analytical results where applicable. The algorithm allows us to identify underexplored regimes of optimal performance, from which we report two insightful findings—one where we observe a state transfer fidelity of about 99 % in the intermediate detuning regime, and the other where we observe a significant boost in fidelity with qubits that are moderately detuned from each other by coupling them unequally to a far-detuned cavity. Furthermore, we illustrate the breakdown of the rotating wave approximation as we approach the ultrastrong coupling and the deep-dispersive regimes. While the current paper addresses a system that only involves a few qubits and can be solved using classical numerical methods, the algorithm can be readily extended to study a variety of systems and processes in quantum information processing that employ cavity- or photon-mediated coupling between qubits, particularly larger systems such as many-emitter ensembles, cavity arrays, and multimode cavities, that are classically intractable.","authors":"Shreekanth S. Yuvarajan, Vincent Iglesias-Cardinale, David Hucul, Herbert F. Fotso","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-16":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Nature Physics","doi":"10.1038/s41567-026-03493-z","id":"doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03493-z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-026-03493-z","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Extending qubit lifetime beyond break-even and enhancing quantum sensing by autonomous quantum error correction","summary":"","authors":"Zhongchu Ni, Ling Hu, Yanyan Cai, Libo Zhang, Jiasheng Mai, Xiaowei Deng, Pan Zheng, Song Liu, Shi-Biao Zheng, Yuan Xu, Dapeng Yu","topics":["sensing","computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/jvj9-x276","id":"doi:10.1103/jvj9-x276","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jvj9-x276","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Erratum: Ultracold mechanical quantum sensor for tests of new physics [Phys. Rev. Lett. 136, 180802 (2026)]","summary":"","authors":"Andraž Omahen, Simon Storz, Marius Bild, Dario Scheiwiller, Matteo Fadel, Yiwen Chu","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/sv5m-hz64","id":"doi:10.1103/sv5m-hz64","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/sv5m-hz64","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Dynamical axion quasiparticles: Open quantum systems","summary":"","authors":"Daniel Boyanovsky","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/czhh-5l7v","id":"doi:10.1103/czhh-5l7v","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/czhh-5l7v","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Discovery prospects for photophobic axionlike particles at a 100 TeV proton-proton collider","summary":"We study heavy photophobic axionlike particles (ALPs) in the limit of an effectively vanishing diphoton coupling, g a γ γ ≃ 0 , for which diphoton production and decay are suppressed and collider phenomenology is driven by electroweak interactions ( a W W , a Z γ , and a Z Z ). We perform detector-level searches at a future s = 100 TeV p p collider (SppC/FCC-hh), with an integrated luminosity of L = 20 ab − 1 . We consider a → Z γ and a → W + W − decays. For p p → j j a , we include both s -channel electroweak exchange and vector boson fusion (VBF)-like topologies, while the tri- W signature arises from associated production p p → W ± a (via s -channel exchange) followed by a → W + W − . We analyze three final states— Z γ j j with Z → ℓ + ℓ − , tri- W ( W ± W ± W ∓ ) with same-sign dimuons plus jets, and W + W − j j with opposite-sign, different-flavor dilepton ( e ± μ ∓ ) plus jets. Among the two W W final states, the VBF-assisted j j a ( → W + W − ) channel overtakes the purely s -channel tri- W mode for m a ≳ 1 TeV , reflecting 100 TeV signal/background kinematic shifts beyond naive energy/luminosity rescaling. A boosted-decision-tree classifier built from kinematic observables provides the final signal-background separation, using detector-level simulations of signal and high-statistics SM backgrounds. At s = 100 TeV and L = 20 ab − 1 , we present discovery sensitivities to the ALP– W coupling g a W W over m a ∈ [ 100 , 7000 ] GeV . In parallel, we report model-independent discovery thresholds on σ × Br for p p → j j a with a → Z γ and a → W + W − as well as for associated production p p → W ± a with a → W + W − . Taken together, the three channels deliver broad coverage and mutually reinforcing checks across decay modes and production regimes, strengthening robustness against channel-dependent systematics. The 100 TeV program extends the discovery reach well beyond 14 TeV projections within a general effective field theory description that does not assume a specific ultraviolet completion.","authors":"Zilong Ding, Jiaojiao Feng, Ying-nan Mao, Kechen Wang, Yiheng Xiong","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/pjhp-hdxd","id":"doi:10.1103/pjhp-hdxd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/pjhp-hdxd","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Plasma instability splits axion-photon conversion lines","summary":"","authors":"Elizabeth A. Tolman","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/yxt1-bdx7","id":"doi:10.1103/yxt1-bdx7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/yxt1-bdx7","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Keeping it renormalizable: Minimal baryogenesis induced asymmetric dark matter","summary":"Many asymmetric dark matter scenarios have been proposed to date. Among them, perhaps the most motivated ones are those in which the dark matter asymmetry is induced from the baryon/lepton asymmetries via chemical equilibration without any new sources of C P violation. However, most of the models put forward along these lines have been excluded by now and/or are based on complicated setups. In this Letter, we present a new, simple, and viable scenario. It assumes only two new fields: a scalar singlet and an inert scalar doublet, and is based only on interactions, that slowly generate the dark matter asymmetry from the Standard Model Higgs asymmetry. The successful asymmetry transfer requires small (but technically natural) couplings which in turn make the model C P conserving in practice. The model allows for the direct detection of dark matter in the upcoming generation of experiments, and the inert doublet is predicted to be light enough to be potentially produced and observed at the LHC and future colliders, 46 GeV < m H ′ < 580 GeV . Quite remarkably, the mass and lifetime of the inert doublet may be inferred from direct detection experiments, indicating long-lived compressed electroweak states that can be searched for at the High-Luminosity LHC.","authors":"Miguel Escudero Abenza, Thomas Hambye, Chandan Hati","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/tp28-39s6","id":"doi:10.1103/tp28-39s6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/tp28-39s6","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Impact of the axionlike self-interactions in gravitational atoms for LISA","summary":"","authors":"Samuel Gómez Gómez, Xisco Jimenez Forteza, Carlos Palenzuela","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/276c-l98h","id":"doi:10.1103/276c-l98h","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/276c-l98h","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Using the pericentre precession of LAGEOS II to constrain quadratically coupled ultralight dark matter","summary":"It has been proposed that feebly interacting ultralight scalars may constitute the dark matter content of the Universe. Models describing the interactions of a dark matter scalar with standard model fields may feature quadratic interactions at leading order, such that the scalar acquires an effective mass in the neighborhood of a classical matter distribution. The effect of their introduction is to provide effective shifts in fundamental constants of physics, as well as to mediate scalar “fifth forces” between test bodies. We here demonstrate how these fifth forces can result in pericenter precession in the orbital motion of satellites around the Earth. We apply this to the measured pericenter precession of the LAGEOS II experiment, constraining the mass, and couplings to the light standard model fields of a quadratically coupled ultralight dark matter scalar. We observe such an experiment to be effective in constraining parameter space at strong couplings, where existing constraints from satellite and tabletop-level experiments break down.","authors":"Clare Burrage, Angus Macdonald, Elisa Todarello","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8nrw-sw93","id":"doi:10.1103/8nrw-sw93","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8nrw-sw93","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Multipolar neutrino radiation in binary neutron star mergers: Angular structure, rotational variability, and implications for electron fraction","summary":"","authors":"Kutay Ar𝚤nç Çokluk, Albino Perego, Federico Maria Guercilena, Kadri Yakut","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/f4p2-qbdb","id":"doi:10.1103/f4p2-qbdb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/f4p2-qbdb","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Erratum: Graviton photoproduction by a Kerr-Newman black hole with worldline EFT [Phys. Rev. D 114 , 025003 (2026)]","summary":"","authors":"Qinyuan Zheng","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/sngq-mkbh","id":"doi:10.1103/sngq-mkbh","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/sngq-mkbh","published":"2026-11-16","title":"RG-invariant symmetry ratio for QCD: A study of U ( 1 ) A and chiral symmetry restoration","summary":"We introduce a renormalization-group-invariant, scheme-independent symmetry ratio κ A B for the quantitative characterization of symmetry breaking in QCD. As a first application, we employ κ A B to investigate the relative strength of S U ( 2 ) L × S U ( 2 ) R chiral symmetry and U ( 1 ) A axial symmetry breaking in N f = 2 + 1 + 1 lattice QCD using optimal domain-wall fermions at the physical point. Our study covers three lattice spacings and twelve temperatures in the range 164–385 MeV. We examine three independent symmetry-breaking channels in the nonsinglet sector with quark-connected correlators: the U ( 1 ) A -sensitive scalar–pseudoscalar channel ( κ P S ), probing the π – δ system; the S U ( 2 ) L × S U ( 2 ) R -sensitive vector–axial-vector channel ( κ V A ), probing the ρ – a 1 system; and an additional U ( 1 ) A -sensitive tensor vector–axial-tensor vector channel ( κ T X ), probing the ρ T – b 1 system. At each lattice spacing, we observe a clear hierarchy κ P S > κ V A > κ T X . Since temperature and lattice spacing are not varied independently in the present ensembles, the lattice-spacing dependence of the hierarchy cannot be isolated, and a controlled continuum extrapolation is deferred to future work.","authors":"Ting-Wai Chiu, Tung-Han Hsieh","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bqy7-c34z","id":"doi:10.1103/bqy7-c34z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bqy7-c34z","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Toponium effects on quantum steering and Bell nonlocality of top quarks","summary":"We investigate quantum steering and Bell nonlocality in top-quark pair production at the LHC near threshold. The toponium contribution strengthens the spin-singlet component and substantially enhances the entanglement between the two quark spins. With current LHC data, quantum steering appears to be observable at the 5 σ level provided systematic uncertainties are at 5% or below. For Bell nonlocality, the statistical significance can also be high close to threshold, but systematics are expected to limit a possible measurement to around 2 σ sensitivity.","authors":"J. A. Aguilar-Saavedra, S. Rodríguez-Benítez","topics":["foundations","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ypw5-y789","id":"doi:10.1103/ypw5-y789","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ypw5-y789","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Dispersion relations of collective flavor excitations in dense neutrino media","summary":"We apply the Landau damping theory to collective flavor excitations in dense neutrino media. We demonstrate explicitly that the (meta)stable modes of the collective flavor excitations in a physical dense neutrino gas are indeed weakly damped. We show that, in a neutrino medium with a continuous energy distribution, there can exist infinitely many dispersion relation branches of collective flavor excitations that extend to large wave numbers. In a neutrino gas with one or more zero crossings in the distribution of the difference of the (neutrino) lepton numbers (DLNs), the least damped resonant branches and the metastable branches can suffer from inverse Landau damping at certain wave numbers and thus become unstable. We also introduce the concept of convolved DLN distributions, which can be used to understand the physical origin of the slow and fast flavor instabilities in dense neutrino media in core-collapse supernovae and neutron star mergers.","authors":"Anson Kost, Huaiyu Duan","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/fq5z-ckk3","id":"doi:10.1103/fq5z-ckk3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fq5z-ckk3","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Is XTE J1814-338 a dark matter-admixed neutron star?","summary":"","authors":"Hong-Ming Liu, Peng-Cheng Chu, He Liu, Zeng-Hua Li, Xiao-Hua Li","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ys6t-vqtv","id":"doi:10.1103/ys6t-vqtv","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ys6t-vqtv","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Exclusive quark and gluon dijet production as probes of GPDs at collider energies","summary":"We study exclusive electroproduction of dijets in the collinear factorization framework as a probe of generalized parton distributions (GPDs). For quark dijet production, we extend previous analyses by including contributions from helicity GPDs and by assessing an additional leading-order electromagnetic channel governed by elastic nucleon form factors. Furthermore, we investigate exclusive gluon dijet production. We compare our prediction to HERA data and provide projections for measurements at the future Electron-Ion Collider.","authors":"Zhuoyi Pang, Paweł Sznajder, Lech Szymanowski, Jakub Wagner","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/nvss-dhsm","id":"doi:10.1103/nvss-dhsm","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nvss-dhsm","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Model analysis on the effectiveness of the HAL QCD method for hadron-hadron interactions","summary":"The HAL QCD method has been one of the powerful tools to extract hadron-hadron interactions directly from lattice QCD simulation data. In this paper, we aim at examining the effectiveness of the HAL QCD method by deriving a formula to calculate quantities in the HAL QCD method, such as the so-called R correlators and HAL QCD local potentials, from the hadron-hadron scattering amplitudes within effective models. In this framework, we can judge whether the HAL QCD local potential, evaluated in the present formula, reproduces the properties of the original hadron-hadron interaction or not via the scattering amplitude, which is a solution of the Lippmann-Schwinger equation with the original hadron-hadron interaction as an input. In an analysis within a simple model of elastic scattering, we show that, when the original interaction is predominantly local, the HAL QCD local potentials quantitatively reproduce phase shifts of the hadron-hadron scatterings and correctly indicate the existence/absence of the bound state with its binding energy ∼ MeV . Higher-order corrections in the derivative expansion of the HAL QCD nonlocal potentials can provide a better description of the binding energies of the bound states. The lattice discretization and finite-volume effects of spacetime modify the results only slightly, as long as their scale is not comparable to the interaction range. Furthermore, we consider the K ¯ N potential in a bare to K ¯ N transition amplitude, which shows singular behavior around the origin in the recent HAL QCD results of the lattice QCD simulation data, and discuss the cause of such singular behavior in the HAL QCD method through our model analysis of the K ¯ N scattering.","authors":"Takayasu Sekihara, Kei Fujiwara","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4tzy-gbfy","id":"doi:10.1103/4tzy-gbfy","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4tzy-gbfy","published":"2026-11-16","title":"LIGO, LISA, and ultralight axionlike dark matter","summary":"Interferometric searches for axion-like dark matter (ALP DM) via the induced photon birefringence in a coherent background have been proposed and analyzed by several authors over the past decade, with tabletop experiments (LIDA, ADBC, DANCE) reporting first results and detailed calculations available for both ground-based (LIGO, GEO600 via DarkGEO) and space-based (LISA, TianQin, Taiji, BBO via Gué [.] and Yao [.]) gravitational-wave detectors. We revisit this class of measurements within the framework of Krauss [.] and provide a detailed treatment of the interplay between the DM coherence length λ coh , the interferometer arm length L , and the detector transfer function, with attention to the distinct suppression regimes λ coh > L (cancellation) and λ coh < L (random walk). For LIGO, the sensitivity is set by the Fabry-Pérot arm-cavity response peaking sharply near the cavity free spectral range, reproducing the results of Nagano ; the naive single-pass estimate substantially overstates the reach and cannot be applied directly. For LISA, with the polarization-sensitive modification proposed by Gué [.] or Yao [.], the natural mass range m a ∼ 4 × 10 − 19 – 4 × 10 − 16 eV (LISA science band) is accessible and the projected reach g a γ γ ≲ few × 10 − 13 GeV − 1 (realistic optical-metrology noise) improves on the CAST bound over most of the band by up to about 2 orders of magnitude. We discuss directional signatures from the Galactic DM wind, both annual and sidereal, as a discrimination tool for candidate signals. Local DM substructure with anisotropic velocity dispersion can shift and enhance the reach in specific mass windows.","authors":"Lawrence M. Krauss","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/lwf7-ysl7","id":"doi:10.1103/lwf7-ysl7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lwf7-ysl7","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Search for heavy H γ and Z γ resonances with a bottom quark-antiquark pair in the final state in proton-proton collisions at s = 13 TeV","summary":"","authors":"A. Hayrapetyan, V. Makarenko, A. Tumasyan, W. Adam, J. W. Andrejkovic, L. Benato, T. Bergauer, M. Dragicevic, C. Giordano, P. S. Hussain, M. Jeitler, N. Krammer, A. Li, D. Liko, M. Matthewman, I. Mikulec, J. Schieck, R. Schöfbeck, D. Schwarz, M. Shooshtari et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/9zsr-h4yl","id":"doi:10.1103/9zsr-h4yl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/9zsr-h4yl","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Axions create singularities on extremal horizons","summary":"","authors":"Gary T. Horowitz, Maciej Kolanowski, Grant N. Remmen, Jorge E. Santos","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bd9d-frkb","id":"doi:10.1103/bd9d-frkb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bd9d-frkb","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Neutrino lines and photon continua from cascade dark matter decay","summary":"","authors":"Jun Guo, Shi-ying Zhao, Bin Zhu","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2zxc-4b6l","id":"doi:10.1103/2zxc-4b6l","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2zxc-4b6l","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Endothermic Z ′ -portal dark matter: LZ-LHC complementarity","summary":"","authors":"Nobuchika Okada, Digesh Raut","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/hyrk-1ftz","id":"doi:10.1103/hyrk-1ftz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/hyrk-1ftz","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Collective Many-Body Dynamics in a Solid-State Quantum Sensor Controlled through Nanoscale Magnetic Gradients","summary":"Coherent collective dynamics of strongly interacting qubits are a central resource in quantum information science, with applications from quantum computing and simulation to metrology. While electronic spins interact strongly via dipolar couplings in dense solid-state ensembles, imperfections and positional disorder pose major obstacles to coherent correlated behavior, limiting their usefulness. Here, we realize collective many-body dynamics by combining time-dependent magnetic field gradients with global coherent control of dense electron spin ensembles in diamond. We control and probe the dynamics of nanometer-scale spin spirals, and, by exploiting Hamiltonian engineering that enhances the microscopic symmetry of the interactions, we observe a disorder-resilient collective spin evolution. Our results establish a pathway to interaction-enhanced quantum metrology and nanoscale imaging of materials and biological systems under ambient conditions.","authors":"Piotr Put, Nathaniel T. Leitao, Haoyang Gao, Christina Spaegele, Oksana Makarova, Lillian B. Hughes Wyatt, Andrew C. Maccabe, Mathew Mammen, Bartholomeus Machielse, Hengyun Zhou, Szymon Pustelny, Ania C. Bleszynski Jayich, Federico Capasso, Leigh S. Martin, Hongkun Park, Mikhail D. Lukin","topics":["sensing","computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/8nkv-46hc","id":"doi:10.1103/8nkv-46hc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8nkv-46hc","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Optimized permanent-magnet loops and arcs for improved field uniformity","summary":"Simple, low-cost, permanent magnet geometries are frequently used for field generation in cases where a constant high-strength magnetic field is required. This is the situation across many emerging quantum platforms, primarily for bias fields in quantum computing or nitrogen-vacancy centers. Finding optimal arrangements of primitive magnet geometries such as these can be difficult, often escalating in complexity, as further higher-order field harmonics are considered. This is further complicated by the need to adhere to strict system constraints, for instance, those implied by geometric or optical access requirements. Here we illustrate a method that encodes loop and arc magnet geometries into an analytical form, attaining solutions for the field harmonics produced by both axial and radial magnetizations for each form. These harmonic descriptions then form the basis of a numerical design procedure. Our approach facilitates solutions that improve fidelity of the target field by reducing error harmonic contributions while satisfying constraints on magnet position. This allows for considerations such as optical access or maximum system extents to be accounted for. To demonstrate this optimization procedure, we produce four system designs: two axially magnetized loops to produce a uniform axial field and two radially magnetized arcs, which, respectively, produce a radial-uniform and radial-gradient field. Their performance is then compared against standard designs, obtained from analytical solutions alone. In each case, when using our approach, the volume of field uniformity within 99% agreement of the target field increases by a minimum factor of 1.37. Designs generated by this procedure therefore enable improved field fidelity and reduce magnetic field-driven errors.","authors":"Chris Morley, Peter J. Hobson, Noah Hardwicke, Harriet O’Brien, Niall Holmes, Mark Fromhold","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/sm6w-5ttl","id":"doi:10.1103/sm6w-5ttl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/sm6w-5ttl","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Quantum eigenvalue transformations for arbitrary matrices","summary":"Quantum signal processing (QSP) and quantum singular value transformation (QSVT) provide an efficient framework for implementing polynomials of block-encoded matrices, and thus offer a systematic approach to quantum algorithm design. However, despite a number of recent advances, important limitations remain. In particular, QSP can only transform unitary matrices, by applying a polynomial to their eigenvalues, while QSVT is a singular value transformation and thus one can only obtain the polynomial of Hermitian matrices. As a consequence, these techniques do not directly apply to an arbitrary non-Hermitian matrix that is not diagonalizable. In this work, we propose a simple yet powerful method to extend these ideas to arbitrary square matrices by acting on their eigenvalues. To this end, we introduce the notion of an n -regular block encoding, namely, a block encoding whose k th power reproduces the k th power of the encoded matrix for every 0 ≤ k ≤ n . We show that applying QSP to any unitary with this property is equivalent to applying a polynomial of degree at most n to the block-encoded matrix, independently of its internal structure. Moreover, we provide a simple construction that transforms any block encoding into an n -regular one using only O ( log n ) ancillary qubits and O ( n log ( n / ε ) ) operations. Finally, we show that this construction induces the desired transformation on the eigenvalues associated with the Jordan normal form of the matrix; for nondiagonalizable matrices, this yields the full matrix function P ( A ) , whose action on each Jordan block involves the derivatives of P , and not merely the map λ ↦ P ( λ ) .","authors":"Xabier Gutiérrez, Lorenzo Laneve, Mikel Sanz","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/t9wk-62kf","id":"doi:10.1103/t9wk-62kf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/t9wk-62kf","published":"2026-11-16","title":"Rapid spin-resolved single-atom imaging of alkaline-earth fermions","summary":"Local Hilbert spaces with large dimension are of key interest for quantum information with applications in quantum computing and memories, quantum simulations, and metrology. Thanks to its weak coupling to external perturbations, the large ground-state nuclear spin manifold of fermionic alkaline-earth atoms is an exciting resource to explore for quantum information. Simultaneous single-atom and nuclear-spin-resolved detection, however, remains an outstanding challenge limiting the development of novel quantum computing and simulation schemes beyond qubits. Here, we report on an imaging technique enabling the simultaneous detection of up to four quantum states encoded in the nuclear spin manifold of a single fermionic strontium atom within ∼ 100 µ s , with conditional spin-resolved detection fidelities ranging from 93.6 % to 99.7 % . This technique is further used to track the highly coherent nuclear spin dynamics after a quench, highlighting the potential of this system for quantum information. These results offer fascinating perspectives for quantum science with multielectron atoms ranging from qudit-based quantum computing to quantum simulations of the SU( N ) Fermi-Hubbard model.","authors":"Thies Plassmann, Leon Schäfer, Meny Menashes, Guillaume Salomon","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-13":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/8x23-v9n2","id":"doi:10.1103/8x23-v9n2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8x23-v9n2","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Single programmable photonic circuit for universal quantum measurements","summary":"","authors":"Wen-Zhe Yan, Lan-Tian Feng, Zhibo Hou, Yuan-Yuan Zhao, Carles Roch i Carceller, Armin Tavakoli, Huangjun Zhu, Guang-Can Guo, Xi-Feng Ren, Guo-Yong Xiang","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/4121-2r9z","id":"doi:10.1103/4121-2r9z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4121-2r9z","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Higgsino dark matter interpretation of the LUX-ZEPLIN 248 keV nuclear-recoil event","summary":"","authors":"Katherine Freese, Dionysios P. Theodosopoulos","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/y9ln-b4t9","id":"doi:10.1103/y9ln-b4t9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/y9ln-b4t9","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Synthesis of fault-tolerant state preparation circuits using Steane-type error detection","summary":"Fault-tolerant state preparation is essential for reliable quantum error correction, particularly in Steane-type error correction, which relies on robust ancilla states for syndrome readout. One method of fault-tolerant state preparation is to initialize multiple ancilla states and check them against each other to detect problematic errors. In the worst case, the number of states required for successful initialization grows polynomially with the code distance, but it has been shown that this can be reduced to constant ancilla overhead; in the best case, only four states are required. However, existing techniques for finding low-overhead initialization schemes are limited to codes with large symmetry groups, such as the code. In this work we propose a general, automated synthesis methodology for Steane-type fault-tolerant state preparation circuits that applies to arbitrary Calderbank-Shor-Steane (CSS) codes and does not rely on code symmetries. We apply the proposed methods to various codes up to a distance of 7 and simulate the successful fault-tolerant initialization of logical basis states under circuit-level depolarizing noise. The circuits synthesized using the proposed methodology provide an important step towards experimental realizations of high-fidelity ancilla states for near-term demonstration of fault-tolerant quantum computation.","authors":"Erik Weilandt, Tom Peham, Robert Wille","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/n4rw-wgx1","id":"doi:10.1103/n4rw-wgx1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/n4rw-wgx1","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Fully integrated entangled photon-pair source on a photonic chip for a wavelength-multiplexed multiparty quantum key distribution network","summary":"The development of QKD networks as a possible answer to the threat to encrypted communication posed by quantum computers has given rise to a variety of concepts and implementations in recent years. In terms of the scalability of such systems, photonic integrated circuits (photonic chips) are a promising approach. This makes the systems not only much more miniaturized and robust, but also more attractive for industrial production. Here we present the concept and characterization of a photonic chip that can act as a source in an entanglement-based QKD network employing spontaneous four-wave mixing in microring resonators. In addition to photon-pair production, the chip also directly performs essential filtering tasks. Moreover, the photon-pair rate can be maintained constant over an extended period using a Pound-Drever-Hall locking method.","authors":"Maximilian Mengler, Jakob Kaltwasser, Erik Fitzke, Thomas Hoffmann, Thomas Walther","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/4945-qcb9","id":"doi:10.1103/4945-qcb9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4945-qcb9","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Quantum annealing through a first-order phase transition: Field theory approach","summary":"","authors":"Diego Andrés Rivera Orona, Predrag Punoševac, Nikolai A. Sinitsyn","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/fn99-hq7q","id":"doi:10.1103/fn99-hq7q","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fn99-hq7q","published":"2026-11-13","title":"First full physics benchmark for highly granular calorimeter surrogates","summary":"The physics programs of current and future collider experiments necessitate the development of surrogate simulators for calorimeter showers. While much progress has been made in the development of generative models for this task, they have typically been evaluated in simplified scenarios and for single particles. This is particularly true for the challenging task of highly granular calorimeter simulation. For the first time, this work studies the use of highly granular generative calorimeter surrogates in a realistic simulation application. We introduce DDML (4hep with machine learning), a generic library which enables the combination of generative calorimeter surrogates with realistic detectors implemented using the 4hep toolkit. We compare two different generative models—one operating on a regular grid representation and the other using a less common point cloud approach. In order to disentangle methodological details from model performance, we provide comparisons to idealized simulators which directly sample representations of different resolutions from the full simulation ground truth. We then systematically evaluate model performance on post-reconstruction benchmarks for electromagnetic shower simulation. Beginning with a typical single-particle study, we introduce a first multiparticle benchmark based on diphoton separations, before studying a first full physics benchmark based on hadronic decays of the tau lepton. Our results indicate that models operating on a point cloud can achieve a favorable balance between speed and accuracy for highly granular calorimeter simulation compared to those which operate on a regular grid representation.","authors":"Thorsten Buss, Henry Day-Hall, Frank Gaede, Gregor Kasieczka, Katja Krüger, Anatolii Korol, Thomas Madlener, Peter McKeown","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1dc1-c7d9","id":"doi:10.1103/1dc1-c7d9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1dc1-c7d9","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Negative energies and the breakdown of bulk geometry","summary":"One central question in quantum gravity is to understand how and why predictions from semiclassical gravity can break down in regimes with low spacetime curvature. One diagnostic of such a breakdown is that states which are orthonormal at the semiclassical level can receive large corrections to their inner products from quantum fluctuations. We study this effect by examining inner products in pure 2D Jackiw-Teitelboim gravity. Previous work showed that black hole states with long interiors exhibit a breakdown at length scales of order e S 0 , where S 0 is a parameter analogous to 1 / G N in higher dimensions. This breakdown is caused by the discreteness of the spectrum of the dual boundary random matrix theory. We show that the full sum over quantum fluctuations indicates a more dramatic breakdown at parametrically shorter lengths of order e S 0 / 3 . In the dual boundary description, these corrections arise from negative energy states appearing in rare members of the random matrix ensemble. These results demonstrate that nonperturbative effects can invalidate the semiclassical description at much smaller length scales than previously expected, providing a new mechanism for the breakdown of effective gravitational theories.","authors":"John Preskill, Mykhaylo Usatyuk, Shreya Vardhan","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/53hv-yxhl","id":"doi:10.1103/53hv-yxhl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/53hv-yxhl","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Exotic Higgs decays at a muon collider","summary":"We study the sensitivity of a future muon collider to exotic Higgs decays in a minimal scenario of Standard Model (SM) augmented with a light singlet scalar S . We consider the decay h → S S and S ’s subsequently decay back to SM. In particular, we focus on final states with four bottom quarks ( 4 b ), and two bottom quarks and two muons ( 2 b 2 μ ). Analyses are performed for two muon collider benchmark configurations: center-of-mass collision energy s = 3 TeV with 1 ab − 1 data and s = 10 TeV with 10 ab − 1 data. Machine-learning techniques are applied to suppress backgrounds and mitigate jet-combinatorics effects in both channels. We find that the 4 b mode could be sensitive to the branching ratio, BR ( h → S S → 4 b ) , of O ( 10 − 2 ) at 3 TeV and O ( 10 − 3 ) at 10 TeV, significantly improving upon high-luminosity LHC projections. In the Higgs-portal model with S coupling to SM only through mixing with the Higgs, the sensitivities to BR ( h → S S ) remain at the same level given O ( 1 ) branching fraction of S decaying into b -quarks. The 2 b 2 μ mode benefits from a clean dimuon resonance and can probe BR ( h → S S → 2 b 2 μ ) down to 10 − 5 level at a 10 TeV muon collider. But the sensitivity to BR ( h → S S ) will be significantly reduced due to the small branching fraction of S decaying into muons in the Higgs portal model.","authors":"JiJi Fan, Lingfeng Li, Tao Liu, Yanhan Wang, Mingrui Zhou","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/b3bj-q8fn","id":"doi:10.1103/b3bj-q8fn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/b3bj-q8fn","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Neutrino fog in the light dark sector: The role of isospin violation","summary":"Dark matter (DM) direct detection is now standing at an interesting juncture, where the Standard Model (SM) neutrino background and the upper bound on the DM signal cross section are starting to overlap in the DM mass region around 10 GeV. The neutrino floor, which defines the extent of the neutrino background, can be modified in different beyond Standard Model (BSM) setups. We work in a simple BSM dark sector extension of the SM visible sector, where isospin-violating interactions occur naturally. In this model, both DM and neutrinos have, in general, isospin-violating (IV) interactions with nuclei, through a newly added local U(1) gauge boson Z ′ . Depending on the choice of the model parameters, the coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering cross section can either increase or decrease, shifting up or down the neutrino floor in the parameter space. The same is true for the DM experimental upper bound, whose change is driven exclusively by the IV parameter f n / f p . Several scenarios are constructed, based on the interplay between the two regions and the allowed parameter space left between them, and discussed. The potential observation of solar neutrinos in DM direct detection experiments is also discussed in the context of our framework.","authors":"Víctor Martín Lozano, Shankar Pramanik, Soumya Sadhukhan, Adrián Terrones","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/z54p-4g3q","id":"doi:10.1103/z54p-4g3q","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/z54p-4g3q","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Constants in sequences of M2-brane partition functions","summary":"We determine in closed form the N -independent constant terms in the all-order 1 / N expansions of the topologically twisted index and of the associated Bethe potential for the Aharony–Bergman–Jafferis–Maldacena (ABJM) theory, as well as the Bethe potential constant of the Atiyah–Drinfeld–Hitchin–Manin (ADHM) theory. We reconstruct these constants analytically from high-precision Bethe-ansatz numerics, verify them down to the level of nonperturbative corrections, and find them to be closely related to the constant map function A governing the round three-sphere partition function. The resulting expressions for the ADHM and ABJM constants pass the nontrivial test dictated by 3d mirror symmetry. Via recently established factorization relations, they also determine in closed form the N -independent constant contribution to the squashed three-sphere partition function, through the first two leading orders in its large-squashing expansion. These constants supply precisely the piece left undetermined in the recent exact results for 3d supersymmetric partition functions to all orders in the 1 / N expansion and thereby mark an important step toward completing them, both in field theory and in the dual quantum gravity description.","authors":"Junho Hong","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/6f1s-8l23","id":"doi:10.1103/6f1s-8l23","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6f1s-8l23","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Investigating the P -wave D K * molecular interpretation of the D s 1 ( 2700 ) via its strong decays","summary":"In this work, we investigate the possibility of interpreting the D s 1 ( 2700 ) as a P -wave D K * hadronic molecular state through a systematic study of its strong decay properties. Using the effective Lagrangian approach, we calculate the strong decay widths of the D s 1 ( 2700 ) into the two-body final states D K , D * K , D s η , and D s * η , as well as the three-body D π K channel. The cutoff parameter Λ and the coupling constant g D s 1 between D s 1 ( 2700 ) and its molecular constituents are determined by fitting the B 0 → D − D 0 K + decay distribution calculated by the Collaboration and the corresponding decay width, both of which include only the contribution from D s 1 ( 2700 ) . With the obtained coupling constant and cutoff parameter, the total decay width and the ratio R D s 1 = Γ ( D s 1 ( 2700 ) → D * K ) / Γ ( D s 1 ( 2700 ) → D K ) can be readily calculated, both of which are found to be in good agreement with the experimental measurements, providing strong support for interpreting the D s 1 ( 2700 ) as a P -wave D K * molecular state. The experimentally unobserved D s 1 ( 2700 ) → D s ( * ) η decay channel provides a further test of this interpretation, as its predicted decay width differs from the conventional quark model prediction.","authors":"Qing Lu, ZheHao Zhu, Yin Huang, Cai Cheng","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/j7z8-kbvr","id":"doi:10.1103/j7z8-kbvr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/j7z8-kbvr","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Spectroscopy as a probe of spacetime foam","summary":"Quantum-gravitational spacetime foam varying in space and in time imprints, respectively, a transverse phase decoherence and an achromatic, stochastic arrival-time jitter on light from distant sources. We show that the jitter modifies the first-order temporal coherence g ( 1 ) ( τ ) and hence the observed spectrum, providing a spectroscopic probe of the temporal variation, complementary to existing imaging-channel constraints on the spatial variation. In the dynamical-foam limit, where the foam decorrelates within the light’s coherence time, the spectrum splits into a coherent line core suppressed by e − σ ϕ 2 , with σ ϕ 2 the variance of the foam-induced phase jitter, and an incoherent pedestal, yielding a spectral shape-distortion bound on the foam exponent α . Three probes follow: the outright survival of broadband flux, the fractional width of a spectral line, and a chromatic deficit across a broadband continuum. Applied to the MeV emission lines of GRB 221009A we obtain α ≳ 0.63 , a weak, lever-arm-dominated floor. For the broadband CMB the foam induces an e − σ ϕ 2 ( ν ) deficit ( σ ϕ 2 ∝ ν 2 ); a fit to the Cosmic Background Explorer/Far-InfraRed Absolute Spectrophotometer monopole spectrum reaches α ≳ 0.52 (95% CL), and next-generation spectrometers reach only α ≳ 0.54 – 0.55 (forecast), limited by the foam-temperature degeneracy. The pedestal-independent line-width and CMB bounds are floors below the holographic value α = 2 / 3 , consistent with and excluding no part of the imaging window reported for GRB 221009A; only the conditional TeV survival bound ( α ≳ 0.74 ), which holds only for near-Planck-refresh foam ( τ foam ≲ 4 × 10 − 28 s ), would exclude it—and, with it, the window. All bounds assume foam that decorrelates within the light’s coherence time; they vanish in the quasistatic or long-temporal-correlation limits.","authors":"A. G. Kim, P. E. Nugent, L. Wang","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/kfn5-km83","id":"doi:10.1103/kfn5-km83","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kfn5-km83","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Inelastic scattering effects on attenuation of boosted dark matter","summary":"Earth attenuation is crucial for interpreting direct-detection constraints on boosted dark matter (DM), since scatterings with terrestrial nuclei can significantly modify the flux and energy spectrum reaching underground detectors. At boosted energies, inelastic nuclear channels beyond ordinary elastic scattering can become relevant, including quasi-elastic scattering, deep-inelastic scattering, and resonant scattering. In this work, we incorporate the resonant scattering of boosted dark matter (DM) off nuclei into the Earth-attenuation framework, in combination with the elastic, quasielastic, and deep-inelastic channels. We find that, in the heavy-mediator regime, resonant scattering can give a non-negligible contribution to the attenuation of boosted DM. Using the latest PandaX-4T data, we derive new constraints on the spin-independent boosted DM-nucleon cross section σ ¯ n .","authors":"Guanhua Gu, Liangliang Su, Lei Wu, Jin Min Yang","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/439q-t2h3","id":"doi:10.1103/439q-t2h3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/439q-t2h3","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Spin-resolved decay of axionlike particles into electron-positron pairs in strong electromagnetic fields","summary":"We investigate the spin-resolved decay of an axionlike particle (ALP) into an electron-positron pair in an intense laser field. Using the Baier–Katkov quasiclassical operator formalism in a constant crossed field, we derive a compact analytic rate retaining finite ALP-mass effects and the lepton spin degrees of freedom. In the massless limit, the spin-summed rate has the same weak- and strong-field asymptotic scalings as the corresponding photon-induced pair-creation rate, while the pseudoscalar coupling induces distinct spin-resolved channels and spin correlations. A finite ALP mass reorganizes the spectrum across the vacuum threshold, producing purely field-induced pair creation below threshold and spin-dependent oscillatory modulations above threshold through the coherent interplay of vacuum and field-assisted contributions. The entanglement of the produced pair reflects the dominant pair-creation mechanism. After the emission angles have been traced out, the reduced spin state near the vacuum threshold in weak fields is nearly maximally entangled and singletlike. In the strong-field regime, it becomes tripletlike while retaining a concurrence approaching 1 / 2 , whereas deep in the vacuum-dominated above-threshold regime it becomes tripletlike and separable. These results establish spin-resolved spectra and entanglement as characteristic signatures of finite-mass and threshold effects in strong-field ALP decay.","authors":"Xiaodan Mao, Yue-Yue Chen, Pei-Lun He","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/64qx-5xts","id":"doi:10.1103/64qx-5xts","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/64qx-5xts","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Quantum simulation of QCD in axial gauge","summary":"We study quantum simulation of SU(3) non-Abelian gauge theory dynamically coupled with fundamental fermions in 3 + 1 dimensions by employing the lattice Hamiltonian in axial gauge under fixed boundary conditions, which avoids Gauss’s law constraints. The temporal component of the gauge field is analytically solved in terms of independent field degrees of freedom and a lattice regulated Green’s function. The axial gauge condition is trivially maintained in time evolution, even under Trotterization. The gauge field degrees of freedom are expressed in the local field basis and can be efficiently transformed into the canonical conjugate momentum basis by local quantum Fourier transforms. We prove the number of qubits needed for describing all states up to an energy E with an accuracy ε on a lattice of volume V at bare coupling g is bounded as 16 n A V + 12 n f V , where n A ≈ log 2 ( 64 E ′ V 4 / 3 π 2 ε + 32 2 g n f E ′ 1 / 2 V 7 / 6 3 π 3 ε 1 / 2 ) is the number of qubits needed for each independent gauge field per site with a shifted energy E ′ , and n f denotes the number of fermion flavors. We then analyze a quantum algorithm for time evolution that is based on Trotterization, quantum Fourier transform, and Jordan-Wigner transformation, for which quantum circuits can be explicitly constructed under arbitrary gauge field truncation and digitization. We find the numbers of and single-qubit rotation gates scale as O ( n A 4 V 4 / 3 ) + O ( V 5 / 3 ) and O ( n A 4 V 4 / 3 ) , respectively, per Trotter step for fixed n f ≤ 6 . Together with the bounded Trotterization error, we conclude that quantum resources needed for simulating real-time dynamics of lattice QCD scale polynomially with volume, energy, time, accuracy, and bare Hamiltonian parameters.","authors":"Xiaojun Yao","topics":["computing","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/b4d3-cr4q","id":"doi:10.1103/b4d3-cr4q","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/b4d3-cr4q","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Investigating three-body resonances in α α Ω / Ω c c c clusters within the Ω / Ω c c c 9 Be nucleus","summary":"We investigate the bound and resonant states of the α + α + Ω and α + α + Ω c c c three-body systems, corresponding to Be Ω 9 and Be Ω c c c 9 , within the Gaussian expansion method combined with the complex scaling method. The α Ω and α Ω c c c interactions are constructed by folding the N Ω and N Ω c c c potentials obtained from lattice QCD calculations by the HAL QCD Collaboration with the nucleon density distribution of the α particle. The uncertainty associated with the α -particle matter radius is also examined. For the Ω sector, the strong N Ω attraction generates deeply bound 0 1 + , 2 1 + , and 4 1 + states in Be Ω 9 , accompanied by a pronounced contraction of the α α core, demonstrating a strong gluelike effect of the Ω baryon. An unconventional inversion between the 0 1 + and 4 1 + levels is also predicted. In contrast, the Ω c c c baryon produces considerably weaker attraction: the 0 1 + state of Be Ω c c c 9 is weakly bound, whereas the 2 1 + and 4 1 + states remain resonances. Their resonance energies and widths exhibit a clear dependence on the strength of the α Ω c c c interaction. These results reveal qualitatively different gluelike behaviors of the Ω and Ω c c c baryons and provide predictions for the spectroscopy of exotic multistrange and triply charmed hypernuclei.","authors":"Hao Zhou, Xiang Liu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/cmcj-bj3r","id":"doi:10.1103/cmcj-bj3r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/cmcj-bj3r","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Probing SU(2) quark flavor asymmetry with W bosons at RHIC","summary":"This work examines the effects of multiple parton radiation on proton-proton W ± boson production at the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider, focusing on the ratio of charged lepton pseudorapidity differential cross sections to test the SU(2) flavor symmetry violation in the proton quark sea. I show that the measured ratio is marginally affected by the fiducial constraints imposed on the QCD recoil radiation by the STAR experiment, and that the ratio is also stable with respect to transverse momentum resummation effects. Hence, the STAR data on the W + / W − cross section ratio provide a robust discrimination of d ¯ ( x ) and u ¯ ( x ) parton distribution functions (PDFs) at momentum fractions of order 0.1, shown by comparing predictions with various PDF models and using the L 2 sensitivity analysis and reweighting methods.","authors":"Maximiliano Ponce-Chavez","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/5nbm-pc3w","id":"doi:10.1103/5nbm-pc3w","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5nbm-pc3w","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Magnetic moments of decuplet baryons in isospin asymmetric magnetized strange matter","summary":"We investigate the in-medium masses and magnetic moments of decuplet baryons ( Δ , Σ * , Ξ * , Ω − ) in isospin asymmetric magnetized strange matter at finite temperature within a unified chiral effective framework. Medium modifications of baryons are implemented using the chiral SU(3) quark mean-field (CQMF) model, where constituent quarks interact via scalar ( σ , ζ , δ ) and vector ( ω , ρ , ϕ ) meson fields considering the Dirac sea effects. The external magnetic field is incorporated through Landau quantization of charged particles together with anomalous magnetic moments of baryons. The resulting in-medium mass of constituent quarks and decuplet baryons obtained from the CQMF model are subsequently employed as inputs to the chiral constituent quark model ( χ CQM ) to evaluate magnetic moments of baryons. Our results provide a systematic understanding of how dense, hot, and magnetized environments influence the magnetic properties of decuplet baryons.","authors":"Hrishika P, Manpreet Kaur, Suneel Dutt, Harleen Dahiya, Arvind Kumar","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/rrlx-3jmj","id":"doi:10.1103/rrlx-3jmj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/rrlx-3jmj","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Quartic scalar clouds on fixed Kerr backgrounds","summary":"We study stationary nonlinear clouds of a complex scalar field on fixed Kerr and Schwarzschild backgrounds, focusing on an effective quartic model with an attractive self-interaction and an unbounded potential. The quartic model is used as a minimal framework to isolate the effect of the leading attractive nonlinear interaction, and we compare its solutions with those obtained for a bounded, axion-inspired cosine potential. On Kerr backgrounds we find finite-amplitude Q-clouds satisfying the synchronization condition ω = m Ω H . They occupy two-dimensional regions of the ( Ω H , M K ) plane and reduce to the corresponding linear clouds as the scalar field amplitude tends to zero. The quartic domain can continue to Ω H = 0 , where both the synchronized frequency and the Noether charge vanish and the solutions become static nonlinear scalar clouds on a fixed Schwarzschild background. We also compute the existence domain for a normalized, axion-inspired cosine potential.","authors":"Hendrik Mennenga","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/nlhb-8vfz","id":"doi:10.1103/nlhb-8vfz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nlhb-8vfz","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Probing velocity-dependent WIMP dark matter annihilation in large-scale structure","summary":"","authors":"Wen-Qing Guo","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/gz4j-kjnc","id":"doi:10.1103/gz4j-kjnc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gz4j-kjnc","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Resolving the twofold ambiguity in τ momentum reconstruction via a self-consistent weighting method","summary":"The reconstruction of τ lepton momenta in e + e − → τ + τ − processes suffers from a twofold kinematic ambiguity due to undetected neutrinos. We show that the common practice of retaining both solutions with equal weight systematically biases the extracted angular coefficients: the bias grows with the size of the cos 2 θ modulation of the production distribution f ( cos θ ) , but remains resolvable even in the nearly uniform QED limit at the ψ ( 2 S ) threshold. We propose a self-consistent weighting method based on a fixed-point theorem: assigning normalized weights w i = f ( cos θ i ) / [ f ( cos θ 1 ) + f ( cos θ 2 ) ] yields a weighted distribution h ( x ) = f ( x ) [ 1 + ϵ ( x ) ] with ϵ ( x ) ≪ 1 under a detailed balance condition. Monte Carlo simulations at s = 3.686 GeV and 4.8 GeV show that the fixed-point iteration converges to the true angular coefficient α within statistical uncertainty, providing a robust framework for angular analysis without event-by-event ambiguity resolution.","authors":"Rong-Gang Ping","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/snpk-2vvt","id":"doi:10.1103/snpk-2vvt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/snpk-2vvt","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Avoided crossings and resonances in black hole ringdown with coupled fields: A case study of the Einstein-Maxwell-axion system","summary":"","authors":"Takuya Takahashi, Hayato Motohashi, Kazufumi Takahashi","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/yhls-52r2","id":"doi:10.1103/yhls-52r2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/yhls-52r2","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Quantum computation of the mass gap in an asymptotically free theory","summary":"","authors":"Paulo F. Bedaque, Edison M. Murairi, Gautam Rupak, Valery S. Simonyan","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ktgp-g8mx","id":"doi:10.1103/ktgp-g8mx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ktgp-g8mx","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Primordial quark stars made of a long-lived false vacuum","summary":"","authors":"Jingdong Shao, Mei Huang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ym82-2n13","id":"doi:10.1103/ym82-2n13","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ym82-2n13","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Design of ALPHA phase I: A plasma haloscope for 10-20 GHz postinflation axions","summary":"","authors":"Xiran Bai, Rustam Balafendiev, Sean E. Barrett, Eunice Beato, Pavel Belov, Charles D. Brown, Eduardo A. Castro Muñoz, Jan Conrad, Marcel Demarteau, Alex Droster, Joseph Dubois, Jonathan Echevers, Ali Elhadi, Jim Enriquez, Maryam Haytham Esmat, Andrea Gallo Rosso, Eleanor Graham, Chloe Greenstein, Jon E. Gudmundsson, Karsten M. Heeger et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/3v7h-7dx9","id":"doi:10.1103/3v7h-7dx9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/3v7h-7dx9","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Temporal Complexity Hierarchies in Solvable Quantum Many-Body Dynamics","summary":"The influence matrix (IM) provides a powerful framework for characterizing nonequilibrium quantum many-body dynamics by encoding multitime correlations into tensor-network states. Understanding how its computational complexity relates to underlying dynamics is crucial for both theoretical insight and practical utility yet remains largely unexplored despite a few case studies. Here, we address this question for a family of brickwork quantum circuits ranging from integrable to chaotic regimes. Using tools from geometric group theory, we identify three qualitatively distinct scalings of temporal entanglement entropy, establishing a hierarchy of computational resources required for accurate tensor-network representations of the IM for these models. We further analyze the memory structure of the IM and distinguish between classical and quantum temporal correlations. In particular, for certain examples, we identify effectively classical IMs that admit an efficient Monte Carlo algorithm for computing multitime correlations. In more generic settings without an explicit classical description of the IM, we introduce an operational measure of quantum memory with an experimental protocol and discuss examples exhibiting long-time genuinely quantum correlations. Our results establish a new connection between quantum many-body dynamics and group theory, providing fresh insights into the complexity of the IM and its intricate connection to the physical characteristics of the dynamics.","authors":"He-Ran Wang, Ilya Vilkoviskiy, Dmitry A. Abanin","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/92c4-g7qs","id":"doi:10.1103/92c4-g7qs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/92c4-g7qs","published":"2026-11-13","title":"A Guide to Higher-Order Quantum Operations","summary":"Many complex quantum phenomena require a description that goes beyond the standard paradigm of state preparations, evolutions, and measurements. A suitable framework for tackling such scenarios is that of , i.e., quantum operations that transform quantum operations. These objects are fundamental to modern quantum theory, and recent years have seen a proliferation of technical and conceptual results that fundamentally rely on them; they naturally emerge in quantum circuit architectures, correlated open dynamics, and investigations of quantum causality, to name but a few fields of application. In this tutorial, we provide a pedagogical introduction to higher-order quantum operations: we offer the tools to work with them in various contexts and firmly embed them within the current research landscape. Through this approach, our intent is to enable multiple points of access to the field, highlight the interconnectedness of distinct areas, and provide the means to meaningfully engage with state-of-the-art research developments. We conclude by identifying open problems and future research directions in this rapidly evolving field.","authors":"Philip Taranto, Simon Milz, Mio Murao, Marco Túlio Quintino, Kavan Modi","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/bgbx-ph24","id":"doi:10.1103/bgbx-ph24","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bgbx-ph24","published":"2026-11-13","title":"High-Performance Spin-State Memory for Telecom Quantum Information Applications","summary":"Optical quantum memories based on Er, with its telecom-compatible optical transition, could benefit quantum networking and linear optics quantum computing applications, if the requirements for high memory efficiency, fidelity, and storage capacity can be met simultaneously. Here, we demonstrate the first high efficiency memory in Er, with spin-state storage for 25 μ s and classical efficiencies up to 81%, using the rephased amplified spontaneous emission protocol. We show quantum storage along with high-fidelity entanglement of an output state and the state stored in memory, and a demonstration of multiplexing, storing 70 modes in the temporal domain. We use the results to discuss how, with modifications to the pulse sequences and experimental geometry only, the performance can be improved to meet the requirements for practical quantum applications.","authors":"James Stuart, Kieran Smith, Morgan Hedges, Rose Ahlefeldt, Matthew Sellars","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/6tt6-vpf2","id":"doi:10.1103/6tt6-vpf2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6tt6-vpf2","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Autonomous multi-ion optical clock with on-chip integrated photonic light delivery","summary":"Integrated photonics in trapped-ion systems are critical for applications such as portable optical atomic clocks and scalable quantum computers. System-level integration of all required functionalities remains a key challenge. In this work, we demonstrate an autonomously operating optical clock having a short-term frequency instability of 3.14 ( 5 ) × 10 − 14 / τ using an ensemble of four Yb 171 + ions trapped in a multisite surface-electrode trap at room temperature. All clock operations are performed with light delivered via on-chip waveguides. We showcase the system’s resilience through sustained, autonomous operation featuring automated ion shuttling and reloading to mitigate ion loss during interleaved clock measurements. This work paves the way beyond component-level functionality to establish a viable and robust architecture for the next generation of portable, multi-ion quantum sensors and computers.","authors":"Tharon D. Morrison, Joonhyuk Kwon, Matthew A. Delaney, Michael Gehl, David R. Leibrandt, Daniel Stick, Hayden J. McGuinness","topics":["sensing","computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/8wmd-2s5q","id":"doi:10.1103/8wmd-2s5q","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8wmd-2s5q","published":"2026-11-13","title":"Atomic density optimization of quasi-one-dimensional diffuse laser cooling under a two-dimensional magnetic-field gradient","summary":"Diffuse laser cooling (DLC) provides a compact, robust, and scalable approach to generating cold-atom ensembles, offering significant potential for ground-based and spaceborne microwave atomic clocks. However, the performance of DLC systems remains limited by the weak atom-light interaction arising from their relatively low atomic density. This limitation is characterized by the optical density (OD), which quantifies the effective interaction strength between the atomic ensemble and the probe light and strongly influences the achievable signal-to-noise ratio. Although quasi-one-dimensional (quasi-1D) DLC extends the probe interaction length and improves atomic utilization, increasing the local atomic density within the interrogation region remains challenging. Here, we demonstrate a magnetically enhanced quasi-1D DLC system that simultaneously maintains the extended interaction length and increases the atomic density through a two-dimensional magnetic-field gradient. By spatially compressing and accumulating atoms near the probe axis, we enhance the OD of a 70-cm-long cold rubidium ensemble from 2.49 to 3.94, representing a 58% enhancement and an equivalent increase in the local atomic density. The dependence of the OD on the magnetic-field gradient, probe intensity, beam size, and cooling-light polarization is systematically investigated. This work establishes a practical pathway for enhancing optical interrogation efficiency and could pave the way for the development of high-performance DLC-based cold-atom clocks and quantum sensing systems.","authors":"Xiaolei Guan, Mengyuan Han, Jia Zhang, Xun Gao, Yu Wang, Wei Zhuang, Tiantian Shi, Jingbiao Chen","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-12":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/l5j1-7b4d","id":"doi:10.1103/l5j1-7b4d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/l5j1-7b4d","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Long-Range and Short-Range Longitudinal Flow Decorrelations in the Quark-Gluon Plasma","summary":"","authors":"Jiangyong Jia, Shengli Huang, Chunjian Zhang, Somadutta Bhatta","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/dgdr-mx1d","id":"doi:10.1103/dgdr-mx1d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dgdr-mx1d","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Is the Large Uncertainty of δ C P Fundamentally Encoded in the Neutrino Quantum State?","summary":"The precise measurement of the leptonic -violating phase δ C P remains one of the major open challenges in neutrino physics, as current experiments achieve only very limited accuracy. We address this issue through the lens of quantum estimation theory. A distinctive feature of neutrino oscillation experiments is that they cannot freely optimize the probe or measurement, since both are constrained by the production and detection of flavor eigenstates. We therefore examine whether the large uncertainty in δ C P originates from intrinsic properties—either of the neutrino quantum state or of flavor measurements—or if it instead stems from experimental limitations. By comparing quantum and classical Fisher information, we demonstrate that the limited sensitivity to δ C P originates primarily from the information content of flavor measurements. Furthermore, we show that, while targeting the second oscillation maximum, as in the ESS ν SB proposal, typically enhances δ C P information, this feature in fact strongly depends on the actual values of δ C P .","authors":"Michela Ignoti, Claudia Frugiuele, Matteo G. A. Paris, Marco G. Genoni","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/6lkb-18jb","id":"doi:10.1103/6lkb-18jb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6lkb-18jb","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Axion magnetohydrodynamics and reconnection-driven axion bursts","summary":"","authors":"Hugo Terças","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/vyqz-mtmc","id":"doi:10.1103/vyqz-mtmc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vyqz-mtmc","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Continuous-time evolution via probabilistic angle interpolation and its applications","summary":"We explore the applicability of a stochastic time-evolution algorithm based on probabilistic angle interpolation. To simplify the preprocessing of the algorithm, we take the continuous-time limit, thereby explicitly eliminating Trotter errors and streamlining the resource analysis. We also introduce a noise-mitigation method tailored to it. As demonstrations, we apply the algorithm to two representative problems: estimating the ground-state energy of the H 3 + molecular Hamiltonian and computing out-of-time-ordered correlators in the sparse Sachdev-Ye-Kitaev model. We evaluate the protocol's performance through numerical simulations and experiments on a trapped-ion quantum computer, Quantinuum Reimei.","authors":"Tomoya Hayata, Yuta Kikuchi","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/28zn-ch8y","id":"doi:10.1103/28zn-ch8y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/28zn-ch8y","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Exact quantum speed limits for quantum gates from space curve geometry","summary":"","authors":"Hunter Nelson, Edwin Barnes","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/nrf9-rx7w","id":"doi:10.1103/nrf9-rx7w","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nrf9-rx7w","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Spatial symmetry in few-photon interference and quantum metrology","summary":"","authors":"Éloi Descamps, Pérola Milman","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/5yrq-qjkh","id":"doi:10.1103/5yrq-qjkh","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5yrq-qjkh","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Beyond the lowest Landau level: Unlocking more robust fractional states using flat Chern bands with higher vortexability","summary":"Enhancing the many-body gap of a fractional state is crucial for realizing robust fractional excitations. For fractional Chern insulators, existing studies suggest that making flat Chern bands closely resemble the lowest Landau level (LLL) seems to maximize the excitation gap, providing an apparently optimal platform. In this work, we demonstrate that from the LLL limit can, in fact, produce substantially larger fractional quantum Hall gaps. Using moiré flat bands with strongly non-Landau-level wave functions, we show that the gap can exceed that of the LLL by more than two orders of magnitude for short-range interactions and by factors of two to three for long-range interactions. This enhancement is generic across Abelian fractional Chern insulator states and follows a universal enhancement factor within each hierarchy. In the Landau-level framework, we identify the amplification of pseudopotentials as the microscopic origin of the observed enhancement. More generally, in a Chern band system where the pseudopotential decomposition is not well defined, we find a sharp scaling distinction: in the strongly screened regime, the ν = 1 / 3 gap scales cubically with gate distance d s in the LLL and vortexable Chern bands, but linearly in higher-vortexable Chern bands. The activation of this leading O ( d s ) term by deviating from ideal quantum geometry is the key origin of the gap enhancement, and this criterion remains meaningful even without rotational symmetry. We further examined non-Abelian states and found that within finite-size resolution, this wave-function construction method can also be used to manipulate and enhance the gap for certain interaction parameters. We further discuss experimental implications of our findings in twisted bilayer MoTe 2 .","authors":"Yitong Zhang, Siddhartha Sarkar, Xiaohan Wan, Daniel E. Parker, Shi-Zeng Lin, Kai Sun","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/x5ym-1w9j","id":"doi:10.1103/x5ym-1w9j","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/x5ym-1w9j","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Signatures of Gaussian superconducting fluctuations in nonlocal noise magnetometry","summary":"","authors":"Dror Orgad","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/265q-w4rw","id":"doi:10.1103/265q-w4rw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/265q-w4rw","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Kinetically coupled dark matter condensates","summary":"","authors":"Michael W. Toomey, Savvas M. Koushiappas, Stephon Alexander","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/651s-h6v3","id":"doi:10.1103/651s-h6v3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/651s-h6v3","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Constraining self-interacting scalar field dark matter with strong gravitational lensing in cluster scale?","summary":"","authors":"Raquel Galazo García, Eric Jullo, Emmanuel Nezri, Marceau Limousin","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/l2xp-jqxt","id":"doi:10.1103/l2xp-jqxt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/l2xp-jqxt","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Baryon enhancement in jets","summary":"The enhancement of the baryon production relative to mesons in small-collision systems is considered a breakthrough result of the Large Hadron Collider since a similar effect in heavy-ion collisions is understood by invoking the formation of the strongly interacting quark-gluon plasma. In this paper, a baryon enhancement is reported for p T ch , jet > 15 GeV / c jets produced in pp collisions at s = 13 TeV simulated with ythia8. The effect can be explained as a transition between quark-initiated jets (low jet multiplicities) to gluon-initiated jets (high jet multiplicities). The present result contributes to a deeper understanding of the multiplicity dependence of baryon enhancement across different collision systems.","authors":"Antonio Ortiz, Róbert Vértesi","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/3d7g-l1t4","id":"doi:10.1103/3d7g-l1t4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/3d7g-l1t4","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Ω c baryon spectrum and strong decays in a constituent quark model","summary":"In this work, we study the Ω c baryon spectrum up to the 2 P excitations within a semirelativistic constituent quark potential model, where the mixing between different configurations with the same spin-parity numbers is dynamically considered. Furthermore, the strong decay properties for the excited Ω c states are evaluated within an improved chiral quark model by including the relativistic correction term. In a unified framework, we provide a reasonable explanation of the widths, masses, and mass splittings, for the newly observed Ω c resonances Ω c ( 3000 ) , Ω c ( 3050 ) , Ω c ( 3065 ) , Ω c ( 3090 ) , Ω c ( 3120 ) , Ω c ( 3185 ) , and Ω c ( 3327 ) . It is found that the configuration mixing is crucial for understanding the strong decay properties and mass splittings, while the relativistic correction term of the strong transition operator plays an important role in the states dominated by the radial excitations. We expect our study can provide useful references for establishing a more abundant Ω c spectrum.","authors":"Hui-Hua Zhong, Ming-Sheng Liu, Li-Ye Xiao, Kai-Lei Wang, Qi Li, Xian-Hui Zhong","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/q8tn-gq1b","id":"doi:10.1103/q8tn-gq1b","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/q8tn-gq1b","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Updated constraints on a long-lived X 17 vector boson","summary":"Since its first observation by the ATOMKI experiment in 2018, the *Be anomaly has attracted considerable interest within the dark sector community as it may indicate the existence of a new fundamental particle with a mass of about 16.9 MeV, the X 17 . However, the minimal model describing X 17 as a new vector boson is severely constrained by null results from legacy beam-dump experiments. Among these, the E141 experiment at SLAC places stringent limits on the X 17 coupling to electrons ϵ e in the 5.1 × 10 − 5 ≲ ϵ e ≲ 1.7 × 10 − 4 region. This excludes the possibility of a long-lived X 17 , potentially in contrast with the preliminary estimate of the particle lifetime recently reported by the ATOMKI Collaboration. The E141 limits commonly adopted in the literature rely on reinterpretations of the original analysis under solid but simplifying assumptions. While these studies provide a reliable estimate of the experiment’s reach, they rely on approximations that were well justified when the boson mass was largely unconstrained. With the X 17 mass now confined to a narrow region by recent experimental observations, a more refined treatment of the E141 sensitivity becomes necessary. In this work, we revisit the E141 exclusion limits in the X 17 scenario by performing a dedicated reanalysis that incorporates a more accurate treatment of the experimental setup and signal prediction. We quantify the impact of these refinements on the excluded parameter space and discuss their implications for the compatibility between the E141 constraints and the vector-boson interpretation of the ATOMKI anomalies.","authors":"A. Celentano, A. Marini, L. Marsicano","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/53ns-mxv3","id":"doi:10.1103/53ns-mxv3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/53ns-mxv3","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Enhanced cosmic-ray cooling in AGN from dark matter deep inelastic scattering","summary":"The diffusion of high-energy cosmic rays (CRs) through the dark matter (DM) spikes of active galactic nuclei entails significant energy loss via interactions with DM. While previous studies of sub-GeV DM have focused on elastic scattering, this process becomes insufficient at higher proton energies and DM masses. In this work, we investigate the CR-DM deep inelastic scattering (DIS) as mediated by a vector portal. We calculate the DIS contribution to the CR energy loss rate and derive stringent exclusion limits on the CR-DM scattering cross section for DM masses between 10 − 6 GeV and 1 GeV. For higher CR energies and mediator masses, the resulting CR cooling timescales are reduced by orders of magnitude after involving the DIS contribution, producing stringent constraints that surpass most of current experimental limits.","authors":"Linjie Li, Chih-Ting Lu, Arvind Kumar Mishra, Liangliang Su, Lei Wu","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1vbs-h2h2","id":"doi:10.1103/1vbs-h2h2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1vbs-h2h2","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Universal seesaw mechanism for fermion masses and strong C P problem","summary":"We propose a framework for fermion mass generation based on a universal seesaw. The Standard Model is extended by an Abelian gauge symmetry acting on right-handed fermions, together with vectorlike fermions and scalar fields. The ordinary Yukawa couplings are forbidden, except for the top-quark coupling to the Higgs, which is allowed at the renormalizable level and remains unsuppressed. The charged-fermion hierarchies then arise from mixing with the vectorlike sector, and light neutrino masses emerge from a neutral sector Majorana seesaw. C P is exact in the ultraviolet and broken spontaneously by scalar vacuum expectation values. The resulting C P -violating phase is transmitted to the quark sector and generates the Cabibbo-Kobyashi-Maskawa (CKM) phase, while a Nelson-Barr structure, realized through the universal seesaw block form, keeps the physical QCD vacuum angle zero at tree level. Consistency with electric dipole moment (EDM) bounds beyond tree level requires moderately suppressed Yukawa couplings between SM doublets to the vectorlike sector. If the leading higher-dimensional operators are unsuppressed, the same requirement can favor a low breaking scale for the new Abelian symmetry. In this regime the vectorlike fermions can lie at the TeV scale, with suppressed mixing with the electroweak sector. This framework provides a simple setting in which the hierarchies of charged-fermion masses, neutrino masses, and C P -violating parameters can be accounted for within a common extension of the Standard Model.","authors":"Pavel Fileviez Pérez, Clara Murgui","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8gny-jmm4","id":"doi:10.1103/8gny-jmm4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8gny-jmm4","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Absorption spectrum and greybody factors of charged black holes in loop quantum gravity","summary":"","authors":"Marco A. A. de Paula, Valdir B. Bezerra, Luiz C. S. Leite","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/wny1-xklc","id":"doi:10.1103/wny1-xklc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wny1-xklc","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Reflection polarization of close binaries as a probe of axion dark matter birefringence","summary":"","authors":"Tomoki Matsuoka, Kimihiro Nomura, Hidetoshi Omiya","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dtls-gx2m","id":"doi:10.1103/dtls-gx2m","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dtls-gx2m","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Mixing-suppressed inelastic dark matter: A minimal model for the LZ 248 keV event","summary":"","authors":"Seung J. Lee, Taewook Youn","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/jgdt-d61y","id":"doi:10.1103/jgdt-d61y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jgdt-d61y","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Realization of a Cavity-Coupled Rydberg Array","summary":"Scalable quantum computers and quantum networks require the combination of quantum processing nodes with efficient light-matter interfaces to distribute quantum information in local or long-distance quantum networks. Neutral-atom arrays have both been coupled to Rydberg states to enable high-fidelity quantum gates in universal processing architectures, and to optical cavities to realize interfaces to photons. However, combining these two capabilities and coupling atom arrays to highly excited Rydberg states in the mode of an optical cavity has been an outstanding challenge. Here we present a novel cavity-coupled Rydberg array that achieves this long-standing goal. We prepare, detect, and control individual atoms in a scalable optical tweezer array, couple them strongly to the optical mode of a high-finesse optical cavity and excite them in a controlled way to Rydberg states. We show that high-cooperativity coupling to an optical cavity—demonstrated via the dispersive shift of the resonance of the cavity in presence of the atoms—and strong Rydberg interactions—demonstrated via the collective enhancement of Rydberg coupling in the atomic array—can be achieved in our setup at the same spatial location. Our presented experimental platform opens the path to several new directions, including the realization of quantum network nodes, quantum simulation of long-range interacting, open quantum systems, and photonic-state engineering leveraging high-fidelity Rydberg control.","authors":"Jacopo De Santis, Balázs Dura-Kovács, Mehmet Öncü, Adrien Bouscal, Dimitrios Vasileiadis, Johannes Zeiher","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/1m4s-kky2","id":"doi:10.1103/1m4s-kky2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1m4s-kky2","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Extracting Information from a Superradiant Burst Using Simple Measurements","summary":"It is well known that superradiant decay of an ensemble of N spins generates a complex non-classical state of light. Here, we consider the estimation of a phase encoded in the initial spin state from a superradiant burst of photons: how is information about this phase distributed among the emitted photons and can the phase be estimated using simple measurements? Despite the complexity of the photonic burst state, we show that a simple homodyne measurement combined with an optimized filter and linear estimator recovers the N -scaling of the quantum Fisher information of the initial spin state (including cases exhibiting N 2 Heisenberg scaling). Even more surprising, the temporal mode with optimal information content contains a vanishing fraction of the total emitted photons in the large- N limit, suggesting an effective compression of phase information. Our results and setup represent a new approach to quantum metrology for spin systems, in that they enable cavity-based readout of solid-state spin ensembles that utilizes resonant spin-photon interactions.","authors":"Federico Belliardo, Anjun Chu, Martin Koppenhöfer, Aashish A. Clerk","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/dwjs-7p9k","id":"doi:10.1103/dwjs-7p9k","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dwjs-7p9k","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Noise-protected two-qubit gate using anisotropic exchange interaction","summary":"Hole spin qubits hosted in germanium quantum dots are promising candidates for scalable quantum computing. The strong spin-orbit interaction can enable fast and all-electrical quantum control. Furthermore, the platform can implement universal quantum control using only baseband signals, which may mitigate the impact of crosstalk and microwave-induced heating. At the same time, spin-orbit interaction gives rise to an anisotropic exchange interaction, whose potential for implementing two-qubit gates has remained largely unexplored. However, the current performance of operating a hole-based quantum computer is mostly limited by dephasing due to low-frequency charge noise. In this work, we propose a two-qubit gate protocol for germanium hole spin qubits operated near the gapless regime. This gate protocol exploits the anisotropic exchange interaction between neighboring spins and utilizes a composite-pulse scheme implemented solely through electrical baseband signals. Using this approach, we predict high-fidelity two-qubit controlled- Z operations that can suppress exchange-energy fluctuations, offering a pathway toward fault-tolerant semiconductor quantum processors.","authors":"Zizheng Wu, Maximilian Rimbach-Russ","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/1sv5-qzbz","id":"doi:10.1103/1sv5-qzbz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1sv5-qzbz","published":"2026-11-12","title":"Quantum metrological advantage of high-order squeezed states","summary":"Quantum correlations can be harnessed to improve the precision in parameter estimation beyond classical capabilities. Under a standard interferometric or rotation protocol, it is well established that the optimal single-mode Gaussian state is a standard squeezed vacuum, which enables Heisenberg-limited precision. In this work, we investigate the potential metrological advantage of two distinct families involving high-order squeezing, namely, m th -phase and multisqueezed states. Our results show that these non-Gaussian states can grant a significant metrological advantage with respect to the optimal squeezed vacuum under equivalent conditions, i.e., at equal occupations. Their advantage holds at both low and large occupations, but its behavior critically depends on the chosen family of high-order squeezing. While higher squeezing orders enhance the advantage, this comes at the cost of higher-order observables in the measurement for full metrological performance. Finally, we study their robustness to standard decoherence channels, i.e., pure dephasing and zero-temperature damping. Employing standard squeezing as reference state, our results indicate a reasonable robustness against damping up to a certain noise strength, while their metrological advantage becomes fragile under pure dephasing. Our work shows the potential enhancement in quantum metrology beyond Gaussian states, carefully detailing the main challenges and limitations.","authors":"Rubén Gordillo-Hachuel, Erik Torrontegui, Cristina de Dios, Ricardo Puebla","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-10":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/7cjk-63m2","id":"doi:10.1103/7cjk-63m2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7cjk-63m2","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Emergent-Gravity Hall Effect from Quantum Geometry","summary":"","authors":"Hiroki Yoshida, Takehito Yokoyama","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/65r9-lxc7","id":"doi:10.1103/65r9-lxc7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/65r9-lxc7","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Pair size versus coherence length and quantum geometry in the spin-orbit coupled BCS-BEC crossover","summary":"","authors":"R. N. Kalkan, M. Iskin","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/5clr-mhwn","id":"doi:10.1103/5clr-mhwn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5clr-mhwn","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Statistical benchmarking of optimization methods for a variational quantum eigensolver under quantum noise","summary":"","authors":"Silvie Illésová, Tomáš Bezdĕk, Vojtĕch Novák, Bruno Senjean, Martin Beseda","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8d2s-t5b9","id":"doi:10.1103/8d2s-t5b9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8d2s-t5b9","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Revisiting curvature corrections for field theory cosmic strings","summary":"We present a combined analytical and numerical study of the effective action of field theory cosmic strings in the Abelian-Higgs model in flat space. Starting directly from the underlying solitonic field theory description, we provide a systematic derivation of the low energy effective action and present evidence for the absence of nontrivial curvature correction terms when only the translational Goldstone modes are retained. Using this framework, we extend the effective theory to include higher energy fluctuations of the soliton profile, which map to massive degrees of freedom propagating on the worldsheet. We show that the leading curvature contribution enters only through the coupling between these massive modes and the worldsheet Ricci scalar. We validate the resulting effective theory via lattice simulations of the full field theory equations of motion in flat space, implemented with adaptive mesh refinement to capture the string dynamics across different scales. The numerical simulations confirm the dynamics obtained using the effective action in its validity range. Furthermore, they also demonstrate the existence of the predicted parametric instability of excited strings that drives the transfer of energy from massive excitations to the Goldstone sector.","authors":"Josu C. Aurrekoetxea, Jose J. Blanco-Pillado, Alberto García Martín-Caro, J. M. Queiruga","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/72mz-x21q","id":"doi:10.1103/72mz-x21q","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/72mz-x21q","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Deep-learning-based low-energy trigger algorithms for the Hyper-Kamiokande experiment","summary":"Modern machine learning techniques have become increasingly important in particle physics because of their powerful pattern-recognition capabilities, including in real-time data acquisition where stringent runtime constraints apply. This paper details the performance of deep-learning-based trigger algorithms for a large water Cherenkov detector such as Hyper-Kamiokande, aimed at low-energy neutrino events (below 7 MeV). The performance of custom neural-network supervised classifiers is shown alongside two anomaly-detection approaches trained solely on detector noise: a pure autoencoder and a model based on manifold projection-diffusion recovery. The supervised model shows signal identification efficiencies of 76.7% for single electrons of 3 MeV kinetic energy, significantly exceeding signal efficiencies obtained from a traditional hit-count-based trigger of 26.4%, while the manifold projection-diffusion recovery approach reaches 35.4% at the same operating point. Runtime evaluations on graphical processing unit yield per-window inference latencies well below the millisecond scale.","authors":"Katharina Lachner, Saúl Alonso-Monsalve, Benjamin Richards, Davide Sgalaberna","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2dyh-j2gw","id":"doi:10.1103/2dyh-j2gw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2dyh-j2gw","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Constraining inhomogeneous energy injection from annihilating dark matter and primordial black holes with 21-cm cosmology","summary":"The cosmic dawn 21-cm signal is a highly sensitive probe of any process which injects energy into the intergalactic medium, enabling novel searches for anomalous energy injection through dark matter interactions. In addition to modifying the global 21-cm signal, these processes would leave distinct imprints on the frequency-resolved 21-cm power spectrum with a morphology jointly set by the time and spatial dependence of energy emission and absorption. In this work, we extend the DM21 code package, which models the effects of spatially inhomogeneous energy emission and deposition on 21-cm cosmology, to study three well-motivated scenarios which 21-cm measurements are particularly well suited to probe dark matter annihilation through p -wave processes, the Hawking radiation of light primordial black holes, and energetic emission from accreting solar-mass primordial black holes. We project sensitivities to each of these scenarios, demonstrating that leading or near-leading sensitivity to each can be achieved through 21-cm probes. We also make public our updates to the DM21 code package that enable it to accommodate energy injection processes for general continuum spectra with arbitrary spatial and temporal dependence in an accompanying release.","authors":"Yitian Sun, Joshua W. Foster, Julian B. Muñoz","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dds1-t759","id":"doi:10.1103/dds1-t759","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dds1-t759","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Neutrino flavor evolution in high-flux astrophysical environments","summary":"We examine neutrino evolution in astrophysical environments where the neutrino flux is very large, including core-collapse supernovae and neutron star mergers. In these environments, the neutrino-neutrino and neutrino-antineutrino interactions are crucial. We include nonforward scattering of neutrinos and antineutrinos in a semiclassical treatment. Because of the large scale of neutrino momenta (2–10 MeV), the quantum evolution problem can be treated as a sum over incoherent paths in the momentum and flavor of each neutrino. The phases between different neutrinos are essentially random because of the large kinetic terms. Momentum is conserved at each vertex, and important flavor symmetries are retained. Dynamics in the many-body neutrino system enable rapid equilibration in the energy and angular distributions of all flavors, and an equilibration of products of neutrino and antineutrino densities for each flavor at either large or zero background matter density. We also describe the evolution at moderate densities where the mass eigenstates differ for neutrinos and antineutrinos, and with time-varying background matter densities. The evolution maintains relevant symmetries and reduces to standard Mikheyev-Smirnov-Wolfenstein oscillations in the appropriate limits. The rapid equilibration in energy and flavor can significantly impact energy deposition and nucleosynthesis in high-flux astrophysical environments, and potentially flavor energy relations in terrestrial supernovae neutrino observations.","authors":"J. Carlson, A. Roggero, Duff Neill","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/yzmm-t2cs","id":"doi:10.1103/yzmm-t2cs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/yzmm-t2cs","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Insights into one-loop corrections to the neutrino low-scale type-I seesaw mechanism","summary":"The standard type-I seesaw can also be regarded as a low-scale seesaw by using the freedom of the Casas-Ibarra parametrization. In this framework, radiative corrections to the neutrino mass matrix can dominate over the tree-level contribution, so that the tree-level Casas-Ibarra construction no longer solves the equation that determines the physical neutrino masses and mixings. We show how the Casas-Ibarra parametrization can be consistently extended to this loop-corrected equation, by reabsorbing the one-loop corrections into the right-handed neutrino mass matrix, so that the resulting light neutrino mass matrix is consistent with experimental values. On the other hand, we show that physical processes related to right-handed neutrino propagation, such as heavy neutral lepton searches, do not depend on the one-loop corrections. Moreover, we show that Br ( μ → e γ ) provides competitive constraints on the parameter space of heavy neutral lepton search experiments for masses above 100 GeV.","authors":"Gennaro Miele, Stefano Morisi, Eduardo Peinado, Kainat Qamar","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/jrrj-qy9w","id":"doi:10.1103/jrrj-qy9w","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jrrj-qy9w","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Higgs-portal Stueckelberg dark matter","summary":"We study dark photon dark matter X associated with a dark U ( 1 ) X gauge symmetry. To evade laboratory and cosmological constraints on kinetic mixing with the Standard Model U ( 1 ) Y , we assign a Z 2 -odd dark parity to X that forbids such mixing. The leading interactions then arise from gauge-invariant dimension-6 Higgs-portal operators, including both parity-even and parity-odd terms. We assume that the dark matter mass is generated via the Stueckelberg mechanism. In the interacting Stueckelberg EFT, a gauge-invariant dimension-6 parent reduces in unitary gauge to the nominal dimension-4 Higgs-portal interaction ( H † H ) X μ X μ , with its coefficient correlated with m X 2 / Λ 2 , while additional derivative operators are organized by the same longitudinal-mode power counting. We investigate freeze-in production of X from Higgs-pair annihilations after reheating, incorporating both field-strength and Stueckelberg-allowed operators. First, we consider the case in which the Wilson coefficients of the gauge-invariant dimension-6 operators, C and C ˜ , are of order unity. Under a conservative single-cutoff power counting in which the strong-coupling scales of the longitudinal mode lie above the cutoff of the field-strength effective theory, we find that the Stueckelberg contributions remain subdominant in dark matter production. We emphasize that this ordering of scales is an explicit assumption rather than a universal consequence of perturbative unitarity. Next, we explore a more general situation in which C and C ˜ are smaller than unity. In this second case, Stueckelberg-allowed operators can become comparable and generate contributions proportional to T reh 3 , T reh 5 , and T reh 7 , including sign-dependent interference effects. For both cases, we show that there exists a wide parameter space consistent with the observed dark matter relic density.","authors":"Antonio De Felice, Takehiro Ogura, Shinji Tsujikawa, Kimiko Yamashita","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/14wc-dmgf","id":"doi:10.1103/14wc-dmgf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/14wc-dmgf","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Bridging superfluid and nonminimally coupled BEC dark matter through relativistic aquadratic Lagrangian theory","summary":"Motivated by their common condensed-matter inspiration and their shared aim of reconciling MOND-like phenomenology on galactic scales with particle dark matter on larger scales, we investigate the relation between Superfluid Dark Matter (SFDM) and Bose-Einstein Condensate Dark Matter (BECDM). Since SFDM is formulated in the Newtonian regime whereas BECDM is fully relativistic, we first show that the MONDian formulation of SFDM arises as the Newtonian, low-acceleration limit of a RAQUAL theory in the Einstein frame. We then transform its covariant interaction sector to the Jordan frame and compare it with BECDM. The phonon–baryon interaction of SFDM maps onto the BECDM derivative coupling to the Einstein tensor, supplemented by a small nonminimal coupling to the Ricci scalar. The interaction sectors are therefore equivalent up to a linear perturbation of the Einstein–Hilbert term. Their kinetic sectors, however, remain inequivalent: the standard quadratic kinetic term of BECDM cannot be mapped onto the nonanalytic kinetic term required by SFDM. The two models are consequently related but not dynamically equivalent. This mapping provides a covariant interpretation of the SFDM interaction and clarifies which theoretical properties can be transferred between the two frameworks.","authors":"Stefano Liberati, Valentin Pomakov, Samuele Silveravalle","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/6y56-hhgs","id":"doi:10.1103/6y56-hhgs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6y56-hhgs","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Can γ γ collisions rival e − e + in probing doubly charged Higgs bosons?","summary":"High-energy γ γ collisions, realizable as an operational mode of future lepton linear colliders such as the ILC and CLIC, provide a promising environment to probe extended Higgs sectors. We investigate the sensitivity of such colliders to doubly charged Higgs bosons within the 2-Higgs doublet model with type-II seesaw (2HDMcT). Focusing on the three-body production channels γ γ → H ± ± H 1 ∓ H 1 ∓ and γ γ → H ± ± H 1 ∓ W ∓ , we perform a parameter space scan consistent with theoretical constraints as well as current collider, flavor, and electroweak precision observables (EWPOs). We show that γ γ collisions can rival the discovery potential of the conventional e + e − mode for probing doubly charged Higgs bosons through a 4 ℓ + E T signature ( ℓ = e , μ ). Despite the reduced effective luminosity resulting from the photon spectrum, the significantly enhanced production cross sections, exceeding those in electron-positron collisions by more than one order of magnitude, compensate for this limitation. By performing a detailed signal-to-background analysis at center-of-mass energies of s = 830 and 1245 GeV, we demonstrate that a discovery significance at the 5 σ level can be achieved for viable benchmark points (BPs).","authors":"Abdesslam Arhrib, Rachid Benbrik, Mohammed Boukidi, Mohamed Chabab, Khalid Goure, Stefano Moretti","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dv8w-vypf","id":"doi:10.1103/dv8w-vypf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dv8w-vypf","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Electromagnetic form factors of singly charmed baryons Σ c and Λ c in a covariant quark-diquark model","summary":"We present a systematic study of the spacelike electromagnetic form factors of the ground-state singly charmed baryons, Σ c ( Σ c + + , Σ c + , Σ c 0 ) and Λ c + , within a covariant quark-diquark model. Based on this framework, we obtain their magnetic moments as well as electric charge and magnetic moment radii. Such observables are important to understand the internal structure and the inner dynamics of these heavy baryon states. Our theoretical calculations are in qualitative agreement with available lattice QCD results of Σ c + + and Σ c 0 baryons. We also discuss the mechanism behind the dependence of the numerical results on the charm quark and light diquark. A key finding is that the electric form factors of the singly charmed baryons fall off much more slowly with momentum transfer Q 2 than that of the proton, indicating a more compact electric charge distribution, which is attributed to the heavy charm quark acting as a localized core. More importantly, we observe a striking difference in the magnetic structure: while the magnetic form factors of Σ c are dominated by the light axial-vector diquark, those of Λ c + are unexpectedly governed by the charm quark due to the vanishing contribution of the scalar diquark. This highlights the decisive role of the light-diquark spin configuration in determining the magnetic properties. Finally, using an empirical asymptotic relation connecting the spacelike and timelike regions, we predict the total cross section for e + e − → Σ c Σ ¯ c . Our findings can be tested at existing facilities including the BESIII, Belle II and LHCb, as well as the proposed Super Tau-Charm Facility.","authors":"Ye Cao, Cheng Chen, Dongyan Fu, Ju-Jun Xie","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/58g7-jp57","id":"doi:10.1103/58g7-jp57","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/58g7-jp57","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Probing quadratically coupled ultralight dark matter with the laser interferometer space antenna","summary":"Ultralight dark matter can interact with Standard Model particles via gravitational and nongravitational interactions. Through such interactions, it can leave distinctive signals in gravitational-wave experiments. In this work, we investigate signals induced by ultralight dark matter quadratically coupled to the Standard Model in the future space-borne gravitational-wave detector, the Laser Interferometer Space Antenna (LISA). Due to the quadratic nature of the coupling, dark matter signals appear at two distinct frequencies: the frequency corresponding to twice the dark matter mass, and frequencies below the typical dark matter kinetic energy. We analyze both contributions and show that LISA can surpass current constraints from terrestrial and astrophysical probes in certain mass ranges. We also find that dark matter signals in LISA are free from screening effects which significantly limit the sensitivity of terrestrial experiments.","authors":"Xucheng Gan, Hyungjin Kim, Anna-Malin Lemke, Andrea Mitridate","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/fbh9-chx7","id":"doi:10.1103/fbh9-chx7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fbh9-chx7","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Quantum interferometric probe of neutron-hidden neutron oscillations","summary":"The nature of dark matter remains an outstanding problem in particle physics and cosmology. Hidden-sector extensions of the Standard Model predict a neutral partner of the neutron, whose weak mixing with ordinary neutrons induces oscillations between visible and dark baryonic states. We propose and analyze a macroscopic quantum interferometric strategy that could provide a direct and experimentally accessible probe of this phenomenon. In particular, a Mach-Zehnder interferometer with very cold neutrons converts neutron–hidden neutron oscillations into measurable phase-dependent intensity modulations. By combining controlled phase shifts with tunable magnetic fields and material potentials, the setup enables a resonant exploration of the hidden-sector parameter space. Under representative experimental conditions on neutron flux, magnetic-field homogeneity, vertical-aperture and beam-overlap control in the presence of gravity, and interferometric stability, the expected reach extends to mixing amplitudes down to ε n n ′ ∼ 10 − 14 eV for mass splittings δ m ∼ 10 − 9 eV , accessing a previously unexplored region of parameter space and relevant to baryonic dark matter scenarios. These results suggest that neutron interferometry may become a precision laboratory tool for testing hidden-sector physics.","authors":"A. Capolupo, G. Pisacane, A. Quaranta, P. Böni","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1df4-5cbj","id":"doi:10.1103/1df4-5cbj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1df4-5cbj","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Cosmological evolution with decaying dark matter: An integral-equation approach","summary":"","authors":"Nanoom Lee, Anna Bencke, Marc Kamionkowski, José Luis Bernal","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8xzq-rgjn","id":"doi:10.1103/8xzq-rgjn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8xzq-rgjn","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Beyond leading-logarithm photon production from two-loop diagrams in a hot QCD medium","summary":"We investigate high-energy photon production from 2 ↔ 2 processes in a quark-gluon plasma by evaluating the imaginary part of the two-loop photon self-energies in thermal QCD. Working in the imaginary-time formalism, we derive analytical expressions for the leading-logarithmic and beyond-leading-logarithmic contributions to the photon production rate from 2 ↔ 2 processes. We retain the full Fermi-Dirac and Bose-Einstein statistical factors and compare the resulting Compton and annihilation rates with those obtained from kinetic theory and the closed-time-path formalism. The summed rates agree with the corresponding established results, providing a nontrivial consistency check of the calculation. Furthermore, when we consider the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution for incoming particles, we find that quantum corrections are absent in the individual 2 ↔ 2 processes. However, the total rate agrees with that of full statistics.","authors":"Sumit, Ritesh Ghosh, Munshi G. Mustafa","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/t3qf-t6bp","id":"doi:10.1103/t3qf-t6bp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/t3qf-t6bp","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Small-instanton effects in an atlas of Kim-Shifman-Vainshtein-Zakharov axion models","summary":"We investigate small-instanton contributions to the axion potential across a range of Kim-Shifman-Vainshtein-Zakharov models containing vector-like quarks (VQs), using naive dimensional analysis. We consider scenarios containing a single VQ, multiple identical copies, and sets of distinct VQs, requiring in each case that the gauge couplings remain perturbative up to the Planck scale under the two-loop gauge running. The associated fermion zero-mode content varies between these cases, requiring different combinations of mass insertions and scalar-Yukawa contractions for its saturation. Increasing the copies of VQs can render the instanton-size integral dominated by instantons of the smallest size, corresponding to the scale near the ultraviolet (UV) cutoff. The resulting contribution then becomes sensitive to the UV completion, and the induced potential can compete with, or dominate over, the ordinary QCD contribution. Assuming that the QCD and small-instanton potentials are aligned, we determine the resulting axion-mass shift and its consequences for the axion-photon coupling. When the small-instanton-induced susceptibility becomes comparable to or larger than the QCD susceptibility, the physical axion mass m a is enhanced at fixed decay constant f a , while the axion-photon coupling g a γ γ remains controlled by f a and the anomaly ratio of E / N . The standard QCD relation among m a , f a , and g a γ γ is consequently modified, opening new regions of the ( m a , g a γ γ ) plane for axion searches.","authors":"Ning Chen, Saurabh K. Shukla","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/h8rs-slt2","id":"doi:10.1103/h8rs-slt2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/h8rs-slt2","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Atacama Cosmology Telescope: Probing new signatures of ultralight axions with gravitational lensing","summary":"","authors":"Alex Laguë, Keir K. Rogers, Mathew S. Madhavacheril, J. Richard Bond, Erminia Calabrese, Mark J. Devlin, Jo Dunkley, Vera Gluscevic, Renée Hložek, Hidde T. Jense, Thibaut Louis, Frank J. Qu, Bernardita Ried Guachalla, Neelima Sehgal, Blake D. Sherwin, Suzanne T. Staggs, Alexander van Engelen","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/71gk-6cl1","id":"doi:10.1103/71gk-6cl1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/71gk-6cl1","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Neutrino quantum kinetics for fast flavor conversion in a time-dependent environment","summary":"","authors":"Zewei Xiong, Meng-Ru Wu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/v9cl-1lkk","id":"doi:10.1103/v9cl-1lkk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v9cl-1lkk","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Azimuthal asymmetry in exclusive quasielastic neutrino-nucleus interactions","summary":"","authors":"Marco Vanderpoorten, Ashish Kumar Jha, Mathias El Baz, Kajetan Niewczas, Federico Sánchez, Natalie Jachowicz","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/prv8-ljk1","id":"doi:10.1103/prv8-ljk1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/prv8-ljk1","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Light leptoquarks in a dark sector: Scalar dark matter, neutrino mass, and collider signatures","summary":"","authors":"Priyotosh Bandyopadhyay, Kirtiman Ghosh, Anirban Karan, Snehashis Parashar, Debabrata Sahoo","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/4xr6-2w3d","id":"doi:10.1103/4xr6-2w3d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4xr6-2w3d","published":"2026-11-10","title":"High-Rate Scalable Entanglement Swapping Between Remote Entanglement Sources on Deployed New York City Fibers","summary":"Entanglement swapping between photon pairs generated at physically separated nodes over telecommunication fiber infrastructure is an essential step toward the quantum internet, enabling applications such as quantum repeaters, blind quantum computing, distributed quantum computing, and distributed quantum sensing. However, successful networked entanglement swapping relies on generating indistinguishable pairs of photons and preserving them over deployed fibers. This has limited most previous demonstrations to laboratory settings or relied on sophisticated methods to maintain the necessary indistinguishability. Here, we demonstrate a scalable entanglement-swapping experiment using naturally indistinguishable entanglement sources based on warm atomic vapor cells. Without sharing lasers or optical frequency references between nodes, nor the need for pulsing the sources, we achieve a swapping rate of nearly 500 pairs/s in a local configuration while maintaining the Clauser–Horne–Shimony–Holt (CHSH) parameter above 2. Additionally, we demonstrate the scalability of our method by maintaining the quality of the entanglement swapping on 17.6 km of deployed fibers in New York City, relying on commercially available single-photon avalanche detectors at the spoke nodes, superconducting nanowire detectors at the hub and standard time-synchronization techniques. Our work paves the way for the practical deployment of large-scale hub-and-spoke quantum networks within cities and data centers.","authors":"Alexander N. Craddock, Tyler Cowan, Niccolò Bigagli, Suresh Yekasiri, Dylan Robinson, Gabriel Bello Portmann, Aditya Verma, Ziyu Guo, Michael Kilzer, Jiapeng Zhao, Mael Flament, Javad Shabani, Reza Nejabati, Mehdi Namazi","topics":["sensing","computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/k4tb-q2pd","id":"doi:10.1103/k4tb-q2pd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/k4tb-q2pd","published":"2026-11-10","title":"Nb 3 Sn superconducting microwave cavity tunable over 1 GHz for dark-matter searches","summary":"We demonstrate a wide-frequency tuning mechanism for superconducting microwave cavities designed for dark-matter searches. Using a NbSn-coated cigar-shaped cavity operating at approximately 9 GHz, we achieve continuous frequency tuning exceeding 1 GHz by mechanically separating the two cavity halves: a “tuning-by-opening” technique. Finite-element method simulations predict that radiative losses do not degrade the quality factor even for large openings, as a closed cavity with an intrinsic quality factor maintains this value for apertures up to 9 mm, corresponding to a tuning range from 9.0 to 7.5 GHz. Experimental validation using both copper ring spacers and a continuous sliding mechanism confirms values exceeding the dark-matter quality factor across the entire explored frequency range, despite mechanical imperfections and film nonuniformities. This tuning approach avoids inserting elements into the resonant volume, making it particularly suitable for high- Q superconducting cavities in axion haloscope experiments and readily applicable to rare-earth barium copper oxide (REBCO)-based implementations capable of operating in multi-tesla magnetic fields.","authors":"D. Maiello, R. Di Vora, D. Ahn, G. Carugno, R. Cervantes, B. Giaccone, A. Ortolan, S. Posen, G. Ruoso, G. Sardo Infirri, B. Tennis, S. Tocci, C. Braggio","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-09":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/pppb-9sm1","id":"doi:10.1103/pppb-9sm1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/pppb-9sm1","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Dark-Matter-Induced Nuclear Deexcitation at the Short-Baseline Near Detector with Ab Initio Nuclear Theory","summary":"We explore the sensitivity of the short-baseline near detector (SBND) experiment to light dark matter using MeV-scale electromagnetic activity. Inelastic scattering of dark matter with argon nuclei can excite nuclear states that subsequently deexcite via the emission of MeV-scale photons, producing localized low-energy “blip” signatures in a liquid argon time projection chamber. We perform state-of-the-art nuclear calculations, including all relevant argon excited states with energies up to 18 MeV, to provide reliable predictions for these signals. After accounting for relevant backgrounds, we find that SBND can probe previously unexplored regions of parameter space for light dark matter.","authors":"Bhaskar Dutta, Debopam Goswami, Baishan Hu, Wei-Chih Huang, Vishvas Pandey","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/j57s-pp4y","id":"doi:10.1103/j57s-pp4y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/j57s-pp4y","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Complementarity between neutrino neutral-current and charged-current events in the search for new physics","summary":"","authors":"Julia Gehrlein, Jaime Hoefken Zink, Pedro A. N. Machado, João Paulo Pinheiro","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/phl4-j4gs","id":"doi:10.1103/phl4-j4gs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/phl4-j4gs","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Decoherence-resilient distributed quantum sensing beyond the standard quantum limit","summary":"","authors":"Dong-Hyun Kim, Ilhwan Kim, Yosep Kim, Seongjin Hong, Yong-Su Kim, Kwang Jo Lee, Changhyoup Lee, Hyang-Tag Lim","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/5hzz-v77n","id":"doi:10.1103/5hzz-v77n","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5hzz-v77n","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Minimizing classical resources in variational measurement-based quantum computation for generative modeling","summary":"Measurement-based quantum computation (MBQC) is a framework for quantum information processing in which a computational task is carried out through one-qubit measurements on a highly entangled resource state. Due to the indeterminacy of the outcomes of a quantum measurement, the random outcomes of these operations, if not corrected, yield a variational quantum channel family. Traditionally, this randomness is corrected through classical processing in order to ensure deterministic unitary computations. Recently, variational measurement-based quantum computation (VMBQC) has been introduced to exploit this measurement-induced randomness to gain an advantage in generative modeling. A limitation of this approach is that the corresponding channel model has twice as many parameters compared with the unitary model, scaling as N × D , where N is the number of logical qubits (width) and D is the depth of the VMBQC model. This can often make optimization more difficult and may lead to poorly trainable models. In this paper, we present a restricted VMBQC model that extends the unitary setting to a channel-based one using only a single additional trainable parameter. We show, both numerically and algebraically, that this minimal extension is sufficient to generate probability distributions that cannot be represented by the corresponding unitary model.","authors":"Arunava Majumder, Hendrik Poulsen Nautrup, Hans J. Briegel","topics":["foundations","computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/bg36-b6bs","id":"doi:10.1103/bg36-b6bs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bg36-b6bs","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Electron spin relaxation and coherence times of silicon vacancies in 4 H − SiC","summary":"Color defect centers carrying electron spins in solid crystals such as diamond and silicon carbide (SiC) are promising candidates for qubits and quantum sensors, with SiC material being particularly interesting due to its compatibility with the existing semiconductor technology. Here we investigated the electron spin properties of silicon vacancy centers (V1 and V2), both having electron spin S = 3 / 2 in 4 H − SiC in the temperature range T = 80 – 300 K using electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR). The EPR spectrum shows central and satellite lines, where the former ones originate from V1 and V2 centers, as well as from unknown defect center X. The analysis of continuous-wave EPR measurements revealed a spin concentration of c V 1 = 1.47 ± 0.3 × 10 14 , c V 2 = 5.6 ± 1.12 × 10 13 , and c X = 1.03 ± 0.21 × 10 13 spins / cm 3 . Additionally, electron spin coherence T 2 and relaxation times T 1 have been measured on the central and on the satellite spectral lines, resulting in longer T 1 and T 2 for the satellite transition, compared to the central one, with maximum values of 42.8 ± 1.3 ms and 0.708 ± 0.004 ms, respectively, at T = 80 K . We reveal that decoherence is mainly caused by nuclear and electron spin diffusion.","authors":"Irene Donà, Michele Segantini, Gianluca Marcozzi, Nguyen Tien Son, Takeshi Ohshima, Ha Duong Ngo, Boris Naydenov","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/z8qv-yf7c","id":"doi:10.1103/z8qv-yf7c","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/z8qv-yf7c","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Detecting quantum phase transitions via shallow variational quantum circuits","summary":"","authors":"Ching-Yu Huang, Min-Fong Yang","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/pmx2-vzyw","id":"doi:10.1103/pmx2-vzyw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/pmx2-vzyw","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Constraining the neutrino-nucleon cross section with the ultrahigh-energy KM3NeT event","summary":"KM3NeT’s detection of a muon track produced by a ∼ 220 PeV neutrino provides an opportunity to probe physics at center-of-momentum energies greater than those probed by the LHC or other existing particle accelerators. In this study, we use this single event to place an upper limit on the neutrino-nucleon cross section of σ ν N < 26 σ ν N SM at E CM ∼ 20 TeV . This result can be used to constrain a variety of scenarios beyond the Standard Model. With future very large volume neutrino telescopes, constraints on the neutrino-nucleon scattering cross section are expected to become substantially more stringent and, in some scenarios, could become competitive with accelerator probes of new physics.","authors":"Toni Bertólez-Martínez, Dan Hooper","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/278k-lmv9","id":"doi:10.1103/278k-lmv9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/278k-lmv9","published":"2026-11-09","title":"How bright in gravitational waves are millisecond pulsars for the Galactic Center GeV gamma-ray excess? A systematic study and implications for dark matter","summary":"","authors":"Ming-Yu Lei, Bei Zhou, Xiaoyuan Huang","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/rg73-x5r9","id":"doi:10.1103/rg73-x5r9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/rg73-x5r9","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Non-Markovian heavy-quark equilibration and equilibrium correlation function in a thermal medium","summary":"We compute the charm-quark current–current correlation function within the Langevin framework and extract the heavy-quark diffusion coefficient using the Green–Kubo formula. The formalism is further extended to evaluate the correlation function using a generalized Langevin equation that incorporates memory effects via an exponentially decaying memory kernel. We find that while memory effects qualitatively modify the transient structure of the current correlations, the value of the transport coefficient remains unchanged. In addition, we investigate heavy-quark thermalization in the presence of memory and compare the nonequilibrium solution of a generalized Fokker–Planck equation with the one obtained from the generalized Langevin equation in the nonrelativistic limit. We also consider the relativistic case with correlated noise and observe that memory effects can give rise to a damped, oscillatory equilibration of the heavy-quark momentum distribution in a non-Markovian bath.","authors":"Monisha Nair, Juan M. Torres-Rincon, Santosh K. Das","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8xhv-hbw3","id":"doi:10.1103/8xhv-hbw3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8xhv-hbw3","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Two-variable longitudinal distribution of the Λ b in a three-quark light-front model","summary":"We determine a normalized two-variable longitudinal distribution of the Λ b = ( u d ) b baryon from a covariant three-quark light-front wave function. The scalar u d channel is selected with the good-component structure C γ 5 γ + , and the two independent transverse momenta are integrated without introducing an elementary diquark. The resulting model-scale valence distribution Ψ 2 LF ( x u , x d ) retains both the heavy-light and internal-light momentum scales of the parent wave function. It is symmetric under x u ↔ x d , is concentrated where both light-quark fractions are small and separates the total light-pair momentum from its internal sharing. For the central parameter set we find ⟨ x u ⟩ = ⟨ x d ⟩ = 0.130 , ⟨ x b ⟩ = 0.739 , ⟨ ζ 2 ⟩ = 0.153 , and ⟨ ζ 4 ⟩ = 0.055 , where ζ = ( x u − x d ) / ( x u + x d ) . Parameter variations give ⟨ x u ⟩ = 0.13 0 − 0.007 + 0.007 , ⟨ x b ⟩ = 0.73 9 − 0.014 + 0.014 , ⟨ ζ 2 ⟩ = 0.15 3 − 0.016 + 0.014 , and ⟨ ζ 4 ⟩ = 0.05 5 − 0.009 + 0.009 . The heavy-light momentum partition is controlled mainly by β b , u d and m b , whereas the internal u − d sharing is most sensitive to β u d . The result provides a compact finite-mass benchmark for three-quark longitudinal structure, with distinct observables for heavy-light momentum sharing and correlations inside the light pair.","authors":"Shuai Xu, Xiao-Nan Li","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/prk2-z6vl","id":"doi:10.1103/prk2-z6vl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/prk2-z6vl","published":"2026-11-09","title":"D * D * π and B * B * π couplings from light-cone sum rules","summary":"We revisit the calculation of the strong couplings D * D * π and B * B * π from the light-cone sum rules (LCSR) using the pion light-cone distribution amplitudes. The accuracy of the underlying correlation function is upgraded by establishing the hard-collinear factorization formula at the leading power up to the next-to-leading order in α s . Furthermore, the next-to-leading power contributions are incorporated at the leading order by including the two-particle and three-particle higher-twist pion distribution amplitudes up to twist-4 accuracy. We perform a comprehensive numerical analysis with a systematic evaluation of theoretical uncertainties, predicting g D * D * π = 5.7 1 − 0.89 + 0.70 GeV − 1 and g B * B * π = 4.9 0 − 0.67 + 0.59 GeV − 1 . Finally, by parametrizing the 1 / m H (with H = D , B ) power corrections to extract the universal static coupling g ^ = 0.30 ± 0.04 , we compare our results with previous theoretical determinations and experimental data, and discuss the current limitations in disentangling the heavy quark spin symmetry breaking effects.","authors":"Chao Wang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/97vm-tftt","id":"doi:10.1103/97vm-tftt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/97vm-tftt","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Broadband constraints on axionlike particle dark matter from the Coma cluster radio halo and prospects for the SKA","summary":"","authors":"Ramananda Santra, Samik Mitra, Aniket Nath","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/5853-6d3f","id":"doi:10.1103/5853-6d3f","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5853-6d3f","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Constraints on a light leptophilic scalar from dark-sector couplings","summary":"We study a minimal framework where a Majorana fermion dark matter particle interacts with a light scalar mediator coupled mainly to electrons. We examine both freeze-out and freeze-in production to determine the regions of parameter space that yield the correct relic abundance with particular emphasis on a detailed comparison with results in the recent literature. The analysis includes cosmological and astrophysical constraints as well as laboratory bounds from electron-recoil experiments, fixed target searches, and precision measurements. The combined results identify a narrow but viable parameter region, favoring sub-GeV dark matter, and define clear targets for future experimental tests. This highlights the strong complementarity between direct-detection experiments and collider searches. We additionally investigate the mediator-mass region around 17 MeV, motivated by the hints reported by the ATOMKI experiment and the PADME collaboration, including couplings between the mediator and light quarks. Direct searches already constrain a large region of the parameter space, even when dark matter is produced via freeze-in, pointing again to sub-GeV dark matter.","authors":"Marco Graziani, Giacomo Landini, Federico Mescia, Claudio Toni, Ludovico Vittorio","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2lmc-nb3w","id":"doi:10.1103/2lmc-nb3w","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2lmc-nb3w","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Simulating fully gauge-fixed SU(2) Hamiltonian dynamics on digital quantum computers","summary":"","authors":"Henry Froland, Dorota M. Grabowska, Zhiyao Li","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/n6lg-xbks","id":"doi:10.1103/n6lg-xbks","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/n6lg-xbks","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Four-loop quark-to-quark splitting function in QCD","summary":"","authors":"Thomas Gehrmann, Andreas von Manteuffel, Vasily Sotnikov, Tong-Zhi Yang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/smzy-dwp5","id":"doi:10.1103/smzy-dwp5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/smzy-dwp5","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Cosmic neutrino background is within reach of future neutrino telescopes","summary":"","authors":"Gonzalo Herrera, Shunsaku Horiuchi, Xiaolin Qi, Ian M. Shoemaker","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/6h95-91bz","id":"doi:10.1103/6h95-91bz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6h95-91bz","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Minimal quark-lepton complementarity from a rigid deformation of tribimaximal mixing","summary":"","authors":"Gazal Sharma, Gaurav Katoch","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/rv36-hrzg","id":"doi:10.1103/rv36-hrzg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/rv36-hrzg","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Probing sub-GeV dark matter with the Migdal effect at JUNO","summary":"","authors":"Stefano Scopel, Gaurav Tomar","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/5btm-vwgy","id":"doi:10.1103/5btm-vwgy","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5btm-vwgy","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Rapid Multi-Mode Trapped-Ion Laser Cooling in a Phase-Stable Standing Wave","summary":"Laser cooling is fundamental to quantum computing and metrology using atomic systems. Precise control often requires cooling atoms’ motional degrees of freedom to the quantum ground state, imposing operation time and architectural limitations particularly in large-scale systems. Here we demonstrate how the integrated optical control of interest for scaling trapped-ion systems additionally enables laser cooling that bypasses limitations of conventional schemes. Leveraging multi-channel integrated delivery of ultraviolet to infrared wavelengths for calcium ion control including in passively phase-stable ultraviolet standing waves (SWs), we experimentally verify a long-standing prediction by Cirac ., realizing Doppler cooling to below the conventional Doppler limit at a SW node. We also present the first realization of ground-state cooling via electromagnetically induced transparency (EIT) using a “probe” beam delivered as a SW with atoms positioned at a node, predicted to enable multi-mode sub-recoil-limit laser cooling. We demonstrate cooling of motional modes spanning an approximately 5 MHz bandwidth from the Doppler temperature to near the ground state within 150 μ s , reaching n ¯ ≈ 0.05 phonon number occupancies for the target mode. Direct evaluation against the comparable running-wave (RW) scheme shows the SW implementation’s simultaneous advantage in cooling rate, motional mode bandwidth, and final phonon number, as previously theoretically predicted. Our work leverages capabilities enabled by integrated optical delivery to demonstrate fast cooling of multiple modes to the quantum ground state, and more broadly how scalable approaches to optical control can enable enhancements in fundamental atomic functionalities.","authors":"Zhenzhong Xing, Hamim Mahmud Rivy, Vighnesh Natarajan, Aditya Milind Kolhatkar, Gillenhaal Beck, Karan K. Mehta","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/qzlv-8y21","id":"doi:10.1103/qzlv-8y21","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/qzlv-8y21","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Polarized lepton source via target-normal sheath fields","summary":"","authors":"Xinyu Liu, Yue Cao, Siman Liu, Jingyi Wang, Lixiang Hu, Ke Liu, Tongpu Yu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/j8yl-nn8x","id":"doi:10.1103/j8yl-nn8x","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/j8yl-nn8x","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Learning unified control of intrinsic nonlinear spin dynamics in atomic qudits for magnetometry","summary":"","authors":"C.-Z. Cao, J.-Z. Han, M. Xiong, M. Deng, L. Wang, Q. Liu, X. Lv, M. Xue","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/xrpd-4xdg","id":"doi:10.1103/xrpd-4xdg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xrpd-4xdg","published":"2026-11-09","title":"Sensitivity threshold defines the optimal spin subset for ensemble quantum sensing","summary":"","authors":"Suwan I. Kang, Minhyeok Kim, Sanghyo Park, Heonsik Lee, Keunyoung Lee, Donggyu Kim","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]}]},"2026-11-06":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/hlk5-589d","id":"doi:10.1103/hlk5-589d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/hlk5-589d","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Ultrastrong-Coupling Signatures in Photon Statistics from Terahertz Higgs Polaritons","summary":"","authors":"Spenser Talkington, Benjamin Kass, Martin Claassen","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/8dj9-xp98","id":"doi:10.1103/8dj9-xp98","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8dj9-xp98","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Minimal example of quantum network Bell nonlocality","summary":"","authors":"Erwan Don, Jessica Bavaresco, Patryk Lipka-Bartosik, Nicolas Gisin, Nicolas Brunner, Alejandro Pozas-Kerstjens","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/nzhn-34tp","id":"doi:10.1103/nzhn-34tp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nzhn-34tp","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Atomic thermometry in optical lattice clocks: Model discrepancies and their impact on lattice light shifts","summary":"Accurate measurement of atomic temperature is fundamental for a wide range of applications, from quantum sensing to precision metrology. In optical lattice clocks, precise characterization of atomic temperature is required to minimize systematic uncertainties at the 10 − 18 level. In this work we investigate atomic temperature measurements in the ytterbium optical clock developed at INRIM, IT-Yb1, employing sideband and transverse Doppler spectroscopy across a wide range of trapping conditions in a one-dimensional optical lattice. By implementing clock-line-mediated Sisyphus cooling [], we reduce the atomic temperature and enable operation at shallower lattice depths to D = 50 E R , where E R is the photon recoil energy. We compare temperature estimates obtained from the perturbed harmonic oscillator model with those derived using a Born-Oppenheimer model []. Discrepancies of up to a factor of four are observed between the temperature estimates obtained with the two approximations. We assess the impact of these modeling differences on the evaluation of lattice light shifts and find relative frequency deviations of up to 8 × 10 − 17 . Although sufficiently low atomic temperatures and the consistent use of theoretical models reduce these discrepancies to the low 10 − 18 level, these results motivate further investigations that will be relevant to state-of-the-art optical lattice clocks.","authors":"Irene Goti, Tommaso Petrucciani, Stefano Condio, Filippo Levi, Davide Calonico, Marco Pizzocaro","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/fjzj-9vt3","id":"doi:10.1103/fjzj-9vt3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fjzj-9vt3","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Johnson-spectral recycling for photon-loss mitigation in linear-optical quantum circuits","summary":"","authors":"Yu. A. Biriukov","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/qwgz-k91b","id":"doi:10.1103/qwgz-k91b","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/qwgz-k91b","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Criterion for qubit-assisted quantum metrology exhibiting Heisenberg scaling","summary":"","authors":"Peng Chen, Jun Jing","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/k4w9-8xwk","id":"doi:10.1103/k4w9-8xwk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/k4w9-8xwk","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Counterfactual quantum sensing: What interaction-free measurement can and cannot buy","summary":"","authors":"Christoph F. Wildfeuer","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/nh5n-7srt","id":"doi:10.1103/nh5n-7srt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nh5n-7srt","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Semi-device-independent self-testing of bipartite pure quantum states without nonlocality","summary":"","authors":"Lijinzhi Lin, Zhenyu Chen, Xiaodie Lin, Zhaohui Wei","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/n78v-py2j","id":"doi:10.1103/n78v-py2j","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/n78v-py2j","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Noise spectroscopy of insulating and itinerant altermagnets","summary":"One of the central goals in the emergent field of altermagnetism is the unambiguous experimental identification and characterization of altermagnetic order across a variety of compounds. This motivates exploring tools that can clearly distinguish altermagnets from antiferromagnets, based on symmetry signatures, and offer access to the dominant orbital character (e.g., d -wave versus g -wave) of the magnetic order parameter. In this work, we theoretically explore the potential of noise magnetometry for this task, studying contributions from both magnons and itinerant electrons in different regimes and scenarios. While altermagnetism and antiferromagnetism also lead to different noise spectra for magnons, we find the most striking and symmetry-sensitive signatures in the charge fluctuations of itinerant altermagnets. Both for the homogeneous bulk case and in the presence of strain and/or around domain walls, we identify noise contributions that are only permitted by symmetry in the altermagnet and, thus, provide a unique signature of altermagnetism. Furthermore, the angular dependence of noise around domain walls also offers access to the orbital character of the altermagnet. On a more technical note, we discuss the role and relevance of lattice effects related to the dipole tensor. We hope that our work will help pave the way toward the clear experimental identification of altermagnetism across a wide range of candidate materials.","authors":"Lucas V. Pupim, Mathias S. Scheurer","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/f66n-c6sm","id":"doi:10.1103/f66n-c6sm","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/f66n-c6sm","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Robust valley splitting against interface atomic disorder towards scalable silicon quantum computing: Computational design","summary":"","authors":"Yang Liu, Gang Wang, Shan Guan, Jun-Wei Luo, Shu-Shen Li","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/kd5j-d4fv","id":"doi:10.1103/kd5j-d4fv","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kd5j-d4fv","published":"2026-11-06","title":"ANUBIS detector and its sensitivity to neutral long-lived particles","summary":"Long-lived particles (LLPs), i.e., particles with macroscopic lifetimes τ > 10 ps , appear in various extensions of the Standard Model (SM) that address fundamental questions like the particulate nature of dark matter or baryogenesis. The ANUBIS detector will achieve unprecedented sensitivity to such models compared to existing and approved experiments by instrumenting a large decay volume adjacent to the ATLAS experiment at the high-luminosity LHC with tracking detectors. This paper outlines the proposed detector layouts for ANUBIS, explores their physics potential with a scalar LLP model, and identifies the preferred layout, comparing it to other experiments. The potential background contributions are estimated using a data-driven method, and the topology of potential background events is studied using Monte Carlo simulations. Overall, the ANUBIS detector is expected to probe branching ratios down to O ( 10 − 6 ) for exotic decays of the Higgs boson to scalar long-lived particles with masses of 10, 15, 40, and 60 GeV and proper lifetimes of c τ = 2.4 , 3.0, 12, and 18 m, respectively. Moreover, for branching ratios of 0.1% of the Higgs boson into long-lived scalars with a mass of 15 GeV, the ANUBIS detector can probe a c τ range between 1.1 × 10 − 1 m and 4.0 × 10 3 m .","authors":"Thomas Adolphus, Giulio Aielli, Jahanzeb Akhtar, Arun Atwal, Martin Bauer, Rachel Bentham, Oleg Brandt, Jude Burling, Jon Burr, Patrick Collins, Louie Dartmoor Corpe, Matthew Coxon, Jonas Dej, Sofie Nordahl Erner, Cayetano Fernandez Ruiz, Jindrich Jelinek, Oliver Kortner, Hubert Kroha, Lawrence Lee, Christopher Lester et al.","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/gs8j-1s7r","id":"doi:10.1103/gs8j-1s7r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gs8j-1s7r","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Exploring the growth-index ( γ ) tension with Λ s CDM","summary":"Recent observational analyses have revealed a significant tension in the growth index γ , which characterizes the growth rate of cosmic structures. When γ is treated as a free parameter within lambda cold dark matter ( Λ CDM ), the PL 18 + f σ 8 combination yields γ ≈ 0.64 , in ∼ 4 σ tension with the theoretically expected value γ ≈ 0.55 (assuming general relativity), suggesting a preference for suppressed late-time growth relative to the standard expectation. In this work, we investigate whether the Λ s CDM framework—featuring a rapid sign-switching cosmological constant (mirror anti–de Sitter-to-de Sitter (AdS-to-dS) transition) in the late universe at redshift z † ∼ 2 —can alleviate the γ tension together with the H 0 and S 8 tensions. We find that γ Λ s CDM is able to reduce the γ and H 0 tensions relative to γ Λ CDM while maintaining low S 8 values. For PL 18 + f σ 8 , the combination underlying the γ tension, γ Λ s CDM yields z † > 1.58 (95% CL) and reduces the γ and H 0 discrepancies from 3.7 σ and 4.2 σ in γ Λ CDM to 2.1 σ and 1.1 σ , respectively, while leaving χ min 2 essentially unchanged. The largest improvement among the free- z † analyses is obtained for PL 18 + f σ 8 + PP & SH 0 ES , for which the marginalized posterior constraint is z † = 1.8 2 − 0.28 + 0.32 (95% CL); the γ and H 0 tensions are reduced to 1.8 σ and 0.6 σ , respectively, and the model also provides an improved fit to the data. The efficacy of the mechanism is, however, dataset dependent: the standard 3D BAO measurements used in the present analysis—from SDSS-IV/eBOSS and the first year of DESI observations—tend to push the transition to higher redshifts, z † ≳ 2.3 – 2.4 , and weaken the simultaneous alleviation, particularly of the H 0 tension. We also examine the benchmark case with fixed z † = 1.7 , previously identified in studies of the standard ( γ ≃ 0.55 ) Λ s CDM framework as a redshift of interest for alleviating multiple major cosmological tensions—including those in H 0 , M B , and S 8 . For the restricted combinations in which it is considered, this benchmark yields low residual tensions (e.g., a 1.6 σ γ tension and an effectively vanishing H 0 tension for PL 18 + f σ 8 ), albeit with somewhat worse fits than γ Λ CDM . It is, however, excluded at more than 2 σ by the free- z † analyses once either the uncalibrated PantheonPlus SN Ia sample or the 3D BAO data are included. Our results indicate that Λ s CDM can contribute to the joint alleviation of multiple cosmological tensions, although its impact is dataset dependent.","authors":"Luis A. Escamilla, Özgür Akarsu, Eleonora Di Valentino, Emre Özülker, J. Alberto Vazquez","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/73pl-ywzw","id":"doi:10.1103/73pl-ywzw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/73pl-ywzw","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Background of the BULLKID detector array operated with a moderate shield on the surface","summary":"We present the operation with moderate radiation shield in a surface laboratory of BULky and Low-threshold Kinetic Inductance Detector) (BULLKID), a cryogenic detector for searches of light dark matter or coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering. The detector consists of an array of 60 cubic silicon particle absorbers of 0.34 g each, sensed by cryogenic kinetic inductance detectors. The analysis presented focuses on data from 15 elements of the array, with two central units used to evaluate the background and with their surrounding elements used as veto. The low energy spectrum resulting from an exposure of 290 hours to ambient backgrounds, acquired with the use of external and internal radiation shields, is compatible with the simulations at the level of ( 6.8 ± 0.4 stat . ± 0.1 syst . ) × 10 4 counts / keV kg days from 2 keV down to an energy of 600 eV. The region between 225 eV and 600 eV shows a rise in background in disagreement with the simulations, while not sharing some of the key traits of the low energy excess observed in other cryogenic experiments. The high energy spectrum shape is in overall agreement with the simulations and displays the typical particle-induced x-ray emission of the surrounding lead.","authors":"D. Delicato, A. Acevedo-Rentería, M. Folcarelli, G. Del Castello, M. Cappelli, L. E. Ardila-Perez, L. Bandiera, C. Bonomo, M. Calvo, R. Caravita, F. Carillo, F. Cescato, U. Chowdhury, D. A. Crovo, A. Cruciani, A. D’Addabbo, M. De Lucia, M. del Gallo Roccagiovine, F. Ferraro, S. Fu et al.","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/24dn-vndz","id":"doi:10.1103/24dn-vndz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/24dn-vndz","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Numerical method for the efficient calculation of scattering form factors","summary":"Scintillating molecular crystals have emerged as prime candidates for directional dark matter detector targets. This anisotropy makes them exquisitely sensitive due to the daily modulation induced by the directional dark matter wind. However, predicting the interaction rate for arbitrary molecules requires accurate modeling of the many-body ground as well as excited states, a task that has been historically computationally expensive. Here, we present a theory and computational framework for efficiently computing dark matter scattering form factors for molecules. We introduce ar, a GPU-accelerated code to compute the fully three-dimensional anisotropic molecular form factor for arbitrary molecules. We use a full time-dependent density functional theory framework to compute the lowest-lying singlet excited states. Once the many-body electronic structure is computed, the form factors are computed in a small fraction of the time from the transition density matrix. We show that ar can compute the first 12 form factors for a molecule of 10 heavy atoms in approximately 5 s, opening the door to accurate, high-throughput material screening for optimal directional dark matter detector targets. Our code can perform the calculation in three independent ways, two semianalytical and one fully numeric, providing optimized methods for every precision goal.","authors":"Benjamin Lillard, Jack D. Shergold, Carlos Blanco","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/fspm-jw37","id":"doi:10.1103/fspm-jw37","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fspm-jw37","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Is the conventional picture of coherence time complete? Dark matter recoherence from substructures","summary":"The local solar gravitational potential forms a basin for ultralight dark matter (ULDM) with discrete energy levels. Even if barely populated, it introduces a new characteristic timescale in DM dynamics. This necessitates a generalization of the notion of coherence time. We find that, at long times, the phenomenon of “recoherence” emerges, whereby a subcomponent of ULDM exhibits a formally divergent coherence time. The fact that this generalized coherence time can significantly exceed the naive estimate implies an enhanced sensitivity for dark matter searches that accumulate data over extended observation periods.","authors":"Chaitanya Paranjape, Gilad Perez, Wolfram Ratzinger, Somasundaram Sankaranarayanan","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/3s33-c5n5","id":"doi:10.1103/3s33-c5n5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/3s33-c5n5","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Radiative decays of the h c meson from lattice QCD","summary":"We explore, for the first time in lattice QCD, the radiative decays to γ η and γ η ′ of the J P C = 1 + − charmonium state, h c . This work expands upon a previous calculation of J / ψ decays to the same final states, incorporating novel technology to access the h c where it is an excited state and to separate the independent electric dipole and longitudinal form factors. Results for the radiative decay rates, computed on three-flavor lattices with m π ∼ 391 MeV , show a suppression relative to experiment that is similar to that observed in the prior calculation of J / ψ → γ η ( ′ ) . The timelike Q 2 dependence of both form factors together influence the Dalitz decays, h c → ℓ + ℓ − η ( ′ ) , which are as yet unmeasured experimentally.","authors":"Mischa Batelaan, Jozef J. Dudek, Robert G. Edwards, for the Hadron Spectrum Collaboration","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dkq1-kbs5","id":"doi:10.1103/dkq1-kbs5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dkq1-kbs5","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Quantum gravitational contrast in creating Schrödinger cat state","summary":"","authors":"Anupam Mazumdar, Tian Zhou","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/nlyh-kzq3","id":"doi:10.1103/nlyh-kzq3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nlyh-kzq3","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Quantum states prepared by wormholes: Long-wavelength deviations from the Bunch-Davies vacuum","summary":"Wineglass wormholes mediate the nucleation of baby universes out of an asymptotically anti-de Sitter or flat spacetime. Upon materialization, the new universe naturally undergoes an inflationary phase. Here, we study the quantum state of tensor and probe scalar field fluctuations that these wormhole geometries prepare, finding that they reproduce the Bunch-Davies vacuum for short-wavelength modes but lead to deviations from Bunch-Davies on large scales. These deviations, which depend on the charge of the wormhole, cause a small shift as well as oscillations in the angular power spectrum generated by an ensuing inflationary phase, and thus provide a distinctive cosmological observable. The wormholes are supported by either axionic or magnetic charges. In the limit of vanishing charge, they undergo a topological transition after which they contain no-boundary instantons. We find that the small-charge limit suppresses deviations from the Bunch-Davies state for both scalar and tensor fluctuations, indicating that the topological transition is smooth.","authors":"George Lavrelashvili, Jean-Luc Lehners","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/7crr-s6k5","id":"doi:10.1103/7crr-s6k5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7crr-s6k5","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Searching for symmetric mass generation with staggered fermions in four dimensions","summary":"We conduct numerical simulations to map out the phase diagram and critical behavior of a lattice Higgs model composed of two massless staggered fermion fields forming a doublet under a global S U ( 2 ) and coupled to a scalar field in the adjoint representation of the group. The scalar action consists of a potential comprising quadratic and quartic terms and a scalar kinetic term. At fixed quartic coupling we explore a two-dimensional parameter space finding a massless symmetric phase at weak coupling and a massive symmetric phase (SMG phase) at strong coupling. An intermediate antiferromagnetic phase separates these two regimes. These results are consistent with leading order weak and strong coupling expansions. We find that the critical lines bounding the intermediate phase merge at a unique point where all fermion bilinear condensates vanish but fermion susceptibilities diverge as nontrivial powers of the lattice size. We conjecture that this merged point corresponds to a multicritical point and may describe a phase consisting of a condensate of certain topological defects.","authors":"Nouman Butt, Simon Catterall, Gwen Hartshaw, Anna Hasenfratz","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/mmvw-gcct","id":"doi:10.1103/mmvw-gcct","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mmvw-gcct","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Strong C P phase and the PMNS phase in Dirac left-right symmetry","summary":"Left-right symmetric theories with generalized parity restrict the bare QCD angle to a C P -conserving value and relate the physical strong- C P phase θ ¯ to a C P -odd parity-breaking parameter ε . We show that, in the Dirac-neutrino realization, imposing a sectorial reality condition on the Dirac lepton Yukawa matrices turns observable leptonic C P violation from an independent input into a branch-dependent prediction. Parity reconstructs the right-handed leptonic mixing matrix, whereas leptonic reality requires it to be rephasing-equivalent to the complex conjugate of the left-handed one. For generic three-generation Yukawas, compatibility is equivalent to the vanishing of a single Jarlskog-type C P -odd invariant. In the physical Dirac hierarchy, for fixed oscillation data, mass ordering, and discrete leptonic branch, compatibility determines the Pontecorvo-Maki-Nakagawa-Sakata (PMNS) Jarlskog invariant as a function of the lightest neutrino mass and ε . We derive its leading analytic behavior and obtain the nonlinear compatibility branches numerically. In compressed mixed-sign Dirac-neutrino spectra, small signed mass sums can amplify a tiny parity deformation into order-one values of the normalized PMNS Jarlskog invariant, including maximal C P violation. The corresponding quark reconstruction independently provides a calculable, branch-dependent conversion between ε and θ ¯ . Together, the leptonic and quark relations define a family of correlations among leptonic C P violation, the absolute neutrino mass scale, and strong C P .","authors":"Vladimir Tello","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ys59-qb6s","id":"doi:10.1103/ys59-qb6s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ys59-qb6s","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Next-to-leading-order QCD corrections to the radiative decays χ b J → J / ψ + γ","summary":"Within the framework of nonrelativistic quantum chromodynamics (NRQCD) factorization, we calculate the next-to-leading-order (NLO) QCD corrections to χ b J → J / ψ + γ with J = 0 , 1, 2. We determine all six independent helicity amplitudes, including the one-loop corrections to the quantum electrodynamics (QED) amplitude and the two-loop corrections to the QCD amplitude, and retain the interference between the QED and QCD amplitudes in the decay widths. The NLO corrections substantially enhance the χ b 0 and χ b 1 decay widths, while reducing the χ b 2 decay width by approximately 36%. Including the QED amplitude leads to sizable destructive interference for χ b 0 and an enhancement for χ b 2 , but has only a small effect on the χ b 1 decay width. Two treatments of photon fragmentation, based on the NRQCD matrix element and the experimentally determined J / ψ decay constant, yield NLO unpolarized decay widths that agree at the few percent level or better. Using the decay constant scheme at μ R = m b , we obtain NLO central branching fractions of 1.03 × 10 − 6 , 1.45 × 10 − 5 , and 1.99 × 10 − 6 for the three channels, respectively. The NLO corrections reduce the renormalization scale dependence, particularly for χ b 2 , although a sizable dependence remains for χ b 0 . At fixed m b , the branching fractions decrease with increasing r = m c / m b , more strongly for χ b 1 and χ b 2 than for χ b 0 . For p p collisions at s = 14 TeV and an integrated luminosity of 100 fb − 1 , we estimate approximately 1.9 × 10 4 and 4.8 × 10 4 ℓ + ℓ − γ events from χ b 0 and χ b 2 decays, respectively. Measurements with the existing Belle ϒ ( 2 S ) sample remain challenging because of the small expected yields. A substantially larger ϒ ( 2 S ) sample at Belle II would improve the prospects for observing these radiative decays.","authors":"Yu-Dong Zhang, Zai-Fu Jiang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/7mvj-3frb","id":"doi:10.1103/7mvj-3frb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7mvj-3frb","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Momentum entanglement at colliders: The H → W W , Z Z case","summary":"We address momentum entanglement in Higgs decays to weak boson pairs, H → W W , Z Z , by discretizing momentum space. The momenta of the two weak bosons are entangled, as well as the degrees of freedom in momentum and spin spaces. For H → Z Z in the four-lepton final state, we also estimate the statistical sensitivity of entanglement measures at the Large Hadron Collider and future upgrades. The discretization method introduced here is broadly applicable, offering a framework for studies of momentum entanglement at the energy frontier.","authors":"J. A. Aguilar-Saavedra","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dbn4-jjh5","id":"doi:10.1103/dbn4-jjh5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dbn4-jjh5","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Constraints on vectorlike top dipole interactions from top-associated photon measurements at the LHC","summary":"Precision measurements of top-quark production with energetic photons provide a complementary probe of vectorlike quarks with nonstandard radiative decays. We reinterpret the CMS differential t t ¯ γ measurement and the ATLAS fiducial t t ¯ γ γ cross section to constrain the electromagnetic and chromomagnetic transition interactions of a charge- 2 / 3 vectorlike top partner, T . We consider effective interactions written in terms of physical fields after electroweak symmetry breaking, retaining only the decays T → t γ and T → t g . The CMS signal includes both the mixed ( t γ ) ( t g ) topology and double-radiative t t ¯ γ γ events entering the exactly-one-photon fiducial region, while the ATLAS rate is interpreted using a signal-specific detector-response transfer factor. Signal uncertainties are included, and the interpretation is restricted point by point to the domain satisfying the effective field theory and narrow-width requirements. Denoting the electromagnetic and chromomagnetic transition coefficients by c t γ and c t g , respectively, we find that, for c t g = 1.0 × 10 − 5 GeV − 1 and m T = 500 GeV , the observed Run 2 exclusions at 95% confidence level extend down to c t γ ≃ 4.7 × 10 − 7 GeV − 1 for CMS and 2.3 × 10 − 6 GeV − 1 for ATLAS within the valid domain. At m T = 1000 GeV , the corresponding values are 2.1 × 10 − 6 and 8.5 × 10 − 6 GeV − 1 . Projections to the HL-LHC indicate improved sensitivity to these radiative transition interactions. These results establish top-associated photon measurements as complementary probes of radiative top-partner interactions beyond searches targeting conventional decay modes.","authors":"M. Sahraei, Y. Hosseini, M. Mohammadi Najafabadi","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/gf43-8hgp","id":"doi:10.1103/gf43-8hgp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gf43-8hgp","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Self-consistently solving the quantum kinetic equation for a hot, dense, homogeneous, and isotropic neutrino gas","summary":"","authors":"Lila Fowler, Macie M. Medes, Emma L. Horner, Chad T. Kishimoto","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/nn51-zg5k","id":"doi:10.1103/nn51-zg5k","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nn51-zg5k","published":"2026-11-06","title":"High energy photon polarimetry at lepton colliders: Quantum information from converted photons","summary":"","authors":"Carlos Henrique de Lima, Navin McGinnis, David McKeen","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4s26-75w2","id":"doi:10.1103/4s26-75w2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4s26-75w2","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Multiparameter sensing of axion dark matter with superconducting-qubit networks","summary":"","authors":"Le Bin Ho","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/z2k9-mjbr","id":"doi:10.1103/z2k9-mjbr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/z2k9-mjbr","published":"2026-11-06","title":"High energy probes of Higgs boson self-coupling via W boson fusion at future lepton colliders","summary":"","authors":"Amir Subba, Hrishikesh Deka, Subhaditya Bhattacharya, Abhik Sarkar","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/26cd-dq7y","id":"doi:10.1103/26cd-dq7y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/26cd-dq7y","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Physics-informed quantum algorithms for glueball-like excitations in a Z2 lattice gauge theory","summary":"","authors":"Dan-Bo Zhang","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/jc77-2nvb","id":"doi:10.1103/jc77-2nvb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jc77-2nvb","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Surface Adsorbates Suppress Low-Frequency Noise for Shallow Nitrogen-Vacancy Centers","summary":"Shallow nitrogen-vacancy (NV) centers in diamond are promising nanoscale quantum sensors, yet their coherence is strongly limited by surface-induced noise. Surface adsorbates are widely believed to be a major source of decoherence. Here, we test this assumption by characterizing shallow single NV centers under ultrahigh vacuum (UHV) conditions, where the diamond surface is kept free of adsorbates, and comparing their behavior to ambient conditions. Surprisingly, we observe a ∼ 4 × reduction in the Hahn echo coherence time T 2 in UHV. By combining Hahn echo measurements in the single-quantum and double-quantum (DQ) bases, we separate contributions from different noise sources and find that both electric and magnetic noise are enhanced in UHV. In contrast, T 1 measurements reveal an increased DQ T 1 in UHV, indicating suppressed electric field noise in the ∼ 100 MHz frequency regime. These results point to a modification of the surface noise spectrum upon adsorbate removal, with different frequency regimes arising from distinct microscopic mechanisms. Specifically, we find that the low-frequency noise is consistent with increased surface charge in UHV that can be compensated by surface adsorbates in ambient conditions. Our findings highlight a complex and previously underappreciated role of surface adsorbates in shaping the noise environment of shallow NV centers, with important implications for nanoscale quantum sensing.","authors":"Zhiyang Yuan, David A. Fehr, Kalliope Zervas, Sorawis Sangtawesin, Lila V. H. Rodgers, Patryk Gumann, Michael E. Flatté, Nathalie P. de Leon","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/5vv7-y2rj","id":"doi:10.1103/5vv7-y2rj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5vv7-y2rj","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Two-qubit-gate operation in a high-connectivity transmon lattice utilizing a tunable coupling to a shared mode","summary":"Increasing connectivity and decreasing qubit-state delocalization without compromising the speed and accuracy of elementary gate operations are topical challenges in the development of large-scale superconducting quantum computers. In this theoretical work, we study a special honeycomb qubit lattice where each qubit inside a unit cell is coupled to every other one via two dedicated tunable couplers and a common central element. This results in an effective multimode interaction enabling tunable, on-demand, all-to-all connectivity between each qubit pair within the unit cell. We provide a thorough analysis of the unit cell, including a proposal for a novel and efficient conditional- Z gate scheme, which takes advantage of the effective multimode coupling. We develop an experimentally viable pulse protocol for a single-step gate implementation, which considerably improves the gate speed compared to the previous two-qubit-gate realizations suggested for architectures utilizing a center mode. We also show numerical results on how the presence of spectator qubits affects the average two-qubit-gate fidelity and analyze how the multimode coupling structure mitigates the delocalization-induced crosstalk during simultaneous single-qubit gates within the unit cell. We also provide analytical estimates for the errors caused by relaxation and dephasing during a two-qubit-gate operation, including noise terms for the multimode coupling structure. Our multimode coupling architecture results in a good balance between increased connectivity and available parallelism, especially when several interacting unit cells form a quantum-processing unit. We anticipate that the obtained results pave the way toward high-connectivity quantum processors with efficient and low-overhead quantum algorithms.","authors":"Tuure Orell, Hao Hsu, Joona Andersson, Jani Tuorila, Frank Deppe, Hsiang-Sheng Ku","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/m9nn-cgww","id":"doi:10.1103/m9nn-cgww","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/m9nn-cgww","published":"2026-11-06","title":"Non-Markovian delay-assisted sensing with waveguide-coupled quantum emitters","summary":"We show that in a minimal setup of two waveguide-coupled quantum emitters, separated by long distances and subject to an external field, time-delayed feedback can be a resource for sensing field gradients. While the field gradient induces a detuning between the emitters, the large interatomic separations render the system dynamics non-Markovian. We show that the quantum Fisher information for estimating the detuning parameter, and thereby the field gradient, is enhanced in the presence of non-Markovian delay. Such an enhancement can be attributed to the formation of atom-photon quasibound states that enable the field to interact with the emitters for longer times, thereby gaining more information about their relative detunings. Additionally, in the presence of delay, the interaction between the emitters is mediated via multiple spectral modes of the field, further enhancing the sensing capabilities of the system. Our results establish non-Markovian time-delayed feedback and multimode reservoirs as a resource for distributed quantum sensing with waveguide-coupled quantum emitters.","authors":"Prajit Dhara, Isack Padilla, Saikat Guha, Annyun Das, Kanu Sinha","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-05":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Science","doi":"10.1126/science.adv1247","id":"doi:10.1126/science.adv1247","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adv1247","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Quantum gas microscopy of three-flavor Hubbard systems","summary":"Hubbard systems are paradigmatic realizations of strongly correlated many-body systems. Introducing additional species breaks the SU(2) symmetry of the Hubbard model and leads to a wide variety of new exotic quantum phases. Here, we extended quantum gas microscopy to three-flavor Fermi lattice gases in the Hubbard regime. Relying on site- and flavor-resolved detection, we studied the phase diagram of the three-flavor Hubbard model and found signatures of flavor-selective localization and selective pairing at temperatures down to the tunneling energy scale. Our measurements were then compared with numerical linked-cluster expansion calculations. Further increase of phase space density may enable the observation of a distinct pair Mott phase at half filling and shows a path toward the study of color superfluidity and other aspects of quantum chromodynamics.","authors":"Jirayu Mongkolkiattichai, Liyu Liu, Sohail Dasgupta, Kaden R. A. Hazzard, Peter Schauss","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/vd45-l8d5","id":"doi:10.1103/vd45-l8d5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vd45-l8d5","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Path to Quantum Simulations of Topological Phases: ( 2 + 1 ) D Quantum Electrodynamics with Wilson Fermions","summary":"Quantum simulation offers a powerful approach to studying quantum field theories, particularly ( 2 + 1 ) D quantum electrodynamics ( QED 3 ) with Wilson fermions, which hosts a rich landscape of physical phenomena. A key challenge in lattice formulations is the proper realization of topological phases and the Chern-Simons terms, where fermion discretization plays a crucial role. In this Letter, we highlight the differences between staggered and Wilson fermions coupled to U(1) gauge fields in the Hamiltonian formulation. We analyze why staggered fermions fail to induce ( 2 + 1 ) D topological phases, while Wilson fermions admit a variety of topological phases including Chern insulator and quantum spin Hall phases. Additionally, we uncover a rich phase diagram for the two-flavor Wilson fermion model in the presence of a chemical potential. Our findings resolve existing ambiguities in Hamiltonian formulations and provide a theoretical foundation for future quantum simulations of lattice field theories with topological phases. We further outline connections to experimental platforms, offering guidance for implementations on near-term quantum computing architectures. A complementary presentation of the analytical calculations, the identification of robust topological structure and response, and extensive numerical results is contained in a joint submission [S. Bharadwaj , , 094502 (2026)].","authors":"Sriram Bharadwaj, Emil Rosanowski, Simran Singh, Alice Di Tucci, Changnan Peng, Karl Jansen, Lena Funcke, Di Luo","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/7fd5-1kq5","id":"doi:10.1103/7fd5-1kq5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7fd5-1kq5","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Heavy Quark Transport Is Non-Gaussian beyond Leading Logarithm","summary":"We find that heavy quark transport beyond leading logarithm at weak coupling is intrinsically non-Gaussian: the longitudinal momentum transfer distribution has that are crucial for equilibration dynamics. We show this by computing the leading-order momentum transfer kernel for relativistic heavy quarks in weakly coupled non-Abelian plasmas, matching perturbative momentum transfer on the thermal scale to hard-thermal-loop-resummed soft physics. This is the same structure previously found in strongly coupled holographic plasmas, showing that it is not peculiar to weak or strong coupling, conformality, or supersymmetry. We therefore expect that this is a robust feature that physical quark-gluon plasma should also exhibit.","authors":"Jean F. Du Plessis, Bruno Scheihing-Hitschfeld","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/2vg9-34y9","id":"doi:10.1103/2vg9-34y9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2vg9-34y9","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Bag Parameters for Heavy Meson Lifetimes from a Lattice QCD Calculation at the SU ( 3 ) F -symmetric Point","summary":"We calculate the dimension-six Δ Q = 0 four-quark matrix elements describing heavy-meson lifetime ratios using the gradient flow with its short flow-time expansion (SFTX) as a renormalization procedure. On six RBC/UKQCD 2 + 1 -flavor domain-wall fermion ensembles, we determine flowed bag parameters for physical charm and strange quarks and match to the MS ¯ scheme with perturbative SFTX coefficients through next-to-next-to-leading order (NNLO). A multiscale matching procedure using renormalization-group running improves the extrapolation to zero flow time. For the operators relevant to τ ( D s ) / τ ( D 0 ) at the SU ( 3 ) F symmetric point, we obtain B 1 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = 1.0524 ( 97 ) , B 2 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = 0.9621 ( 70 ) , ε 1 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = − 0.2275 ( 76 ) , and ε 2 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = − 0.0005 ( 8 ) using a specific choice of evanescent operators. This is the first lattice-QCD determination of Δ Q = 0 four-quark operators with a full error budget. It opens the path toward higher-precision predictions of heavy-meson lifetimes and similar quantities exhibiting operator mixing under renormalization.","authors":"Matthew Black, Robert V. Harlander, Jonas T. Kohnen, Fabian Lange, Antonio Rago, Andrea Shindler, Oliver Witzel","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/t2qc-469r","id":"doi:10.1103/t2qc-469r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/t2qc-469r","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Searching for Axion Dark Matter via Superconductor-Spin Hybrid Magnetometers","summary":"","authors":"Runqi Kang, Jian Zhang, Liangliang Rong, Xing Rong","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/y942-mxtx","id":"doi:10.1103/y942-mxtx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/y942-mxtx","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Inclusive P -wave quarkonium decay widths from lattice QCD and potential nonrelativistic QCD","summary":"","authors":"Nora Brambilla, Viljami Leino, Julian Mayer-Steudte, Panayiotis Panayiotou, Andrea Shindler, Antonio Vairo, Xiang-Peng Wang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/my8d-tdpk","id":"doi:10.1103/my8d-tdpk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/my8d-tdpk","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Contextuality tests in photonic integrated chips with imperfect compatibility","summary":"","authors":"Patrick M. R. Lima, Wilder R. Cardoso, Sebastião Pádua","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/ng5j-lw7n","id":"doi:10.1103/ng5j-lw7n","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ng5j-lw7n","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Classical limit: Dissipation of Spekkens’ generalized contextuality under decoherence","summary":"","authors":"Enrico Bozzetto, Jonte R. Hance","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/47j9-h8s5","id":"doi:10.1103/47j9-h8s5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/47j9-h8s5","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Heavy-meson bag parameters using gradient flow","summary":"We demonstrate the use of the gradient flow combined with the short flow-time expansion ( GF + SFTX ) as a renormalization procedure for four-quark operator matrix elements and associated bag parameters relevant to neutral heavy-meson mixing ( Δ Q = 2 ) and heavy-meson lifetimes ( Δ Q = 0 ). Using six RBC/UKQCD 2 + 1 -flavor domain-wall fermion ensembles, we calculate for a charm-strange system with physical quark masses flowed bag parameters and match them to the MS ¯ scheme using perturbative SFTX coefficients up to next-to-next-to-leading order in QCD. We employ a multiscale matching strategy and a renormalization-group improved flow-time evolution which allows for a reliable estimate of systematic uncertainties. For a fictitious neutral D s meson, we obtain the Δ Q = 2 MS ¯ bag parameter B 1 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = 0.7673 ( 123 ) , consistent with existing short-distance D 0 mixing determinations. For the Δ Q = 0 lifetime-ratio operator basis, we find the MS ¯ results B 1 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = 1.0524 ( 97 ) , B 2 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = 0.9621 ( 71 ) , ε 1 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = − 0.2275 ( 76 ) , and ε 2 MS ¯ ( 3 GeV ) = − 0.0005 ( 8 ) . We provide conversion formulae to reexpress these results for an arbitrary choice of evanescent operators. These results demonstrate that GF + SFTX can deliver precise determinations of dimension-six four-quark operators and establish a framework for future lattice computations including more complex operator bases, where the challenge of power-divergent mixing is shifted to the continuum and handled in the SFTX.","authors":"Matthew Black, Robert V. Harlander, Jonas T. Kohnen, Fabian Lange, Antonio Rago, Andrea Shindler, Oliver Witzel","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/rwv6-8hr3","id":"doi:10.1103/rwv6-8hr3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/rwv6-8hr3","published":"2026-11-05","title":"DMRadio-Core: A new approach for GUT-scale axion searches","summary":"Searches for QCD axions with masses in the n eV / c 2 mass range are strongly motivated by new physics at the grand unified theory (GUT) scale and by well-motivated preinflationary axion symmetry breaking scales. This parameter space is challenging to probe due to the small axion-photon couplings, which typically require large, high-field magnets with substantial stored energy. In this paper, we propose a new experimental geometry based on a narrow-bore, segmented solenoid that optimizes the collection of the axion-induced signal using LC resonators outside the high-field region of the magnet bore. This alternative optimization significantly reduces the required stored magnetic energy while preserving sensitivity, thus enabling a near-term experiment in the 30–200 MHz ( 120 − 830 n eV / c 2 ) range, with a cost-effective, staged scaling to a GUT-scale experiment in the 100 kHz–30 MHz ( 0.4 − 120 n eV / c 2 ) range.","authors":"V. Ankel, C. Bartram, J. Begin, C. Bell, S. Chaudhuri, H.-M. Cho, J. Corbin, W. Craddock, S. Cuadra, A. Droster, J. Echevers, E. Engelhardt, J. T. Fry, K. D. Irwin, A. Keller, R. Kolevatov, A. Kunder, N. Kurita, N. Otto, E. Pariset et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/djwc-193y","id":"doi:10.1103/djwc-193y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/djwc-193y","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Testing exotic electron-electron interactions with the helium ionization-energy anomaly","summary":"Precision atomic spectroscopy provides a sensitive probe of physics beyond the Standard Model. A recently reported 9 σ theory-experiment discrepancy in the ionization energy of metastable helium has motivated the hypothesis of a new boson mediating exotic electron-electron interactions. Using a general sign-consistency analysis of the induced energy shifts, we show that the sign requirement alone excludes vector-vector and pseudoscalar-pseudoscalar interactions as possible explanations of the anomaly. Incorporating existing constraints together with independent limits obtained in this work further excludes axial-vector scenarios. Within the single-boson framework considered in this work, only a narrowly constrained scalar-mediated interaction remains viable. The remaining parameter space could be probed in several different ways—for example, by modest improvements in the determination of the electron gyromagnetic ratio.","authors":"Lei Cong, Filip Ficek, Rinat Abdullin, Mikhail G. Kozlov, Dmitry Budker","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1sr6-rb39","id":"doi:10.1103/1sr6-rb39","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1sr6-rb39","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Correlation of galactic very-high-energy gamma-ray sources detected by LHAASO with high-energy neutrinos","summary":"","authors":"Long-Yu Zhang, Kai Wang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/lb32-vp2m","id":"doi:10.1103/lb32-vp2m","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lb32-vp2m","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Graviton floor","summary":"","authors":"Himeka Matsuo, Asuka Ito, Kazunori Kohri, Teruaki Suyama, Ryutaro Tomomatsu","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/jvw4-pdg1","id":"doi:10.1103/jvw4-pdg1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jvw4-pdg1","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Asymmetric dark matter from low-scale spontaneous leptogenesis","summary":"We investigate a novel type of asymmetric dark matter (ADM) model in which the dark matter asymmetry and the baryon asymmetry in our Universe (BAU) are produced simultaneously via low-scale spontaneous leptogenesis, where the mass scale of right-handed neutrino is much lower than the Davidson–Ibarra bound M 1 ≪ 10 9 GeV . In our scenario, both asymmetries are predominantly sourced by a dynamical C P phase, namely, the Majoron. Its kinetic misalignment provides a sufficiently large, time-dependent effective C P phase, allowing efficient asymmetry production even for low-scale right-handed neutrinos. In our framework, the sources of C P violation responsible for the BAU and ADM are correlated with each other, leading to a predictive relation for the dark matter mass. In particular, when the dark matter asymmetry reaches its equilibrium value before freeze-out, the dark matter mass is typically predicted to lie in the range O ( 0.1 ) GeV ≲ m χ ≲ O ( 100 ) GeV , which lies within the sensitivity of direct detection experiments. On the other hand, if the dark matter asymmetry does not reach its equilibrium value due to weak coupling, the allowed mass range extends over a broader interval, O ( 0.1 ) GeV ≲ m χ ≲ O ( 10 ) TeV .","authors":"Hiroki Takahashi, Juntaro Wada","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/w7zp-vz6k","id":"doi:10.1103/w7zp-vz6k","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/w7zp-vz6k","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Validity domains for semiclassical time in quantum cosmology","summary":"We investigate the domain in which a semiclassical gravitational Wentzel–Kramers–Brillouin (WKB) flow can serve as a quantitatively controlled temporal reference for subordinate quantum degrees of freedom. Rather than introducing WKB time at the outset, we formulate the Born-Oppenheimer adiabatic condition directly on gravitational configuration space using the Hamilton-Jacobi vector field. This separates adiabatic control from the local WKB validity of the gravitational branch and from the sharpness of the candidate reference against quantum fluctuations. For a single selected semiclassical branch, these requirements define the validity domain D t = D WKB ∩ D ad ∩ D sharp , with an additional decoherence condition when coherent branches coexist. In a closed Friedmann-Robertson-Walker (FRW) minisuperspace coupled to a homogeneous massive scalar mode, the distinction is explicit: approaching the gravitational turning point from the oscillatory region, the ordinary WKB control parameter diverges while the configuration-space adiabatic parameter tends to zero. A uniform Airy treatment of the turning point removes the formal WKB divergence while preserving the inequivalence: the corresponding local control parameter remains of order unity, whereas the configuration-space adiabatic parameter is parametrically suppressed as O ( M − 1 / 3 ) . Thus Born-Oppenheimer adiabaticity and gravitational WKB validity define inequivalent domains even within the simplest minisuperspace model. We conclude that the existence of an oscillatory semiclassical branch is not sufficient for a reliable WKB temporal description: semiclassical temporal reliability is instead determined by the overlap of the validity domains supporting the reference flow.","authors":"Gianpaolo Rosati, Sergio L. Cacciatori","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/3w9f-wyvl","id":"doi:10.1103/3w9f-wyvl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/3w9f-wyvl","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Quasisteady flavor configuration of multienergy neutrino ensembles","summary":"","authors":"Manuel Goimil-García, Irene Tamborra","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dds6-q2wy","id":"doi:10.1103/dds6-q2wy","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dds6-q2wy","published":"2026-11-05","title":"High-precision numerical framework for the time-varying solar neutrino flux with full Earth matter oscillation corrections for global underground laboratories","summary":"","authors":"Keyu Han, Isabella Yin, Kaoru Yagi, Kevin Yifan Jiang, Xiangpan Ji, Shaomin Chen","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2v46-g6vv","id":"doi:10.1103/2v46-g6vv","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2v46-g6vv","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Singlet-doublet dark matter beyond freeze-out","summary":"","authors":"Prudhvi N. Bhattiprolu, Aaron Pierce, Alexander Takla","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/gfv4-qc3s","id":"doi:10.1103/gfv4-qc3s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gfv4-qc3s","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Quantum Gibbs Sampling Through the Detectability Lemma","summary":"Gibbs state preparation is an important subroutine in quantum computing. In this work we use the detectability lemma to improve Gibbs state preparation. Specifically, we design new Gibbs state preparation methods that do not rely on simulating Lindbladian evolution, thus avoiding the overhead from it. For local Lindbladians consisting of M terms, this approach reduces the cost by a factor of O ( M ) . We also combine the detectability lemma operator and quantum singular value transformation to implement ground state projection operators of frustration-free Hamiltonians, resulting in a quadratic speedup in the spectral gap dependence. Applying this method to Lindbladians for the Gibbs state of local commuting Hamiltonians, we achieve quadratically better dependence on the Lindbladian spectral gap.","authors":"Di Fang, Jianfeng Lu, Yu Tong, Chu Zhao","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/x996-xl38","id":"doi:10.1103/x996-xl38","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/x996-xl38","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Fine-structure transfer as a fluorescence readout channel in optically pumped magnetometers","summary":"","authors":"Wanqing Li, Wei Li, Jiaxin Liu","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/jp17-mvpr","id":"doi:10.1103/jp17-mvpr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jp17-mvpr","published":"2026-11-05","title":"Measurement-informed dynamical decoupling for fidelity preservation on large-scale quantum processors","summary":"","authors":"Jeongwoo Jae, Changwon Lee, Juzar Thingna, Yeong-Dae Kwon, Daniel K. Park","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]}]},"2026-11-04":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/flqh-zxzr","id":"doi:10.1103/flqh-zxzr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/flqh-zxzr","published":"2026-11-04","title":"First measurement of the D s + → K * ( 892 ) 0 μ + ν μ decay, study of dynamics and test of lepton universality with D s + → K * ( 892 ) 0 ℓ + ν ℓ decays","summary":"","authors":"M. Ablikim, M. N. Achasov, P. Adlarson, X. C. Ai, C. S. Akondi, R. Aliberti, A. Amoroso, Q. An, Y. H. An, Y. Bai, O. Bakina, Y. Ban, H.-R. Bao, X. L. Bao, V. Batozskaya, K. Begzsuren, N. Berger, M. Berlowski, M. B. Bertani, D. Bettoni et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/nj6b-msgw","id":"doi:10.1103/nj6b-msgw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nj6b-msgw","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Quantum algorithms for photoreactivity in cancer-targeted photosensitizers","summary":"","authors":"Yanbing Zhou, Pablo A. M. Casares, Diksha Dhawan, Ignacio Loaiza, Soran Jahangiri, Robert A. Lang, Juan Miguel Arrazola, Stepan Fomichev","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/czyc-4qgq","id":"doi:10.1103/czyc-4qgq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/czyc-4qgq","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Emergence of dual axion response in condensed matter","summary":"","authors":"Elina Kokurina, Dmitry Vagin, Eduardo Barredo-Alamilla, Maxim A. Gorlach","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/ysgv-4dd7","id":"doi:10.1103/ysgv-4dd7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ysgv-4dd7","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Gradient flow towards quantum states with ideal quantum geometry","summary":"","authors":"Takumi Shiga, Takahiro Fukui","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1rsq-cv2m","id":"doi:10.1103/1rsq-cv2m","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1rsq-cv2m","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Evolving dark sector and the dark dimension scenario","summary":"String theory naturally leads to the expectation that dark energy is not stable, and may be evolving as captured by the Swampland de Sitter conjectures. Moreover, motivated by the distance conjecture, a unification of dark sector has been proposed, where the smallness of dark energy leads to one extra dimension of micron size with dark matter being the Kaluza-Klein graviton excitations in this extra dimension. We consider the natural possibility that the radius of the dark dimension varies as the energy decreases, leading to the variation of the dark matter mass. This correlates the variation of the dark energy with the variation of the dark matter mass as they depend on the variations of a scalar field ϕ controlling the radius of the extra dimension. A simple realization of this idea for small range of ϕ is adequately captured by choosing a potential which is locally of the form V = V 0 exp ( − c ϕ ) and dark matter mass m DM = m 0 exp ( − c ′ ϕ ) as was proposed in [1]. We find excellent agreement with recent experimental data from DESI DR2 combined with SN measurements (from DES, Union3 or Pantheon+) and reproduces the same significance as Chevallier–Polarski–Linder parametrization with the added benefit of providing a natural explanation for the apparent phantom behavior ( w < − 1 ) reported by DESI and DES based on a physical model. DESI and SN datasets independently favor nonzero values of c ′ and c , respectively, both lying within the expected O ( 1 ) range suggested by the Swampland criteria. Moreover, our best fit value c ′ ≃ 0.05 ± 0.01 is remarkably consistent with the experimental upper bound of c ′ ≲ 0.2 demanded by the lack of detection of fifth force in the dark sector.","authors":"Alek Bedroya, Georges Obied, Cumrun Vafa, David H. Wu","topics":["dark","qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/29wm-ycwp","id":"doi:10.1103/29wm-ycwp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/29wm-ycwp","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Spin entanglement signatures of proton from a light-front Hamiltonian","summary":"Quantum entanglement provides a quantitative probe of the internal structure of hadrons and offers a sensitive means to study the quantum correlation in the hadron wave functions. For baryons, the spin state of the three valence quarks forms a tripartite qubit system, whose entanglement structure can be characterized by the four classes of three-qubit states. In this work, we compare the proton spin entanglement obtained from Basis Light-Front Quantization (BLFQ) with that from a quark-diquark model. By analyzing both bipartite and tripartite entanglement, we find that the quark-diquark model yields a substantially more entangled spin state than the BLFQ wave function in the valence Fock sector. This difference mainly originates from the larger W-type and Bell-type entanglement in the quark-diquark model. Within BLFQ, a larger coupling constant and smaller quark mass drive the spin correlation among the valence quarks towards an effective quark-diquark configuration with an active d quark and a correlated u u pair.","authors":"Chen Qian, Siqi Xu, Yang-Guang Yang, Xingbo Zhao","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/jf2z-7ws8","id":"doi:10.1103/jf2z-7ws8","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jf2z-7ws8","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Static quark-antiquark interactions in rotating SU(3) gluodynamics","summary":"We study static quark-antiquark interactions in rotating SU(3) gluodynamics using quenched lattice simulations at imaginary angular velocity. At zero temperature, we extract the static potential from Wilson loops for quark-antiquark pairs aligned with the rotation axis, for transverse pairs with one source on the rotation axis, and for symmetric transverse pairs across the rotation axis. Within the present accuracy, no significant rotation dependence or anisotropy is observed in the zero-temperature potential. At finite temperature, imaginary rotation suppresses the color-averaged free energies obtained from Polyakov-loop correlators in both longitudinal and transverse geometries. Axial-diagonal comparisons are used to identify a bulk region where open-boundary artifacts are reduced. In this region, the large-distance longitudinal free-energy shift is well described by Δ F z ( R x y ) = A R x y 2 + B . The transverse channels exhibit the same qualitative suppression, while their distance dependence additionally reflects the radial arrangement of the static sources and is compatible with a radial single-source free-energy shift in the bulk region. For the finite-temperature observables studied above T c , the response weakens as the temperature is increased. These results provide lattice evidence for a position- and geometry-dependent response of bare static-source free energies to imaginary rotation in a gluonic medium.","authors":"Heng-Tong Ding, Olaf Kaczmarek, Ran Luo, Hai-Tao Shu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/wm41-k9xw","id":"doi:10.1103/wm41-k9xw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wm41-k9xw","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Photon emission from rotating plasmas: A generalized McLerran-Toimela formula and the onset of superradiance","summary":"The photon emission and absorption spectra of a plasma rotating with constant angular velocity Ω are derived in terms of the spectral function of the current-current correlator. In contrast to the nonrotating case, the leading contribution arises already at one-loop order. The photon emission spectrum and its elliptic flow are calculated for a rotating quark-gluon plasma and compared with the leading two-loop result for a nonrotating plasma. The rotating plasma is found to emit significantly more soft photons than the nonrotating one. An analysis of the emission and absorption of cylindrical waves indicates that photons with energy ω and azimuthal quantum number m satisfying ω < m Ω are emitted at a higher rate than they are absorbed, thereby exhibiting the phenomenon of superradiance. It is further argued that these superradiant modes induce an instability of the magnetic field that is generated concurrently with the plasma in relativistic heavy-ion collisions.","authors":"Kirill Tuchin","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/nlgk-tfq5","id":"doi:10.1103/nlgk-tfq5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nlgk-tfq5","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Testing the robustness in the evidence for dynamical dark energy through minimal and generalized modeling approaches","summary":"In this letter we argue that the commonly adopted Chevallier-Polarski-Linder (CPL) parametrization for the dark energy equation of state (EoS) is biased toward preferring such model over the constant w while the latter bounds are still compatible with their Λ cold dark matter ( Λ CDM ) values. For that we compare constraints on the EoS parameters w 0 and early type w a (CPL) against those with a late-time parametrization on w a (hereafter LZ) and the constant w model, using cosmic microwave background, Supernovae, and baryonic acoustic oscillations from Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument datasets. We found, the same as was the case with the CPL model, preference for dynamical dark energy within the LZ model, but for values almost symmetrically distributed with respect to their Λ CDM limits. This is due to the fact that the presence of w 0 allows to recast each parametrization into making it compensate the preference for w ∼ − 1 in the opposite direction. To further test our hypothesis, we fixed w 0 to − 1 and followed a minimal investigation approach by considering models that deviate only by one free parameter following the same previous parametrizations, making them now genuinely different from each other. On the other hand, we extend our models to more general ones by either incorporating parametrizations that group both late and early w a effects, which we name the generalized dark energy equation of state (GEoS) model, or by allowing the presence of two dark energy components, fluidlike Ω DE and constantlike Ω CC . We also complement conclusions drawn from the significance levels from the resulting confidence contours with that of the Bayes factor calculation. We found that all the variants, except the original CPL or LZ, are still compatible with Λ CDM , with likelihoods peaking close to w 0 = − 1 , w a = 0 , or 0.68 for Ω CC , with the constant w and the late time w a (minimal LZ) being the ones with the tightest constraints. Although we found that the evidence from CPL or LZ is stronger than those for the more minimal cases, however, the preference increases further for the more generalized parametrizations, instead of penalizing the addition of the extra degrees of freedom, while still staying compatible with Λ CDM in terms of the significance levels. We conclude that considering the CPL model is not sufficient on its own to test deviations from the simple cosmological constant model, and that it is necessary to conduct further minimal or more general approaches when confronting models to observations, so as to localize biases, if they exist, or to better understand the outcomes from model testing and inference methods. Note that the word “bias” here is considered to mean any artifact effect in general and not specifically only a shift in the parameter confidence contours.","authors":"Ziad Sakr","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bz2v-mls7","id":"doi:10.1103/bz2v-mls7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bz2v-mls7","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Non-Abelian A 4 vortices in S O ( 3 ) gauge theory and noninvertible symmetries","summary":"We construct finite-tension non-Abelian vortex solutions in a renormalizable ( 3 + 1 )-dimensional S O ( 3 ) gauge theory Higgsed to the tetrahedral group A 4 by a Higgs field in the spin-3 representation. Since the vacuum manifold is S O ( 3 ) / A 4 , the vortices are characterized by the non-Abelian fundamental group π 1 ( S O ( 3 ) / A 4 ) ≃ A ˜ 4 , the binary tetrahedral group. We obtain explicit axisymmetric vortex solutions carrying holonomies corresponding to the order-two and order-three conjugacy classes of A 4 , determine their tensions numerically, and show that they exhibit type-I, type-II, and Bogomol’nyi-Prasad-Sommerfield-like behavior depending on the Higgs and gauge boson mass ratios. The vortices are classified by conjugacy classes of A ˜ 4 , while their infrared descriptions are labeled by conjugacy classes of A 4 . We further demonstrate that the smooth finite-tension vortices reduce in the infrared to Gukov-Witten surface operators of the A 4 discrete gauge theory, thereby establishing a finite-energy ultraviolet completion of noninvertible defects in a renormalizable gauge-Higgs theory.","authors":"Yoshihiko Abe, Tetsutaro Higaki, Kazuya Murakami, Muneto Nitta, Ryo Yokokura","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8j6x-94gf","id":"doi:10.1103/8j6x-94gf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8j6x-94gf","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Precision solar system dynamics for ultralight dark matter search","summary":"Ultralight dark matter exhibits an order-1 density fluctuation at the scale of its wavelength. This density fluctuation exists across the entire dark matter halo and interacts with stars and planets, perturbing their motion via gravitational interactions. We investigate the possibility of using precision solar system dynamics to search for ultralight dark matter. We examine this possibility with interplanetary radio range measurements. We show that the precision of current range measurements can probe ultralight dark matter at masses around 10 − 15 eV , had its density in the solar system been 10 5 larger than the so-called local dark matter density. This limit complements other constraints, such as the one from analyses of pulsar timing observations.","authors":"Jonas Frerick, Hyungjin Kim, Felix Kling","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/svh2-j38f","id":"doi:10.1103/svh2-j38f","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/svh2-j38f","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Bayesian inference of the magnetic component in the quark-gluon plasma","summary":"The chromomagnetic monopoles (CMM), emergent topological excitations of non-Abelian gauge fields carrying chromomagnetic charge, have long been postulated to play an important role in the vacuum confinement of quantum chromodynamics (QCD), the deconfinement transition at temperature T c ≈ 160 MeV , as well as the strongly coupled nature of quark-gluon plasma (QGP). While such CMMs were found to help provide resolutions for challenging puzzles from heavy-ion collision measurements, they were typically introduced as model assumptions in the past. Here we perform a data-driven Bayesian inference of the chromomagnetic component, within the CUJET framework, and extract its temperature-dependent abundance for the first time. Using the 3 framework for calculations of jet energy loss and analyzing a comprehensive experimental dataset for nuclear modification factor ( R AA ) and elliptic flow ( v 2 ) of high-transverse-momentum hadrons, the fraction of CMMs in the QGP is obtained by Bayesian inference and is found to be substantial in the 1 ∼ 2 T c region. The posterior CMM fraction is further tested against additional data and is also shown to predict QGP transport properties that are quantitatively consistent with the state-of-the-art knowledge.","authors":"Yu Guo, Jinfeng Liao, Shuzhe Shi","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8xjl-cssp","id":"doi:10.1103/8xjl-cssp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8xjl-cssp","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Tidal heating of stellar clusters in fuzzy dark matter halos","summary":"","authors":"Yiheng Liu, Xinyu Li","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/th33-bhyd","id":"doi:10.1103/th33-bhyd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/th33-bhyd","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Scalar vacuum polarization in loop-quantum-gravity black holes","summary":"","authors":"Antonino Flachi, Marco Pasini","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/9cvt-lt8d","id":"doi:10.1103/9cvt-lt8d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/9cvt-lt8d","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Updated analysis of charmonium states in a relativized quark potential model","summary":"Motivated by recent experimental observations of charmonium(like) states, we investigate their interpretation as conventional charmonium states within the framework of relativized quark potential model. We find a consistent description of the updated masses of charmonium, bottomonium and some selected charmed mesons, then systematically compute open-charm strong decay widths and electromagnetic transition widths for radially excited charmonium states up to n = 5 . The numerical results show significantly improved agreement with experimental masses and widths. Notably, the newly observed χ c 1 ( 4010 ) and χ c 1 ( 4274 ) are tentatively associated with the χ c 1 ( 2 P ) and χ c 1 ( 3 P ) , respectively, while the χ c 0 ( 4500 ) and χ c 0 ( 4700 ) are interpreted as the χ c 0 ( 4 P ) and χ c 0 ( 5 P ) states. For the controversial χ c 1 ( 3872 ) state, we suggest its potential connection to χ c 1 ( 4010 ) , as a two-pole structure due to the χ c 1 ( 2 P ) coupling to D D ¯ * channels. Intriguingly, the predicted mass of χ c 0 ( 2 P ) is about 3851 MeV, diverging from the original estimate of 3916 MeV in the Godfrey and Isgur’s work but consistent with nonrelativistic potential model predictions. This calculation might provide more insight to the future experimental investigation of charmonium(like) states.","authors":"Xiu-Li Gao, Jun-Xi Cui, Yu-Hui Zhou, Zhi-Yong Zhou","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/zm48-b96z","id":"doi:10.1103/zm48-b96z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/zm48-b96z","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Neutrino production at the extreme vicinity of the supermassive black hole in NGC 1068","summary":"","authors":"Zhi-Peng Ma, Kai Wang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/87vq-rn6f","id":"doi:10.1103/87vq-rn6f","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/87vq-rn6f","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Anisotropic response in bilayer graphene for light dark matter detection","summary":"","authors":"Rinchen Sherpa, Anuvab Sarkar, Tarak Nath Maity, Paramita Dutta, Ranjan Laha, Anirban Das","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/l2dt-2rd2","id":"doi:10.1103/l2dt-2rd2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/l2dt-2rd2","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Strongly lensed gravitational waves as a probe of axion-induced amplitude birefringence","summary":"","authors":"Tonghua Liu, Pan-Pan Wang, Cheng-Gang Shao, Bing Sun","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/7m4p-fzll","id":"doi:10.1103/7m4p-fzll","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7m4p-fzll","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Wave optics effects from gravitational wave propagation through dark matter halos","summary":"","authors":"Annamalai P. Shanmugaraj, Roland Haas, Erik Schnetter, Sofie Marie Koksbang","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/l3r3-mqqg","id":"doi:10.1103/l3r3-mqqg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/l3r3-mqqg","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Search for active-sterile neutrino transitions using Pierre Auger Observatory data","summary":"","authors":"A. Abdul Halim, P. Abreu, M. Aglietta, M. Ahmed, I. Allekotte, K. Almeida Cheminant, R. Aloisio, J. Alvarez-Muñiz, A. Ambrosone, J. Ammerman Yebra, L. Anchordoqui, B. Andrada, L. Andrade Dourado, L. Apollonio, C. Aramo, J. C. Arteaga Velázquez, P. Assis, G. Avila, E. Avocone, A. Bakalova et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/b29h-c1hc","id":"doi:10.1103/b29h-c1hc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/b29h-c1hc","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Lattice study of spin interactions between heavy quarks in the quark-gluon plasma","summary":"","authors":"Dibyendu Bala, Olaf Kaczmarek, Sayantan Sharma, Swagatam Tah","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/qp81-81mz","id":"doi:10.1103/qp81-81mz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/qp81-81mz","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Erratum: Searching for ultralight axions with twisted cavity resonators of anyon rotational symmetry with bulk modes of nonzero helicity [Phys. Rev. D 108, 052014 (2023)]","summary":"","authors":"J. F. Bourhill, R. C. Crew, E. C. I. Paterson, M. Goryachev, M. E. Tobar","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/x8tn-vtsl","id":"doi:10.1103/x8tn-vtsl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/x8tn-vtsl","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Confinement transition to gravitational waves in the one-flavor S U ( 4 ) hyperstealth dark matter theory","summary":"","authors":"V. Ayyar, R. C. Brower, G. T. Fleming, J. Ingoldby, X. Y. Jin, N. Matsumoto, A. S. Meyer, E. T. Neil, J. C. Osborn, S. Park, C. T. Peterson, D. Schaich, P. Vranas, O. Witzel","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/s39j-czpf","id":"doi:10.1103/s39j-czpf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/s39j-czpf","published":"2026-11-04","title":"Temporal Entanglement Transition in Chaotic Quantum Many-Body Dynamics","summary":"Temporal entanglement (TE) of an influence matrix (IM) has been proposed as a measure of the complexity of simulating dynamics of local observables in many-body systems. Foligno recently argued that the TE in chaotic 1D quantum circuits obeys linear (volume law) scaling with evolution time. To reconcile this apparent high complexity of IM with the rapid thermalization of local observables, here we study the relation between TE, non-Markovianity, and local temporal correlations for chaotic quantum baths. By exactly solving a random-unitary bath model and bounding distillable entanglement between future and past degrees of freedom, we identify a regime where TE is extensive and reflects genuine non-Markovianity. This memory, however, is entirely contained in highly complex temporal correlations, and its effect on few-point temporal correlators is negligible. An IM coarse-graining procedure, reducing the allowed frequency of measurements of the probe system, results in a transition from volume- to subvolume- (area or logarithmic) law TE scaling. We demonstrate the generality of this TE transition in 1D circuits by analyzing the kicked Ising model analytically at dual-unitary points, as well as numerically away from them. This finding indicates that dynamics of local observables are fully captured by a subvolume-law IM. We provide evidence that the compact IM MPS obtained via standard compression algorithms accurately describes local evolution.","authors":"Ilya Vilkoviskiy, Michael Sonner, Qi Camm Huang, Wen Wei Ho, Alessio Lerose, Dmitry A. Abanin","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-03":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/k5r2-q11w","id":"doi:10.1103/k5r2-q11w","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/k5r2-q11w","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Kekulé Superconductivity Induced by Quantum Geometry in Haldane Phases","summary":"","authors":"Yafis Barlas, Fan Zhang, Enrico Rossi","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/fyv5-zmx4","id":"doi:10.1103/fyv5-zmx4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fyv5-zmx4","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Lepton double charge exchange reactions as probes for lepton number violation","summary":"","authors":"H. Lenske, F. Cappuzzello, A. Spatafora","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/qdlj-d839","id":"doi:10.1103/qdlj-d839","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/qdlj-d839","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Scalable subsystem-optimized robust control for parallel quantum gates","summary":"","authors":"Xiaodong Yang, Ran Liu, Jun Li","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/lg1w-g9dq","id":"doi:10.1103/lg1w-g9dq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lg1w-g9dq","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Resource generation and dynamical complexities in open random quantum circuits","summary":"","authors":"Paranjoy Chaki, Arkaprava Sil, Priya Ghosh, Ujjwal Sen, Sudipto Singha Roy","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/l95r-wy1x","id":"doi:10.1103/l95r-wy1x","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/l95r-wy1x","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Charmonium production at energies available at the CERN Super Proton Synchrotron and Facility for Antiproton and Ion Research","summary":"In this study we apply the Remler formalism to charmonium production at Super Proton Synchrotron (SPS) and GSI/Facility for Antiproton and Ion Research (FAIR) energies in order to investigate the effects of baryon-rich matter on charmonium production and dissociation in heavy-ion collisions within the parton-hadron-string dynamics. As a first step the Remler formalism is tested in p + p collisions and then applied to p + A collisions in order to extract the nuclear absorption cross section of charmonium, which is then utilized in heavy-ion collisions. We find that the Remler formalism successfully describes charmonium production in heavy-ion collisions at SPS energies when an in-medium heavy quark potential is implemented, in which J / ψ dissociates near T c . Finally, the same formalism is applied to the low CERN/SPS energy and GSI/FAIR energies, where we estimate charmonium production.","authors":"Taesoo Song, Jiaxing Zhao, Joerg Aichelin, Elena Bratkovskaya","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/xw6h-qx5c","id":"doi:10.1103/xw6h-qx5c","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xw6h-qx5c","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Seasonal variation of polar ice: Implications for ultrahigh energy neutrino detectors","summary":"","authors":"A. Kyriacou, S. Prohira, D. Besson","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/tkt6-p2qx","id":"doi:10.1103/tkt6-p2qx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/tkt6-p2qx","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Higgs boson production classifier using weak supervision","summary":"A reliable determination of the Higgs production mechanism in hadron collider experiments is essential to the program of Higgs coupling measurements. We employ weak supervision, classification without labels (CWoLa) in particular, to train deep neural networks to distinguish vector boson fusion from gluon-gluon fusion events using simulated datasets as pseudoexperimental data, demonstrating the potential of reducing biases resulting from Monte Carlo simulations. Models based on the convolutional neural network and the transformer are tested and compared. In particular, the classification performance gets slightly better when the photon information is removed from training on the low-luminosity region of H → γ γ . We explicitly show that the performance can be improved when the training dataset is enlarged by data augmentation using physics-motivated methods. We further demonstrate that the trained model can be successfully applied to the H → Z Z and H → Z γ events, showing that such classifiers are agnostic to Higgs decay modes, provided these decay modes do not involve strong QCD corrections.","authors":"Kai-Feng Chen, Yi-An Chen, Cheng-Wei Chiang, Feng-Yang Hsieh","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/j99v-l6zq","id":"doi:10.1103/j99v-l6zq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/j99v-l6zq","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Machine learning modularity","summary":"Based on a transformer-based sequence-to-sequence architecture combined with a dynamic batching algorithm, this work introduces a machine learning framework for automatically simplifying complex expressions involving multiple elliptic gamma functions, including the q − θ function and the elliptic gamma function. The model is trained to apply algebraic identities, particularly modular transformations associated with the special linear (SL) groups SL ( 2 , Z ) and SL ( 3 , Z ) modular transformations, to reduce heavily scrambled expressions to their canonical forms. Experimental results show that the proposed framework achieves high accuracy on in-distribution tests and maintains robust performance under extrapolation, including substantially deeper scrambling depths than those seen during training. Our results demonstrate that modern sequence-to-sequence transformer models can identify canonical representatives of expressions related by modular identities, providing a practical framework for automating symbolic manipulations involving special functions arising in supersymmetric quantum field theory and string theory.","authors":"Yi Fan, Vishnu Jejjala, Yang Lei","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/75ps-w7b3","id":"doi:10.1103/75ps-w7b3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/75ps-w7b3","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Constraints with CMB lensing on dark matter decays to massive decay products","summary":"","authors":"Anna Bencke, Nanoom Lee, Marc Kamionkowski","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/sm2n-d1mj","id":"doi:10.1103/sm2n-d1mj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/sm2n-d1mj","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Radiative lifting of Z 3 domain-wall degeneracy in a type-III seesaw model: Implications for leptogenesis and gravitational waves","summary":"In this work, we study a Z 3 -symmetric extension of the Standard Model with three hyperchargeless S U ( 2 ) L fermion triplets responsible for neutrino mass generation via the type-III seesaw mechanism, together with a complex scalar singlet χ whose vacuum expectation value spontaneously breaks the Z 3 symmetry. Radiative corrections induced by the Yukawa interactions between the S U ( 2 ) L fermion triplets and the complex scalar singlet χ generate a Coleman-Weinberg vacuum bias that lifts the degeneracy among the Z 3 vacua, leading to the annihilation of unstable domain walls. Consequently, the degeneracy among the Z 3 vacua is lifted radiatively through the Coleman-Weinberg effective potential, generating a dynamical bias term that triggers the annihilation of unstable domain walls. We perform a numerical analysis consistent with current neutrino oscillation data and identify viable regions of parameter space accommodating the observed neutrino masses and leptonic mixing parameters. The observed baryon asymmetry of the Universe is generated through thermal leptogenesis via the out-of-equilibrium decay of the lightest fermion triplet for masses around O ( 10 9 ) GeV , consistent with the type-III seesaw framework. Depending on the choice of model parameters, the predicted gravitational-wave spectrum can fall within the sensitivity reach of future space-based and ground-based gravitational-wave detectors. Our framework therefore establishes a correlation between neutrino mass generation, leptogenesis, radiative domain-wall instability, and gravitational-wave phenomenology.","authors":"Priya, Labh Singh, B. C. Chauhan, Surender Verma","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/3n3s-8t46","id":"doi:10.1103/3n3s-8t46","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/3n3s-8t46","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Freeze-in S U ( 2 ) vector dark matter at low reheating temperature","summary":"The freeze-in mechanism for dark matter (DM) requires extremely feeble interactions with the Standard Model, preventing thermal equilibrium in the early Universe and typically evading experimental detection. However, for sufficiently low reheating temperatures ( T RH ), the observed relic abundance can be realized with larger couplings, opening prospects for experimental searches. In this work, we investigate freeze-in production of S U ( 2 ) HS vector dark matter (VDM) in a low- T RH cosmology. The framework naturally contains three mass-degenerate stable VDM candidates without the need for any additional discrete symmetry. We perform a systematic study of the dark matter phenomenology and identify the parameter space consistent with the observed relic abundance. We work with the approximation of instantaneous reheating and consider the DM abundance to be negligible at T RH . In contrast to conventional freeze-in scenarios, the required DM couplings can be sizable, rendering part of the parameter space already constrained by existing direct searches like PandaX-4T and LZ, while a significant region remains within the reach of future experiments such as DARWIN. Though one can realize the freeze-in mechanism for an Abelian U ( 1 ) X vector-DM model as well, we find that the non-Abelian structure of the S U ( 2 ) HS scenario leads to a distinct feature due to a larger number of dark matter particles, resulting in an enlarged viable parameter space due to the multiplicity of dark matter states.","authors":"Dilip Kumar Ghosh, Sourav Gope, Xiao-Gang He, Xuan Hong, Sk Jeesun","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/n2yv-hlq5","id":"doi:10.1103/n2yv-hlq5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/n2yv-hlq5","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Matter spectral functions from quantum gravity","summary":"We investigate Lorentzian quantum gravity coupled to a template matter sector with gauge fields, scalars, and fermions. In the absence of quantized gravity, the matter sector by itself is renormalizable, but UV-incomplete. Provided quantum gravity offers an asymptotically safe UV completion, we determine the photon and scalar two-point functions in the presence of gravitational fluctuations, and show that both possess a Källén-Lehmann spectral representation. Our results are achieved using functional renormalization adapted for theories in Lorentzian signature. We explain why and how interactions with gravity modify both the infrared as well as the ultraviolet behavior of matter spectral functions. We further determine the corresponding form factors on the level of the quantum effective action. Limitations and extensions of our study are discussed alongside implications for particle physics and unitarity of quantum gravity with matter.","authors":"Varun Kher, Brandon King, Daniel F. Litim, Manuel Reichert","topics":["qgravity","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/lr59-b552","id":"doi:10.1103/lr59-b552","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lr59-b552","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Resonant heterodyne conversion applied to a low-frequency haloscope for dark matter axion searches in the 1–35 MHz range","summary":"We study resonant heterodyne up conversion in the RADES-BabyIAXO haloscope as a method to search for low-mass dark matter axions using microwave cavities. Starting from axion electrodynamics, we derive the axion-induced source term and the power extracted through a readout mode, explicitly accounting for the finite axion linewidth. This leads to effective quality factors that determine the pump-axion mixing, detection bandwidth, and detected signal power. We extend the BI-RME 3D full-wave formulation to heterodyne axion detection in a realistic two-port cavity, including pump leakage into the readout channel. Applying the formalism to the largest RADES-BabyIAXO cavity identifies the quasi − TE 011 − quasi − TM 010 mode pair as a favorable configuration, enabling sensitivity to axion frequencies between 0.9 and 34.6 MHz. Analytical and full-wave predictions show excellent agreement at resonance, while the full-wave model provides a more accurate description off resonance and allows a precise characterization of the pump leakage. We also derive the optimal port couplings that maximize the scanning rate. Upper-limit sensitivity for cryogenic copper and superconducting niobium cavities indicate that, under thermal-noise-limited conditions and assuming sufficient pump-leakage rejection, the experiment could probe axion-photon couplings down to 10 − 15 GeV − 1 at 90% confidence level, representing a significant improvement over previous heterodyne-based searches.","authors":"Jose R. Navarro-Madrid, José Reina-Valero, Alejandro Díaz-Morcillo, Benito Gimeno","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/syx3-t6xt","id":"doi:10.1103/syx3-t6xt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/syx3-t6xt","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Four-fermion condensates in curved spacetimes: A functional approach","summary":"Four-fermion interactions appear in effective descriptions of particle physics, many-body systems, and gravitational theories with fermions. Although the same local operator may enter perturbative scattering, a vacuum Nambu–Jona-Lasinio (NJL) instability, or finite-density Bardeen-Cooper-Schrieffer (BCS) pairing, these regimes are distinguished by their interaction channels, quadratic kernels, and quantum states. We give a pedagogical functional account of these distinctions and compute the local one-loop contribution of a scalar-channel NJL mean field to the energy-momentum tensor in curved spacetime. We first display the state data in the in-out functional and construct the closed-time-path functional required for an in-in expectation value. For the NJL saddle, a Hubbard-Stratonovich field shifts the fermion mass, and the parity-even Dirac determinant generates local volume, curvature, and curvature-squared operators. We find the covariant quantum effective action before specializing to a spatially flat FLRW background and derive the corresponding energy density and pressure. The constant-condensate limit agrees with the direct flat-space mean-field calculation. We also explain which additional state and channel data are required for finite-density BCS pairing and comment on renormalization conditions in curved spacetimes.","authors":"Stephon Alexander, Heliudson Bernardo, Jiatong Yan","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/mw5s-cd4r","id":"doi:10.1103/mw5s-cd4r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mw5s-cd4r","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Holographic QCD axion in five dimensions","summary":"","authors":"Csaba Csáki, Eric Kuflik, Wei Xue, Taewook Youn","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/vztf-s6t6","id":"doi:10.1103/vztf-s6t6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vztf-s6t6","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Coherent collective response in many-qubit systems for dark matter detection","summary":"","authors":"Ryuichiro Kitano, Ryoto Takai","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/pxm4-ljwr","id":"doi:10.1103/pxm4-ljwr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/pxm4-ljwr","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Partial relief of the Hubble tension and a natural self-interacting dark matter candidate from staged symmetry breaking","summary":"","authors":"Zachary J. Hoelscher, Thomas W. Kephart, Robert J. Scherrer, Kelly Holley-Bockelmann","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/266n-9qrs","id":"doi:10.1103/266n-9qrs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/266n-9qrs","published":"2026-11-03","title":"New constraints on nonunitary neutrino mixing from 8 years of IceCube DeepCore atmospheric neutrino data","summary":"","authors":"Sharmistha Chattopadhyay, Anil Kumar, Sanjib Kumar Agarwalla","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/d59r-yk66","id":"doi:10.1103/d59r-yk66","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/d59r-yk66","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Generalized chiral U ( 1 ) B − L : Toward a less constrained Z ′ and dark matter","summary":"","authors":"Ranjeet Kumar, Hemant Kumar Prajapati","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/ffxz-ttw7","id":"doi:10.1103/ffxz-ttw7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ffxz-ttw7","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Quantum algorithm for the collision-coalescence of cloud droplets","summary":"","authors":"Kazumasa Ueno, Hiroaki Miura","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/kg8g-r3qk","id":"doi:10.1103/kg8g-r3qk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kg8g-r3qk","published":"2026-11-03","title":"Nanophotonic quantum magnetometry in a spin-dense diamond cavity","summary":"","authors":"Nicholas J. Sorensen, Elham Zohari, Joshua S. Wildeman, Sigurd Flågan, Vinaya K. Kavatamane, Paul E. Barclay","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-02":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/wg2x-z2vm","id":"doi:10.1103/wg2x-z2vm","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wg2x-z2vm","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Exploring Noisy Quantum Thermodynamical Processes via the Depolarizing-Channel Approximation","summary":"Noise and errors are unavoidable in any realistic quantum process, including processes designed to reduce noise and errors in the first place. In particular, quantum thermodynamical protocols for cooling can be significantly affected, potentially altering both their performance and efficiency. Analytically characterizing the impact of such errors becomes increasingly challenging as the system size grows, particularly in deep quantum circuits where noise can accumulate in complex ways. To address this, we introduce a general framework for approximating the cumulative effect of gate-dependent noise using a global depolarizing channel. We specify the regime in which this approximation provides a reliable description of the noisy dynamics. Applying our framework to the thermodynamical two-sort algorithmic cooling (TSAC) protocol, we analytically derive its asymptotic cooling limit in the presence of noise. Using the cooling limit, the optimal cooling performance is achieved by a finite number of qubits—distinguished from the conventional noiseless TSAC protocol by an infinite number of qubits—and fundamental bounds on the achievable ground-state population are derived. This approach opens new avenues for exploring noisy quantum thermodynamical processes.","authors":"Jian Li, Xiaoyang Wang, Marcus Huber, Nicolai Friis, Pharnam Bakhshinezhad","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/v86t-jr6y","id":"doi:10.1103/v86t-jr6y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v86t-jr6y","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Dynamical and stable spin squeezing via magnon Kerr nonlinearity in a hybrid solid-state system","summary":"","authors":"Deyi Kong, Xiaokai Zhang, Fei Wang, Xin-You Lü","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/8bzz-rw2j","id":"doi:10.1103/8bzz-rw2j","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8bzz-rw2j","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Singlet-only always-on gapless exchange spin qubits: Charge noise effects and two-qubit gates","summary":"","authors":"Nathan L. Foulk, Katharina Laubscher, Silas Hoffman, Sankar Das Sarma","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/bg21-83qt","id":"doi:10.1103/bg21-83qt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bg21-83qt","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Magneto-oscillations, nonlinearity, and nonreciprocity of Coulomb drag in quantum circuits","summary":"","authors":"Alex Levchenko, Mingyang Zheng, Dominique Laroche","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/dg6d-6cv3","id":"doi:10.1103/dg6d-6cv3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dg6d-6cv3","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Neutrino absorption in two-flavor color-superconducting quark matter","summary":"We calculate the absorption mean free paths of electron and muon neutrinos in two-flavor color-superconducting (2SC) quark matter in the density and temperature range that is relevant to binary neutron star mergers. We model the strong interaction between quarks using a Nambu–Jona-Lasinio model, performing calculations self-consistently in the mean-field approximation. Since the 2SC gap is large we restrict our analysis to the contribution of unpaired quarks. We find that at low temperatures absorption by a down quark ν + d → u + e − / μ − is kinematically not allowed, so absorption by a strange quark, ν + s → u + e − / μ − , dominates the mean free path. As temperature or neutrino energy rises, the d quark absorption channel becomes active, and the mean free path shrinks. We find that in equilibrated 2SC matter with an electron lepton fraction Y L e = 0.1 , the neutrinos form a degenerate gas with a mean free path of meters or less, independently of the temperature.","authors":"M. G. Alford, L. Brodie, M. Buballa, H. Gholami, A. Haber, M. Hofmann","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/xf8c-823x","id":"doi:10.1103/xf8c-823x","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xf8c-823x","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Crossing into the m a > f a region for leptophilic ALPs","summary":"Axion-like particles (ALPs) are typically identified as pseudoscalars whose couplings are shift-symmetry invariant with the exception of their couplings to gauge bosons and their mass term. Additionally, the ALP mass m a is usually assumed to be (much) smaller than the ALP decay constant f a . The latter condition is conservative, at best, and excludes part of the ALP parameter space that is presently viable. We revisit the criterion of the m a ≪ f a and perform an analysis focusing on leptophilic ALPs. In particular, we explore regions of the parameter space still uninvestigated, where m a > f a , thus providing a phenomenological study of the ALP-lepton couplings complementary to the existing literature. We point out that a leptophilic ALP may explain the − 3.8 σ tension in the anomalous magnetic dipole moment of the electron for the cesium determination in a large region of the m a × f a parameter space, testable in the near future through studies on μ → e conversion in nuclei.","authors":"Marta F. Zamoro, Álvaro Lozano-Onrubia, Luca Merlo, Samuel Rosende Herrero","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ghxk-bdbw","id":"doi:10.1103/ghxk-bdbw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ghxk-bdbw","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Complementary probes of bilinear R -parity violating SUSY models with a winolike LSP via neutrino oscillations and at the LHC","summary":"In this work, we explore the bilinear R -parity violating supersymmetry model’s parameter space by performing a Markov Chain Monte Carlo scan with neutrino oscillation data, Higgs mass and its coupling strengths, and flavor observables such as B -hadron decay branching ratios. From the allowed parameter space, we analyze the decay patterns of wino-like lighter charginos and lightest neutralinos and demonstrate how the branching ratios to different neutrino and charged lepton flavors depend on the neutrino mass hierarchy. Furthermore, we investigate the impact of current LHC bounds and projected future sensitivities from trilepton resonance searches on the allowed parameter space. We show that considering the branching ratio Br ( χ ˜ 1 ± → Z l ± ; l = e , μ , τ ) ∼ 23 % , obtained at the best-fit point, the wino-like mass degenerate χ ˜ 1 ± / χ ˜ 1 0 are excluded up to 565 GeV from LHC Run-II data. The projected exclusion reach with a similar branching ratio at high-luminosity LHC (HL-LHC) is around 950 GeV. For a simplified scenario where χ ˜ 1 ± / χ ˜ 1 0 decays via a Z boson with branching ratios of 1%, 50%, and 100%, wino masses can be excluded up to approximately 600 GeV, 1185 GeV, and 1350 GeV, respectively. Our analysis shows that the HL-LHC can probe a significant portion of the 1 σ allowed parameter space by neutrino oscillation measurements and other experimental constraints.","authors":"Arghya Choudhury, Arpita Mondal","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/vdtw-k6fb","id":"doi:10.1103/vdtw-k6fb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vdtw-k6fb","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Semileptonic sum rules in heavy-to-light charm decays","summary":"We investigate semileptonic sum rules in heavy-to-light charm decays, motivated by analogous relations in b → c and b → u transitions. Focusing on the c → d ℓ ¯ ν decays, D → π ℓ ¯ ν , D → ρ ℓ ¯ ν , and Λ c → n ℓ ¯ ν , we examine a relation among their lepton-flavor universality ratios R H μ e . Although the charm sum rule is less precise than the relations in bottom-hadron decays, current low-energy and high- p T constraints on new physics restrict the actual deviation from the relation to below the percent level. The relation can therefore provide a useful consistency check of charm semileptonic measurements. As an application, we derive a prediction for the yet-unmeasured ratio R n μ e in Λ c → n ℓ ¯ ν .","authors":"Motoi Endo, Syuhei Iguro, Satoshi Mishima, Takeru Uchiyama, Ryoutaro Watanabe","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/yxqm-vzqf","id":"doi:10.1103/yxqm-vzqf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/yxqm-vzqf","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Precision tests of SM and new physics with the COHERENT Ge-mini and TEXONO data","summary":"A comprehensive numerical analysis of the latest germanium based coherent elastic neutrino nucleus scattering ( CE ν NS ) data from the COHERENT Ge-mini and TEXONO experiments has been conducted to test the Standard Model (SM) and search for new physics. By combining CE ν NS and E ν ES signals with a consistent treatment of detector effects and systematic uncertainties, we obtain a low energy determination of the weak mixing angle from COHERENT Ge-mini, in agreement with the SM prediction. We derive novel constraints on neutrino electromagnetic properties, including the magnetic moment, millicharge, charge radius, and anapole moment, with TEXONO providing particularly competitive bounds. Inclusion of E ν ES significantly improves the sensitivity to the neutrino millicharge by up to three orders of magnitude. We also investigated light scalar and vector mediators, finding striking complementarity between reactor and stopped pion sources across different mediator mass regimes. Finally, we put bounds on sterile neutral lepton production through transition dipole, scalar, and vector portals, probing masses from the sub-MeV to tens of MeV scale. Our results demonstrate that current germanium based CE ν NS experiments provide a powerful low energy laboratory for precision electroweak tests and complementary probes of a broad class of physics beyond the SM.","authors":"Tousib Ahmed, Ayan Chattaraj, Anirban Majumdar, Newton Nath, Rahul Srivastava","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/t57j-7ygv","id":"doi:10.1103/t57j-7ygv","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/t57j-7ygv","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Stochastic effect on ultralight dark matter searches with astronomical polarized sources","summary":"","authors":"Kyohei Yamada","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/zdyq-bwbp","id":"doi:10.1103/zdyq-bwbp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/zdyq-bwbp","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Bayesian constraints on bosonic dark matter in neutron stars within a covariant density-functional equation-of-state ensemble","summary":"","authors":"Davood Rafiei Karkevandi, Alexander Ayriyan, Armen Sedrakian","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bgr1-bfff","id":"doi:10.1103/bgr1-bfff","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bgr1-bfff","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Revealing precision bounds on neutrino oscillation parameters with quantum estimation theory","summary":"","authors":"Jihong Huang, Tommy Ohlsson, Sampsa Vihonen, Shun Zhou","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4bjg-kkn5","id":"doi:10.1103/4bjg-kkn5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4bjg-kkn5","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Coexistence of strange quark stars and neutron stars: Metastability and nucleation in protoneutron stars","summary":"","authors":"Mirco Guerrini, Giuseppe Pagliara, Luca Passarella, Alessandro Drago","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/nd42-szrf","id":"doi:10.1103/nd42-szrf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/nd42-szrf","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Quantum gravitational mechanism for isotropization of de Sitter cosmologies","summary":"","authors":"Stephon Alexander, Bruno Alexandre, Daine L. Danielson, David N. Spergel","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/r9gz-6g6s","id":"doi:10.1103/r9gz-6g6s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/r9gz-6g6s","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Velocity distribution of dark matter around a Kerr black hole and its influence on the waveforms of extreme mass-ratio inspirals","summary":"","authors":"Yuezhe Chen, Pan-Pan Wang, Cheng-Gang Shao","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/gx3d-v5t7","id":"doi:10.1103/gx3d-v5t7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gx3d-v5t7","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Nonstandard solution for anomaly cancellation as seesaw neutrino origin in the SM","summary":"","authors":"Zi-Yue Zou, Chia-Wei Liu, Zhong-Lv Huang, Xiao-Gang He","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/krsl-rfzq","id":"doi:10.1103/krsl-rfzq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/krsl-rfzq","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Landau-Zener formula and resonant axion conversion in neutron star magnetospheres","summary":"","authors":"Matti Heikinheimo, Topi Sirkiä, Kimmo Tuominen","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/v7p6-qcq3","id":"doi:10.1103/v7p6-qcq3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v7p6-qcq3","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Probabilistic causality from graviton fluctuations","summary":"","authors":"Giordano Cintia, Federico Piazza, Samuel Ramos","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/kq3v-whcq","id":"doi:10.1103/kq3v-whcq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kq3v-whcq","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Pauli Propagation Enables Fast Classical Simulation of Strongly Correlated Quantum Systems","summary":"Ground-state energy estimation for strongly correlated quantum systems remains a central challenge in computational physics and chemistry. While tensor network methods like Density Matrix Renormalization Group (DMRG) provide efficient solutions for one-dimensional systems, higher-dimensional problems remain difficult. Here we present a variational double bracket flow (vDBF) algorithm that leverages Pauli Propagation, a technique originally developed for classical simulation of quantum circuits, to efficiently approximate ground-state energies. By combining greedy operator selection with coefficient-based fluctuation truncation and energy-variance extrapolation, we obtain sub-1% relative errors for Heisenberg and Hubbard lattices of up to 128 qubits. For a 10 × 10 Heisenberg lattice (100 qubits), vDBF obtains accurate results in approximately 2 min on a single CPU thread, compared to over 50 h on 64 threads for DMRG. Accurate results are also obtained for systems that are difficult for quantum Monte Carlo: on the maximally frustrated 8 × 8 J 1 – J 2 lattice and the hole-doped 8 × 8 Hubbard model, the tightest thresholds extrapolate to within a few tenths of a percent of the best published variational estimates. We further test vDBF on the 84-qubit π -valence active space of hexabenzocoronene, where the most reliable extrapolated correlation energies agree with DMRG to within 1%. These results demonstrate that classical simulation techniques developed in the context of quantum advantage benchmarking can provide practical tools for many-body physics.","authors":"Chinmay Shrikhande, Arnab Bachhar, José Aarón Rodríguez-Jiménez, Edward F. Valeev, Nicholas J. Mayhall","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/vjtb-d8kb","id":"doi:10.1103/vjtb-d8kb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vjtb-d8kb","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Machine-learning-assisted tracking of magnetic objects using quantum diamond magnetometry","summary":"Remote magnetic sensing can be used to monitor the position of objects in real time, enabling ground transport monitoring, underground infrastructure mapping, and hazardous detection. However, magnetic signals are typically weak and complex, requiring sophisticated physical models to analyze them and a detailed knowledge of the system under study, factors that are frequently unavailable. In this work, we provide a solution to these limitations by demonstrating a machine-learning (ML) method that can be trained exclusively on experimental data, without the need of any physical model, to predict the position of a magnetic target in real time. The target can be any object with a magnetic signal above the floor noise, and in this case, we use a quantum diamond magnetometer to track variations of a few hundred nanoteslas produced by an elevator moving along a single axis. The one-dimensional movement is a simple yet challenging scenario, resembling realistic environments such as high buildings, tunnels, or train circuits, and is the first step toward building broader applications. Our ML algorithm can be trained in approximately 40 min, achieving over 80% accuracy in predicting the target’s position at a rate of 10 Hz, for a positional error tolerance of 30 cm, which is well within the the 4-m spacing between parking levels. Our results open up the possibility to apply this ML method more generally for real-time monitoring of magnetic objects, which will broaden the scope of magnetic detection applications.","authors":"Fernando Meneses, Christopher T.-K. Lew, Anand Sivamalai, Andy Sayers, Brant C. Gibson, Andrew D. Greentree, Lloyd C. L. Hollenberg, David A. Simpson","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/snzq-fp39","id":"doi:10.1103/snzq-fp39","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/snzq-fp39","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Quantum simulation of wave optics in weakly inhomogeneous media using block encoding","summary":"We propose a quantum algorithm that simulates the propagation of a light field through a weakly inhomogeneous medium. In the paraxial approximation, the wave equation in an inhomogeneous material takes the form of the Schrödinger equation with a time-dependent Hamiltonian. This reduction is used to simulate wave optical dynamics on a quantum computer. Beam propagator operators for a short propagation distance are constructed using an efficient and flexible block encoding that enables the simulation of various optical setups. The algorithm is showcased by simulating the propagation of a one-dimensional Gaussian beam through a lens of finite thickness, and the resulting spherical aberration is demonstrated.","authors":"Siavash Davani, Martin Gärttner, Falk Eilenberger","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/4rv2-8jxw","id":"doi:10.1103/4rv2-8jxw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4rv2-8jxw","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Quantum computing and quantum optics with recoiled free electrons","summary":"Coherent interactions between free electrons and optical fields offer a promising route to quantum control, nonclassical state generation, and programmable quantum dynamics. At kiloelectron-volt electron energies, the emission or absorption of optical photons produces appreciable quantum recoil, resolving the electron into a discrete and controllable energy ladder. Starting from relativistic quantum electrodynamics, we derive an exact recoil-resolved interaction Hamiltonian in a traveling-wave picture. The resulting recoil ladder forms a high-dimensional qudit with programmable couplings and sufficient controllability for universal quantum computation. We demonstrate its use for analog quantum simulation, including one-dimensional curved-spacetime models and synthetic black-hole horizons, as well as for quantum information processing, where multiple logical qubits and high-fidelity gates can be realized within a single electron. In parallel, the same recoil-enabled dynamics allow the controlled creation of complex hybrid electron-photon states, in which engineered ladder transitions imprint nonclassical correlations and structure onto the emitted light. Together, these results establish recoiled free electrons as a versatile platform bridging quantum optics, quantum control, Hamiltonian engineering, quantum simulation, and quantum information processing.","authors":"Maxim Sirotin, Andrei Rasputnyi, Tomáš Chlouba, Roy Shiloh, Peter Hommelhoff","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/cbcv-fnm2","id":"doi:10.1103/cbcv-fnm2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/cbcv-fnm2","published":"2026-11-02","title":"Imaginary-time-enhanced feedback-based quantum algorithms for ground-state preparation","summary":"Preparing ground states of strongly correlated quantum systems is a central goal in quantum simulation and optimization. The feedback-based quantum algorithm (FALQON) provides an attractive alternative to variational methods with a fully quantum feedback rule, but it fails in the presence of spectral degeneracies, where the feedback signal collapses and the evolution cannot reach the ground state. Using the Fermi-Hubbard model on lattices up to 3 × 3 , we show that this breakdown appears at half filling on the 2 × 2 lattice and extends to both half-filled and doped configurations on the 3 × 3 lattice. We then introduce an imaginary-time-enhanced FALQON ( i FALQON ) scheme, which inserts short imaginary-time evolution steps into the feedback loop. This proposed method suppresses excited-state components, escapes degenerate subspaces, restores monotonic energy descent, and improves ground-state convergence across the tested fillings. We further benchmark its performance against existing methods, showing improved results for i FALQON , demonstrating the potential of hybrid real-imaginary-time feedback dynamics for ground-state preparation in strongly correlated quantum systems.","authors":"Thanh Nguyen Van Long, Lan Nguyen Tran, Le Bin Ho","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-11-01":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/klq1-5rwp","id":"doi:10.1103/klq1-5rwp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/klq1-5rwp","published":"2026-11-01","title":"Accelerating noisy variational quantum algorithms with physics-informed denoising networks","summary":"","authors":"Jie Liu, Xin Wang","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]}]},"2026-10-30":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/jydv-n2js","id":"doi:10.1103/jydv-n2js","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jydv-n2js","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Observation of Electroweak Production of Pairs of Z Bosons in Proton-Proton Collisions at 13 TeV","summary":"The first evidence of electroweak (EW) production of pairs of Z bosons in association with two jets (jj) in the final state Z Z jj → ℓ ℓ ν ν jj , where ℓ = e , μ , is reported by the CMS experiment. The analysis is based on a data sample of proton-proton ( p p ) collisions at s = 13 TeV , corresponding to an integrated luminosity of 138 fb − 1 . Events are selected by requiring exactly two leptons of same flavor and opposite charge, large missing transverse momentum, and two jets with a large rapidity separation and large invariant mass. The EW production cross section in a fiducial volume is σ EW ( p p → Z Z jj → ℓ ℓ ν ν jj ) = 0.3 7 − 0.12 + 0.14 ( stat ) − 0.06 + 0.06 ( syst ) fb , in agreement with the standard model prediction of 0.39 ± 0.06 fb . The observed (expected) significance of the signal is 3.1 (2.8) standard deviations. Limits on anomalous quartic gauge couplings are derived in terms of dimension-8 effective field theory operators. A combination with the previously reported result from the Z Z decay channel with four charged leptons yields an observed (expected) significance of 5.0 (4.5) standard deviations for the EW production of Z boson pairs.","authors":"A. Gevorgyan, A. Hayrapetyan, V. Makarenko, A. Tumasyan, W. Adam, L. Benato, T. Bergauer, M. Dragicevic, S. Gundacker, A. K. Guven, P. S. Hussain, M. Jeitler, N. Krammer, A. Li, D. Liko, M. Matthewman, J. Schieck, R. Schöfbeck, M. Shooshtari, M. Sonawane et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/q4lp-htgw","id":"doi:10.1103/q4lp-htgw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/q4lp-htgw","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Rare-Event Quantum Sensing Using Logical Qubits","summary":"We present a novel protocol to detect rare signals in a noisy environment using quantum error correction (QEC). The key feature of our protocol is the discrimination between signal and noise through distinct higher-order correlations, realized by the nonlinear processing that occurs during syndrome extraction in QEC. In this scheme, QEC has two effects. First, it sacrifices part of the signal ε by recording a reduced, stochastic, logical phase ϕ L = O ( ε 3 ) . Second, it corrects the physical noise and extends the (logical) coherence time for signal acquisition. For rare signals occurring at random times in the presence of local Markovian noise, we explicitly demonstrate an improved sensitivity of our approach over more conventional sensing strategies.","authors":"Robert Ott, Torsten V. Zache, Soonwon Choi, Adam M. Kaufman, Hannes Pichler","topics":["sensing","computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/jckx-z293","id":"doi:10.1103/jckx-z293","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jckx-z293","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Particle-Soliton Degeneracy in 2D Quantum Chromodynamics","summary":"Quantum chromodynamics in two spacetime dimensions admits a finite noninvertible symmetry described mathematically by a fusion category. This symmetry is spontaneously broken at long distances, leading to distinct vacua. When the theory has a mass gap, the spectrum is therefore characterized by particle excitations above a single vacuum and soliton sectors interpolating between vacua. We use anyon condensation and the representation theory of fusion categories to obtain exact results about this spectrum, exhibiting the allowed multiplets. Often, particles and solitons are in the same representation and therefore must have equal masses. Furthermore, the fusion category symmetry frequently implies the existence of certain stable states in the spectrum. The resulting degeneracies are encoded in quiver diagrams where nodes are vacua and arrows are excited states.","authors":"Clay Córdova, Diego García-Sepúlveda, Nicholas Holfester","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/63lg-r5fg","id":"doi:10.1103/63lg-r5fg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/63lg-r5fg","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Unifying charge-learnability transitions in monitored U(1)-symmetric quantum circuits through informational power of local measurement","summary":"","authors":"Yi-Fan Gong, Dan-Bo Zhang","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/xwwb-gnf5","id":"doi:10.1103/xwwb-gnf5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xwwb-gnf5","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Infrared foundations for quantum geometry. I. Catalog of totally symmetric rank-three field theories","summary":"We systematically obtain all linear models which propagate a totally symmetric rank-three field without parity violation on a flat background. Each such model is defined exclusively by its gauge symmetry, which is usually a necessary property of effective field theories in the infrared limit. By comparison, models obtained by other means (tuning couplings and operators) may be unstable against radiative corrections. For each model, we compute the spectrum of massless and massive particles, and the no-ghost-no-tachyon constraints on the couplings. We conclude that foundational models exist which can propagate one massless particle of spin one or spin three in isolation, or both particles simultaneously, generalizing the model of Campoleoni and Francia. Our algorithm for detecting symmetric models is grounded in particle physics methods, being based directly on the Wigner decomposition of the field. Compared to our recent analysis of the totally symmetric rank-three field (whose results we confirm and extend) our new algorithm does not require an ansatz for the symmetry transformation, and is not restricted to so-called “free” symmetries.","authors":"Will Barker, Carlo Marzo, Alessandro Santoni","topics":["qgravity","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dwfl-y5bj","id":"doi:10.1103/dwfl-y5bj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dwfl-y5bj","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Infrared foundations for quantum geometry. II. Catalog of all torsionlike theories including new ghost-tachyon-free cases","summary":"The construction of consistent effective field theories in the infrared suggests that models be defined by their underlying gauge symmetries, rather than by a tuning of couplings of operators which may not be stable against radiative corrections. Adhering to this principle, we systematically derive all linear, parity-conserving models that propagate a pair-antisymmetric rank-three field on a Minkowski background. Such models are relevant not only to torsion, but to many areas in high-energy physics ranging from dual graviton formulations to string theory and higher-spin theories. Starting from the general 12-parameter quadratic action, we obtain 206 gauge-symmetric specializations, of which 22 are free of ghosts and tachyons. Following this exhaustive classification, none of the unitary models are found to propagate scalar or pseudoscalar torsion, in stark contrast to the literature focus on spin-zero torsion. Instead, all models propagate one or more vector torsion modes.","authors":"Will Barker, Carlo Marzo, Alessandro Santoni","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4fjp-82qg","id":"doi:10.1103/4fjp-82qg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4fjp-82qg","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Bypassed core formation in Milky Way-mass self-interacting dark matter halos: Implications for the local group past-pericenter scenario","summary":"","authors":"Zhichao Carton Zeng, Odelia V. Hartl, Louis E. Strigari, Annika H. G. Peter, Xiaolong Du, Charlie Mace, Andrew Benson","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/yjg3-83g1","id":"doi:10.1103/yjg3-83g1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/yjg3-83g1","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Constraining the resolution length of quark-gluon plasma with new jet substructure measurements","summary":"We show that recent measurements of substructure-dependent jet suppression constrain the value of the resolution length of the droplets of quark-gluon plasma (QGP) formed in heavy ion collisions. This resolution length, L res , is defined such that the medium can only resolve partons within a jet shower that are separated by more than L res . We first use calculations done in the hybrid strong/weak coupling model for jet quenching (Hybrid Model) to reproduce ALICE measurements of the scaled soft-drop angle θ g for anti- k t R = 0.2 jets reconstructed from charged-particle tracks. We find that the narrowing of the θ g -distribution in PbPb collisions compared to pp collisions that is seen in the ALICE data rules out a picture of fully coherent energy loss ( L res = ∞ ) where each entire parton shower loses energy to the plasma as if it were a single unresolved colored object. We then use Hybrid Model calculations to reproduce ATLAS measurements of d R 12 , the soft-drop angle obtained by applying the Soft Drop grooming procedure to all charged-particle tracks in R = 1 jets reconstructed from R = 0.2 skinny subjets. Our analysis demonstrates that the ATLAS measurements of R AA for such R = 1 jets as a function of d R 12 are inconsistent with a picture of fully incoherent energy loss ( L res = 0 ) in which every splitting in a parton shower is immediately resolved by the plasma. We find that our Hybrid Model calculations agree best with the ATLAS measurements if QGP has a finite, nonzero, resolution length L res ∼ ( 1 – 2 ) / ( π T ) . For the first time, jet substructure measurements are constraining the resolution length of QGP from below, as well as from above.","authors":"Arjun Srinivasan Kudinoor, Daniel Pablos, Krishna Rajagopal","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bl2f-xygk","id":"doi:10.1103/bl2f-xygk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bl2f-xygk","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Quasiparton distribution functions in covariant quark models","summary":"Quasiparton distribution functions (QPDFs) are defined in terms of QCD fields at spacelike separations evaluated in matrix elements of hadrons moving with velocity v . These objects can be studied in lattice QCD. In the limit when v approaches the speed of light, QPDFs converge to parton distribution functions. It is insightful to study QPDFs and their convergence in models. In this work, we first study the QPDFs in a broad class of quark models characterized by one common feature, namely the absence of gauge degrees of freedom. We provide general proofs for the convergence and sum rules of the unpolarized quark and antiquark QPDFs for both choices γ 0 and γ 3 . We choose the covariant parton model as an illustration, and derive analytical results for the small- x v behavior of QPDFs and the energy-momentum tensor form factor c ¯ q ( t ) at zero momentum transfer. These results are of interest as they correspond to a Wandzura-Wilczek-type approximation.","authors":"F. Aslan, A. Tandogan, P. Schweitzer","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bk55-z2mq","id":"doi:10.1103/bk55-z2mq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bk55-z2mq","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Toward measuring the low-energy cosmic-ray spectrum with next-generation neutrino detectors","summary":"We investigate the capabilities of upcoming kiloton-scale neutrino detectors, such as Hyper-Kamiokande, in determining the primary cosmic-ray spectrum. These detectors provide full-sky coverage and long-term monitoring, unlike traditional satellite and balloon experiments that measure cosmic-ray flux at specific altitudes and locations. By analyzing the atmospheric neutrino flux generated by cosmic-ray interactions, we demonstrate that future detectors can differentiate between various cosmic-ray models with high statistical significance, even when accounting for uncertainties in neutrino cross sections and hadronic interactions. We introduce a technique for reconstructing the primary cosmic-ray spectrum using neutrino measurements, which reduces the flux uncertainty from approximately 20% to about 7%. We then show that Hyper-K has the potential to increase sensitivity to neutrino oscillation parameters, such as sin 2 θ 23 , by a factor of 2. Our results highlight the complementary role of neutrino detectors in cosmic-ray physics and their critical importance for precision measurements in particle astrophysics.","authors":"Stephan A. Meighen-Berger, Jayden L. Newstead, Louis E. Strigari","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/44kt-wqn4","id":"doi:10.1103/44kt-wqn4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/44kt-wqn4","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Multipolar dark matter freeze-out in an early matter-dominated universe","summary":"The relic abundance of thermal dark matter depends not only on its particle interactions but also on the expansion history of the early Universe. We study the freeze-out of fermionic dark matter interacting with the Standard Model through higher-dimensional electromagnetic operators in an early matter-dominated cosmology. In particular, we consider magnetic-dipole, electric-dipole, anapole, and charge-radius interactions and compute the interaction strengths required to reproduce the observed dark matter relic abundance, accounting for the subsequent entropy injection from the decay of a long-lived heavy field. We compare the resulting parameter space with that obtained in the standard radiation-dominated freeze-out scenario and confront it with current constraints from direct-detection experiments and solar-neutrino searches. We find that the entropy dilution associated with an early matter-dominated epoch significantly reduces the interaction strength required to obtain the observed relic abundance, thereby opening regions of parameter space that are excluded within the conventional cosmological history.","authors":"Debajit Bose, Prolay Chanda, Suvam Maharana, Poulami Mondal","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dcmw-n3dc","id":"doi:10.1103/dcmw-n3dc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dcmw-n3dc","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Exploring anomaly-free dark photon models through solar neutrino-electron scattering in ton-scale dark matter detectors","summary":"We study the phenomenological implications of anomaly-free dark photon models using low-energy solar neutrino-electron elastic scattering data from the ton-scale PandaX-4T and XENONnT experiments. Focusing on U ( 1 ) X gauge extensions that incorporate both kinetic and mass mixing, we derive novel exclusion limits across the parameter spaces of six benchmark models. Our analysis demonstrates that the stringency of these limits is directly correlated with the theoretical leptonic charge assignments of the respective models. Additionally, we provide model-independent limits by isolating the pure kinetic and pure mass mixing scenarios. In the sub-MeV mediator mass regime, our results meaningfully improve upon existing limits from previous neutrino-electron scattering experiments, such as CHARM-II, TEXONO, and GEMMA. Ultimately, these findings yield improved limits on low-mass dark photon models, demonstrating their complementarity with other ventures in the global search for dark portals.","authors":"M. Demirci, G. Kaz𝚤l, M. F. Mustamin","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/yh1b-mf64","id":"doi:10.1103/yh1b-mf64","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/yh1b-mf64","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Neutrinoless double-beta decay of the Δ − resonance","summary":"The subprocess n n → p p e − e − is a key ingredient in the interpretation of nuclear neutrinoless double- β decay. Intermediate Δ resonances may provide additional enhancements to this transition. We take a first step toward a Δ -full description of n n → p p e − e − by investigating the neutrinoless double- β decay Δ − → p e − e − in the framework of chiral effective field theory. We systematically derive the long-range contribution from light-Majorana-neutrino exchange through loop diagrams and incorporate the short-range part through counterterms required by renormalization. We predict the pion-mass dependence of the decay amplitude in the kinematic configuration with collinear electrons. Furthermore, to facilitate lattice-QCD matching, we calculate the decay amplitude in the degenerate Δ -nucleon mass limit and provide the corresponding long-range prediction.","authors":"Li-Ping He, Feng-Kun Guo, Ulf-G. Meißner, De-Liang Yao, Xiao-Yu Zhang, Zhen-Hua Zhang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/6ldz-y3vh","id":"doi:10.1103/6ldz-y3vh","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6ldz-y3vh","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Selected open-charm decays of the narrow P c states: Quark-interchange predictions and pion-exchange sensitivity","summary":"The proximity of the narrow P c ( 4312 ) , P c ( 4440 ) , and P c ( 4457 ) structures to the Σ c D ¯ ( * ) thresholds motivates their interpretation as hadronic molecules. We treat the P c ( 4312 ) as an S -wave Σ c D ¯ state with J P = 1 / 2 − and examine both J P = 1 / 2 − and 3 / 2 − Σ c D ¯ * assignments for each heavier peak. The selected S -wave open-charm transitions to Λ c D ¯ ( * ) are calculated at Born order in the quark-interchange model, including the prior and post rearrangement diagrams and their color, flavor, spin, and spatial matrix elements. Across the input variations considered, the quark-interchange widths robustly favor Λ c D ¯ * over Λ c D ¯ for every J P = 1 / 2 − assignment. At either heavier peak mass, the Λ c D ¯ * width for J P = 3 / 2 − is suppressed by approximately one order of magnitude relative to J P = 1 / 2 − , providing a potential decay-based discriminator of the spin assignments. The large channel hierarchies arise from spatial overlaps and interference between spin-independent and hyperfine contributions and are not universal heavy-quark-spin-symmetry ratios. We also explore a retarded one-pion-exchange transition at first Born order. It affects P c ( 4312 ) → Λ c D ¯ * most strongly, while the coherent widths remain sensitive to the cutoff, energy-transfer prescription, short-distance subtraction, and unresolved relative matching sign. The quark-interchange hierarchies are therefore the central predictions, whereas the pion-exchange results quantify matching sensitivity. Measurements of the Λ c D ¯ * modes can directly test these patterns.","authors":"Vandan Patel, Ajay Kumar Rai","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/xvsp-mh91","id":"doi:10.1103/xvsp-mh91","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xvsp-mh91","published":"2026-10-30","title":"QCD crossover temperature and curvature coefficient from unbiased exponential resummation at physical quark masses in ( 2 + 1 )-flavor lattice QCD","summary":"We present the first application of the unbiased exponential resummation (UER) method to the determination of the QCD pseudocritical temperature T pc at vanishing baryon chemical potential μ B . By reconstructing the finite-density baryon number susceptibility χ 2 B , we define two thermal-derivative observables whose peak positions yield T pc T = 160.2 ( 4 ) ( 3 ) MeV and T pc C = 158.1 ( 5 ) ( 3 ) MeV . We also show that the pseudocritical temperature, T pc χ obtained from the peak of the fourth-order baryon number susceptibility χ 4 B is consistent with these determinations and with previous lattice-QCD results based on Taylor expansions [1]. Further, we demonstrate that scaling relations [2] between χ 4 B and the thermal derivatives of χ 2 B yield mutually consistent estimates of the leading second order curvature coefficient κ 2 B at the corresponding pseudocritical temperatures. A direct analysis of the μ B dependence of the pseudocritical temperatures provides an independent but substantially less constrained determination of the curvature, which remains statistically consistent with the scaling-based estimates. These results demonstrate the consistency of this UER method with the expected scaling behavior and establish it as a complementary approach for probing the QCD crossover at small finite baryon density.","authors":"Sabarnya Mitra","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4nhz-gs2f","id":"doi:10.1103/4nhz-gs2f","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4nhz-gs2f","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Neutrino transport and flavor instabilities in a postmerger disk","summary":"","authors":"Erick Urquilla, Swapnil Shankar, Debraj Kundu, Julien Froustey, Sherwood Richers, Jonah M. Miller, Gail C. McLaughlin, James P. Kneller, Francois Foucart","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/gnhb-zmhn","id":"doi:10.1103/gnhb-zmhn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gnhb-zmhn","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Erratum: Symmetries of spatial correlators of light and heavy mesons in high temperature lattice QCD [Phys. Rev. D 110 , 014502 (2024)]","summary":"","authors":"Ting-Wai Chiu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/pbs4-gg4r","id":"doi:10.1103/pbs4-gg4r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/pbs4-gg4r","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Suppressing extra-dimensional axion isocurvature dynamically","summary":"","authors":"Gongjun Choi, Tony Gherghetta","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4k8t-zqyp","id":"doi:10.1103/4k8t-zqyp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4k8t-zqyp","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Sterile neutrino dark matter in conformal Majoron models","summary":"","authors":"João Gonçalves, Danny Marfatia, António P. Morais, Vinícius Oliveira, Roman Pasechnik","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/5s7s-vljb","id":"doi:10.1103/5s7s-vljb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5s7s-vljb","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Spin identification of dark sector mediators through angular distributions","summary":"","authors":"D. Aristizabal Sierra, S. Fuenzalida Garrido, F. Kling, T. Mäkelä, N. Viaux","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/wlnt-tgn7","id":"doi:10.1103/wlnt-tgn7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wlnt-tgn7","published":"2026-10-30","title":"WIMP dark matter and S O (10) unification with parity","summary":"","authors":"Matthew J. Baldwin, Keisuke Harigaya","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/ymwh-j65z","id":"doi:10.1103/ymwh-j65z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ymwh-j65z","published":"2026-10-30","title":"Scattering zones in quantum speckle propagation","summary":"Quantum speckles exhibit significantly richer behavior than their classical counterparts due to their higher dimensionality. A simple example is the far-field speckle pattern in one-dimensional (1D) light scattering: Classical light forms 1D speckles defined by the numerical aperture, whereas biphoton scattering depends in addition on the photon correlation length, forming two-dimensional elliptical speckles. To date, the behavior of quantum speckles for shorter propagation distances has not been considered. We remedy this here by considering the paraxial evolution of two-photon entanglement at arbitrary propagation distances from a scatterer. We show, theoretically, numerically, and experimentally, that the two length scales of the biphoton introduce a new Fresnel regime between the conventional near and far fields. Further, we show that the quantum near field is characterized by speckles with a square shape that remain constant during propagation. In contrast, the intermediate regime can be engineered to have a constant speckle size along the sum coordinate but a linearly expanding speckle size along the difference coordinate, with a speckle shape that transitions from square to elliptical. The results merge quantum coherence with scattering statistics and suggest new regimes of operation for correlation-based quantum sensing and imaging.","authors":"Shaurya Aarav, S. A. Wadood, Jason W. Fleischer","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-10-29":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Nature","doi":"10.1038/d41586-026-03386-4","id":"doi:10.1038/d41586-026-03386-4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-03386-4","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Even stupendous black holes can’t explain dark matter","summary":"","authors":"","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/866f-xmqx","id":"doi:10.1103/866f-xmqx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/866f-xmqx","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Sequential measurements as a resource in ancilla-assisted quantum metrology","summary":"","authors":"Koray Mentesoglu, Rahul Trivedi, Sara Mouradian","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2vms-z2j8","id":"doi:10.1103/2vms-z2j8","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2vms-z2j8","published":"2026-10-29","title":"First search for sterile neutrino oscillation leading to ν μ disappearance in the Booster Neutrino Beam at ICARUS","summary":"We present a search for muon neutrino disappearance in the Booster Neutrino Beam (BNB) at Fermilab using the ICARUS detector. Neutrino interactions identified as muon neutrinos interacting with argon nuclei via the charged current interaction and having only a muon and at least one proton in the final state ( 1 μ Np ) have been selected from data collected in 2022–2023 (ICARUS Run 2) and compared with a simulation-based expectation. In the context of a fit to a two-neutrino approximation of the sterile 3 + 1 model, including the impact of systematic uncertainty from the flux, neutrino interaction, and detector models, we find no statistically significant muon neutrino disappearance at the ICARUS baseline of 600 meters from the BNB target. Corresponding 90% CL exclusion contours in the Δ m 41 2 − sin 2 2 θ μ μ space are presented. This is the first oscillation analysis produced by ICARUS exposed to the BNB. We note that the analysis is systematics limited due to large unconstrained uncertainties from the flux and interaction models. In future joint analyses, data from ICARUS and the Short Baseline Near Detector (SBND), exposed to the BNB at 110 meters from target, will be combined to provide significant constraint of these uncertainties, enabling a robust, world-leading two-detector analysis.","authors":"F. Abd Alrahman, P. Abratenko, N. Abrego-Martinez, A. Aduszkiewicz, F. Akbar, L. Aliaga Soplin, M. Artero Pons, J. Asaadi, W. F. Badgett, B. Baibussinov, F. Battisti, B. Behera, V. Bellini, R. Benocci, J. Berger, S. Bertolucci, M. Betancourt, A. Blanchet, F. Boffelli, M. Bonesini et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/h7bx-zj4b","id":"doi:10.1103/h7bx-zj4b","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/h7bx-zj4b","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Search for the charged lepton flavor violating decay η → e ± μ ∓","summary":"Based on ( 10.087 ± 0.044 ) × 10 9 J / ψ events collected at the center-of-mass energy s = 3.097 GeV with the BESIII detector, we search for the charged lepton flavor violating decay η → e ± μ ∓ through the process J / ψ → γ η ′ with η ′ → π + π − η . No signal is observed, and an upper limit on the branching fraction is determined to be B ( η → e ± μ ∓ ) < 6.8 × 10 − 7 at the 90% confidence level. This result improves the previous best limit by one order of magnitude.","authors":"M. Ablikim, M. N. Achasov, P. Adlarson, X. C. Ai, C. S. Akondi, R. Aliberti, A. Amoroso, Q. An, Y. H. An, Y. Bai, O. Bakina, H. R. Bao, X. L. Bao, M. Barbagiovanni, V. Batozskaya, K. Begzsuren, N. Berger, M. Berlowski, M. B. Bertani, D. Bettoni et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/7vkn-kb1l","id":"doi:10.1103/7vkn-kb1l","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7vkn-kb1l","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Quark number susceptibilities and conserved charge fluctuations in ( 2 + 1 )-flavor QCD with Möbius domain wall fermions","summary":"We calculate second- and selected fourth-order conserved-charge fluctuations in ( 2 + 1 )-flavor QCD using Möbius domain-wall fermions (MDWF) along a line of constant physics. Gauge ensembles were generated for two light-to-strange quark-mass ratios, m l / m s = 1 / 10 and 1 / 27.4 , corresponding to heavier-than-physical and physical pion masses, respectively. For m l / m s = 1 / 10 , calculations were carried out on lattices with temporal extents N τ = 12 and 16, enabling an assessment of lattice-spacing effects at heavier pion mass. For m l / m s = 1 / 27.4 , calculations were performed at N τ = 12 , allowing us to study the light-quark-mass dependence down to the physical point. Several second-order conserved-charge fluctuations show qualitative consistency with the QMHRG2020 hadron resonance gas baseline below the pseudocritical region within present uncertainties. Across the crossover region, these observables rise rapidly and tend toward their Stefan-Boltzmann limits. Selected fourth-order cumulants were also computed at the physical pion mass. Although these observables are statistically more demanding, several channels with controlled uncertainties permit a first comparison with hadron resonance gas calculations.","authors":"Jishnu Goswami, Yasumichi Aoki, Hidenori Fukaya, Shoji Hashimoto, Issaku Kanamori, Takashi Kaneko, Yoshifumi Nakamura, David Ward, Yu Zhang, JLQCD Collaboration","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/wj7w-m4nc","id":"doi:10.1103/wj7w-m4nc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wj7w-m4nc","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Leading order quark–antiquark– W -boson vertex in the presence of a magnetic field","summary":"We compute the leading order modification to the quark–antiquark– W -boson vertex in the presence of a constant and uniform magnetic field, specifically for the case where the quark and antiquark are the u and d ¯ , respectively. We find the selection rules for transitions where the u , d ¯ , and W boson occupy arbitrary Landau levels. We show that, for the particular case where the initial particles each occupy the lowest Landau level, the W + is also produced in the lowest Landau level with a single polarization aligned with the magnetic field. We also illustrate the general findings by computing the case of transitions between low-lying Landau levels and show the correspondence between the polarization vectors for the W boson and the polarization states for the quark and the antiquark.","authors":"Alejandro Ayala, Alejandro López, Ana Julia Mizher, Manuel Emiliano Monreal Cancino, Norberto Scoccola","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/xtsm-rw6d","id":"doi:10.1103/xtsm-rw6d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xtsm-rw6d","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Continuum limit of a qubit-regularized SU(3) lattice gauge theory on a plaquette chain","summary":"We show that a simple qubit-regularized SU(3) lattice gauge theory on a plaquette chain admits a continuum limit with massive gauge-invariant excitations, providing a minimal toy model of strong interactions without quarks. By mapping the plaquette-chain Hamiltonian to the three-state quantum clock model in a magnetic field, we demonstrate that the theory can be tuned to a continuum limit governed at short distances by the Z 3 parafermion conformal field theory, which serves as the UV fixed point. A small relevant magnetic perturbation then drives the system to a massive continuum quantum field theory in the IR, featuring relativistic particles. In this continuum theory, we compute two universal ratios: the mass ratio of the lightest massive particles with opposite charge conjugation, m − / m + = 1.459 ( 2 ) , and σ / m + = 0.2648 ( 2 ) , where σ denotes the string tension between a static quark and antiquark.","authors":"Rui Xian Siew, Shailesh Chandrasekharan, Tanmoy Bhattacharya","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/62lc-f435","id":"doi:10.1103/62lc-f435","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/62lc-f435","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Two-loop renormalization and Higgs phenomenology in the five-dimensional MSSM","summary":"The five-dimensional minimal supersymmetric standard model (5D MSSM) provides an attractive framework in which power-law renormalization group evolution naturally generates a sizeable trilinear stop coupling, allowing the observed Higgs boson mass to be reproduced without requiring too heavy a supersymmetric spectra. In this work we present a comprehensive two-loop analysis of the 5D MSSM, deriving the complete renormalization group equations governing the gauge, Yukawa and soft supersymmetry-breaking sectors and examining the perturbative consistency of the theory. Particular attention is given to the ultraviolet behavior of the model, where we demonstrate that the potentially dangerous leading two-loop contributions cancel as a consequence of the underlying N = 2 supersymmetry, leaving a well-behaved perturbative expansion. Building upon this framework, we compute the radiatively corrected Higgs effective potential including the complete Kaluza-Klein (KK) contributions through KK resummation, and investigate the resulting implications for the Higgs sector. The phenomenological viability of the model is subsequently explored through detailed numerical studies of the Higgs boson mass, electroweak precision observables, Higgs coupling modifiers and signal strengths, allowing constraints on the compactification scale and supersymmetric parameter space to be established. We find that the inclusion of the two-loop corrections preserves the characteristic power-law behavior of the five-dimensional theory while yielding a phenomenologically viable parameter space consistent with current collider and precision measurements. Furthermore, as the KK tower provides the dominant correction to the Higgs quartic coupling, raising m h to 125 GeV, over the viable parameter space, the effective Higgs couplings remain close to their Standard Model values, showing a strong decoupling of the heavy KK modes.","authors":"Ammar Abdalgabar, Alan S. Cornell, Aldo Deandrea, Howeida M. Esmaeil, Mohammed Omer Khojali","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/3kqg-c7bp","id":"doi:10.1103/3kqg-c7bp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/3kqg-c7bp","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Laboratory constraints on peV-scale mass splitting between ordinary and sterile neutron states","summary":"Sterile states of matter, represented by a parallel “mirror” sector, may contribute to the observed dark matter in the Universe. We investigated the parameter space of neutron ( n ) to mirror-neutron ( n ′ ) oscillations, in the case where the two states are not necessarily mass degenerate, taking into account interactions in the mirror sector. By tuning the magnitude of an applied magnetic field in the range 5 μ T < B < 360 μ T to corresponding resonance conditions for finite mass splitting, we derive exclusion limits for the n − n ′ oscillation time constant reaching about 20 s over the mass-difference range 0.3 − 22 p eV . In parts of this parameter range, our limits exceed the model-dependent neutron-star-cooling bound, providing the first experimental constraints in this scenario that are more stringent than this astrophysical estimate.","authors":"N. J. Ayres, Z. Berezhiani, G. Bison, K. Bodek, V. Bondar, P.-J. Chiu, M. Daum, C. B. Doorenbos, S. Emmenegger, K. Kirch, V. Kletzl, J. Krempel, B. Lauss, D. Pais, I. Rienäcker, D. Ries, D. Rozpędzik, P. Schmidt-Wellenburg, K. S. Tanaka, J. Zejma et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/5jxc-7fxb","id":"doi:10.1103/5jxc-7fxb","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5jxc-7fxb","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Cosmic evolution of the standard model flavor charges","summary":"Since all the Standard Model (SM) parameters have been measured to precision at the percent level or smaller, one would aim to have a framework which describes as as possible, where the measured SM parameters are used as inputs. At high temperature before the electroweak symmetry breaking, the SM contains 15 approximate U ( 1 ) symmetries with the associated flavor charges. To describe the evolutions of these flavor charges in a manner such that physical observables are , a total of five density matrices in flavor spaces are required: three for quarks and two for leptons. Taking into account the quantum chromodynamics and electroweak sphaleron interactions together with all the Yukawa interactions, we obtain the complete flavor-covariant Boltzmann equations. By imposing chemical equilibrium in the lepton sector, we further derive a new formalism which can be used when baryogenesis occurs through number asymmetry generation in the quark sector. To verify the consistency of this complete formalism, we first apply it to leptogenesis scenarios, reproducing previous results which utilize the formalism up to small corrections due to the quark chemical equilibrium approximations used in the latter formalism. Then, for the first time, we apply the complete formalism as well as the effective quark-flavor-covariant formalism to a cloistered baryogenesis scenario where number asymmetries are generated in the quark sector, showcasing the good agreement between the results from the two formalisms. Finally, we release the first generic baryogenesis public code (BaryOn and Lepton charge Evolution in the Hot big bang) where all the SM interactions are taken into account.","authors":"Chee Sheng Fong, Sergio Manuel Cubides Pérez","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ym6v-19b2","id":"doi:10.1103/ym6v-19b2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ym6v-19b2","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Lepton flavor violating Higgs boson decays at the Compact Linear Collider","summary":"Lepton flavor violating decays of the Higgs boson h could appear in scenarios beyond the Standard Model of particle physics. In this article, we study the sensitivity of a future Compact Linear Collider, operating in e + e − collisions, to such processes, namely, h → e μ , h → τ μ , and h → e τ . In the absence of any observation, 95% CL upper limits in the region of 10 − 4 to 10 − 5 could be placed on the branching fractions of these processes, assuming integrated luminosities of 4 ab − 1 at s = 1.4 TeV and 5 ab − 1 at s = 3 TeV .","authors":"Francisca Garay, Gabriel Vega, Philipp Roloff","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8vbs-8zql","id":"doi:10.1103/8vbs-8zql","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8vbs-8zql","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Second-order effective renormalized Hamiltonian of quantum chromodynamics","summary":"","authors":"Kamil Serafin, Carter M. Gustin, Peter J. Love","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/cz9b-kzqr","id":"doi:10.1103/cz9b-kzqr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/cz9b-kzqr","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Effect of quark-hadron phase transitions on the neutron star mass distribution","summary":"","authors":"Renyu Luo, Xu-Run Huang, Shuai Zha, Guoliang Lü, Chunhua Zhu, Sufen Guo, Helei Liu, Zhijun Wang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/tt8z-vdqd","id":"doi:10.1103/tt8z-vdqd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/tt8z-vdqd","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Searches for heavy neutral lepton decays at spallation neutron sources","summary":"","authors":"Valentina De Romeri, A. Galindo-Uribarri, Ana Martín-Galán, Víctor Martín Lozano, G. Sanchez Garcia","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/z66w-rl89","id":"doi:10.1103/z66w-rl89","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/z66w-rl89","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Precision luminosity measurement in proton-proton collisions at a center-of-mass energy of 13 TeV with the CMS detector at the Large Hadron Collider","summary":"","authors":"A. Gevorgyan, A. Hayrapetyan, V. Makarenko, A. Tumasyan, W. Adam, L. Benato, T. Bergauer, M. Dragicevic, S. Gundacker, A. K. Guven, P. S. Hussain, M. Jeitler, N. Krammer, A. Li, D. Liko, M. Matthewman, J. Schieck, R. Schöfbeck, M. Shooshtari, M. Sonawane et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/k1tk-wh93","id":"doi:10.1103/k1tk-wh93","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/k1tk-wh93","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Modeling Logical Gates and Readout of Superconducting Gottesman-Kitaev-Preskill Qubits","summary":"The Gottesman-Kitaev-Preskill (GKP) code is an exciting route to fault-tolerant quantum computing since Gaussian resources and GKP Pauli-eigenstate preparation are sufficient to achieve universal quantum computing. However, there is a disconnect between the noise model that GKP qubits are in theory designed to correct—uniform random displacement errors—and the conditions that affect GKP qubits in superconducting devices in practice: realistic noise channels, logical gates, and inefficient measurements. In this work we bridge this gap in three ways. First, we approximate the effect loss and dephasing on approximate GKP codestates using a random displacement channel, and show that this approximation matches well with numerics. Second, we analyze the error-spreading properties of GKP Clifford gates and describe how a modification in the decoder following the implementation of each gate can reduce the gate infidelity by multiple orders of magnitude. Finally, we consider the effect of homodyne measurement inefficiencies on logical state readout and analyze a scheme to improve the measurement efficiency using the theory of quantum trajectories.","authors":"Mackenzie H. Shaw, Andrew C. Doherty, Arne L. Grimsmo","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/t2ry-ll9n","id":"doi:10.1103/t2ry-ll9n","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/t2ry-ll9n","published":"2026-10-29","title":"SpinHex: A low-crosstalk, spin-qubit architecture based on multielectron couplers","summary":"Semiconductor spin qubits are an attractive quantum computing platform that offers long qubit coherence times and compatibility with existing semiconductor fabrication technology for scale-up. Here, we propose a spin-qubit architecture based on spinless multielectron quantum dots that act as low-crosstalk couplers between a two-dimensional arrangement of spin qubits in a hexagonal lattice. The multielectron couplers are controlled by voltage signals, which mediate fast Heisenberg exchange and thus enable coherent multiqubit operations. For the proposed architecture, we discuss the implementation of the rotated XZZX surface code and numerically study its performance for a circuit-level noise model. We predict a threshold of 0.18 % for the error rate of the entangling gates. We further evaluate the scalability of the proposed architecture and predict the need for 4480 physical qubits per logical qubit with logical error rates of 10 − 12 considering entangling gate fidelities of 99.99 % , resulting in a chip size of 2.6 cm 2 to host 10 000 logical qubits.","authors":"Rubén M. Otxoa, Josu Etxezarreta Martinez, Paul Schnabl, Javier Oliva del Moral, Normann Mertig, Charles Smith, Frederico Martins","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/m538-45pk","id":"doi:10.1103/m538-45pk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/m538-45pk","published":"2026-10-29","title":"Trainable, self-verifying variational quantum eigensolver using adiabatic warm starts","summary":"We study an adiabatic variant of the variational quantum eigensolver (VQE) in which VQE is performed iteratively for a sequence of Hamiltonians along an adiabatic path. We derive the conditions under which gradient-based optimization successfully prepares the adiabatic ground states. These conditions define a trainable tracking region. Additionally, when combined with a gap lower bound, energy-standard-deviation measurements certify eigenstate accuracy at run-time and verify convergence to the global optimum.","authors":"Bojan Žunkovič, Marco Ballarin, Lewis Wright, Michael Lubasch","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-10-28":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/fpnh-n3m5","id":"doi:10.1103/fpnh-n3m5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fpnh-n3m5","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Probing Extragalactic Ultralight Axion Dark Matter with Decade-Long Blazar Optical Polarimetry of 1ES 1959 + 650","summary":"","authors":"Qiu-Ju Huang, Bao Wang, Jun-Jie Wei, Xue-Feng Wu","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/vkyn-sp78","id":"doi:10.1103/vkyn-sp78","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vkyn-sp78","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Search for heavy resonances decaying into four-lepton final states via light bosons in proton-proton collisions at s = 13 TeV","summary":"","authors":"A. Hayrapetyan, V. Makarenko, A. Tumasyan, W. Adam, L. Benato, T. Bergauer, M. Dragicevic, P. S. Hussain, M. Jeitler, N. Krammer, A. Li, D. Liko, M. Matthewman, J. Schieck, R. Schöfbeck, M. Shooshtari, M. Sonawane, W. Waltenberger, C.-E. Wulz, T. Janssen et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/sbb6-t133","id":"doi:10.1103/sbb6-t133","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/sbb6-t133","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Light-induced line narrowing of the magnetic resonance in a parametric resonance optically pumped rubidium magnetometer","summary":"","authors":"Yongbiao Yang, Yang Li, Zhengyu Su, Jun He, Xiaojun Jia, Yanhua Wang, Junmin Wang","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/zgxw-xghd","id":"doi:10.1103/zgxw-xghd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/zgxw-xghd","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Cyclotron radiation signal characterization in resonant cavities for the Project 8 neutrino mass experiment","summary":"","authors":"A. Ashtari Esfahani, S. Bhagvati, H. P. Binney, S. Böser, M. J. Brandsema, N. Buzinsky, R. Cabral, M. C. Carmona-Benitez, C. Claessens, L. de Viveiros, A. El Boustani, M. G. Elliott, S. Enomoto, M. Fertl, J. A. Formaggio, B. T. Foust, J. K. Gaison, P. Harmston, K. M. Heeger, B. J. P. Jones et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/tsgs-hj99","id":"doi:10.1103/tsgs-hj99","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/tsgs-hj99","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Pion valence structure at intermediate x in the residual-field approach","summary":"We calculate the valence parton distribution function (PDF) of pion within the theoretical approach based on spectral function representation of valence quarks in the pion. In this approach we assume that the soft partonic structure of pion is defined by the valence q q ¯ cluster, consisting of current quarks, embedded in the residual field of the pion. The valence PDF is calculated using phenomenological light-front wave functions for the q q ¯ cluster and the residual field. Our result indicates that the peak position of the x weighted valence PDF (xPDF) depends on parameters characterizing the virtuality of the valence quark cluster and the mass of the residual system. Magnitudes of these parameters are obtained by fitting to the height and the peak position of empirical pion xPDF evaluated at starting Q 0 . They indicate that unlike the nucleon case, very little residual mass is needed to describe existing pion PDFs at intermediate x . They also indicate that the q q ¯ cluster is highly virtual and on average the interacting quark carries almost all of the momentum of the q q ¯ cluster. This picture is consistent with the dominance of the Feynman mechanism leaving little room for the hard component in the PDF and practically describing ≈ ( 1 − x ) behavior observed recently at x → 1 limit. Our nontrivial observation is that the height and the peak position of xPDF define the analytic behavior of valence PDF at x → 1 limit.","authors":"Joseph Maerovitz, Misak Sargsian, Christopher Leon","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2bjm-w5yq","id":"doi:10.1103/2bjm-w5yq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2bjm-w5yq","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Denoising graph superresolution toward improved collider event reconstruction","summary":"In preparation for Higgs factories and energy-frontier facilities, future colliders are moving toward high-granularity calorimeters to improve reconstruction quality. However, the cost and construction complexity of such detectors is substantial, making software-based approaches like superresolution an attractive complementary tool. This study explores integrating superresolution techniques into an LHC-like reconstruction pipeline to effectively enhance calorimeter granularity and suppress noise. We find that this software preprocessing step significantly improves reconstruction quality at fixed detector granularity, narrowing the performance gap to a finer calorimeter without closing it. To demonstrate its impact, we propose a novel transformer-based particle-flow model that offers improved particle reconstruction quality and interpretability. Our results serve as a proof of concept, demonstrating the potential of superresolution to enhance reconstruction in collider environments.","authors":"Nilotpal Kakati, Etienne Dreyer, Eilam Gross","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/jw8n-81yg","id":"doi:10.1103/jw8n-81yg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jw8n-81yg","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Multiperiod Fermi-LAT and LHAASO constraints on axionlike particles from Mrk 421 data via χ 2 profile likelihood with Gaussian copula correlations","summary":"","authors":"Longhua Qin, Jiancheng Wang, Chuyuan Yang, Huaizhen Li, Ao Wang, Weiwei Na, Hushan Xu, Zhongtao Han, Xiaogu Zhong, Zunli Yuan, Yubin Li, Guangbo Long","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/g5l2-bzs4","id":"doi:10.1103/g5l2-bzs4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/g5l2-bzs4","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Rolling down the leptonic BSM landscape using machine learning techniques","summary":"In this work, we adapt and apply techniques from machine learning to the exploration of physics beyond the Standard Model in the leptonic sector. Namely, we employ initialization and optimization, as they are applied in machine learning, to minimize a loss function that describes textures or conditions which we want in the neutrino mass matrix. The model free parameters are explored during the optimization, and after training for a number of optimization steps, we obtain matrices that approximately follow the desired forms, as well as their corresponding optimized parameters. We also discuss extensions and additional applications of the ideas presented here in conjunction with other methods based on artificial intelligence.","authors":"Alfredo Aranda, Raymundo Ramos, Alexander J. Stuart","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/fqgp-916r","id":"doi:10.1103/fqgp-916r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fqgp-916r","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Design studies of a pulsed quasimonoenergetic 2-keV neutron source for calibration of low threshold dark matter detectors","summary":"","authors":"L. Chaplinsky, S. Fiorucci, C. W. Fink, M. Garcia-Sciveres, W. Guo, S. A. Hertel, J. K. Wuko, X. Li, J. Lin, R. Mahapatra, W. Matava, D. N. McKinsey, D. Z. Osterman, P. K. Patel, B. Penning, H. Pinckney, M. Platt, Y. Qi, M. Reed, G. R. C Rischbieter et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/vpk9-d7rz","id":"doi:10.1103/vpk9-d7rz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vpk9-d7rz","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Hunting dark matter with the Einstein Telescope","summary":"","authors":"A. J. Iovino, M. Maggiore, N. Muttoni, A. Riotto","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/kwkd-mc8d","id":"doi:10.1103/kwkd-mc8d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kwkd-mc8d","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Neutralino dark matter in gauge mediation","summary":"","authors":"Michihisa Takeuchi, Norimi Yokozaki, Junhao Zhu","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/v4th-yrdh","id":"doi:10.1103/v4th-yrdh","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v4th-yrdh","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Gauge-independent gravitational waves from a minimal dark U (1) sector with viable dark matter candidates","summary":"","authors":"Wan-Zhe Feng, Zi-Hui Zhang","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/xs84-hchv","id":"doi:10.1103/xs84-hchv","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/xs84-hchv","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Field-Level emulator for f ( R ) gravity and massive neutrino cosmologies","summary":"","authors":"J. Bayron Orjuela-Quintana, Mauricio Reyes, Elena Giusarma, Marco Baldi, Neerav Kaushal, César A. Valenzuela-Toledo","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/rd6l-1q9s","id":"doi:10.1103/rd6l-1q9s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/rd6l-1q9s","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Fate of metastable vacua in the type-II two-Higgs-doublet model","summary":"","authors":"Debtosh Chowdhury, Kirtimaan A. Mohan, Poulami Mondal, Subrata Samanta","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dw9z-cn33","id":"doi:10.1103/dw9z-cn33","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dw9z-cn33","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Predictions of modular symmetry fixed points on neutrino masses, mixing, and leptogenesis","summary":"","authors":"Priya, B. C. Chauhan, Deepak Kumar, Takaaki Nomura","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/8t3p-x6s7","id":"doi:10.1103/8t3p-x6s7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8t3p-x6s7","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Fast Single-Atom Preparation in Optical Tweezers via Rydberg Blockade","summary":"Continuously replenished optical tweezer arrays will unlock unlimited-depth quantum circuits with neutral atom qubits. A key bottleneck limiting the cycle time of these systems is removing atoms from tweezers initially loaded with more than one atom. In the conventional technique of light-assisted collisions, slow collisional dynamics limit the timescale for removing excess atoms to several milliseconds. Here, we propose and demonstrate a scheme for selectively removing one atom at a time from multiply occupied tweezers on a microsecond timescale, using intra-tweezer Rydberg blockade and autoionization. We demonstrate the protocol in Yb 171 in two complementary regimes. With two-photon Rydberg excitation from the ground state, we reduce the multi-atom probability to 1% in 64.8 μ s , while retaining single atoms in 58.2 ( 2 ) % of the tweezers, which is comparable to the filling fraction achieved with light-assisted collisions under the same experimental conditions, but over two orders of magnitude faster. With single-photon excitation from the metastable state P 0 3 , reduced single-atom loss enables a higher filling fraction of 74.8 ( 3 ) % , at the cost of additional temporal overhead to prepare the atoms in P 0 3 . The final filling fraction is limited by an unexplained two-body loss mechanism, which, if solved, could enable fast, quasi-deterministic loading.","authors":"Yiyi Li, Vernon M. Hughes, Michael Peper, Yicheng Bao, Chenyuan Li, Sanzhar Bissenali, Jeff D. Thompson","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/kh4h-m8z9","id":"doi:10.1103/kh4h-m8z9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kh4h-m8z9","published":"2026-10-28","title":"Large time-step discretization of adiabatic quantum dynamics","summary":"Adiabatic quantum computing is a general framework for preparing eigenstates of Hamiltonians on quantum devices. However, its digital implementation requires an efficient Hamiltonian simulation subroutine, which may introduce extra computational overhead or complicated quantum control logic. In this work, we show that the time-step sizes in time discretization can be much larger than expected, and the overall complexity is greatly reduced. Remarkably, regardless of the general convergence order of the numerical method, we can choose a uniform time-step size independent of tolerated error and evolution time for sufficiently accurate simulation. Furthermore, with the boundary cancellation condition where the continuous diabatic errors are exponentially suppressed, we provide strong evidence for a superpolynomial convergence of first-order Trotter with uniform time-step size. We apply our analysis to the example of adiabatic unstructured search and show several preferable features of the Trotterized adiabatic approach: It can match the Grover lower bound, it does not require knowledge of the number of marked states, and its performance can be asymptotically comparable with that of the quantum approximate optimization algorithm.","authors":"Dong An, Pedro C. S. Costa, Dominic W. Berry","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-10-27":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/r632-zl7s","id":"doi:10.1103/r632-zl7s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/r632-zl7s","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Hybrid analog-digital simulation of the Abelian Higgs model with transmon qutrits","summary":"To investigate gauge theories with near-term quantum computers warrants exploration of nontraditional quantum simulators to find resource-efficient simulation protocols and ultimately access exotic features of different field theories, including unexplored regimes of the QCD phase diagram. In this work, using superconducting transmon qutrit processors, we formulate and implement a pulse-based, three-level, hybrid analog-digital simulation protocol of the ( 1 + 1 )-dimensional Abelian Higgs model (AHM) on two sites. Alongside this approach, we experimentally realize a gate-based implementation of the same model. Using the natural mapping of the three-level truncation of the transmon Hilbert space to the spin-1 truncated AHM, we observe real-time dynamics of AHM field observables, which are analogous to electric field operators, with both protocols. For the analog-digital protocol, we engineer a Floquet simulation with a combination of local analog drives, driven modification of the natural interaction Hamiltonian of the two transmons, and dynamical decoupling pulses. For the digital protocol, we use a state-of-the-art qutrit processor to implement a Trotterized simulation of the model incorporating advanced error mitigation techniques. We further discuss the scalability of the two approaches, and their potential to be extended to the simulation of other model Hamiltonians. Our experiments demonstrate a viable platform for future studies of spin-1- and SU(3)-based gauge theory models on current and near-term transmon qutrit processors.","authors":"Muhammad Asaduzzaman, Rayleigh W. Parker, Noah Goss, Ahmed I. Mohamed, Max Neiderbach, Zane Ozzello, Ravi K. Naik, Alexander F. Kemper, Irfan Siddiqi, Yannick Meurice, Machiel S. Blok","topics":["computing","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/r71b-6x1t","id":"doi:10.1103/r71b-6x1t","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/r71b-6x1t","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Inability of contextuality analysis of disturbing data to preserve core Kochen-Specker principles","summary":"","authors":"Alisson Tezzin, Elie Wolfe, Barbara Amaral, Matt Jones","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/8qp1-6g7r","id":"doi:10.1103/8qp1-6g7r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8qp1-6g7r","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Development of a variational quantum algorithm for measurement reduction via the block-encoding method","summary":"","authors":"Reo Baba, Mauro Cainelli, Yuki Kurashige","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/9gxh-spl6","id":"doi:10.1103/9gxh-spl6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/9gxh-spl6","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Causally connected regions in relativistic heavy ion collisions","summary":"Quantifying causal connections within fireballs resulting from high energy nucleus-nucleus collisions is pertinent to assessing the viability of several proposed mechanisms that directly influence particle production and correlations. Fireball causal connections have previously been studied in the context of 1 + 1 dimensional Bjorken flow. We expand into 3 + 1 dimensions using Gubser flow, which includes transverse expansion. Our findings suggest that the volume of these causally connected fireballs are on the order of 10–100 fm 3 for observables dependent on the formation of the light quark condensates, but could be larger for other observables.","authors":"Olivia Chabowski, Joseph I. Kapusta","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/cym4-8d9m","id":"doi:10.1103/cym4-8d9m","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/cym4-8d9m","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Baryogenesis and EDMs in the 2HDM with a complex singlet","summary":"We perform the first joint analysis of baryogenesis from initial Higgs charges (also called Higgsogenesis) and electric dipole moments (EDMs) in a model with two Higgs doublets, a complex scalar, and a Majorana fermion. In our proposed scenario, baryogenesis happens in three steps: (1) The decay of the scalar produces an asymmetry between the two Higgs doublets which is partly transferred to fermions via Standard Model processes. (2) This asymmetry is converted into a B − L charge via interactions mediated by the Majorana fermion. (3) The weak sphaleron processes partially convert the resulting B − L charge into a B asymmetry. We perform a numerical analysis of baryogenesis and the EDM contributions and find that, due to resonant enhancement, baryogenesis is possible with singlet masses as low as 10 5 GeV . We also find that for some parameters, the model can give large contributions to the electron and neutron EDM while also producing baryogenesis. The present scenario offers a new perspective on interpreting observational bounds in terms of cosmology: Because it relies on out-of-equilibrium decays, the questions of baryogenesis and C P violation in the Higgs sector may be linked with EDM signals without the additional assumption of a first order electroweak phase transition.","authors":"Björn Garbrecht, Edward Wang","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/h6p4-4qkq","id":"doi:10.1103/h6p4-4qkq","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/h6p4-4qkq","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Enhanced neutrino cooling from parity-doubled nucleons in neutron star cooling simulations","summary":"","authors":"Rodrigo Negreiros, Liam Brodie, Jan Steinheimer, Veronica Dexheimer, Robert D. Pisarski","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/htzs-5yht","id":"doi:10.1103/htzs-5yht","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/htzs-5yht","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Majoron cosmological window: Dark matter and thermal leptogenesis","summary":"The majoron is the Nambu-Goldstone boson associated with the spontaneous breaking of a global B − L symmetry. Remarkably, the minimal majoron framework can simultaneously address three key empirical indications of physics beyond the Standard Model: neutrino masses, the matter-antimatter asymmetry, and dark matter. In this work, we identify the cosmologically viable region in which majoron dark matter and high-scale thermal leptogenesis can be realized simultaneously. We show that successful leptogenesis plays a central role in making this scenario predictive: by constraining the right-handed-neutrino mass scale, it determines the irreducible freeze-in contribution to the majoron abundance and fixes the size of the couplings relevant for visible dark matter decays. Combining the irreducible dark matter production mechanisms with warm dark matter limits and indirect searches for decaying dark matter, we map the resulting majoron cosmological window and show that future x- and gamma-ray telescopes can probe part of the surviving parameter space.","authors":"Arturo de Giorgi, Daniel Naredo-Tuero, Xavier Ponce Díaz","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/rjgy-fcr8","id":"doi:10.1103/rjgy-fcr8","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/rjgy-fcr8","published":"2026-10-27","title":"New energy-loss constraints on dark sectors from deeply inelastic scattering with initial state radiation","summary":"We employ the joint QED and QCD factorization of deeply inelastic, electron-proton scattering with generic initial state radiation to probe the possibility of exotic particle emission—i.e., of weakly coupled particles originating from a dark or hidden sector—through anomalous energy loss. We leverage this possibility through the consideration of phase-space-limited kinematic regions, for which the emission of an additional, undetected particle can particularly impact the associated cross section. In this first paper, as a proof of principle, we focus on radiation from the incoming electron, considering the modification of the lepton distribution function from the emission of particles, that could have spin of up to 2 and various, well-motivated electron couplings. We illustrate the sensitivity of our approach through the computation of the modified cross sections for the emission of MeV-GeV mass-scale, spin 0 particles in kinematics chosen for their sensitivity to initial state electron radiation and suitable to the forward-backward detection sensitivity of the ePIC detector at the Electron Ion Collider.","authors":"Justin Cammarota, John Carlton, Susan Gardner","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/1568-xmzd","id":"doi:10.1103/1568-xmzd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1568-xmzd","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Dynamical effects of gluon mass and small longitudinal momentum in heavy-quark bound states","summary":"Precise derivation of the logarithmically scale-dependent Hamiltonian eigenstate picture for hadrons in the space of virtual quark and gluon states of the canonical front form of QCD requires addressing first the problem of divergences stronger than logarithmic and especially the small- p + divergences in the dynamics of gluons. We propose to facilitate the regularization and cancellation of these divergences using a gluon mass parameter and an auxiliary color-octet scalar field corresponding to the longitudinally polarized gluons. The auxiliary field decouples from the hadronic constituent dynamics when the mass parameter tends to zero, as required in the gauge theory. The same method applies in the cancellation of the quadratic ultraviolet transverse divergences in the self-interactions. After explaining how the method works in the old-fashioned perturbative computations of the scattering amplitudes to all orders, we describe its application to computing the renormalized, scale-dependent Hamiltonian operator acting in the entire Fock space and to deriving its bound-state eigenvalue problems expressed in terms of the correspondingly scale-dependent creation and annihilation operators for effective quarks and gluons. Our detailed discussion focuses on the weak-coupling expansion of second order for the QCD Hamiltonian of effective heavy quarks and the corresponding eigenvalue problems for quarkonia. The results include the concept of confinement in an effective theory with the gluon mass parameter sent to zero and a heuristic scenario concerning extension of the Hamiltonian approach to the dynamics of light quarks.","authors":"Stanisław D. Głazek","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/83wz-blj7","id":"doi:10.1103/83wz-blj7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/83wz-blj7","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Heavy mesons from the QCD instanton vacuum beyond the static limit","summary":"We study pseudoscalar heavy mesons in the QCD instanton vacuum beyond the static limit. Finite-mass effects in the heavy-light loop are encoded in a separable effective vertex built from a profile function ϕ ( p → ) , kept distinct from the static Wilson-line form factor F Q ( ∞ ) ( q → ) of the m Q → ∞ limit. The pseudoscalar two-point function fixes the residual mass Λ and the residue-normalized meson-quark coupling, from which we evaluate the decay constant and the spin-independent kinetic matrix element including the retained 1 / m Q derivative and kinetic contributions. The Isgur-Wise function is evaluated separately in the static heavy-quark limit, and its zero-recoil slope is extracted by using the same residue-normalized heavy-light vertex. The subleading calculation is restricted to the kinetic (derivative) part of the heavy-quark effective theory operators. For a representative vertex, calibrated at fixed m b eff = 5.04 GeV to the leading-order decay constant f B LO = 180.0 MeV and the spin-averaged mass m ¯ B = 5.313 GeV , we obtain δ f B ( 1 / m Q , partial ) = + 121.9 MeV and f B partial = 301.9 MeV , Λ = 190.7 MeV , λ 1 ( ∂ ) = − 0.773 GeV 2 , and ρ IW 2 = 1.153 . The kinetic contribution yields a mass shift of order Λ / 2 and a sizable partial 1 / m Q correction, indicating that the spin-independent nonperturbative 1 / m Q sector is a sensitive probe of the finite-mass heavy-light vertex.","authors":"Ki-Hoon Hong, Yongwoo Choi, Nurmukhammad Rakhimov, Hyun-Chul Kim","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/vjcd-bk2j","id":"doi:10.1103/vjcd-bk2j","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/vjcd-bk2j","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Reply to “Comment on ‘Undecidability in spacetime geometry via the AdS/CFT correspondence”’","summary":"","authors":"Sameer Ahmad Mir, Francesco Marino, Arshid Shabir, Lawrence M. Krauss, Mir Faizal","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/kfyd-zy1m","id":"doi:10.1103/kfyd-zy1m","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/kfyd-zy1m","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Comment on “Undecidability in spacetime geometry via the AdS/CFT correspondence”","summary":"","authors":"Agus Hasan Budiyanto","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ztsv-s7ts","id":"doi:10.1103/ztsv-s7ts","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ztsv-s7ts","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Axionlike particle search with a hybrid Cherenkov-scintillation detector","summary":"This analysis presents the first proof-of-concept search for axionlike particles using a hybrid Cherenkov-scintillation detector at a beam-dump facility. The work is based on the Coherent CAPTAIN-Mills (CCM) experiment, a 10-ton liquid argon light collection detector located at Los Alamos National Laboratory. The CCM200 detector is instrumented with 200 photomultiplier tubes, providing approximately 50% photocathode coverage. To enable optical discrimination, 80% of the photomultiplier tubes are coated with a wavelength-shifting material while the remaining 20% are left uncoated. This configuration, combined with nanosecond-scale timing resolution, provides sensitivity to prompt Cherenkov radiation while maintaining efficient detection of liquid argon scintillation light [; []. This analysis constructs four observables that exploit the Cherenkov emission and event topology expected from axionlike-particle-induced electromagnetic interactions. These observables are combined into a likelihood-ratio classifier that provides powerful rejection of steady-state backgrounds. No statistically significant excess above the expected background prediction is observed for 10 − 3 MeV < m a < 10 MeV . Nevertheless, the improved background rejection enabled by hybrid Cherenkov-scintillation detection allows this analysis to surpass the sensitivity of the previous CCM120 search [] despite less exposure and demonstrate the physics potential for hybrid Cherenkov-scintillation detectors.","authors":"A. A. Aguilar-Arevalo, S. Biedron, J. Boissevain, M. Borrego, L. Bugel, M. Chavez-Estrada, J. M. Conrad, R. L. Cooper, J. R. Distel, J. C. D’Olivo, E. Dunton, B. Dutta, D. E. Fields, M. Gold, E. Guardincerri, E. C. Huang, N. Kamp, D. Kim, K. Knickerbocker, W. C. Louis et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/4y46-ldjv","id":"doi:10.1103/4y46-ldjv","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4y46-ldjv","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Λ b → Λ form factors from light-cone sum rules with Λ -baryon distribution amplitudes","summary":"We compute the Λ b → Λ transition form factors using light-cone sum rules based on Λ -baryon distribution amplitudes, including contributions up to twist 6. The analysis is performed for pseudoscalar, vector, and axial-vector b → s currents and for different choices of the Λ b interpolating current. A central aspect of the study is the explicit inclusion of the negative-parity Λ b * state in the hadronic dispersion relation. Suitable linear combinations of invariant amplitudes are constructed to eliminate the Λ b * pole contribution at the level of the sum rules. All three interpolating currents lead to numerically stable sum rules in the adopted working windows. The pseudoscalar current gives the closest agreement with the expected equation of motion and heavy quark symmetry relations at the level of the central values, while the present leading-order uncertainties do not allow a definitive discrimination among the three currents. The resulting helicity form factors are parametrized using a z expansion and, for the pseudoscalar current, are combined with lattice-QCD tensor form factors to illustrate the low- q 2 differential branching fraction in Λ b → Λ ℓ + ℓ − .","authors":"F. Mahmoudi, D. Mishra","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/qf22-hl52","id":"doi:10.1103/qf22-hl52","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/qf22-hl52","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Ricci flow in quantum gravity","summary":"","authors":"Robert V. Harlander, Yannick Kluth, Jonas T. Kohnen, Henry Werthenbach","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/3myh-zrt6","id":"doi:10.1103/3myh-zrt6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/3myh-zrt6","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Exploring scalar leptoquarks at muon collider via indirect signatures and right-handed neutrino-assisted decays","summary":"","authors":"Subham Saha, Arvind Bhaskar, P. S. Bhupal Dev, Manimala Mitra","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/2tvj-7wfp","id":"doi:10.1103/2tvj-7wfp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2tvj-7wfp","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Nonconservative conformal Killing gravity: Coupling the dark sector with curvature and matter","summary":"","authors":"Salvatore Capozziello, Carlo Alberto Mantica, Luca Guido Molinari","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/442z-3s5v","id":"doi:10.1103/442z-3s5v","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/442z-3s5v","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Irreducible constraints on hadronically interacting sub-GeV dark matter","summary":"","authors":"Peter Cox, Matthew J. Dolan, Avirup Ghosh","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/knpj-2k73","id":"doi:10.1103/knpj-2k73","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/knpj-2k73","published":"2026-10-27","title":"Erratum: Improved local models and new Bell inequalities via Frank-Wolfe algorithms [Phys. Rev. Research 5 , 043059 (2023)]","summary":"","authors":"Sébastien Designolle, Gabriele Iommazzo, Mathieu Besançon, Sebastian Knebel, Patrick Gelß, Sebastian Pokutta","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]}]},"2026-10-26":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/91hd-8k7g","id":"doi:10.1103/91hd-8k7g","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/91hd-8k7g","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Deep Thermalization and Measurements of Quantum Resources","summary":"Quantum resource theories provide a unified framework for characterizing useful quantum phenomena subject to physical constraints, but are notoriously hard to assess in experimental systems. In this Letter, we introduce a unified protocol for quantifying the resource-generating power of arbitrary quantum evolutions applicable to multiple resource theories. It is based on deep thermalization, which has recently gained attention for its role in the emergence of quantum state designs from partial projective measurements. Central to our approach is the use of projected ensembles, recently employed to probe deep thermalization, together with new twirling identities that allow us to directly infer the resource-generating powers of the underlying dynamics. These identities further reveal how resources build up and thermalize in generic quantum circuits. Finally, we show that quantum resources themselves undergo deep thermalization at the subsystem level, offering a complementary and another experimentally accessible route to infer the resource-generating powers. Our Letter connects deep thermalization to resource quantification, offering a new perspective on the essential role of various resources in generating state designs and on efficient estimation of quantum resources in experiment.","authors":"Naga Dileep Varikuti, Soumik Bandyopadhyay, Philipp Hauke","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/tzn2-h5nk","id":"doi:10.1103/tzn2-h5nk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/tzn2-h5nk","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Spreading of Magic Resource under Unitary Clifford Dynamics","summary":"Nonstabilizerness, or quantum magic resource, presents a valuable resource in quantum error correction and computation. We study the dynamics of locally injected nonstabilizerness in unitary Clifford circuits, where the total nonstabilizerness is conserved. However, the absence of physical observables quantifying nonstabilizerness precludes a direct microscopic or hydrodynamic description of its local distribution and dynamics. Using insights from stabilizer quantum error correcting codes, we rigorously show that the spatial distribution of nonstabilizerness can be inferred from a canonical representation of low-magic states, dubbed the bipartite magic gauge. Moreover, we propose two operationally relevant magic length scales. We numerically establish that, at early times, both length scales grow ballistically at distinct velocities set by the entanglement velocity, after which nonstabilizerness delocalizes. Our Letter sheds light on the spatiotemporal structure of quantum resources and complexity in many-body dynamics, opening up avenues for investigating their transport properties and further connections with quantum error correction.","authors":"Mircea Bejan, Pieter W. Claeys, Jiangtian Yao","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/jbqq-v3hh","id":"doi:10.1103/jbqq-v3hh","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jbqq-v3hh","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Nonreciprocal Lensing and Backscattering Suppression via Magneto-optical Nonlocality","summary":"","authors":"Dmitry Vagin, Maxim A. Gorlach","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/v16l-synk","id":"doi:10.1103/v16l-synk","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v16l-synk","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Two-photon Doppler-free spectroscopy of the 6 s 2 S 0 1 → 6 s 23 s S 0 1 Rydberg transition for quantum sensing in ytterbium","summary":"","authors":"Benjamin D. Hunt, Kyle Beloy, Roger C. Brown, Tobias Bothwell, Harikesh Ranganath, Tanner Grogan, Jacob L. Siegel, Andrew D. Ludlow","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/9bcn-q5kg","id":"doi:10.1103/9bcn-q5kg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/9bcn-q5kg","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Nonlinear coupling between motional modes in trapped-ion quantum processors","summary":"","authors":"Wes Johnson, Brandon Ruzic","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/n6zn-xvsw","id":"doi:10.1103/n6zn-xvsw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/n6zn-xvsw","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Raman circular dichroism and spinon quantum geometry of chiral quantum spin liquids","summary":"We show that the quantum geometry of fractionalized spin excitations in Mott-insulating quantum spin liquids (QSLs) gives rise to a finite Raman circular dichroism (RCD) signal. We identify conditions under which the Loudon-Fleury framework, where light modulates the underlying exchange interactions, and the light-matter-coupling approach, where the effective vector potential couples directly to the spinon bands, yield equivalent Raman vertices for the fractionalized quasiparticles. Using the latter, we derive an analytical decomposition of the RCD into different quantum geometric contributions. This reveals the sensitivity of the RCD to the underlying structure of the wave functions and the handedness of the excitations, rather than a nonzero Chern number of spinon excitations. To illustrate this, we apply our approach to two examples, the Kitaev honeycomb model in a magnetic field and a parton mean-field state of the chiral QSL on the triangular lattice.","authors":"Eduard Koller, Valentin Leeb, Natalia B. Perkins, Johannes Knolle","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/frxr-pk3t","id":"doi:10.1103/frxr-pk3t","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/frxr-pk3t","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Search for proton decay via p → e + π 0 π 0 and p → μ + π 0 π 0 in 0.401 megaton-years exposure of Super-Kamiokande I-V","summary":"We searched for proton decay via p → e + π 0 π 0 and p → μ + π 0 π 0 in 0.401 megaton-years of data collected in all pure water detector phases of Super-Kamiokande (SK) I-V. A theoretical study predicts proton decay rates without assuming a particular grand unified theory and suggests that three-body proton decays involving two pions can have decay rates comparable to those of p → e + π 0 and p → μ + π 0 . This is the first search for proton decay into a charged antilepton and two neutral pions in SK. One data candidate event was found for each of the two decay modes, which is compatible with the expected atmospheric neutrino background. We set lower limits on the lifetime of τ / B ( p → e + π 0 π 0 ) > 7.2 × 10 33 and τ / B ( p → μ + π 0 π 0 ) > 4.5 × 10 33 year at 90% confidence level. These limits are more than one order of magnitude better than those of the previous experiment.","authors":"K. Abe, S. Abe, Y. Asaoka, M. Harada, Y. Hayato, K. Hiraide, T. H. Hung, K. Hosokawa, K. Ieki, M. Ikeda, J. Kameda, Y. Kanemura, R. Kaneshima, Y. Kashiwagi, Y. Kataoka, S. Miki, S. Mine, M. Miura, S. Moriyama, K. Nakagiri et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/c2x7-6gq7","id":"doi:10.1103/c2x7-6gq7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/c2x7-6gq7","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Initial performance of the Radar Echo Telescope for Cosmic Rays: Pathfinder for a next-generation neutrino detector","summary":"The Radar Echo Telescope for Cosmic Rays, a pathfinder instrument for the radar echo method of ultrahigh energy neutrino detection, was initially deployed near Summit Station, Greenland, in May 2023. After a four-week commissioning period, nine days of data were taken before the instrument went offline. In this article, we describe the instrument as it was deployed, and the initial performance of the detector. We show that the technical aspects of running a radar-based particle cascade detector in the ice have been demonstrated. Analysis of the 2023 data informed improvements that were incorporated into the May–August 2024 deployment, which has just concluded at time of writing. Results from the 2024 run will be presented in forthcoming publications.","authors":"P. Allison, J. Beatty, D. Besson, A. Connolly, A. Cummings, C. Deaconu, S. De Kockere, K. D. de Vries, D. Frikken, C. Hast, E. Huesca Santiago, C.-Y. Kuo, A. Kyriacou, U. A. Latif, J. Loonen, I. Loudon, V. Lukic, C. McLennan, K. Mulrey, J. Nam et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dr62-483r","id":"doi:10.1103/dr62-483r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dr62-483r","published":"2026-10-26","title":"VERITAS limits on dark matter annihilation in the Galactic Center halo","summary":"","authors":"A. Archer, P. Bangale, J. T. Bartkoske, W. Benbow, N. R. Bond, J. H. Buckley, Y. Chen, J. L. Christiansen, A. J. Chromey, A. Duerr, M. Errando, M. Escobar Godoy, J. Escudero Pedrosa, S. Feldman, Q. Feng, S. Filbert, L. Fortson, A. Furniss, W. Hanlon, O. Hervet et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/k2wg-kwt7","id":"doi:10.1103/k2wg-kwt7","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/k2wg-kwt7","published":"2026-10-26","title":"High-resolution cosmological simulations of primordial dark matter clustering under long-range and fractional forces","summary":"Long-range attractive fifth forces can lead to exponential instabilities in the early Universe. For fermions with a Yukawa coupling to a sufficiently light scalar mediator, rapid oscillations of the scalar field can lead to a conservative force law with fractional behavior on sufficiently large scales. We study cosmological systems evolving under a Newtonian potential and this fractional potential using high-resolution N-body simulations. If compared at sizes approaching an effective scalar Compton length, we find that halos that form under the fractional potential are much more dense than those that form under the Newtonian potential. However, we also find that at this point the scalar field may develop large fluctuations and be nonperturbative, which will modify subsequent clustering and collapse.","authors":"Derek Inman","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/g1gk-mktl","id":"doi:10.1103/g1gk-mktl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/g1gk-mktl","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Comparing relativistic and nonrelativistic quark pair creation models","summary":"We investigate the strong decay properties of light unflavored and strange mesons within a relativistic quark-pair-creation (QPC) framework and compare the results with those obtained in the conventional nonrelativistic QPC model. Our analysis shows that, within the present theoretical and experimental uncertainties, the relativistic QPC model yields predictions for strong decay widths of comparable overall quality to those of the nonrelativistic QPC model. This indicates that the nonrelativistic QPC approach remains adequate for estimating decay widths in most practical applications. Nevertheless, owing to the inclusion of Lorentz boosts and Wigner rotations, the relativistic QPC model exhibits a stronger suppression of the energy-dependent decay amplitudes in the high-energy region. This feature reflects the off-shell behavior of the QPC vertex, rather than the on-shell partial decay widths. It may become relevant in unquenched quark-model studies, where the energy-dependent QPC vertex enters meson-loop self-energy corrections and mass shifts through integrations over a broad energy region.","authors":"Xiu-Li Gao, Yu-Hui Zhou, Bin Wu, Zhi-Yong Zhou","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bbm8-c5yj","id":"doi:10.1103/bbm8-c5yj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bbm8-c5yj","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Mechanical distribution of the pseudoscalar charmonium and bottomonium on the light-front","summary":"We investigate the energy-momentum tensor of pseudoscalar charmonium and bottomonium within the framework of the self-consistent light-front quark model. The gravitational form factors (GFFs), namely the A and D -terms, are evaluated in terms of the light-front wave functions. The corresponding spatial mechanical distributions in the transverse plane are obtained through the Fourier transform of these GFFs. To examine the sensitivity of the results to the internal quark-antiquark distribution inside the meson, two distinct Gaussian forms are employed for the spatial part of the wave function. We analyze several mechanical properties in the transverse plane, including the momentum density, pressure distribution, shear stress, force density, and internal energy density. The pressure distribution exhibits a node where it changes sign from positive (repulsive) to negative (attractive) with increasing transverse distance. The force distribution remains positive throughout the transverse plane, supporting the stability condition proposed in earlier studies. Most of the spatial distributions, except for the shear stress, are found to be sensitive to the choice of the spatial wave function near the center of the meson, while they become nearly insensitive toward the periphery. In contrast, the shear stress distribution exhibits noticeable sensitivity to the choice of wave function in the intermediate transverse region.","authors":"Ashutosh Dwibedi, Satyajit Puhan, Sabyasachi Ghosh","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/phgv-tn2t","id":"doi:10.1103/phgv-tn2t","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/phgv-tn2t","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Twin-peaked gravitational wave signals from a Z 2 phase transition","summary":"We compute the gravitational wave spectrum from a phase transition associated with the spontaneous breaking of a Z 2 DW symmetry. If the transition is second-order, the only source of gravitational waves is the annihilation of domain walls (biased by quantum gravity) formed after this breaking. However, if the transition is first-order, this yields a twin-peaked signal from both the transition itself and the biased domain wall annihilation. Both scenarios originate when a scalar singlet odd under the Z 2 DW obtains a nonzero vacuum expectation value. An additional Z 2 DM odd scalar doublet strengthens the transition by keeping the singlet scalar in thermal equilibrium with the Standard Model plasma at early times. Additionally, the same scalar doublet produces fermionic dark matter via freeze-in, matching observed dark matter relic density.","authors":"Rishav Roshan, Indrajit Saha","topics":["dark","qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/gzgr-gf82","id":"doi:10.1103/gzgr-gf82","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gzgr-gf82","published":"2026-10-26","title":"V / H + jet production with the Cambridge/Aachen algorithm","summary":"We present fixed-order perturbative calculations up to four-loop order for a generic nonglobal QCD observable in hadron-hadron collisions. Specifically, we study the invariant-mass distribution of the highest- p t jet produced in association with a vector boson or a Higgs boson, where jets are defined using the Cambridge/Aachen sequential recombination algorithm. This work is part of a series of papers [K. Khelifa-Kerfa and Y. Delenda, ; K. Khelifa-Kerfa, ; K. Khelifa-Kerfa, ; K. Khelifa-Kerfa and M. Benghanem, ; K. Khelifa-Kerfa, ; K. Khelifa-Kerfa and M. Benghanem, .]examining the impact of various jet algorithms on the perturbative structure of nonglobal observables across different collision environments. We find that, at fixed order, the Cambridge/Aachen algorithm typically suppresses the large nonglobal logarithms more effectively than the anti- k t and k t algorithms; a notable exception occurs at four-loop order for the purely gluonic channel g g → g H , for which the combined clustering and nonglobal logarithm contribution in Cambridge/Aachen clustering exceeds that of k t over a wide range of jet radii. At all orders, the Cambridge/Aachen algorithm performs comparably to the k t algorithm. Furthermore, comparisons of all-orders resummed form factors reveal that finite- N c corrections remain at the percent level, consistent with previous findings.","authors":"K. Khelifa-Kerfa","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/yyj8-lx6w","id":"doi:10.1103/yyj8-lx6w","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/yyj8-lx6w","published":"2026-10-26","title":"From target to projectile: CSS evolution of quark TMD in different light-cone gauges","summary":"We calculate the one-loop corrections to the quark transverse momentum dependent (TMD) in the projectile light-cone gauge using the background field formalism, with the Mandelstam-Leibbrandt (ML) prescription for the extra singularity present in the light-cone gauge propagator. We use the pure rapidity regulator for rapidity divergences. The Collins-Soper-Sterman (CSS) evolution equations are obtained after one-loop renormalization of the quark TMD in this gauge. We discuss how the structure of the rapidity divergences and the double-logarithmic contributions to the CSS resummation compares with the analogous calculation performed in the target light-cone gauge, and discuss the implications for the connection between the TMD factorization and color glass condensate frameworks.","authors":"Tolga Altinoluk, Guillaume Beuf, Jamal Jalilian-Marian, Mirja Tevio","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/lhpl-57zd","id":"doi:10.1103/lhpl-57zd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lhpl-57zd","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Leading-twist to higher-twist generalized parton distributions of the pseudoscalar mesons at nonzero skewness","summary":"We investigate the multidimensional partonic structure of spin-0 mesons, specifically the pion and the kaon, by evaluating their complete set of eight generalized parton distributions (GPDs) up to twist-4. Utilizing the light-front quark model (LFQM) with the Brodsky-Huang-Lepage prescription, we compute these distributions in the kinematically rich nonzero skewness ( ξ ≠ 0 ) domain, strictly within the Dokshitzer-Gribov-Lipatov-Altarelli-Parisi region, x ∈ [ ξ , 1 ] . To construct a three-dimensional tomographic picture, we perform Fourier transforms of the momentum-space GPDs to obtain the impact-parameter-dependent parton distribution functions in the transverse plane and the corresponding diffraction patterns in the longitudinal coordinate space. The numerical results explicitly reveal the consequences of SU(3) flavor symmetry breaking in the LFQM, as the strange quark in the kaon dynamically shifts spatial localizations compared to the lighter up quarks. We also observe that while higher-twist correlations exhibit massive amplitude scaling in the pion, they are heavily suppressed by the larger macroscopic mass of the kaon.","authors":"Abi Jebarson A, Navpreet Kaur, Harleen Dahiya","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8l9b-5kz1","id":"doi:10.1103/8l9b-5kz1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8l9b-5kz1","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Dark dimension and Majorana neutrinos","summary":"","authors":"Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bpgj-ddf9","id":"doi:10.1103/bpgj-ddf9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bpgj-ddf9","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Off-shell Higgs boson measurements: Yukawa couplings, self-coupling, compositeness, and width","summary":"","authors":"A. Gevorgyan, A. Hayrapetyan, V. Makarenko, A. Tumasyan, P. S. Hussain, M. Sonawane, W. Adam, L. Benato, T. Bergauer, M. Dragicevic, S. Gundacker, A. K. Guven, M. Jeitler, N. Krammer, A. Li, D. Liko, M. Matthewman, A. Pfeiffer, J. Schieck, R. Schöfbeck et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/thmz-53f2","id":"doi:10.1103/thmz-53f2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/thmz-53f2","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Magnetic microscopy of skyrmions in magnetic thin films with chiral overlayers","summary":"Topologically nontrivial magnetic textures such as skyrmions offer promising opportunities for spintronic applications. In recent years, it has been shown that the magnetic properties of layered materials can be affected by depositing chiral molecules on the surface, while the influence of chiral overlayers on skyrmion properties such as their stability and interactions remains largely unexplored. To address this challenge, we employ wide-field nitrogen-vacancy ( N − V ) magnetometry to directly image skyrmions in chiral-molecule-functionalized magnetic thin films, enabling quantitative mapping of magnetic stray fields over extended areas under ambient conditions. Using pixel-resolved optically detected magnetic resonance (ODMR) combined with controlled magnetic fields, we reproducibly nucleate and probe skyrmion states in CoFeB ferromagnetic samples, enabling quantitative investigation of their properties. We observe indications of enantioselective and magnetic-field-polarity-dependent modifications of skyrmion properties suggesting possible chirality-dependent modifications of topological spin textures and motivating future theoretical investigations of the underlying coupling mechanisms.","authors":"Buddhika Hondamuni, Théo Balland, Fabian Kammerbauer, Ashish Moharana, Bindu, Amandeep Singh, Meital Ozeri, Shira Yochelis, Yossi Paltiel, Omkar Dhungel, Zeeshawn Kazi, Kai-Mei C. Fu, Hideyuki Watanabe, Mathias Kläui, Arne Wickenbrock, Nir Bar-Gill, Angela Wittmann, Dmitry Budker","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/7dhm-9tvs","id":"doi:10.1103/7dhm-9tvs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7dhm-9tvs","published":"2026-10-26","title":"Tailoring the properties of YBa 2 Cu 3 O 7 − δ thin films by 30-keV He + irradiation: An enabling route to superconducting-device nanopatterning","summary":"Focused helium ion beam (He-FIB) irradiation with 30-keV ions is a key tool for nanoscale patterning and defect engineering in high-transition temperature ( T c ) cuprate superconducting devices, yet its usable fluence window is constrained by the competing requirements of reliable superconductivity suppression and minimal structural degradation. In this work, we provide a comprehensive dataset on the effects of large-area 30-keV He + ion exposure on the electric transport and superconducting properties of epitaxial YBa 2 Cu 3 O 7 − δ (YBCO) thin films. X-ray diffraction shows a fluence-driven loss of crystalline order accompanied by an out-of-plane lattice expansion and an orthorhombic-to-tetragonal transition, culminating in predominant amorphization at the highest fluence of 1 × 10 16 cm − 2 . Raman spectra exhibit increasing disorder while lacking signatures of oxygen depletion, indicating that irradiation mainly generates oxygen-related Frenkel defects rather than changing the carrier concentration. Consistently, with increasing fluence, the normal-state resistivity ρ N ( T ) at temperature T above T c increases strongly, while ∂ ρ N / ∂ T remains nearly unchanged at moderate fluence. The suppression of T c is accurately described by Abrikosov–Gor’kov pair breaking and reaches complete quenching of superconductivity at 4.5 × 10 15 cm − 2 . The anisotropic upper critical fields decrease approximately exponentially with increasing fluence; the vortex activation energy is reduced, and the anisotropy drops, in contrast to oxygen-depleted YBCO. Hall-angle analysis confirms a nearly constant carrier density but a systematic increase in defect scattering and reduced mobility, consistent with a crossover toward the dirty limit at high fluence. These results establish quantitative fluence thresholds and a practical operational window for He-FIB nanopatterning of YBCO quantum circuits.","authors":"Bernd Aichner, Simon Koch, Philipp A. Korner, Max Karrer, Katja Wurster, Christoph Schmid, Ulrich Kentsch, Reinhold Kleiner, Edward Goldobin, Dieter Koelle, Wolfgang Lang","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-10-23":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Nature Physics","doi":"10.1038/s41567-026-03486-y","id":"doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03486-y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-026-03486-y","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Beating the break-even point with autonomous quantum error correction","summary":"","authors":"Yi Li, Qingyuan Mei, Qing-Xuan Jie, Weizhou Cai, Yue Li, Zhiyuan Liu, Zi-Jie Chen, Zihan Xie, Xu Cheng, Xingyu Zhao, Zhenghao Luo, Mengxiang Zhang, Xu-Bo Zou, Chang-Ling Zou, Yiheng Lin, Jiangfeng Du","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Nature Physics","doi":"10.1038/s41567-026-03490-2","id":"doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03490-2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-026-03490-2","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Particle collisions on a quantum computer","summary":"","authors":"Torsten V. Zache","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/4smy-6w2n","id":"doi:10.1103/4smy-6w2n","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4smy-6w2n","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Entangling Ions with Engineered Light Gradients","summary":"Spectral crowding of collective motional modes limits the fidelity of entangling interactions in trapped-ion quantum processors by inducing off-resonant coupling to spectator modes. We introduce a geometric-phase entangling interaction driven by a transverse, time-dependent structured-light force. By applying the force in a plane orthogonal to the optical propagation direction, we reduce the effects of spectral crowding while preserving single-ion addressing. The scheme is compatible with arbitrary qubit encodings, provided that the qubit states experience a differential AC Stark shift. We experimentally realize high-fidelity two-qubit gates with error rates below 5 × 10 − 3 in ion crystals containing up to 12 ions confined within a single potential well. These results establish gradient-field light-shift gates as a scalable approach to high-fidelity entanglement generation in spectrally crowded trapped-ion systems.","authors":"Tommaso Faorlin, Lorenz Panzl, Phoebe Grosser, Pablo Viñas, Alan Kahan, Walter Joseph Hörmann, Yannick Weiser, Giovanni Cerchiari, Thomas Feldker, Alexander Erhard, Georg Jacob, Juris Ulmanis, Rainer Blatt, Alejandro Bermudez, Thomas Monz","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/gk1m-2xsw","id":"doi:10.1103/gk1m-2xsw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gk1m-2xsw","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Erratum: Higgs and Nambu-Goldstone modes in a spin-1 X Y model with long-range interactions [Phys. Rev. A 113, 053325 (2026)]","summary":"","authors":"Daiki Kawasaki, Ippei Danshita","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/5lcd-6ml6","id":"doi:10.1103/5lcd-6ml6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5lcd-6ml6","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Exploring charmonia η c ( 6 S , 7 S ) in the Λ c + Λ ¯ c − invariant mass spectrum of B 0 → K S 0 Λ c + Λ ¯ c −","summary":"We analyze the B 0 → K S 0 Λ c + Λ ¯ c − decay recently observed by the LHCb Collaboration. The Λ c + Λ ¯ c − invariant-mass spectrum exhibits an accumulation near 4.63 GeV, which we investigate together with the accompanying Λ c + K S 0 spectrum constrained by the known Ξ c ( 2923 ) and Ξ c ( 2939 ) states. Our amplitude model includes a nonresonant term, the two established Ξ c contributions, and high-lying charmonium states in the Λ c + Λ ¯ c − channel. Guided by screened Godfrey-Isgur and quark-pair-creation calculations, we test the 4.63 GeV enhancement with a fixed η c ( 6 S ) amplitude at ( M 1 , Γ 1 ) = ( 4629.00 , 24.20 ) MeV , which improves the description of the 4.63 GeV region, and examine a fixed η c ( 7 S ) contribution at ( M 2 , Γ 2 ) = ( 4718.84 , 22.84 ) MeV , which improves the higher-mass region of the spectrum. We also evaluate the η c ( n S ) → Λ c + Λ ¯ c − baryonic decays via a hadron-loop mechanism and estimate the B 0 → K S 0 η c ( n S ) production in naive factorization. Within the narrow width approximation, the fit yields a B ( B 0 → K S 0 η c ( 6 S ) ) × B ( η c ( 6 S ) → Λ c + Λ ¯ c − ) of about 6.2 × 10 − 6 , while the factorization and hadron-loop calculations give rates roughly one to three orders of magnitude lower than the above fitted ratio. These results highlight the need for more precise data and a full amplitude analysis to determine the line shapes and clarify the nature of the higher-mass contribution.","authors":"Cheng-Xi Liu, Jun-Zhang Wang, Xiang Liu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/7tk3-33s9","id":"doi:10.1103/7tk3-33s9","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7tk3-33s9","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Low-energy radon backgrounds from electrode grids in dual-phase xenon TPCs","summary":"The dual-phase xenon time projection chamber (TPC) is a powerful technology to detect rare interactions such as scatters of dark matter particles on nuclei. In particular, the built-in gain of ionization signals in a dual-phase TPC makes it sensitive to events in the few-electron regime, as expected from low-mass dark matter interactions. The pursuit of this low-energy sensitivity through ionization-only signal detection has so far been hindered by excessive electron backgrounds observed across experiments. Much of this background is attributed to the plate-out of Rn 222 decay chain isotopes on the high-voltage electrode grid surfaces that span the full cross section of the TPC. This work presents a first-principles model constructed for this background, the predictions of which are consistent with data from the LZ and LUX experiments. We then discuss strategies to mitigate this background in future dual-phase TPCs and the possibility of applying this grid background model to ionization-only dark matter searches.","authors":"D. S. Akerib, A. K. Al Musalhi, F. Alder, B. J. Almquist, S. Alsum, C. S. Amarasinghe, A. Ames, T. J. Anderson, N. Angelides, H. M. Araújo, J. E. Armstrong, M. Arthurs, X. Bai, A. Baker, J. Balajthy, S. Balashov, J. Bang, J. W. Bargemann, E. E. Barillier, A. Baxter et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/dfrh-vxwm","id":"doi:10.1103/dfrh-vxwm","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dfrh-vxwm","published":"2026-10-23","title":"QCD phase diagram for three-flavor Möbius domain-wall fermions","summary":"We investigate the phase transition of quantum chromodynamics (QCD) with three degenerate quark flavors at zero baryon chemical potential. Using Möbius domain-wall fermions as the lattice fermion formulation, we ensure excellent chiral symmetry preservation. Our simulations are performed at three different temporal lattice extents, N t = 6 , 8, 12, with a fixed lattice spacing a = 0.1361 ( 20 ) fm , corresponding to temperatures of 242(4), 181(3), and 121(2) MeV, respectively. We explore a range of quark masses and spatial volumes with aspect ratios N s / N t spanning from 2 to 4. By analyzing the mass and volume dependencies of the plaquette, plaquette susceptibility, chiral condensate, chiral susceptibilities, and Binder cumulant, we identify the pseudocritical transition quark masses from our largest lattice volumes. For N t = 6 , this is 184(10) MeV (determined from the plaquette susceptibility), corresponding to m π ≃ 0.96 GeV . For N t = 8 and 12, the transition points vary slightly depending on whether the total or disconnected chiral susceptibility is used, yielding ranges of 36(1)–39.1(9) MeV and 3.5(3)–3.7(2) MeV, respectively, in the MS ¯ scheme at a scale of μ = 2 GeV . In terms of the pseudoscalar mass, these correspond to m π ≃ 427 – 445 MeV and 133–137 MeV. The negligible volume dependence at N t = 6 and 8, combined with finite-size scaling analysis at N t = 12 revealing volume growth significantly weaker than expected for a first- or second-order phase transition, points to a continuous crossover at these specific quark mass points. Additionally, we study the effects of residual chiral symmetry breaking on the chiral condensate and chiral susceptibilities using two different values of L s .","authors":"Yu Zhang, Yasumichi Aoki, Jishnu Goswami, Shoji Hashimoto, Issaku Kanamori, Takashi Kaneko, Yoshifumi Nakamura","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/l3fr-9qlz","id":"doi:10.1103/l3fr-9qlz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/l3fr-9qlz","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Muon-induced di-tau production as a probe of new physics","summary":"The muon trident process, which involves coherent scattering to produce tau pairs, is a powerful tool for constraining dark sectors. We propose to explore this channel using future high-energy muon beams with the dedicated active-target detector, High-Energy Muon Electronic Research Apparatus (HEMERA). The detector would complement muon beam-dump searches by resolving prompt signatures and could operate from preparatory facilities involving an individual muon beam of the full muon collider. We illustrate its capabilities for a leptophilic scalar with Yukawa-like couplings that can mediate thermal dark matter production. With 10 18 incident TeV-scale muons, even a compact 10 kg silicon-based HEMERA will extend current B -factory and ( g − 2 ) μ bounds by over an order of magnitude in coupling strength. In the leptophilic thermal dark matter paradigm, this search offers a way to probe dark sector targets beyond the neutrino floor of dark matter direct detection. This provides an additional compelling motivation for the development of high-energy muon beam infrastructure.","authors":"Sudipta Das, Luke Kupari, Mary Hall Reno, Sebastian Trojanowski","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/l88d-nfqp","id":"doi:10.1103/l88d-nfqp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/l88d-nfqp","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Tripartite entanglement in e + e − → t t ¯ Z","summary":"Multipartite entanglement is a uniquely quantum form of correlation that captures collective properties of a composite quantum state beyond those encoded in its bipartite subsystems. We investigate this phenomenon in the process e + e − → t t ¯ Z at a future lepton collider, where the final-state spins span the tripartite Hilbert space H = C 2 ⊗ C 2 ⊗ C 3 . Starting from the standard model helicity amplitudes, we reconstruct the full 12 × 12 spin density matrix and characterize its entanglement structure through one-to-one negativities, one-to-other negativities, and the genuine multipartite negativity, evaluated at three increasingly inclusive levels of phase-space integration. Pairwise entanglement is generally suppressed relative to the collective (one-to-other) and the genuine multipartite entanglement, and all measures decrease as more kinematic information is integrated out. Assuming quantum tomography in the fully leptonic decay channel at s = 1 TeV , we find that collective entanglement should be accessible at a realistic high-luminosity polarized lepton collider. By contrast, certifying genuine multipartite entanglement is more challenging, with only limited sensitivity projected for a specific polarization benchmark within the luminosity expected at the International Linear Collider. The study establishes e + e − → t t ¯ Z as an attractive laboratory for probing multipartite entanglement in high-energy collisions and provides a general mixed state framework that applies to any tripartite spin system.","authors":"Dorival Gonçalves, Alberto Navarro, Kazuki Sakurai","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8zfj-q2qh","id":"doi:10.1103/8zfj-q2qh","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8zfj-q2qh","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Improved approximations for collective neutrino oscillations","summary":"","authors":"Matthew Riccio, A. B. Balantekin","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/zx4x-89k6","id":"doi:10.1103/zx4x-89k6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/zx4x-89k6","published":"2026-10-23","title":"First constraints on long-range neutrino interactions using IceCube DeepCore","summary":"","authors":"Gopal Garg, J. Krishnamoorthi, Anil Kumar, Sanjib Kumar Agarwalla","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/6ps1-mw9l","id":"doi:10.1103/6ps1-mw9l","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6ps1-mw9l","published":"2026-10-23","title":"Quantum Advantage for Sensing Properties of Classical Fields","summary":"Modern precision experiments often probe unknown classical fields with bosonic sensors in quantum-noise-limited regimes where vacuum fluctuations limit conventional readout. We introduce quantum signal learning, a sensing framework that extends metrology to a broader property-learning setting, and propose a quantum-enhanced protocol that simultaneously estimates many properties of a classical signal with shot noise suppressed below the vacuum level. Our scheme requires only two-mode squeezing, passive optics, and static homodyne measurements, and enables classical estimation of many properties from the same experimental dataset. We prove that our protocol enables a quantum speedup for common classical sensing tasks, including measuring electromagnetic correlations, real-time feedback control of interferometric cavities, and Fourier-domain matched filtering. To establish these separations, we introduce an optimal-transport conditioning method and show both worst-case exponential separations from all entanglement-free strategies and practical speedups over homodyne and heterodyne baselines. We further show that when squeezing is treated as a resource, a protocol with squeezed light can sense a structured classical background exponentially faster than any coherent classical probe.","authors":"Jordan Cotler, Daine L. Danielson, Ishaan Kannan","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-10-22":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Nature Physics","doi":"10.1038/s41567-026-03484-0","id":"doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03484-0","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41567-026-03484-0","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Teleportation transition of surface codes on a superconducting quantum processor","summary":"","authors":"Yiren Zou, Hong-Kuan Xia, Aosai Zhang, Xuhao Zhu, Feitong Jin, Qingyuan Wang, Yu Gao, Chuanyu Zhang, Ning Wang, Zhengyi Cui, Fanhao Shen, Zehang Bao, Zitian Zhu, Jiarun Zhong, Gongyu Liu, Jia-Nan Yang, Yihang Han, Yiyang He, Jiayuan Shen, Han Wang et al.","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/cbmf-s76k","id":"doi:10.1103/cbmf-s76k","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/cbmf-s76k","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Dihadron Transverse-Spin Asymmetries in Muon-Deuteron Deep-Inelastic Scattering","summary":"In 2022, the COMPASS collaboration performed semi-inclusive measurements of deep-inelastic muon-scattering on a transversely polarized deuteron ( LiD 6 ) target. From these data, transverse-spin-dependent dihadron asymmetries are extracted using pairs of oppositely charged hadrons. These asymmetries are directly sensitive to the quark transversity distributions and provide an independent handle on these fundamental quantities with respect to the Collins asymmetries measured in single-hadron production. The present results significantly improve upon the previous COMPASS deuteron measurements, which were the only available deuteron data worldwide, and reach a statistical precision comparable to that of the existing proton results from COMPASS. A small but nonzero asymmetry is observed at large Bjorken- x , consistent with theoretical expectations. A point-by-point extraction of the valence-quark transversity distributions yields, in particular, a substantially improved determination of the d -quark transversity. These measurements represent a major step towards a complete flavor mapping of the transverse-spin structure of the nucleon.","authors":"G. D. Alexeev, M. G. Alexeev, C. Alice, A. Amoroso, V. Andrieux, V. Anosov, S. Asatryan, K. Augsten, W. Augustyniak, C. D. R. Azevedo, B. Badelek, R. Beck, J. Beckers, Y. Bedfer, V. Benesova, J. Bernhard, F. Bradamante, A. Bressan, W.-C. Chang, C. Chatterjee et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/6rsq-mjzw","id":"doi:10.1103/6rsq-mjzw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6rsq-mjzw","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Dark Matter, Baryon Number, and Cosmic-Ray Antinuclei","summary":"Antideuterons and antihelium nuclei in the cosmic-ray spectrum have long been considered a smoking gun signature of dark matter annihilation, making the tentative observation of several such events by AMS highly intriguing. Conventional dark matter models, however, can produce only up to O ( 1 ) antideuteron events at AMS and are not capable of generating observable fluxes of antihelium. In this Letter, we propose a class of models in which dark matter annihilates into particles carrying baryon and lepton number, whose subsequent decays produce enhanced fluxes of antinucleons and antinuclei. Such scenarios are motivated by grand unified theories and can lead to an order-of-magnitude or larger enhancement in the resulting antideuteron and antihelium-3 fluxes, providing a means by which to potentially explain the events reported by the AMS Collaboration.","authors":"Caleb Gemmell, Dan Hooper, Seth Koren, Fabrizio Vassallo","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/9zxp-frbf","id":"doi:10.1103/9zxp-frbf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/9zxp-frbf","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Anisotropic flows of light-flavor and charmed hadrons in Pb+Pb collisions at energies available at the CERN Large Hadron Collider","summary":"We apply a constituent quark equal-velocity combination (EVC) model to study the elliptic flow ( v 2 ) and triangular flow ( v 3 ) of light-flavor and single-charmed hadrons in Pb+Pb collisions at s N N = 2.76 and 5.02 TeV. v 2 , 3 of hadrons in the EVC model can be expressed as a linear superposition of the v 2 , 3 of quarks at the same velocity as that of the hadrons. We find that available experimental data for v 2 and v 3 of p , Λ , Ξ , and Ω as a function of transverse momentum ( p T ) can be consistently explained by the EVC formula using the v 2 of up/down quarks and the v 2 of strange quarks. In comparison with v 2 data of ϕ at s N N = 2.76 TeV, which can be naturally explained by the v 2 of strange quarks, explanation of the data of ϕ mesons at s N N = 5.02 TeV requires an additional contribution of two-kaon coalescence, which indicates approximately 20% influence of final-state hadronic rescattering on v 2 of ϕ at s N N = 5.02 TeV. Using v 2 and v 3 of light-flavor quarks obtained in studying light-flavor hadrons and v 2 and v 3 of charm quarks determined from D 0 meson data, we apply the EVC model to predict the anisotropic flows of D s + , Λ c + , Ξ c 0 , and Ω c 0 and compare them with the available experimental data. The preliminary data for v 2 of D 0 , D s + , and Λ c + at s N N = 5.36 TeV are found to be naturally described by the EVC model.","authors":"Yan-ting Feng, Rui-qin Wang, Feng-lan Shao, Jun Song","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/ym8v-cp7j","id":"doi:10.1103/ym8v-cp7j","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ym8v-cp7j","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Charged-current neutrino-nucleus scattering off 129 Xe and 131 Xe including the meson-exchange contributions","summary":"","authors":"Joona Kasurinen, Pekka Pirinen, Jouni Suhonen","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/mvys-wjf4","id":"doi:10.1103/mvys-wjf4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mvys-wjf4","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Ringdown and tide: Fingerprints of dark matter halo profiles","summary":"","authors":"Arkadip Bhowmik, Avijit Chowdhury, Sayan Chakrabarti","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/v9qk-wlnr","id":"doi:10.1103/v9qk-wlnr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v9qk-wlnr","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Higher excited charmed and charmed-strange mesons in an unquenched quark model","summary":"In this paper, as a continuation of our previous work on the low-lying heavy-light meson spectra, we systematically study the mass spectra and Okubo-Zweig-Iizuka-allowed strong decays of the higher 3 S -, 2 P -, 2 D -, and 1 F -wave charmed and charmed-strange mesons within a unified unquenched quark model. It is found that coupled-channel effects induce sizable state dependent mass shifts, which modify the relative level spacings and lower the predicted masses compared with conventional quenched calculations. The newly observed D s 1 ( 2933 ) + reported by the LHCb collaboration could be identified as the low mass axial vector state D s ( 2 P 1 ) via the 2 1 P 1 − 2 3 P 1 mixing. For the broad structure D s J ( 3040 ) + observed earlier by the collaboration, the D s ( 3 1 S 0 ) assignment seems to be favored over the high mass mixed state D s ( 2 P 1 ′ ) . Meanwhile, the D ( 3000 ) 0 signals reported by LHCb cannot be consistently explained by any of the considered 3 S , 2 P , 2 D , or 1 F assignments. Our predicted masses and decay properties of the missing higher D and D s mesons may provide useful information for future experimental searches.","authors":"Ru-Hui Ni, Jia-Jun Wu, Xian-Hui Zhong","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/8wrp-hkth","id":"doi:10.1103/8wrp-hkth","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8wrp-hkth","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Generalized parton distributions of the nucleon in the chiral quark soliton model: Sum over all quark states at nonzero skewness and momentum transfer","summary":"","authors":"Josuke Minamiguchi","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/mjzl-ymsz","id":"doi:10.1103/mjzl-ymsz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mjzl-ymsz","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Probing dark matter interactions with stellar motion near Sagittarius A*","summary":"","authors":"R. Andrew Gustafson, Ian. M. Shoemaker, Volodymyr Takhistov","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/dlfv-z6m2","id":"doi:10.1103/dlfv-z6m2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/dlfv-z6m2","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Cosmological and High-Energy Physics Implications from Gravitational-Wave Background Searches in LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA’s O1–O4a Runs","summary":"We search for gravitational-wave background signals produced by various early Universe processes in the Advanced LIGO O4a dataset, combined with the data from the earlier O1, O2, and O3 (LIGO-Virgo) runs. The absence of detectable signals enables powerful constraints on fundamental physics. We derive gravitational-wave background energy density upper limits from the O1–O4a data to constrain parameters associated with various possible processes in the early Universe: first-order phase transitions, cosmic strings, domain walls, stiff equation of state, axion inflation, second-order scalar perturbations, primordial black hole binaries, and parity violation. In our analyses, the presence of an astrophysical background produced by compact (black hole and neutron star) binary coalescences throughout the Universe is also considered. We address the implications for various cosmological and high-energy physics models based on the obtained parameter constraints. We conclude that LIGO-Virgo data already yield significant constraints on numerous early Universe scenarios.","authors":"A. G. Abac, I. Abouelfettouh, F. Acernese, K. Ackley, C. Adamcewicz, S. Adhicary, D. Adhikari, N. Adhikari, R. X. Adhikari, V. K. Adkins, S. Afroz, A. Agapito, D. Agarwal, M. Agathos, N. Aggarwal, S. Aggarwal, O. D. Aguiar, I.-L. Ahrend, L. Aiello, A. Ain et al.","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review X","doi":"10.1103/8vgt-twh5","id":"doi:10.1103/8vgt-twh5","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/8vgt-twh5","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Nontrivial Boundary-Mediated Superconducting Transport in a TRSB Topological Iron-Based Superconductor","summary":"The interplay of superconductivity, band topology, and spontaneous time-reversal-symmetry breaking (TRSB) is expected to enable topological superconducting boundary states. Intriguingly, the iron-chalcogenide superconductor FeTe 0.55 Se 0.45 exhibits spontaneous magnetization in the superconducting state, motivating its use as a single-material platform in which three ingredients coexist. Here, we report evidence for a nontrivial, boundary-mediated superconducting transport response in exfoliated Fe(Te,Se) devices, exhibiting long-range nonlocality and strongly suppressed thermal broadening. Polar Kerr measurements establish that TRSB emerges below T Kerr < T c and coexists with superconductivity across multiple Fe(Te,Se) compositions studied here, providing an independent symmetry-breaking scale that can be directly compared with transport. To reliably access boundary transport, we engineer crystallographically sharp, continuous edges and implement side-surface-dominant contacts, as validated by contact-size analysis and cross-sectional imaging. Under these conditions, topological FeTe 0.55 Se 0.45 exhibits an anomalous conductance plateau that is absent in topologically trivial FeTe 0.40 Se 0.60 and Fe 1.02 Te 0.55 Se 0.45 under comparable measurements. This plateau requires both the source and drain to be attached to uninterrupted sharp edges, persists over micrometer-scale separations far exceeding the bulk coherence length, has markedly reduced thermal decoherence, and collapses when the drain is moved to the top surface. Its temperature evolution is governed by TRSB: The plateau shows minimal thermal broadening below T Kerr * and disappears near T Kerr rather than T c . Collectively, the doping selectivity, TRSB correlation, edge-geometry requirement, and long-range nonlocal nature establish experimentally grounded criteria for identifying boundary-mediated superconducting transport in FeTe 0.55 Se 0.45 and motivate further phase-sensitive and theoretical work to determine its microscopic origin.","authors":"Wenyao Liu, Gabriel Natale, Camron Farhang, Michael Geiwitz, Qishuo Tan, Xingyao Guo, Mason Gray, Vincent Lamberti, Jazzmin Victorin, Huairuo Zhang, James L. Hart, Vsevolod Belosevich, Xi Ling, Qiong Ma, Wan Kyu Park, Kenji Watanabe, Takashi Taniguchi, Judy J. Cha, Albert V. Davydov, Kin Chung Fong et al.","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"PRX Quantum","doi":"10.1103/51mt-ghpn","id":"doi:10.1103/51mt-ghpn","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/51mt-ghpn","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Efficiently Learning Global Quantum Channels with Local Tomography","summary":"Scalable characterization of quantum processors is crucial for mitigating noise and imperfections. While randomized measurement protocols enable efficient access to local observables, inferring a globally consistent description of multi-qubit processes remains challenging. Here we introduce a local-to-global reconstruction framework for one-dimensional multi-qubit states and channels. The method is efficient provided that correlations, as quantified by the conditional mutual information, decay exponentially. In particular, we prove that under this assumption, the required number of samples scales polynomially with the system size and the inverse of the desired global reconstruction error, which we quantify by the trace-norm distance between the true and the reconstructed Choi states. Our approach is based on combining local shadow tomography with locally optimal recovery maps obtained by convex optimization. We supplement these rigorous guarantees by studying the performance of the protocol numerically for a system evolving under a local Lindbladian and a noisy, shallow circuit. By employing a tensor network representation, we reconstruct channels acting on up to 50 qubits and accurately recover global diagnostics such as the process fidelity, the Choi state purity, and Pauli-weight-resolved process matrix elements. Our work thus extends the powerful toolbox of local shadow tomography to scalable channel characterization with access to global properties.","authors":"Zidu Liu, Dominik S. Wild","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/pg4b-jpg2","id":"doi:10.1103/pg4b-jpg2","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/pg4b-jpg2","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Penning trap single-photon counter for axion detection","summary":"Discovering the microscopic composition of dark matter is one of the most important open problems in physics today. Axions are a leading candidate to be dark matter; however, a search of the full range of all likely axion masses is hampered by the standard quantum noise limit. This makes haloscope searches for axions with masses above ∼ 100 µ eV unfeasible with current technologies. To overcome this limitation, we propose a photon counting technique designed to operate at 30–60 GHz for detecting axions with masses between 124 and 248 µ eV , based on a single electron in a Penning trap. The electron cyclotron mode absorbs microwave photons, and, via the continuous Stern-Gerlach effect, this absorption imparts a measurable phase shift onto the axial motion. In this paper, we comprehensively analyze this photon detection method. We introduce a type of fast, phase-sensitive axial detection technique, using axial-magnetron parametric amplification to overcome detector Johnson noise and cancel associated frequency shifts. This method may find other applications in precision Penning trap frequency measurements. We compare the efficiency of the electron single-photon counter with an ideal device and find that our proposed photon counter has sufficient performance to search for high-mass axions.","authors":"Jack A. Devlin, Marko L. Wojtkowiak, Shreyak R. Banhatti, He Zhang, Jiacheng Shi, Toren S. Dofher, Jonathan M. H. Gosling, Michael R. Tarbutt, Richard C. Thompson","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/2t8j-3wzj","id":"doi:10.1103/2t8j-3wzj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/2t8j-3wzj","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Searching for ultralight dark matter with MOLeQuTE: A massive optically levitated quantum tabletop experiment","summary":"Many well theoretically motivated models of ultralight dark matter are expected to give rise to feeble oscillatory forces on macroscopic objects. Optically trapped sensors have high force sensitivities but have remained relatively unexplored in this context. In this work, we propose a tunable, optically trapped sensor specifically designed to detect such forces. Our design features a high-mass ( ∼ mg ) plate whose high aspect ratio allows its weight to be supported by a vertical beam without excessive heating. We present the first systematic analysis and optimization of quantum noises in optically trapped systems and show that our setup has the potential to operate at the standard quantum limit with current off-the-shelf technologies. We demonstrate that our sensor could offer unique access to large regions of uncharted parameter space of vector baryon minus lepton dark matter, with projected sensitivities that could advance existing limits by several orders of magnitude over a broad range of frequencies.","authors":"Louis Hamaide, Hannah Banks, Peter Barker, Andrew A. Geraci","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/prhg-3t42","id":"doi:10.1103/prhg-3t42","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/prhg-3t42","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Max-Cut graph-driven quantum circuit design for geometrically frustrated planar spin systems with spin-glass-like energy landscapes","summary":"Finding the ground state of geometrically frustrated spin systems is a challenging problem with broad implications. Many hard optimization problems, including NP-complete problems, can be mapped, for instance, to frustrated Ising models, where competing interactions produce rugged, spin-glass-like energy landscapes. The difficulty is particularly pronounced in the weak-field regime, where geometrical frustration dominates, and the spectral gap becomes exponentially small, making it hard to identify the true ground state. In this work, we consider planar frustrated lattices constructed from the triangular motif, the minimal unit of geometrical frustration. We present a graph-based approach that allows for accurate state initialization of a frustrated triangular spin lattice with up to 20 sites while avoiding barren plateaus. To optimize circuit efficiency and trainability, we employ a clustering strategy that organizes qubits into distinct groups based on the maximum cut technique, which divides the lattice into two maximally disconnected subsets. We provide evidence that this Max-Cut-based lattice division offers a robust framework for optimizing circuit design and effectively modeling frustrated systems at polynomial cost. All simulations are performed within the variational quantum eigensolver formalism, the current paradigm for noisy intermediate-scale quantum devices, but can be extended beyond. Our results underscore the potential of hybrid quantum-classical methods in addressing complex optimization problems.","authors":"Seyed Ehsan Ghasempouri, Gerhard W. Dueck, Stijn De Baerdemacker","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/7ddz-xy1y","id":"doi:10.1103/7ddz-xy1y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7ddz-xy1y","published":"2026-10-22","title":"Turning graphene into an intensity gauge for Angle-Resolved Photoemission Spectroscopy","summary":"Angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy (ARPES) provides the most direct experimental access to electronic band structures and many-body interactions in solids. Beyond energy and momentum, the measured intensity also carries detailed information about the initial-state wavefunction through the complex photoemission matrix element governing the transition to—and scattering character of—the photoelectron final state. Orbital tomography seeks to exploit this sensitivity to reconstruct electronic wavefunctions. However, its quantitative implementation has been hindered by the lack of direct information on the phase of the matrix element and by the nontrivial role of solid-state scattering. Here, we overcome these limitations by achieving an experimental reconstruction of the complex photoemission matrix element in quasi-freestanding monolayer graphene (QFMLG), including both amplitude and phase. By combining circular-dichroism ARPES with linearly polarized measurements within a controlled scattered-wave framework, we determine the full matrix element over a broad kinetic-energy range. The reconstructed amplitudes exhibit pronounced channel-selective resonances and Cooper minima, directly revealing substantial solid-state modifications beyond commonly used simplified models. Leveraging this quantitative reconstruction, we establish QFMLG as an easily available intensity reference for ARPES. Its known complex matrix element enables direct normalization for the product of photon flux and detector response, allowing the determination of photoemission cross sections in other materials under identical experimental geometries. This advances orbital tomography by ARPES to a quantitatively normalized approach that separates the initial-state wavefunction from final-state scattering contributions, and more broadly may provide access to the associated quantum geometry.","authors":"Cedric Schmitt, Hans Kirschner, Jonas Erhardt, Lukas Gehrig, Philipp Keßler, Kilian Strauß, Merit Spring, Bing Liu, Stefan Enzner, Alexander Gottwald, Vitaliy Feyer, Timur Kim, Cephise Cacho, Chris Jozwiak, Aaron Bostwick, Eli Rotenberg, Lukasz Plucinski, F. Stefan Tautz, Ralph Claessen, Serguei Soubatch et al.","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-10-21":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/gbkc-6nlj","id":"doi:10.1103/gbkc-6nlj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/gbkc-6nlj","published":"2026-10-21","title":"One-Positron Quantum Cyclotron","summary":"A one-positron quantum cyclotron is realized with a single positron suspended indefinitely in the magnetic field of a Penning trap. This opens the possibility of quantum measurements of the positron magnetic moment at a precision much higher than attained with classical cyclotron motion. Comparing the magnetic moments measured using positron and electron quantum cyclotrons should provide the most stringent test of the fundamental C P T invariance of the standard model of particle physics in the lepton sector.","authors":"T. G. Myers, L. Soucy, B. A. D. Sukra, B. Sinha, G. Gabrielse","topics":["foundations","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/m23z-bt9y","id":"doi:10.1103/m23z-bt9y","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/m23z-bt9y","published":"2026-10-21","title":"First Simultaneous Analysis of Transverse Momentum Dependent and Collinear Parton Distributions in the Proton","summary":"We present the first simultaneous global QCD analysis of unpolarized transverse momentum dependent (TMD) and collinear parton distribution functions (PDFs) in the proton. Our Letter incorporates data from deep-inelastic scattering, Drell-Yan, inclusive weak boson, W + charm, and jet production involving PDFs, as well as TMD Drell-Yan and Z -boson production data from fixed target and collider experiments sensitive to both TMD and collinear distributions. The analysis is performed at next-to-next-to-leading logarithmic accuracy for QCD resummation in TMD observables and next-to-leading order for observables described in collinear factorization. The combined analysis improves knowledge of both TMD and collinear PDFs, particularly in the sea-quark sector, providing a consistent simultaneous description of the aforementioned observables.","authors":"P. C. Barry, A. Prokudin, T. Anderson, C. Cocuzza, L. Gamberg, W. Melnitchouk, E. Moffat, D. Pitonyak, J.-W. Qiu, N. Sato, A. Vladimirov, R. M. Whitehill, JAM Collaboration (TMD Analysis Group","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/wlcl-c61b","id":"doi:10.1103/wlcl-c61b","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wlcl-c61b","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Next-to-Next-to-Next-to-Leading-Order QCD Corrections to Photon-Pair Production","summary":"The production of two isolated photons at the Large Hadron Collider has applications in Higgs-boson phenomenology and in new-physics searches. In fact, measurements would provide more insight if a percent-level theoretical prediction were available. We account for next-to-next-to-next-to-leading-order strong-interaction effects in the cross section for the first time. This is not only necessary to demonstrate the convergence of perturbation theory, but also results in a reduction of the theoretical uncertainty by a factor of 3 down to 3%. With this, we pave the way to improved analyses.","authors":"Michał Czakon, Felix Eschment, Terry Generet, Rene Poncelet","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/f1dk-yk6g","id":"doi:10.1103/f1dk-yk6g","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/f1dk-yk6g","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Quasilinear Theory of Fast Flavor Instabilities","summary":"Dense neutrino plasmas can develop instabilities that drive collisionless flavor exchange, equivalent to the emission of flavomons, the quanta of flavor waves. We treat these waves as independent linear degrees of freedom and develop a quasilinear theory, including backreaction on the neutrino distribution and nonresonant neutrino-flavomon interactions, while neglecting wave-wave processes. In a statistically homogeneous, axisymmetric model, the saturated neutrino and flavomon distributions agree closely with periodic-box solutions of the original quantum kinetic equation. Our results provide the first theoretical interpretation for the numerical observations. They also show that flavomon phases have little influence on the final state, supporting the quasilinear theory as a framework for capturing flavor evolution without resolving nonlinear small-scale dynamics.","authors":"Damiano F. G. Fiorillo, Georg G. Raffelt","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/6jd1-s1b3","id":"doi:10.1103/6jd1-s1b3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6jd1-s1b3","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Quantum Sensing Using Geometrical Phase in Qubit-Oscillator Systems","summary":"","authors":"Nishchay Suri, Zhihui Wang, Tanay Roy, Davide Venturelli, Wibe Albert de Jong","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review A","doi":"10.1103/wgsb-dq54","id":"doi:10.1103/wgsb-dq54","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/wgsb-dq54","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Ensemble engineering to overcome destructive cancellation in quantum measurements","summary":"","authors":"Myeongsu Kim, Manas Sajjan, Sabre Kais","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review B","doi":"10.1103/mcz4-xh6z","id":"doi:10.1103/mcz4-xh6z","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mcz4-xh6z","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Probing antiferromagnetic hysteresis on programmable quantum annealers","summary":"Using programmable analog quantum annealing processors, we implement a sampling-based magnetic hysteresis protocol to probe the counterintuitive notion of magnetic memory in antiferromagnetic models. A key component of this protocol responsible for the hysteresis is a transverse field, which enables state transitions, while the longitudinal magnetic field sweep is done via a longitudinal control field. We observe intriguing nonmonotonic magnetization, alongside full saturation and reversal of the hysteresis curve, Barkhausen-like noise, as well as emergent magnetic domains mediated by quantum fluctuations that give rise to the magnetic memory effect in antiferromagnets.","authors":"Elijah Pelofske, Pratik Sathe, Cristiano Nisoli, Frank Barrows","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/1ptt-tdqx","id":"doi:10.1103/1ptt-tdqx","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/1ptt-tdqx","published":"2026-10-21","title":"3D initial-state dynamics across scales: A comparative study of saturation and string-based descriptions","summary":"We compare the longitudinal deposition of various conserved quantities in the initial condition models of a string based (SMASH) and a saturation based () approach. SMASH has been shown to work reasonably well at lower collision energies as an initial condition for the SMASH-vHLLE hybrid approach, while , based on the color-glass-condensate, works well in the regime of perturbative quantum chromodynamics. The two models are capable of providing longitudinally resolved initial conditions, which is essential for three-dimensional hydrodynamical simulations. The goal of this study is to interface the different regions of applicability of the two models, to investigate the initial state dynamics in the intermediate energy regime. We analyze the deposition of transverse energy, charge and baryon number across a large range of collision energies (62.4 GeV to 5.02 TeV) and find that, while they are good agreement at lower energies, their energy and baryon deposition differs substantially at higher center of mass energies.","authors":"Lucas Constantin, Oscar Garcia-Montero, Niklas Götz, Hannah Elfner","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/f668-vzjw","id":"doi:10.1103/f668-vzjw","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/f668-vzjw","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Semileptonic decays of Λ → p ℓ − ν ¯ ℓ in light-front dynamics","summary":"We investigate the exclusive semileptonic decays of Λ → p ℓ − ν ¯ ℓ ( ℓ = e , μ ) within the standard model using the light-front quark model. The transition form factor behaviors of Λ → p are obtained from the effective treatment of nonvalence contributions in addition to the valence ones in the Drell-Yan-West frame due to the Bethe-Salpeter formalism. Based on these form factors, we obtain that the branching ratios of Λ → p e − ν ¯ e and p μ − ν ¯ μ , including nonvalence contributions, are around 8.32 × 10 − 4 and 1.31 × 10 − 4 , which are consistent with the latest measurements from the BESIII Collaboration, respectively. Our results indicate that nonvalence contributions can play a non-negligible role in the semileptonic baryon decays within the light-front framework.","authors":"Chong-Chung Lih, Chao-Qiang Geng","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/v4nc-phgs","id":"doi:10.1103/v4nc-phgs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v4nc-phgs","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Heavy quark coalescence probability in the presence of a potential","summary":"","authors":"Taesoo Song, Jiaxing Zhao","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/7fjv-rz78","id":"doi:10.1103/7fjv-rz78","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7fjv-rz78","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Lowest-radiation environments in the Solar System: New opportunities for underground rare-event searches","summary":"We study neutrino, muon, and gamma-ray fluxes in extraterrestrial environments in our Solar System via semianalytical estimates and Monte Carlo simulations. In sites with negligible atmosphere, we find a strong reduction in the cosmic-ray-induced neutrino and muon fluxes relative to their intensities on Earth. Neutrinos with energies between 50 MeV and 100 TeV show particularly strong suppression, by as much as 10 3 , even at shallow depths. The solar neutrino suppression increases as the square of the site’s distance from the Sun. Natural radiation due to nuclear decay is also expected to be lower in many of these locations and may be reduced to effectively negligible levels in liquid water environments. The sites satisfying these characteristics represent an opportunity for greatly extending the physics reach of underground searches in fundamental physics, such as searches for weakly interacting massive particle (WIMP) dark matter, neutrinoless double-beta decay, the diffuse supernova neutrinos, and neutrinos from nearby supernova. As a potential near-term target, we propose a measurement of muon and gamma-ray fluxes in an accessible underground lunar site such as the Mare Tranquillitatis pit to perform a first measurement of the prompt component in cosmic-ray-induced particle production, and to constrain lunar evolution models. Although the focus of this work is on fundamental physics, the finding of this work—that the existence of an atmosphere in a planet enhances cosmic-ray-induced radiation in its underground—could be relevant for a broader range of sciences, such as radiobiology deep underground and searches for extraterrestrial life.","authors":"Xilin Zhang, Jason Detwiler, Clint Wiseman","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/b7hg-cpmf","id":"doi:10.1103/b7hg-cpmf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/b7hg-cpmf","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Cosmic-web environmental impacts on the halo substructure boosts to dark matter annihilation signals","summary":"","authors":"Feven Markos Hunde, Wojciech A. Hellwing, Maciej Bilicki","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ddc7-2pch","id":"doi:10.1103/ddc7-2pch","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ddc7-2pch","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Crowdsourcing gravitational waves from superradiant axions","summary":"Black hole superradiance is a powerful probe of ultralight axions. If nature contains a boson with a mass of order 10 − 12 eV , mere vacuum fluctuations will lead to its efficient production around spinning stellar mass black holes, forming a gravitational atom that both drains the black hole spin and decays to produce near-monochromatic gravitational waves. Existing superradiance constraints derive primarily from spin measurements of a handful of identified black holes. Here we instead present a detailed study of the population level effect: gravitational waves arising from both the 100 million black holes in the Milky Way and the stochastic signal from axion clouds throughout the universe. We study the impact of a broad range of systematic uncertainties on the black hole properties and compute the projected axion sensitivity for LIGO, as well as the future instruments Einstein Telescope, Cosmic Explorer, and a high-frequency magnetic Weber bar. We demonstrate that LIGO can robustly probe axion masses from roughly 10 − 13 eV to 4 × 10 − 12 eV . If the black hole population extends to masses slightly below 5 M ⊙ —as hinted for by LIGO inspiral observations—LIGO would approach 10 − 11 eV . Under that same assumption we show that a future high-frequency detector could push considerably higher, potentially beyond 10 − 10 eV in the most optimistic scenarios, reaching toward the lowest masses within the projected sensitivity of axion dark matter searches.","authors":"Sebastian A. R. Ellis, Orion Ning, Nicholas L. Rodd, Jan Schütte-Engel","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/fp1g-tvtv","id":"doi:10.1103/fp1g-tvtv","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/fp1g-tvtv","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Higher Mellin moments of the unpolarized PDF of the pion and the kaon from lattice QCD","summary":"We present results on the Mellin moments of the unpolarized parton distribution function (PDF) of the pion and kaon up to the fourth order. The computation is done using one N f = 2 + 1 + 1 gauge ensemble of twisted mass fermions with quark masses tuned to approximately their physical values. We reconstruct the valence pion and kaon PDFs using the connected contributions to the three Mellin moments. We compare our results on the Mellin moments and the reconstructed PDFs with other lattice QCD and phenomenological determinations.","authors":"Constantia Alexandrou, Simone Bacchio, Priyajit Jana, Marcus Petschlies, Luis Alberto Rodriguez Chacon, Gregoris Spanoudes, Fernanda Steffens, Carsten Urbach, Urs Wenger","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/179b-2qkg","id":"doi:10.1103/179b-2qkg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/179b-2qkg","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Probing an up-phobic Higgs at colliders: The role of b -associated production in the democratic 3HDM","summary":"We show that there exists a theoretically well-motivated Higgs sector in which the conventional heavy-Higgs production mechanisms are suppressed. In this limit, the b -associated production channel assumes the role of the primary production mechanism. We demonstrate that the democratic three Higgs-doublet model (3HDM) provides a viable beyond the SM framework in which this limit can be realized. We further show that existing b -associated searches provide the most sensitive LHC probe of this 3HDM scenario in the up-phobic limit and derive the corresponding constraints on its parameter space. We also present projections for future collider searches, demonstrating their potential to probe further regions of the model’s up-phobic parameter space.","authors":"Dipankar Das, Teesha Khandelwal, Ipsita Saha","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/9hlw-dkwl","id":"doi:10.1103/9hlw-dkwl","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/9hlw-dkwl","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Quantum gravity and spectral running cutoff","summary":"We have recently shown that a natural way to implement the Wilsonian paradigm in gauge theories is through the introduction of a “spectral cutoff,” a cut on the eigenvalues of the covariant Laplacian, pointing out that this provides the route toward the renormalization group (RG) construction. Here, we apply this idea to quantum gravity, resorting to two realizations of the spectral running cutoff: “hard” and “smooth.” We derive the RG equations for the Newton and cosmological constant and find the RG pattern of the asymptotic safety scenario, with a non-Gaussian UV-attractive fixed point.","authors":"C. Branchina, V. Branchina, F. Contino, R. Gandolfo, A. Pernace","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/zs6q-prh4","id":"doi:10.1103/zs6q-prh4","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/zs6q-prh4","published":"2026-10-21","title":"ξ R ϕ 2 nonminimal coupling, and the long range gravitational potential for different spin fields from 2-2 scattering amplitudes","summary":"In this paper, we investigate the long range gravitational effect of curvature-scalar field nonminimal coupling in the form of ξ R ϕ 2 , in the perturbative quantum gravity framework. Such coupling is most naturally motivated from the renormalization of a scalar field theory with a quartic self interaction in a curved spacetime background. This coupling results in two scalar- n graviton vertices which contain no explicit momenta of the scalar, qualitatively different from the usual, e.g., κ h μ ν T μ ν -type minimal matter-graviton vertices. Assuming the dimensionless coupling parameter ξ to be small, we compute the 2-2 scattering Feynman amplitudes between such scalars up to O ( G 2 ξ ) . From the nonrelativistic limit of these amplitudes, we compute the corresponding long range gravitational potential. There exists no tree level contribution ( O ( ξ G ) ) here, and hence the one loop O ( G 2 ξ ) result is leading. Recently, the effect of a cosmological constant in such nonminimal interaction and the subsequent gravitational potential was computed. In this work, we take the cosmological constant to be vanishing. The resulting potential is found to have r − 4 leading behavior. We further extend these results for scalar-massive spin-1 and massive spin- 1 / 2 scattering. Spin and polarization dependence of the two body potential have been explicitly demonstrated. We discuss some possible physical implications of these results.","authors":"Avijit Sen Majumder, Ayan Kumar Naskar, Sourav Bhattacharya","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/624f-mztg","id":"doi:10.1103/624f-mztg","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/624f-mztg","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Diffusion Monte Carlo study of deuteronlike fully light hexaquarks","summary":"We perform a diffusion Monte Carlo study of fully light hexaquarks containing three u quarks and three d quarks within a constituent-quark model. Both compact and baryon-baryon-like arrangements were considered separately. All compact hexaquark configurations are found well above their corresponding baryon-baryon thresholds, suggesting that deeply bound compact six-quark states are not favored in the light-quark sector within this model. By contrast, several dibaryonlike configurations lie close to, but above, the N N , N Δ , and Δ Δ thresholds and show spatial structures compatible with molecular states. One configuration exhibits two well-defined nucleonlike subclusters separated by several femtometers, characteristic of deuteronlike spatial clustering, although its calculated energy remains slightly above the corresponding threshold.","authors":"M. C. Gordillo, J. Segovia","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/77r5-31gd","id":"doi:10.1103/77r5-31gd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/77r5-31gd","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Quantum gravity in the Landau background gauge","summary":"We examine, in the Landau background gauge, the effective action of a quantum theory of gravitational and matter fields. We analyze the Becchi-Rouet-Stora-Tyutin invariance of this theory and study the Ward identities which implement the symmetry of the background effective action. The off-shell and on-shell counterterm Lagrangians are explicitly calculated in this gauge at one-loop order. We argue that the matter self-energy in the Landau background gauge, which is finite to this order, may be finite also at higher loops.","authors":"J. Frenkel, S. Martins-Filho","topics":["qgravity"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/jsm8-d14d","id":"doi:10.1103/jsm8-d14d","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jsm8-d14d","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Radiative transitions for the ground and excited charmonia states","summary":"In this work, we have investigated the physical properties like decay constants, radiative transitions, decay widths, and branching ratios for the ground and radially excited charmonia states. For the numerical calculations, we have adopted the light-front quark model. We have studied χ c 0 → J / ψ + γ and ψ ( 2 S ) → χ c 0 + γ , h c ( 1 P ) → η c ( 1 S ) + γ , and η c ( 2 S ) → h c ( 1 P ) + γ transitions in this work. We have also studied the Q 2 dependence of the electric dipole transition form factors (TFFs) for all four transitions and compared them with the available lattice QCD and basis light-front quantization results. Using the TFF results, we have calculated the decay widths and branching ratios for these transitions. The electric dipole matrix element is decomposed into its Lorentz structures and E 1 ( Q 2 ) is extracted from a combination of the longitudinal and transverse plus-current amplitudes canceling the accompanying Coulomb form factor identically. The light-front spin-orbit wave functions of the P -wave states χ c 0 ( P 0 3 ) and h c ( P 1 1 ) are constructed from the Melosh transformation of the corresponding | ( L S ) J J z ⟩ states, rather than from the S -wave pseudoscalar and vector ones. The charm quark mass and the harmonic scale are fixed by a variational fit to the two 1 S masses alone. Our numerical results of decay constants, decay widths, and branching ratios are overall in good agreement with available experimental, theoretical, and lattice simulation data.","authors":"Anurag Yadav, Satyajit Puhan, Harleen Dahiya","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/jhc8-1tc6","id":"doi:10.1103/jhc8-1tc6","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/jhc8-1tc6","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Determination of the spatial extent of a neutrino wave packet from electron capture decay experiments","summary":"","authors":"Amlan Ray, Arindam Kumar Sikdar, Parnika Das, Abraham G. Kofman, Gershon Kurizki","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/82g5-bsvp","id":"doi:10.1103/82g5-bsvp","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/82g5-bsvp","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Probing the ground state of the antiferromagnetic Heisenberg model on the kagome lattice using geometrically informed variational quantum eigensolver","summary":"This work investigates the nature of the ground state of the antiferromagnetic Heisenberg model on fundamental kagome cells, a triangle and a star, using the variational quantum eigensolver algorithm on real quantum hardware. We demonstrate that the ground-state preparation is achievable using a shallow hardware-efficient quantum circuit with a naturally Euclidean parameter space. Our custom ansatz is capable of accurately recovering meaningful properties of the ground state such as the spin-spin correlation terms and static structure factor without explicit error mitigation. These features are found to be resilient to noise. We exploited the Fubini-Study metric in constructing the ansatz, ensuring a singularity-free parameter space. With this ansatz design, the adaptive gradient descent optimizer achieves a faster convergence in terms of the number of iterations compared to simultaneous perturbation stochastic approximation. We further apply error mitigation techniques, including zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) and qubitwise readout-error mitigation (REM). While ZNE does not obey the Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle, the conditions under which REM preserves it are discussed.","authors":"Abdellah Tounsi, Nacer Eddine Belaloui, Abdelmouheymen Rabah Khamadja, Takei Eddine Fadi Lalaoui, Mohamed Messaoud Louamri, David E. Bernal Neira, Mohamed Taha Rouabah","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/7my9-zgfz","id":"doi:10.1103/7my9-zgfz","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/7my9-zgfz","published":"2026-10-21","title":"Phase-space representations of quantum error-correcting codes","summary":"We connect the structure theorem for quasiprobability representations to quantum error correction. For a code space invariant under a subgroup of an extended Weyl-Heisenberg group, the Brif-Mann construction gives a semifunctorial representation whenever its kernel satisfies the Stratonovich-Weyl axioms and composes on channels covering full error-correction cycles. With phase-space parity available, a kernel other than the ordinary Wigner one exists iff the code space is invariant under a lattice of displacements: Among compact-phase-space bosonic codes, this is only the ideal Gottesman-Kitaev-Preskill family; in finite dimension, this is every qudit stabilizer code, whose negative volume for odd prime dimension is the syndrome-averaged logical magic. For rotation-symmetric, one-photon, non-Pauli qudit and spin codes, only the ordinary kernel remains, its negativity reflecting the physical carrier, not logical content; a sector-graded representation built from recovery isometries restores a resource reading. We add a channel atlas, a closed-form magic lifetime under displacement noise, a Kirkwood-Dirac layer, and finite-energy corrections.","authors":"Enrico Bozzetto, Jonte R. Hance","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true}]},"2026-10-20":{"papers":[{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/999m-b763","id":"doi:10.1103/999m-b763","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/999m-b763","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Measurement of Coherent Elastic Neutrino-Nucleus Scattering on Germanium by COHERENT","summary":"The COHERENT Collaboration reports the most precise measurement of the coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering cross section to date. This measurement was performed with COHERENT’s germanium detector array, Ge-Mini, at the Spallation Neutron Source at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. A cumulative exposure of 4.69 × 10 22 protons on target yielded a total number of observed counts of 124 − 12 + 14 and a flux-averaged cross section of 0.98 ± 0.10 ( statistical ) ± 0.10 ( systematic ) relative to the standard-model expectation of 5.9 × 10 − 39 cm 2 . The well-understood energy and timing distributions of the neutrino source allow for independent measurements of muon- and electron-neutrino scattering rates. This information is used to improve constraints on nonstandard neutrino interactions mediated by heavy particles.","authors":"M. Adhikari, M. Ahn, D. Amaya Matamoros, P. S. Barbeau, V. Belov, I. Bernardi, C. Bock, A. Bolozdynya, R. Bouabid, A. Bracho, J. Browning, B. Cabrera-Palmer, N. Cedarblade-Jones, S. Chen, A. I. Colón Rivera, V. da Silva, Y. Efremenko, S. R. Elliott, A. Erlandson, L. Fabris et al.","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/v3kk-rwk8","id":"doi:10.1103/v3kk-rwk8","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/v3kk-rwk8","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Syndrome resampling enhances quantum error correction thresholds","summary":"","authors":"Luis Colmenarez, Áron Márton, Markus Müller","topics":["computing"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/lwp9-xjym","id":"doi:10.1103/lwp9-xjym","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lwp9-xjym","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Orbital angular momentum transfer from terahertz vortex beam to a superconductor via Higgs mode excitation","summary":"","authors":"Kotaro Tanaka, Daemo Kang, Naotaka Yoshikawa, Fumiya Sekiguchi, Hirotaka Terai, Takahiro Morimoto, Ryo Shimano","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Letters","doi":"10.1103/pvcy-pc82","id":"doi:10.1103/pvcy-pc82","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/pvcy-pc82","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Almost all pure entangled states enable unbounded nonlocality sharing","summary":"","authors":"Anna Steffinlongo, Nicola D’Alessandro, Martin J. Renner","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review C","doi":"10.1103/zc6z-ss9j","id":"doi:10.1103/zc6z-ss9j","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/zc6z-ss9j","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Probing the neutron skin thickness through jet charge in deep-inelastic scattering","summary":"The neutron-skin thickness in neutron-rich nuclei has traditionally been measured using low-energy hadronic and electronic scatterings at hadronic level. In this paper, we propose an alternative probe to the neutron-skin thickness through deep-inelastic scattering in high-energy electron-ion collisions, leveraging the intrinsic correlation between final-state jet charge distribution and initial-state partonic distributions in nucleons. Specifically, we demonstrate the sensitivity of jet charge distribution to the neutron skin-thickness and density profile in e + Pb collisions with varying centralities as determined using Glauber model, and in isobar collisions of e + Ru and e + Zr . We predict a strong suppression of positive jet charge distribution and an enhancement for negative jet charge distribution in peripheral electron-ion collisions, revealing the neutron-skin effect. Premised on the effective determination of centrality, this proposal can also be extended to photon and -boson tagged jet charge distribution in proton-nucleus collisions at the Large Hadron Collider, providing another similar access to neutron-skin structure.","authors":"Shan-Liang Zhang, Enke Wang, Xin-Nian Wang, Hongxi Xing","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/y1qs-vbld","id":"doi:10.1103/y1qs-vbld","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/y1qs-vbld","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Gravitational form factor of the pion in perturbative QCD with a dilaton interaction","summary":"We investigate the pion gravitational form factors (GFFs) at intermediate and large momentum transfer within the framework of QCD factorization. Our analysis centers on the non-Abelian T J J correlator, which couples the local QCD energy-momentum tensor to two external gluon fields and explicitly encodes the perturbative effects of the trace anomaly. We demonstrate how this quantum anomaly induces a scalar, dilatonlike contribution to the hard-scattering kernel. To ensure field-theoretic consistency, a careful separation of the quark and gluon sectors is performed, accounting for the modifications introduced by gauge-fixing terms and Slavnov-Taylor identities on the off-shell gluonic structure. To obtain realistic phenomenological predictions and regulate soft-gluon endpoint divergences in the hard kernel, we implement the Sudakov resummation framework coupled with a Gaussian model for the pion’s transverse-momentum-dependent wave function. We show that the resulting anomaly-induced corrections significantly modify the behavior of the pion GFFs at large momentum transfer, leaving a unique imprint on the trace sector and the D -term.","authors":"Claudio Corianó, Hsiang-Nan Li, Dario Melle, Leonardo Torcellini","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/ljzd-c2mt","id":"doi:10.1103/ljzd-c2mt","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/ljzd-c2mt","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Revisiting predictions for cosmic-ray antinucleon fluxes from galactic dark matter","summary":"The data on cosmic antiprotons have reached an outstanding precision on energies spanning from GeV to hundreds of TeV, thanks to the space-based AMS-02 experiment. The balloon-borne GAPS experiment, which just completed its first Antarctic flight, will address antiproton and antideuteron fluxes well below GeV energies. Antinuclei in cosmic rays, as well as being produced by spallation reactions between cosmic-ray nuclei and the atoms of the interstellar medium, may hide contributions from exotic sources, such as particle dark matter annihilation in the Galaxy. In this paper, we present predictions for cosmic antiproton, antideuteron and antihelium fluxes both from secondary and dark matter origin. We use state-of-the-art production spectra, nuclear coalescence for antinuclei, and Galactic propagation models to derive upper limits on the dark matter annihilation cross section from AMS-02 antiproton data in different propagation scenarios (BIG and QUAINT). We quantify the impact of future GAPS data, showing that its sensitivity to sub-GV antiprotons could improve the ⟨ σ v ⟩ constraints by up to an order of magnitude for light DM ( m χ ≲ 50 GeV ). For heavier antinuclei, the detection perspective with existing and upcoming experiments are derived for those scenarios consistent with AMS-02 antiproton flux. The detectability of such signals strongly depends on the experiment, the propagation model, and the hadronization tuning. Our analysis underscores the complementarity of antinuclei channels for indirect DM searches and the critical role of low-energy windows in constraining light DM candidates.","authors":"Lorenzo Stefanuto, Mattia Di Mauro, Fiorenza Donato, Nicolao Fornengo, Jordan Koechler, David Maurin","topics":["dark"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/5nry-t25l","id":"doi:10.1103/5nry-t25l","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/5nry-t25l","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Formation of shell-crossing singularities in effective gravitational collapse models with bounded and unbounded polymerizations","summary":"","authors":"Francesco Fazzini, Kristina Giesel, Eric Rullit","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/lsrj-27p1","id":"doi:10.1103/lsrj-27p1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/lsrj-27p1","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Emission and cooling of a newborn quark star","summary":"","authors":"Mikalai Prakapenia, Gregory Vereshchagin","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/x5gn-jp4v","id":"doi:10.1103/x5gn-jp4v","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/x5gn-jp4v","published":"2026-10-20","title":"How to identify a Majoron: Effective field theories of spontaneous lepton number breaking","summary":"We revisit the traditional type I, II, and III seesaw mechanisms in the presence of a complex scalar field charged under a global U ( 1 ) symmetry that can be identified with lepton number and the Peccei-Quinn symmetry. After symmetry breaking, the radial mode becomes heavy while the angular mode appears as an axionlike particle, traditionally dubbed the Majoron. We construct the effective field theory obtained after integrating out the heavy states and analyze two matching orders: first removing the radial mode and then the seesaw fields, and vice versa. Both procedures yield the same low-energy Lagrangian containing only Standard Model fields and the Majoron. Because a single vacuum expectation value fixes the mediator masses, the radial mode, and every Majoron coupling, these models predict relations among observables rather than their individual size, and it is these relations that are testable. Indeed, the invisible Higgs width is locked to the universal suppression of the Higgs couplings, while the Majoron-lepton coupling is fixed by the measured nonunitarity of the leptonic mixing matrix, and the two independently give comparable lower bounds on the same lepton-number-breaking scale, of the order of 1–10 TeV. Neutrinoless double beta decay with Majoron emission, by contrast, has no sensitivity in this class of models. The framework is, thus, falsifiable even when the new states lie far beyond experimental reach.","authors":"Carla Biggio, Marta Fuentes Zamoro, Xu Li, Luca Merlo, Luca Ottonello","topics":["dark","particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/hs3z-6tnr","id":"doi:10.1103/hs3z-6tnr","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/hs3z-6tnr","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Searching for lepton flavor violating decays of the Higgs boson into μ τ , e τ , and e μ final states at FCC-ee","summary":"We investigate the projected sensitivity of the Future Circular Collider in e + e − mode (FCC-ee) in probing the lepton flavor violating (LFV) decay of the 125 GeV Higgs boson, h → μ τ , h → e τ , and h → e μ at center-of-mass energy of 240 GeV with an integrated luminosity of 5 ab − 1 . With the clean multilepton final states, we consider the Higgs production modes that contribute to e + e − → ℓ ℓ h , in particular, the Higgs-strahlung and the vector boson fusion production channels. The 95% confidence level upper limits on branching ratio are found to be 5.94 × 10 − 4 , 6.28 × 10 − 4 , and 7.37 × 10 − 5 for the channels h → μ τ , h → e τ , and h → e μ , respectively. In addition, we also consider the LFV decay of a new CP -even Higgs boson with mass in the range of 110–200 GeV. The projected FCC-ee sensitivities are then compared to those from low-energy searches for ℓ → ℓ ′ γ decay. We find that the FCC-ee sensitivity for the LFV decays of the Higgs boson in the e − τ and μ − τ channels is superior to the low-energy constraints. However, in the e − μ case, the low-energy search offers stronger constraints.","authors":"Pongsakorn Sriling, Rutjanaret Uttho, Vichayanun Wachirapusitanand, Norraphat Srimanobhas, Patipan Uttayarat","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/x3hb-qkyj","id":"doi:10.1103/x3hb-qkyj","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/x3hb-qkyj","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Mass spectra of charged mesons and the quenching of vector meson condensation via exact phase-space diagonalization","summary":"We investigate the dynamics and mass spectra of charged pseudoscalar ( π + ) and vector ( ρ + ) mesons in a background magnetic field at finite temperature using the two-flavor Nambu-Jona–Lasinio model. By employing a quark propagator that isolates the Schwinger phase from its Landau level expansion, we formulate an analytically controlled phase-space framework utilizing the Wigner-Weyl transform and the Moyal star product. This approach enables the algebraic diagonalization of the Bethe-Salpeter equations for composite states with asymmetric fractional constituent charges. For the pseudoscalar channel, we analytically verify the exact cancellation between the dynamical random phase approximation spatial sum rules and the vacuum gap equation. This identity preserves the generalized Goldstone theorem, causing the π + pole mass to track the kinematic zero-point energy drift at order of e B . In the vector channel, our full phase-space evaluation reveals that the Zeeman spin-splitting emerges dynamically from microscopic threshold truncations governed by the chiral Dirac algebra. Notably, we find that the tachyonic instability of the spin-aligned ρ + state is quenched. The magnetic catalysis of the chiral condensate drives the continuum threshold ( 2 M ) upwards, overtaking the Zeeman attraction and preventing vector meson condensation within this mean-field framework. Furthermore, finite-temperature evaluations show a monotonic thermal suppression of the meson masses driven by Pauli blocking, yet all modes remain bound without undergoing Mott dissociation prior to chiral symmetry restoration.","authors":"Jingyi Chao, Kun Xu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/n9c8-q7g3","id":"doi:10.1103/n9c8-q7g3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/n9c8-q7g3","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Meson states in the ‘t Hooft model: Hamiltonian approach","summary":"We point out that the masses of the highly excited bound quark-antiquark states in QCD 2 in the infinite N limit may be determined in the framework of a simple quantum-mechanical model with the potential V ( x ) = σ | x | . In the ultrarelativistic case, the masses follow the pattern μ n 2 = π g 2 N 2 n , which coincides with the law derived by ‘t Hooft by solving the Bethe-Salpeter equation. In the nonrelativistic case, the levels follow the asymptotics ε n = μ n − 2 m = C n 2 / 3 , where the constant C can be determined by solving the ‘t Hooft equation or, alternatively, the nonrelativistic Schrödinger equation.","authors":"Andrei Smilga","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bsqc-6q3t","id":"doi:10.1103/bsqc-6q3t","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bsqc-6q3t","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Renormalizing a three-flavor lattice calculation of the two-photon contribution to K L → μ + μ −","summary":"The Standard Model prediction for the rare K L → μ + μ − decay depends critically on the long-distance contribution coming from the exchange of two photons. Such a contribution can be computed using lattice QCD and an effective three-flavor theory including only the u , d and s quarks, provided terms falling as the inverse square of the omitted charm quark mass, 1 / m c 2 , are neglected. Because of the missing Glashow-Iliopoulos-Maiani cancellation, this three-flavor theory contains additional low-energy constants that depend on m c . Here we show how these constants can be determined from a practical four-flavor lattice QCD calculation performed on a small volume with u and d quark masses that are heavier than physical.","authors":"En-Hung Chao, Norman Christ, Ceran Hu","topics":["particle"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/bf4j-b7sm","id":"doi:10.1103/bf4j-b7sm","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/bf4j-b7sm","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Nielsen complexity with multiple cost factors","summary":"We investigate Nielsen’s geometric approach to quantum complexity in the presence of multiple cost factors, extending the standard framework where a single penalty distinguishes easy from hard directions of the group manifold. By introducing a hierarchy of penalties associated with different degrees of nonlocality, we develop a generalized right-invariant complexity geometry and analyze its implications for geodesic evolution. We derive the modified Euler-Arnold and Jacobi equations and study how multiple cost factors reshape the structure and scaling of conjugate points, where geodesic optimality breaks down. The formalism is illustrated in two settings: a single-qubit system with two cost factors, where we develop a semianalytic approach to investigate complexity growth and its dependence on penalty hierarchies, and Sachdev-Ye-Kitaev-type models, where we analyze both free and chaotic regimes. In these many-body systems, we show that distinct nonlocal sectors generate multiple families of conjugate points whose occurrence depends on both the cost hierarchy and the system size. Our results highlight how refining the penalty structure provides a richer and more realistic description of quantum complexity and its dynamical behavior.","authors":"Marcos Rios Ribeiro, Diego Trancanelli","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/9f49-zn38","id":"doi:10.1103/9f49-zn38","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/9f49-zn38","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Charged Higgs boson search at future neutrino telescope and Higgs factory","summary":"","authors":"YiCheng Dai, Wei Liao, Yi-Song Lu, Qi-Shu Yan","topics":["particle"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review D","doi":"10.1103/y256-883q","id":"doi:10.1103/y256-883q","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/y256-883q","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Determining all dark matter-electron scattering rates from material properties","summary":"","authors":"Yonit Hochberg, Majed Khalaf, Alessandro Lenoci, Rotem Ovadia","topics":["dark"],"categories":[]},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/w2v4-7pvc","id":"doi:10.1103/w2v4-7pvc","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/w2v4-7pvc","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Dimensionality reduction for closed-loop quantum gate calibration","summary":"","authors":"Emma Berger, Vivek Maurya, Z. M. McIntyre, Ken Xuan Wei, Holger Haas, Daniel Puzzuoli","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/4zcx-b66r","id":"doi:10.1103/4zcx-b66r","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/4zcx-b66r","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Lamp and laser integrated pulsed optically pumped (LaLI-POP) vapor-cell atomic clock","summary":"","authors":"Michael Huang, James Camparo","topics":["sensing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Applied","doi":"10.1103/q2j3-mftf","id":"doi:10.1103/q2j3-mftf","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/q2j3-mftf","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Inherent flux crosstalk and coupler-driven single-qubit gates in superconducting circuits","summary":"Crosstalk refers to unwanted qubit addressing. This is particularly detrimental when scaling up quantum information systems because unintended interactions limit their overall performance. For superconducting qubits, tunable couplings and frequency tunability achieved through externally applied magnetic fluxes enable high-fidelity entangling gates; however, they also introduce crosstalk through unintended flux coupling. In this work, we investigate the impact of time-dependent external magnetic fluxes in quantized circuits on superconducting qubit couplings. We find that nontrivial cross-voltage driving emerges between capacitively linked qubits when the magnetic flux threading the superconducting loop of a qubit varies in time, in a manner analogous to Faraday’s law of induction. Crucially, we show that this effect enables fast single-qubit control through the coupler element in standard tunable-coupler architectures, potentially eliminating the need for individual microwave X Y control lines.","authors":"Balázs Gulácsi, Guido Burkard","topics":["computing"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/6jtz-g59p","id":"doi:10.1103/6jtz-g59p","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/6jtz-g59p","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Exclusive control of quantum memory erasure","summary":"Erasing memory is a fundamental operational task in quantum information processing, governed by Landauer's principle, which links information loss to thermodynamic work. We introduce and analyze , where correlations with a remote system reduce the energetic cost of resetting a memory. We identify of erasure as the central operational requirement: Only a designated party should be able to achieve the minimal cost, whereas any adversary must fail. In the device-dependent regime, we show that entanglement of formation exactly characterizes exclusivity, establishing entanglement as the decisive thermodynamic resource. Moving to a one-sided device-independent scenario, in which only the memory holder's device is trusted, we develop an operational erasure protocol based on random dephasing and conditional operations. Finally, in a fully device-independent setting, we show how Bell nonlocality and self-testing translate observed violations into lower bounds on any adversary's erasure capability, yielding a device-independent notion of exclusive thermodynamic control. Taken together, these results elevate quantum erasure from a thermodynamic constraint to an operational primitive: The erasure work cost quantifies secure, exclusive control over quantum memory, ensuring that an unauthorized agent cannot fully erase information under a bounded work budget.","authors":"Mir Alimuddin, Nathan Shettell, Raja Yehia, Antonio Acín, Federico Centrone","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[],"peerReviewed":true},{"source":"journal","journal":"Physical Review Research","doi":"10.1103/mk9z-q1m3","id":"doi:10.1103/mk9z-q1m3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/mk9z-q1m3","published":"2026-10-20","title":"Network nonlocality with separable measurements","summary":"","authors":"Emanuele Polino, Davide Poderini, Giorgio Minati, Giovanni Rodari, Rafael Chaves, Fabio Sciarrino","topics":["foundations"],"categories":[]}]}}};
